window.BOOK_TEXTS={"GEN":{"t":{"1":"The creation","2":"The seventh day and the garden of Eden","3":"The fall","4":"Cain and Abel","5":"From Adam to Noah","6":"The corruption of the earth and the ark","7":"The flood","8":"The end of the flood","9":"The covenant with Noah","10":"The table of nations","11":"The tower of Babel; from Shem to Abram","12":"The call of Abram","13":"Abram and Lot part ways","14":"Abram rescues Lot; Melchizedek","15":"The covenant with Abram","16":"Hagar and Ishmael","17":"Circumcision; Abram becomes Abraham","18":"The three visitors; pleading for Sodom","19":"The destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah","20":"Abraham and Abimelech","21":"The birth of Isaac","22":"The offering of Isaac","23":"The death of Sarah","24":"A wife for Isaac","25":"The death of Abraham; Esau and Jacob","26":"Isaac and Abimelech","27":"Jacob receives the blessing","28":"Jacob's dream at Bethel","29":"Jacob, Leah and Rachel","30":"The children of Jacob","31":"Jacob leaves Laban","32":"Jacob wrestles with God at Peniel","33":"Jacob meets Esau again","34":"Dinah at Shechem","35":"Back to Bethel; the death of Rachel","36":"The descendants of Esau","37":"Joseph and his brothers","38":"Judah and Tamar","39":"Joseph in Potiphar's house","40":"The dreams of the cupbearer and the baker","41":"Pharaoh's dreams","42":"Joseph's brothers go to Egypt","43":"The second journey, with Benjamin","44":"The silver cup","45":"Joseph reveals himself to his brothers","46":"Jacob goes down to Egypt","47":"Jacob before Pharaoh; the famine","48":"Jacob blesses Ephraim and Manasseh","49":"Jacob's blessings on his sons","50":"The deaths of Jacob and Joseph"},"about":{"title_orig":"בְּרֵאשִׁית","title_orig_xlit":"Bereshit","summary":"Genesis tells of origins: of the world, of humanity and of the people of Israel. Chapters 1 to 11 narrate the creation, the disobedience in the garden of Eden, the flood and the scattering of the peoples at Babel. From chapter 12 on, the story focuses on one family: Abraham receives from God the promise of land, descendants and blessing, and this promise passes to Isaac, to Jacob and to Jacob's twelve sons. The book ends with the family living in Egypt, which sets the stage for Exodus.","name":"The name “Genesis” comes from Greek and means “origin”; it is the title the book received in the Septuagint, the Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible. In Hebrew the book is called Bereshit, “In the beginning,” which is its first word.","facts":[["Position","1st book of the Torah (Pentateuch)"],["Chapters","50"],["Verses","1,533"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","narrative"]],"structure":[{"title":"Origins of the world and of humanity","from":1,"to":11,"text":"Creation, Eden, Cain and Abel, the genealogies, the flood, the covenant with Noah and the tower of Babel."},{"title":"Abraham","from":12,"to":25,"text":"The call, the promise, the covenant and the birth of Isaac."},{"title":"Isaac and Jacob","from":25,"to":36,"text":"Esau and Jacob, the blessing, Jacob's years with Laban and the new name, Israel."},{"title":"Joseph","from":37,"to":50,"text":"Sold by his brothers, Joseph rises to governor of Egypt and is reunited with his family."}],"extra":{"title":"The generations (toledot)","text":"The book is organized around a formula repeated about ten times: “this is the account (or the generations) of…”, in Hebrew toledot. It opens each new stage of the story, from 2:4 to 37:2."},"people":[{"name":"Adam and Eve","chapters":"2–5","ch":2,"note":"the first couple, placed in the garden of Eden"},{"name":"Cain and Abel","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"sons of Adam and Eve; the first murder"},{"name":"Noah","chapters":"5–10","ch":6,"note":"builds the ark and receives the covenant of the rainbow"},{"name":"Abraham and Sarah","chapters":"11–25","ch":12,"note":"leave Haran for Canaan and receive the promise of descendants"},{"name":"Lot","chapters":"11–19","ch":13,"note":"Abraham's nephew; escapes the destruction of Sodom"},{"name":"Hagar and Ishmael","chapters":"16; 21","ch":16,"note":"Sarah's Egyptian servant and the son she bore to Abraham"},{"name":"Isaac and Rebekah","chapters":"21–35","ch":24,"note":"the son of the promise and the wife brought from the land of Abraham's relatives"},{"name":"Esau and Jacob","chapters":"25–36","ch":25,"note":"twin brothers; Jacob receives the name Israel"},{"name":"Leah and Rachel","chapters":"29–35","ch":29,"note":"daughters of Laban and wives of Jacob"},{"name":"Joseph","chapters":"30; 37–50","ch":37,"note":"sold by his brothers, he becomes governor of Egypt"},{"name":"Judah","chapters":"29; 37–50","ch":38,"note":"son of Jacob, with a central role in chapters 38 and 43–44"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Jewish and Christian traditions attribute the Torah to Moses. On this reading, Genesis would have been written during the wandering in the wilderness, around 1446–1406 BC, or in the 13th century BC for those who adopt a later date for the Exodus.","academic":"Most scholars understand that the book brings together oral and written traditions from different periods, edited over centuries. The final form is usually dated to the periods of the Babylonian exile and Persian rule, around 550–400 BC."},"events":{"intro":"Chapters 1 to 11 narrate origins and give no dates that history can place. For Abraham and his descendants, a traditional chronology adds up the ages and intervals given in the text itself and places the Exodus around 1446 BC; its result is what appears on the timeline. There are no records outside the Bible that mention the patriarchs, and many historians consider these narratives impossible to date; those who place them usually point to the Middle Bronze Age, from about 2000 to 1550 BC.","axis":[-2200,-350],"band":{"label":"Middle Bronze Age","from":-2000,"to":-1550},"timeline":[{"label":"Abraham","from":-2166,"to":-1991,"kind":"pessoa","note":"lives 175 years (25:7)"},{"label":"Isaac","from":-2066,"to":-1886,"kind":"pessoa","note":"lives 180 years (35:28)"},{"label":"Jacob","from":-2006,"to":-1859,"kind":"pessoa","note":"lives 147 years (47:28); goes down to Egypt around 1876 BC"},{"label":"Joseph","from":-1915,"to":-1805,"kind":"pessoa","note":"lives 110 years (50:26)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1446,"to":-1406,"kind":"escrita","note":"Moses, during the wandering in the wilderness"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-550,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"Babylonian exile and Persian period"}]},"context":"The stories of Genesis take place in the ancient Near East, between Mesopotamia and Egypt. Abraham sets out from Ur of the Chaldeans, in southern Mesopotamia, passes through Haran and goes on to Canaan; later, Joseph brings the family to the region of Goshen, in Egypt. Scholars compare the first chapters with older Mesopotamian texts, such as the Enuma Elish, about creation, and the epics of Atrahasis and Gilgamesh, which also tell of a great flood. The similarities and the differences help us see what the biblical text affirms about God and human beings.","places":["Eden","Ur of the Chaldeans","Haran","Canaan: Shechem, Bethel, Hebron, Beersheba","Egypt: Goshen"],"themes":["creation","covenant","promise and blessing","family and election","providence"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"12:1–3","text":"Then the LORD said to Abram, “Leave your country, your kindred, and your father’s household, and go to the land I will show you. I will make you into a great nation, and I will bless you; I will make your name great, so that you will be a blessing. I will bless those who bless you and curse those who curse you; and all the families of the earth will be blessed through you.”"},{"ref":"15:6","text":"Abram believed the LORD, and it was credited to him as righteousness."},{"ref":"50:20","text":"As for you, what you intended against me for evil, God intended for good, in order to accomplish a day like this—to preserve the lives of many people."}],"note":"Dates vary according to the source and the way of counting; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"EXO":{"t":{"1":"Israel multiplies and is oppressed in Egypt","2":"Birth of Moses and flight to Midian","3":"The burning bush and the name of God","4":"Signs for Moses and the return to Egypt","5":"Pharaoh refuses and increases the forced labor","6":"The promise renewed and the family of Moses","7":"Aaron's staff; the water turns to blood","8":"Frogs, gnats and flies","9":"Plague on livestock, boils and hail","10":"Locusts and darkness","11":"The death of the firstborn announced","12":"The Passover and the exodus from Egypt","13":"Consecration of the firstborn; the cloud and the fire","14":"Crossing the sea","15":"The song of Moses; the waters of Marah","16":"Manna and quail","17":"Water from the rock and the battle with Amalek","18":"Jethro visits Moses; judges for the people","19":"The people before Mount Sinai","20":"The Ten Commandments","21":"Laws on servants, assaults and damages","22":"Restitution, loans and protection of the weak","23":"Justice, rest and the three annual feasts","24":"The covenant is sealed with blood","25":"Offerings; the ark, the table and the lampstand","26":"The curtains and the frame of the tabernacle","27":"The altar of burnt offering and the courtyard","28":"The garments of the priests","29":"The consecration of Aaron and his sons","30":"Altar of incense, ransom, basin and anointing","31":"Bezalel and Oholiab; the Sabbath as a sign","32":"The golden calf","33":"Moses intercedes and asks to see the glory","34":"New tablets and the renewal of the covenant","35":"Freewill offerings and craftsmen for the work","36":"More than enough; curtains and frames","37":"The ark and the gold furnishings","38":"Altar, basin, courtyard and the metals counted","39":"The priestly garments and the work completed","40":"The tabernacle is set up and filled with glory"},"about":{"title_orig":"שְׁמוֹת","title_orig_xlit":"Shemot","summary":"Exodus tells how Jacob's descendants, already numerous in Egypt, pass from slavery to freedom and become a people bound to God by a covenant. Chapters 1 to 15 narrate the oppression under Pharaoh, the call of Moses at the bush that burned without being consumed, the plagues on Egypt, the first Passover and the crossing of the sea. After a march through the wilderness, the people reach Mount Sinai, where they hear the Ten Commandments, receive a body of laws and commit themselves to the covenant (chs. 19–24). The last part revolves around the tabernacle, the portable sanctuary that would mark God's presence among Israel: the instructions for making it, the crisis of the golden calf and the carrying out of the work, which ends with the glory of the LORD filling the tent.","name":"In Hebrew the book is called Shemot, “Names,” a word taken from the opening sentence: “These are the names of the sons of Israel” (1:1). The title “Exodus” comes from the Septuagint, in which Exodos means “going out,” and passed into the Latin of the Vulgate; it sums up the central event of the book, the departure from Egypt.","facts":[["Position","2nd book of the Torah (Pentateuch)"],["Chapters","40"],["Verses","1,213"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","narrative and law"]],"structure":[{"title":"Oppression and call","from":1,"to":4,"text":"Israel grows and is enslaved; Moses is born, raised at court, flees to Midian and is called by God at Mount Horeb."},{"title":"The confrontation with Pharaoh","from":5,"to":11,"text":"Moses and Aaron ask for the people's release; in the face of Pharaoh's refusals, nine plagues strike Egypt and the tenth is announced."},{"title":"Passover and deliverance","from":12,"to":15,"text":"The first Passover, the death of Egypt's firstborn, the departure, the crossing of the sea and the song of victory (15:1–21)."},{"title":"Toward Sinai","from":15,"to":18,"text":"Thirst, hunger and conflicts in the wilderness: the waters of Marah, the manna, water from the rock, the battle against Amalek and Jethro's visit."},{"title":"The covenant at Sinai","from":19,"to":24,"text":"God reveals Himself on the mountain, gives the Ten Commandments and the laws of the so-called Book of the Covenant (20:22–23:33), and the covenant is confirmed with blood (24:8)."},{"title":"Instructions for the tabernacle","from":25,"to":31,"text":"On the mountain, Moses receives the pattern of the sanctuary, its furnishings and the priestly garments, as well as the rules for consecrating the priests."},{"title":"The golden calf and the covenant renewed","from":32,"to":34,"text":"While Moses is on the mountain, the people worship a golden calf; Moses intercedes, goes up again with new tablets and the covenant is renewed."},{"title":"The building of the tabernacle","from":35,"to":40,"text":"The people's offerings, the work of Bezalel and Oholiab and the setting up of the sanctuary, on which the cloud descends."}],"extra":{"title":"Order given, order carried out","text":"The instructions for the tabernacle (chs. 25–31) return almost word for word in chapters 35 to 40, now as an account of what was done. The phrase “as the LORD had commanded Moses,” with small variations, is repeated throughout chapters 39 and 40 (seven times in the last one alone) and underlines that the work followed the pattern shown on the mountain (25:40). Some scholars see in this echoes of the creation account in Genesis: the work advances in stages, Moses inspects it and blesses (39:43), and the Sabbath appears between the instructions and their execution (31:12–17; 35:1–3)."},"people":[{"name":"Moses","chapters":"2–40","ch":3,"note":"a Levite raised at the Egyptian court; leads the people out of Egypt and is the mediator of the covenant at Sinai"},{"name":"Aaron","chapters":"4–40","ch":4,"note":"Moses' older brother and his spokesman; chosen for the priesthood, he gives in to the people and makes the golden calf"},{"name":"Pharaoh","chapters":"1–14","ch":5,"note":"the king of Egypt, never called by name; resists the command to let the people go"},{"name":"Shiphrah and Puah","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"midwives of the Hebrew women who refuse to kill the newborn boys"},{"name":"Miriam","chapters":"2; 15","ch":15,"note":"Aaron's sister, called a prophetess; leads the women in song after the crossing of the sea"},{"name":"Jethro","chapters":"2–4; 18","ch":18,"note":"priest of Midian and Moses' father-in-law, also called Reuel (2:18); advises dividing the work of judging"},{"name":"Zipporah","chapters":"2; 4; 18","ch":4,"note":"Moses' wife; circumcises their son during the journey to Egypt (4:24–26)"},{"name":"Joshua","chapters":"17; 24; 32–33","ch":17,"note":"Moses' assistant; commands the battle against Amalek"},{"name":"Bezalel and Oholiab","chapters":"31; 35–38","ch":31,"note":"craftsmen from Judah and Dan in charge of building the tabernacle"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Jewish and Christian traditions attribute Exodus, like the whole Torah, to Moses; the book itself says he wrote certain parts (17:14; 24:4; 34:27). On this reading, the text would have been composed during the wandering in the wilderness, around 1446–1406 BC for those who follow the earlier date of the Exodus, or in the 13th century BC for those who adopt the later date.","academic":"For most scholars, Exodus brings together traditions of different origins and periods, edited over centuries. It is common to distinguish material of a priestly kind, very present in the laws of worship and the description of the tabernacle (chs. 25–31; 35–40), from narratives of other provenance; the Book of the Covenant (20:22–23:33) is usually seen as one of the oldest law collections in the Bible. The final form is generally dated to the exilic and Persian periods, around 550–400 BC. There are also researchers who argue for an ancient core, linked to the memory of Semitic groups who left Egypt."},"events":{"intro":"The book does not give Pharaoh's name, and no known Egyptian document records Israel's departure; for this reason, the date of the Exodus is debated. One position starts from 1 Kings 6:1, which puts 480 years between the departure from Egypt and Solomon's fourth year, and arrives at around 1446 BC, during Egypt's 18th dynasty. Another, based on the name of the city of Rameses (1:11) and on archaeological data from Canaan, prefers the 13th century BC, in the reign of Ramesses II, and reads the number 480 as a symbolic sum of generations. There are also historians who see in the account the memory of smaller groups, enlarged by tradition, or who consider it impossible to date. The timeline shows the two most cited proposals; the periods of Israel in Egypt and of Moses' life follow the traditional chronology.","axis":[-1900,-350],"band":{"label":"Egyptian New Kingdom","from":-1550,"to":-1070},"timeline":[{"label":"Israel in Egypt","from":-1876,"to":-1446,"kind":"evento","note":"430 years (12:40), in the traditional chronology"},{"label":"Moses","from":-1526,"to":-1406,"kind":"pessoa","note":"80 years old when he speaks to Pharaoh (7:7) and 120 at his death (Deuteronomy 34:7), in the traditional chronology"},{"label":"Departure from Egypt, early date","from":-1446,"to":-1445,"kind":"evento","note":"by the reckoning of 1 Kings 6:1; the tabernacle is set up in the second year (40:17)"},{"label":"Ramesses II","from":-1279,"to":-1213,"kind":"pessoa","note":"pharaoh associated with the city of Rameses (1:11); not named in the Bible"},{"label":"Departure from Egypt, late date","from":-1270,"to":-1250,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate range proposed by those who prefer the 13th century BC"},{"label":"Merneptah Stele","from":-1208,"to":-1208,"kind":"evento","note":"Egyptian inscription with the oldest known mention of “Israel,” already in Canaan (approximate date)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1446,"to":-1406,"kind":"escrita","note":"Moses, during the wandering in the wilderness (in the 13th century BC, on the late date)"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-550,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"Babylonian exile and Persian period"}]},"context":"The first part of the book takes place in the Nile delta, in northeastern Egypt, where the region of Goshen lay and where the Israelites are forced to build the store cities of Pithom and Rameses (1:11). Egyptian documents of the New Kingdom record Semites living and working in the delta and herdsmen coming from Canaan in search of water and pasture; the name Moses recalls Egyptian names formed with the element ms, “born of,” as in Ramesses. The route of the departure is uncertain. The “Red Sea” of the text, in Hebrew yam suf (perhaps “sea of reeds”), may be one of the lakes of the Isthmus of Suez or an arm of the Red Sea itself. The location of Mount Sinai, also called Horeb, is likewise debated: tradition identifies it with Jebel Musa, in the south of the Sinai Peninsula, but there are proposals in the north of the peninsula and in northwestern Arabia, near Midian, the land of Jethro.","places":["Egypt: Goshen, Pithom and Rameses (1:11)","Midian","Horeb, the mountain of God (3:1)","the Red Sea (yam suf)","Marah and Elim (15:23–27)","the Wilderness of Sin and Rephidim","Mount Sinai"],"themes":["deliverance","the name of God","covenant","law","God's presence among the people","worship and holiness"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"3:14","text":"God said to Moses, “I AM WHO I AM. This is what you are to say to the Israelites: ‘I AM has sent me to you.’”"},{"ref":"19:5–6","text":"Now if you will indeed obey My voice and keep My covenant, you will be My treasured possession out of all the nations—for the whole earth is Mine. And unto Me you shall be a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.’ These are the words that you are to speak to the Israelites.”"},{"ref":"20:2–3","text":"“I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of slavery. You shall have no other gods before Me."},{"ref":"34:6–7","text":"Then the LORD passed in front of Moses and called out: “The LORD, the LORD God, is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in loving devotion and faithfulness, maintaining loving devotion to a thousand generations, forgiving iniquity, transgression, and sin. Yet He will by no means leave the guilty unpunished; He will visit the iniquity of the fathers on their children and grandchildren to the third and fourth generations.”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and depend on the position taken on the Exodus. The numbering follows that of English Bibles; in the Hebrew text, 8:1–4 appears as 7:26–29 and 22:1 as 21:37, which shifts the following verses of those chapters. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"LEV":{"t":{"1":"The burnt offering, consumed whole","2":"The grain offering","3":"The peace offering","4":"Atonement for unintentional sins","5":"Offerings for sin and for guilt","6":"Restitution and rules for the priests","7":"Laws of the guilt and peace offerings","8":"The ordination of Aaron and his sons","9":"Aaron's first sacrifices","10":"Nadab, Abihu and the unauthorized fire","11":"Clean and unclean animals","12":"Purification after childbirth","13":"Leprosy of the skin and of clothing","14":"Cleansing the leper and the houses","15":"Bodily discharges and uncleanness","16":"The Day of Atonement","17":"The blood and the place of sacrifice","18":"Forbidden sexual relations","19":"Holiness in daily life and love of neighbor","20":"Penalties for idolatry and forbidden unions","21":"The holiness of the priests","22":"Holy things and acceptable animals","23":"The Sabbath and the feasts of the year","24":"The lamps, the bread and the blasphemer","25":"The sabbatical year and the Jubilee","26":"Blessings and punishments of the covenant","27":"Vows, redemption and tithes"},"about":{"title_orig":"וַיִּקְרָא","title_orig_xlit":"Vayikra","summary":"Leviticus gathers the instructions Moses receives in the newly erected tabernacle about how Israel can live close to a holy God. Chapters 1 to 7 describe the sacrifices and offerings; chapters 8 to 10 tell of the ordination of Aaron and his sons and the death of Nadab and Abihu. Then come the laws on clean and unclean (food, childbirth, skin disease, bodily discharges) and the annual ritual of the Day of Atonement (ch. 16). Chapters 17 to 26 extend the demand for holiness to daily life: family, sexuality, work, trade, the calendar of feasts and the possession of the land, with the sabbatical year and the Jubilee. There is almost no narrative, and the whole book takes place at the foot of Mount Sinai.","name":"In Hebrew the book is called Vayikra, “And He called,” its first word (1:1); in rabbinic tradition it is also known as Torat Kohanim, “instruction of the priests.” The name “Leviticus” comes from the Greek Leuitikon, of the Septuagint, through the Latin Leviticus, and means “relating to the Levites,” that is, to the tribe of Levi, from which the priests came.","facts":[["Position","3rd book of the Torah (Pentateuch)"],["Chapters","27"],["Verses","859"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","ritual and ethical laws, with brief narratives"]],"structure":[{"title":"Sacrifices and offerings","from":1,"to":7,"text":"The main kinds of offering (burnt offering, grain offering, peace offering, sin offering and guilt offering) and the instructions to the priests about each one."},{"title":"The beginning of the priesthood","from":8,"to":10,"text":"Moses consecrates Aaron and his sons; Aaron offers the first sacrifices, and Nadab and Abihu die when they present unauthorized fire."},{"title":"Clean and unclean","from":11,"to":15,"text":"Animals that may or may not be eaten, purification after childbirth, skin disease and mildew in clothing and houses, and bodily discharges."},{"title":"The Day of Atonement","from":16,"to":16,"text":"The annual rite in which Aaron enters behind the veil, before the ark, and a goat is sent into the wilderness carrying the people's iniquities."},{"title":"Laws of holiness","from":17,"to":26,"text":"Blood and the place of sacrifice, forbidden relations, everyday ethics, rules for the priests, the feasts, the sabbatical year and the Jubilee, and the blessings and punishments tied to the covenant."},{"title":"Vows and dedications","from":27,"to":27,"text":"An appendix on the value of persons, animals and lands vowed to the LORD and on the redemption of tithes."}],"extra":{"title":"The refrain “I am the LORD”","text":"In chapters 18 to 26, many laws end with the phrase “I am the LORD” or “I am the LORD your God,” which appears more than forty times in this section and fifteen in chapter 19 alone. The refrain works like a signature: it grounds each rule in God's authority and echoes the request that opens chapter 19, “Be holy because I, the LORD your God, am holy” (19:2). Because of this emphasis on holiness, scholars gave this part, at the end of the 19th century, the name “Holiness Code.”"},"people":[{"name":"Moses","chapters":"1–27","ch":8,"note":"receives the laws in the tabernacle and passes them on; leads the consecration of the priests"},{"name":"Aaron","chapters":"8–10; 16","ch":8,"note":"Moses' brother and the first high priest; officiates the ritual of the Day of Atonement"},{"name":"Nadab and Abihu","chapters":"10","ch":10,"note":"Aaron's eldest sons, killed when they offered fire that had not been commanded"},{"name":"Eleazar and Ithamar","chapters":"10","ch":10,"note":"Aaron's other sons, who carry on the priestly service"},{"name":"The son of Shelomith","chapters":"24","ch":24,"note":"son of an Israelite mother and an Egyptian father; stoned for blaspheming the Name"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Like the rest of the Torah, Leviticus is attributed to Moses by Jewish and Christian traditions. The book itself presents its laws as words of the LORD to Moses, spoken in the tabernacle and on Mount Sinai (1:1; 7:38; 26:46; 27:34). On this reading, it would have been written during the wandering in the wilderness, around 1446–1406 BC, or in the 13th century BC for those who adopt the later date of the Exodus.","academic":"For most scholars, Leviticus belongs almost entirely to the priestly layer of the Pentateuch, linked to circles of priests in Jerusalem. Chapters 17 to 26 are usually treated as a collection of their own, the Holiness Code, which many consider the work of a later school that revised the priestly material. The proposed dates range from the end of the monarchy in Judah to the Persian period, and the final form is generally placed around 550–400 BC. Other researchers, drawing on parallels with Near Eastern rituals of the second millennium BC, argue that much of these practices and texts goes back to the time of the first temple or earlier."},"events":{"intro":"Leviticus narrates almost no events: its laws are given within about a month, between the setting up of the tabernacle, on the first day of the first month of the second year (Exodus 40:17), and the census on the first day of the second month (Numbers 1:1). The date of this period depends on the position taken on the Exodus: c. 1445 BC in the traditional chronology, or some time in the 13th century BC for those who prefer the late date. The timeline shows both possibilities, the lives of Moses and Aaron in the traditional chronology and the proposals for the writing.","axis":[-1600,-350],"band":{"label":"First temple in Jerusalem","from":-960,"to":-586},"timeline":[{"label":"Moses","from":-1526,"to":-1406,"kind":"pessoa","note":"80 years old before Pharaoh (Exodus 7:7) and 120 at his death (Deuteronomy 34:7), in the traditional chronology"},{"label":"Aaron","from":-1529,"to":-1406,"kind":"pessoa","note":"83 years old before Pharaoh (Exodus 7:7) and 123 at his death (Numbers 33:39), in the traditional chronology"},{"label":"At Sinai, early date","from":-1445,"to":-1445,"kind":"evento","note":"about a month between Exodus 40:17 and Numbers 1:1"},{"label":"At Sinai, late date","from":-1270,"to":-1250,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate range, for those who place the Exodus in the 13th century BC"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1446,"to":-1406,"kind":"escrita","note":"Moses, during the wandering in the wilderness"},{"label":"Composition, according to scholarship","from":-700,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"from the end of the monarchy in Judah to the Persian period; the final form is usually placed c. 550–400 BC"}]},"context":"Leviticus assumes Israel's camp in the wilderness, with the tabernacle at the center, and describes rites that would later be practiced in the temple in Jerusalem. Animal sacrifices, purity rules and festival calendars were common in the ancient Near East: texts from Ugarit, on the Syrian coast, and Hittite and Mesopotamian documents describe offerings and purifications with parallels in vocabulary and procedure, including rites in which an animal is sent away carrying evil with it, comparable to the goat of chapter 16. Alongside these similarities, the book ties worship and purity to a single God and to a social ethic that includes the poor, the foreigner, the deaf, the blind and the day laborer (19:9–18, 33–34).","places":["the Wilderness of Sinai, at the foot of the mountain (7:38; 25:1)","the tabernacle of the Testimony and its courtyard","the camp and the space “outside the camp”","the wilderness where the goat goes (16:21–22)","Egypt and Canaan, as customs to avoid (18:3)"],"themes":["holiness","sacrifice and atonement","clean and unclean","priesthood","sacred calendar","social justice and Jubilee"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"16:30","text":"because on this day atonement will be made for you to cleanse you, and you will be clean from all your sins before the LORD."},{"ref":"17:11","text":"For the life of the flesh is in the blood, and I have given it to you to make atonement for your souls upon the altar; for it is the blood that makes atonement for the soul."},{"ref":"19:2","text":"“Speak to the whole congregation of Israel and tell them: Be holy because I, the LORD your God, am holy."},{"ref":"19:18","text":"Do not seek revenge or bear a grudge against any of your people, but love your neighbor as yourself. I am the LORD."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and depend on the position taken on the Exodus. The numbering follows that of English Bibles; in the Hebrew text, 6:1–7 appears as 5:20–26 and 6:8–30 as 6:1–23. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"NUM":{"t":{"1":"The first census of the tribes","2":"The arrangement of the tribes in the camp","3":"The Levites and the redemption of the firstborn","4":"The duties of Kohath, Gershon and Merari","5":"Purity of the camp; the test of jealousy","6":"The Nazirite vow and the priestly blessing","7":"The offerings of the tribal leaders","8":"The lampstand and the consecration of the Levites","9":"The second Passover; the cloud over the tabernacle","10":"The silver trumpets; departure from Sinai","11":"Complaints in the wilderness; quail and seventy elders","12":"Miriam and Aaron against Moses","13":"The twelve spies in Canaan","14":"The people rebel; the forty years","15":"Laws on offerings; tassels on garments","16":"The rebellion of Korah, Dathan and Abiram","17":"Aaron's staff buds","18":"Duties and support of priests and Levites","19":"The red heifer and the water of purification","20":"Deaths of Miriam and Aaron; water from the rock","21":"The bronze serpent; Sihon and Og","22":"Balak summons Balaam; the donkey","23":"Balaam's first oracles","24":"Balaam's last oracles","25":"Idolatry at Baal of Peor; the zeal of Phinehas","26":"The second census on the plains of Moab","27":"The daughters of Zelophehad; Joshua succeeds Moses","28":"Daily, Sabbath and festival offerings","29":"The feasts of the seventh month","30":"Vows and oaths","31":"The war against Midian","32":"Reuben, Gad and Manasseh east of the Jordan","33":"The stages of the journey from Egypt","34":"The borders of Canaan","35":"Levitical cities and cities of refuge","36":"The inheritance of Zelophehad's daughters"},"about":{"title_orig":"בְּמִדְבַּר","title_orig_xlit":"Bemidbar","summary":"Numbers follows Israel from Mount Sinai to the plains of Moab, by the Jordan, across from Jericho. It begins with the census of the tribes and the organization of the camp around the tabernacle and, after the departure from Sinai, narrates a series of complaints and rebellions along the way. When the people refuse to enter Canaan after the spies' report, the generation that came out of Egypt is condemned to wander forty years in the wilderness. The last part takes place on the plains of Moab: Balaam's oracles, a new census of the next generation, the choice of Joshua and laws for life in the land. Narratives and laws alternate throughout the book.","name":"The name “Numbers” comes from the Latin Numeri, which translates the Greek Arithmoi, the title given in the Septuagint because of the two censuses (chs. 1 and 26). In Hebrew the book is called Bemidbar, “In the wilderness,” a word that already appears in the first verse.","facts":[["Position","4th book of the Torah (Pentateuch)"],["Chapters","36"],["Verses","1,288"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","narrative and law, with lists and poems"]],"structure":[{"title":"At Sinai","from":1,"to":10,"text":"Census, the order of the camp, the duties of the Levites, purity laws and the dedication of the tabernacle, up to the departure in 10:11."},{"title":"On the way, through the wilderness","from":10,"to":21,"text":"Complaints, the refusal before Canaan, Korah's rebellion, the deaths of Miriam and Aaron and the first victories east of the Jordan."},{"title":"On the plains of Moab","from":22,"to":36,"text":"Balaam, Baal of Peor, the second census, Joshua as successor, the calendar of offerings, the war against Midian and the preparation for dividing the land."}],"extra":{"title":"Two generations, two censuses","text":"The censuses of chapters 1 and 26 frame the book. The first counts the generation that came out of Egypt; the second, taken almost forty years later, counts their children, and the text notes that of those counted at Sinai only Caleb and Joshua remained (26:64–65). Many readers see this as the key to the book: the passage from a generation that dies in the wilderness to another that is ready to enter the land."},"people":[{"name":"Moses","chapters":"1–36","ch":1,"note":"leads the people, intercedes for them and receives the laws; will not be able to enter Canaan (20:12)"},{"name":"Aaron","chapters":"1–20","ch":17,"note":"high priest; his staff buds, and he dies on Mount Hor"},{"name":"Miriam","chapters":"12; 20","ch":12,"note":"Moses' sister; becomes leprous for a week and dies at Kadesh"},{"name":"Eleazar","chapters":"3–36","ch":20,"note":"Aaron's son; succeeds his father in the priesthood"},{"name":"Joshua and Caleb","chapters":"13–14; 26–27; 32; 34","ch":14,"note":"the two spies who argue for entering Canaan; Joshua is chosen as Moses' successor"},{"name":"Korah, Dathan and Abiram","chapters":"16","ch":16,"note":"lead a revolt against Moses and Aaron"},{"name":"Balaam and Balak","chapters":"22–24; 31","ch":22,"note":"the diviner summoned by the king of Moab to curse Israel, who ends up blessing it"},{"name":"Phinehas","chapters":"25; 31","ch":25,"note":"Aaron's grandson; acts violently at Baal of Peor and receives a covenant of priesthood"},{"name":"The daughters of Zelophehad","chapters":"27; 36","ch":27,"note":"Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah and Tirzah ask for the inheritance of their father, who had no sons"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Like the rest of the Torah, Numbers is attributed to Moses by Jewish and Christian traditions; the book itself says that Moses recorded the stages of the journey (33:2). On this reading, it would have been written during the wandering in the wilderness, around 1446–1406 BC, or in the 13th century BC for those who adopt the later date of the Exodus.","academic":"For most scholars, Numbers combines priestly material (lists, censuses, laws of worship and purity) with non-priestly narratives, such as those of the spies and of Balaam, often with the two layers interwoven in the same episode. Some consider the book one of the latest parts of the Pentateuch, written to link the laws of Sinai to the conquest, and the final form is usually placed in the Persian period, around 500–400 BC. Some poetic passages, such as the fragment of the “Book of the Wars of the LORD” (21:14) and Balaam's oracles, are considered by many to be older than the whole."},"events":{"intro":"Numbers claims to cover almost forty years: from the second month of the second year after the departure from Egypt (1:1) to the fortieth year, in which Aaron dies (33:38). Most of this period passes in silence; the book concentrates on the beginning and the end of the journey. The dates depend on the position taken on the Exodus: around 1446–1406 BC in the traditional chronology, or the 13th century BC for those who prefer the late date. Outside the Bible, an inscription found at Deir Alla, in Jordan, mentions a seer named Balaam son of Beor; it is much later than the setting of the book and does not confirm the account, but it shows that the figure was known in the region.","axis":[-1550,-350],"band":{"label":"Late Bronze Age","from":-1550,"to":-1200},"timeline":[{"label":"Moses","from":-1526,"to":-1406,"kind":"pessoa","note":"120 years old at his death (Deuteronomy 34:7), in the traditional chronology"},{"label":"Wandering in the wilderness, early date","from":-1446,"to":-1406,"kind":"evento","note":"the forty years (14:33–34); the departure from Sinai in 10:11 falls c. 1445 BC"},{"label":"Death of Aaron","from":-1407,"to":-1406,"kind":"evento","note":"in the fortieth year, at 123 years of age (33:38–39), in the traditional chronology"},{"label":"Wandering in the wilderness, late date","from":-1270,"to":-1220,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate range, for those who place the Exodus in the 13th century BC"},{"label":"Deir Alla inscription","from":-840,"to":-760,"kind":"evento","note":"plaster text that speaks of Balaam son of Beor; approximate date"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1446,"to":-1406,"kind":"escrita","note":"Moses, during the wandering in the wilderness"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-500,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian period, drawing on older traditions and poems"}]},"context":"The book moves between Sinai, whose exact location is debated, the Wilderness of Paran, the oasis of Kadesh (Kadesh-barnea), in the south of Canaan, and Transjordan, where Israel goes around Edom and confronts the Amorite kings Sihon of Heshbon and Og of Bashan. The plains of Moab, on the east bank of the Jordan across from Jericho, are the setting of the final chapters. The kingdoms of Edom, Moab and Ammon, mentioned in the book, are known from outside sources mainly from the Iron Age on; the stele of Mesha, king of Moab (c. 840 BC), names places such as Nebo, Heshbon and Dibon, which also appear in Numbers.","places":["the Wilderness of Sinai","the Wilderness of Paran","Kadesh, in the Wilderness of Zin","Mount Hor","Heshbon and Bashan","the plains of Moab, by the Jordan across from Jericho","Baal of Peor and Shittim"],"themes":["God's presence in the camp","grumbling and rebellion","intercession","holiness and priesthood","the promise of the land","a new generation"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"6:24–26","text":"‘May the LORD bless you and keep you; may the LORD cause His face to shine upon you and be gracious to you; may the LORD lift up His countenance toward you and give you peace.’"},{"ref":"21:8–9","text":"Then the LORD said to Moses, “Make a fiery serpent and mount it on a pole. When anyone who is bitten looks at it, he will live.” So Moses made a bronze snake and mounted it on a pole. If anyone who was bitten looked at the bronze snake, he would live."},{"ref":"24:17","text":"I see him, but not now; I behold him, but not near. A star will come forth from Jacob, and a scepter will arise from Israel. He will crush the skulls of Moab and strike down all the sons of Sheth."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and depend on the position taken on the Exodus. The census totals (603,550 in 1:46 and 601,730 in 26:51, adult men only) are debated: some read them literally, some take them as symbolic or enlarged numbers, and some propose that the Hebrew word translated “thousand” (elef) indicated there a military unit or a clan. The numbering follows that of English Bibles; in the Hebrew text, 16:36–50 appears as 17:1–15, 17:1–13 as 17:16–28 and 29:40 as 30:1. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"DEU":{"t":{"1":"From Horeb to Kadesh-barnea","2":"Edom, Moab, Ammon and the victory over Sihon","3":"The victory over Og; Moses' request","4":"A call to faithfulness; cities of refuge","5":"The Ten Commandments at Horeb","6":"Hear, O Israel: loving the LORD","7":"A people chosen and set apart","8":"Remember the wilderness in the good land","9":"Not for your own righteousness; the golden calf","10":"The new tablets; what God requires","11":"Love and obey; blessing and curse","12":"One place of worship","13":"Against those who entice to idolatry","14":"Clean foods and the tithe","15":"The year of release; slaves and firstborn","16":"The three annual feasts; just judges","17":"Judges, the central court and the king","18":"Priests, Levites and a prophet like Moses","19":"Cities of refuge and false witnesses","20":"Laws of warfare","21":"Unsolved murder; family and inheritance","22":"Various laws; marriage and sexual purity","23":"Admission to the assembly; various laws","24":"Divorce, loans and protection of the poor","25":"Justice, levirate marriage and honest weights","26":"Firstfruits and tithes; the covenant confirmed","27":"An altar on Ebal and solemn curses","28":"Blessings and curses","29":"The covenant on the plains of Moab","30":"Returning to God and choosing life","31":"Joshua succeeds Moses; the law is written","32":"The song of Moses","33":"Moses blesses the tribes","34":"The death of Moses"},"about":{"title_orig":"דְּבָרִים","title_orig_xlit":"Devarim","summary":"Deuteronomy presents itself as the words of Moses to Israel on the plains of Moab, in the last year of the wandering, shortly before the people cross the Jordan. Moses recalls the journey from Horeb, repeats the Ten Commandments and urges the people to love and serve the LORD alone. At the center is a long code of laws on worship, justice, family, economy and war, with emphasis on the demand for a single place of worship. The book ends with blessings and curses, the renewal of the covenant, the choice of Joshua as successor, the song and blessing of Moses and the account of his death on Mount Nebo.","name":"The name “Deuteronomy” comes from Greek and means “second law.” It arose from the way the Septuagint translated the expression in 17:18, which in Hebrew speaks of a “copy of this law” the king was to make for himself. In Hebrew the book is called Devarim, “Words,” from its opening: “These are the words…”.","facts":[["Position","5th book of the Torah (Pentateuch)"],["Chapters","34"],["Verses","959"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","speeches, laws and poems, within a narrative frame"]],"structure":[{"title":"First speech: memory of the journey","from":1,"to":4,"text":"From Horeb to the plains of Moab, with an appeal to faithfulness (1:1–4:43)."},{"title":"Second speech: exhortation","from":4,"to":11,"text":"The Ten Commandments, the “Hear, O Israel” and the memory of the wilderness and of the golden calf (4:44–11:32)."},{"title":"Second speech: the law code","from":12,"to":26,"text":"Place of worship, feasts, authorities, war, family, social justice and the firstfruits."},{"title":"Blessings and curses","from":27,"to":28,"text":"The ceremony on Mounts Ebal and Gerizim and the consequences of obedience and disobedience."},{"title":"Third speech: the covenant in Moab","from":29,"to":30,"text":"The renewal of the covenant and the choice between life and death."},{"title":"The last days of Moses","from":31,"to":34,"text":"Joshua as successor, the law written and read, the song, the blessing of the tribes and the death of Moses."}],"extra":{"title":"Speeches of Moses and ancient treaties","text":"The book is built as a series of first-person speeches, introduced by titles that work as headings: “These are the words…” (1:1), “This is the law…” (4:44), “These are the terms of the covenant…” (29:1) and “This is the blessing…” (33:1). A narrator speaks of Moses in the third person only in these frames and in the final chapters. Since the mid-20th century, scholars have observed that the sequence of the book recalls the form of the vassal treaties of the ancient Near East, in which a sovereign set the obligations of a subject king: introduction of the parties, historical review of the relationship (chs. 1–11), general and specific stipulations (chs. 12–26), instructions for keeping the document and reading it in public (31:9–13, 24–26), witnesses (heaven and earth, 30:19; 32:1) and blessings and curses (ch. 28). There are two main comparisons. One points to the Hittite treaties of the 14th and 13th centuries BC, which have a historical prologue and blessings alongside curses, and is used by some to argue for the book's antiquity. The other points to the succession treaties of Esarhaddon, king of Assyria (672 BC), whose clauses require the vassal to “love” the heir with all his heart, to denounce anyone who incites revolt and to fear curses very close to those of Deuteronomy 28 (such as the sky of bronze and the earth of iron, 28:23); a copy of these treaties was found in 2009 at Tell Tayinat, in present-day Turkey. For many, this indicates that the book adapted the Assyrian model, putting the LORD in the place of the emperor. Others think the treaty form was known in several periods and that the parallel is not enough to date the text."},"people":[{"name":"Moses","chapters":"1–34","ch":1,"note":"speaks to the people on the plains of Moab and dies on Mount Nebo, at 120 years of age"},{"name":"Joshua","chapters":"1; 3; 31; 34","ch":31,"note":"son of Nun; receives the mission of leading Israel into the land"},{"name":"Caleb","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"the only one of the Exodus generation, besides Joshua, who is promised entry into the land (1:36)"},{"name":"Aaron","chapters":"9–10; 32","ch":9,"note":"remembered in the episode of the golden calf and in the news of his death"},{"name":"Sihon and Og","chapters":"1–4; 29; 31","ch":2,"note":"Amorite kings of Heshbon and Bashan, defeated before the crossing of the Jordan"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Jewish and Christian traditions attribute Deuteronomy to Moses, and the book itself says he wrote “this law” and gave it to the priests (31:9, 24). The account of his death (ch. 34) is usually attributed, in this tradition, to Joshua or to a continuator; the Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) records different opinions about the last eight verses. On this reading, the book would have been written around 1406 BC, at the end of the wandering, or in the 13th century BC for those who adopt the late date of the Exodus.","academic":"Since the 19th century, most scholars have associated the core of the book, especially the code of chapters 12 to 26, with the “Book of the Law” found in the temple in Jerusalem in the reign of Josiah (2 Kings 22–23), around 622 BC, whose reform also centralized worship in Jerusalem. Many think this core may have older roots, perhaps in traditions of the Northern Kingdom, and that the book was expanded during and after the exile, when it received the opening and closing speeches and came to serve as the introduction to the so-called Deuteronomistic History (Joshua to Kings). The final form is usually placed between c. 620 and 450 BC. Other researchers, relying above all on the comparison with the Hittite treaties, argue for a much older origin."},"events":{"intro":"According to the book, Moses' speeches take place on the first day of the eleventh month of the fortieth year after the departure from Egypt (1:3), a few weeks before the crossing of the Jordan; by the traditional chronology, c. 1406 BC, or at the end of the 13th century BC for those who adopt the late date of the Exodus. The timeline also shows the periods of the ancient Near Eastern treaties with which the book is compared and the proposals for its writing.","axis":[-1550,-350],"band":{"label":"Neo-Assyrian Empire","from":-911,"to":-609},"timeline":[{"label":"Moses","from":-1526,"to":-1406,"kind":"pessoa","note":"120 years old at his death (34:7), in the traditional chronology"},{"label":"Speeches in Moab, early date","from":-1406,"to":-1406,"kind":"evento","note":"fortieth year, eleventh month (1:3)"},{"label":"Hittite vassal treaties","from":-1400,"to":-1200,"kind":"escrita","note":"approximate period of the treaties with historical prologue, blessings and curses"},{"label":"Speeches in Moab, late date","from":-1240,"to":-1220,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate range, for those who place the Exodus in the 13th century BC"},{"label":"Succession treaties of Esarhaddon","from":-672,"to":-672,"kind":"escrita","note":"oath imposed on the vassals of Assyria; copy found at Tell Tayinat"},{"label":"Josiah's reform","from":-622,"to":-609,"kind":"evento","note":"the “Book of the Law” found in the temple (2 Kings 22:8)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1406,"to":-1406,"kind":"escrita","note":"Moses, at the end of the wandering (in the 13th century BC, on the late date)"},{"label":"Composition, according to scholarship","from":-650,"to":-450,"kind":"escrita","note":"core linked to the time of Josiah; expansions in the exile and afterward"}]},"context":"The whole book takes place in a single place, the plains of Moab, east of the Jordan, facing the land Israel has not yet occupied. The speeches look back, to Egypt, Horeb and the forty years in the wilderness, and forward, to life in Canaan, with cities, kings, judges, harvests and a central sanctuary at “the place the LORD your God will choose.” This place is not named in the book; later tradition and most scholars identify it with Jerusalem, while some recall that Mounts Ebal and Gerizim, near Shechem, also have a prominent role (11:29; 27:4–13). The language of covenant, loyalty and curse brings the book close to the political documents of the empires of the ancient Near East.","places":["the plains of Moab, beyond the Jordan","Horeb","Kadesh-barnea","Heshbon and Bashan","Mounts Ebal and Gerizim","Mount Nebo, at the top of Pisgah","the place the LORD will choose"],"themes":["covenant","love and loyalty to one God","memory and teaching across generations","one place of worship","justice and care for the fatherless, the widow and the foreigner","blessing and curse"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"6:4","text":"Hear, O Israel: The LORD our God, the LORD is One."},{"ref":"8:3","text":"He humbled you, and in your hunger He gave you manna to eat, which neither you nor your fathers had known, so that you might understand that man does not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of the LORD."},{"ref":"30:19","text":"I call heaven and earth as witnesses against you today that I have set before you life and death, blessing and cursing. Therefore choose life, so that you and your descendants may live,"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and depend on the position taken on the Exodus and on the composition of the book. The numbering follows that of English Bibles; in the Hebrew text, 12:32 appears as 13:1, 22:30 as 23:1 and 29:1 as 28:69, with the rest of those chapters shifted by one verse. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"JOS":{"t":{"1":"Joshua takes the leadership of Israel","2":"Rahab hides the spies in Jericho","3":"Crossing the Jordan","4":"The twelve stones from the Jordan at Gilgal","5":"Circumcision, Passover and the commander of the army","6":"The fall of Jericho","7":"Defeat at Ai and the sin of Achan","8":"Ai is taken; the altar on Ebal","9":"The treaty with the Gibeonites","10":"The battle of Gibeon and conquests in the south","11":"Hazor and the northern campaign","12":"The list of defeated kings","13":"Land still to take; the eastern tribes","14":"Caleb receives Hebron","15":"The territory of Judah","16":"The territory of Ephraim","17":"Manasseh and the daughters of Zelophehad","18":"The assembly at Shiloh; the territory of Benjamin","19":"Six tribes and Joshua's city","20":"The cities of refuge","21":"The cities of the Levites","22":"The altar of the tribes by the Jordan","23":"Joshua exhorts the leaders of Israel","24":"Covenant at Shechem; death of Joshua"},"about":{"title_orig":"יְהוֹשֻׁעַ","title_orig_xlit":"Yehoshua","summary":"Joshua tells how Israel, after the death of Moses, crosses the Jordan River and settles in Canaan, the land promised to the patriarchs. The first half of the book narrates the crossing, the capture of Jericho and Ai, the treaty with the Gibeonites and two great military campaigns, one in the south and the other in the north of the region. The second half describes the division of the land among the tribes, with lists of borders and cities, and sets apart the cities of refuge and the cities assigned to the Levites. The book ends with Joshua's farewell speeches and the renewal of the covenant at Shechem, where the people commit themselves to serve the LORD alone.","name":"The book bears the name of its main character, Joshua son of Nun. In Hebrew, Yehoshua means “the LORD saves” or “the LORD is salvation”; according to Numbers 13:16, it was Moses who gave him this name, in place of Hoshea. In the Septuagint the name appears as Iēsous, the same Greek form the New Testament uses for Jesus.","facts":[["Position","6th book of the Bible, the first of the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, it opens the Former Prophets (Nevi’im Rishonim)"],["Chapters","24"],["Verses","658"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative, with lists of borders and cities"]],"structure":[{"title":"Entering the land","from":1,"to":5,"text":"Joshua takes command, the spies are taken in by Rahab in Jericho, the people cross the Jordan on dry ground and, camped at Gilgal, practice circumcision and celebrate the Passover."},{"title":"The conquests","from":6,"to":12,"text":"The fall of Jericho, the failure and then the victory at Ai, the treaty with Gibeon, the campaigns in the south and the north and the list of the 31 defeated kings."},{"title":"Dividing the land","from":13,"to":21,"text":"The portions of the tribes east and west of the Jordan, Caleb's inheritance, the assembly at Shiloh, the six cities of refuge and the 48 cities of the Levites."},{"title":"Farewells and covenant","from":22,"to":24,"text":"The eastern tribes return home and build an altar that almost causes a war; Joshua takes leave of the leaders and renews the covenant at Shechem before he dies."}],"extra":{"title":"A new Moses and the notes “to this day”","text":"The book draws Joshua in parallel with Moses: the LORD promises to be with him “just as I was with Moses” (3:7), the waters of the Jordan part like those of the Red Sea (4:23), and the commander of the LORD's army tells him to take off his sandals, as in the scene of the burning bush (5:15; Exodus 3:5). Another frequent feature are the notes that link a monument or a place name to the events narrated with the expression “to this day”: the stones in the bed of the Jordan (4:9), the name Gilgal (5:9), the heaps of stones in the Valley of Achor and at Ai (7:26; 8:29) and the Gibeonites in the service of the altar (9:27)."},"people":[{"name":"Joshua","chapters":"1–24","ch":1,"note":"son of Nun, of the tribe of Ephraim; Moses' assistant and his successor at the head of the people"},{"name":"Rahab","chapters":"2; 6","ch":2,"note":"a resident of Jericho who hides the two spies; she and her family are spared"},{"name":"Achan","chapters":"7","ch":7,"note":"of the tribe of Judah; keeps objects devoted to destruction and is executed in the Valley of Achor"},{"name":"The Gibeonites","chapters":"9–10","ch":9,"note":"inhabitants of Gibeon who obtain a treaty with Israel by pretending to come from a distant land"},{"name":"Adoni-zedek","chapters":"10","ch":10,"note":"king of Jerusalem; leads the coalition of five Amorite kings against Gibeon"},{"name":"Jabin","chapters":"11","ch":11,"note":"king of Hazor, at the head of the alliance of the northern kings"},{"name":"Caleb","chapters":"14–15","ch":14,"note":"one of the twelve spies sent by Moses; at 85 years of age, he asks for and receives Hebron"},{"name":"Eleazar","chapters":"14–24","ch":14,"note":"priest, son of Aaron; presides over the division of the land alongside Joshua"},{"name":"The daughters of Zelophehad","chapters":"17","ch":17,"note":"Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah and Tirzah, who receive an inheritance because their father had no sons"},{"name":"Phinehas","chapters":"22","ch":22,"note":"son of Eleazar; heads the delegation sent to the eastern tribes because of the altar by the Jordan"}],"writing":{"tradition":"According to the Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 14b–15a), Joshua wrote the book that bears his name and also the last verses of Deuteronomy; the account of his death would have been completed by Eleazar, and that of Eleazar's death by Phinehas. The text itself says that Joshua recorded the words of the covenant at Shechem “in the Book of the Law of God” (24:26). On this reading, the book was written shortly after the events, around 1400–1370 BC, or at the end of the 13th century BC for those who adopt a later date for the Exodus.","academic":"Since the studies of Martin Noth (1943), many researchers have read Joshua as part of a larger work, the so-called Deuteronomistic History, which would run from Deuteronomy to 2 Kings and interpret Israel's history in light of the law of Deuteronomy. A widespread proposal speaks of a first edition in the reign of Josiah, around 640–609 BC, and a revision during the Babylonian exile, after 562 BC. There are also those who prefer to link Joshua to the Pentateuch, as the conclusion of a Hexateuch, and those who doubt the existence of a single Deuteronomistic work. It is common to hold that the book draws on older sources, such as local traditions, lists of borders and cities and the poetry quoted from the “Book of Jashar” (10:13)."},"events":{"intro":"The book gives no dates. A traditional chronology starts from 1 Kings 6:1, which puts the departure from Egypt 480 years before the start of the building of Solomon's temple, and arrives at the entry into Canaan around 1406 BC; those who place the Exodus in the 13th century BC put the entry around 1250–1200 BC. Archaeology makes the picture more complex: Hazor has a destruction layer from the 13th century BC, but Jericho and Ai (et-Tell) show little or no occupation in that period, and the reading of the older data is debated. Many historians relate Israel's origins to the gradual appearance of villages in the hill country from c. 1200 BC on. The oldest mention of “Israel” outside the Bible is on a stele of Pharaoh Merneptah, from c. 1208 BC.","axis":[-1550,-500],"band":{"label":"Late Bronze Age","from":-1550,"to":-1200},"timeline":[{"label":"Entry into Canaan, traditional chronology","from":-1406,"to":-1399,"kind":"evento","note":"40 years after an Exodus dated c. 1446 BC; about seven years of campaigns (14:7–10)"},{"label":"Joshua at the head of Israel","from":-1406,"to":-1380,"kind":"pessoa","note":"by the traditional chronology; dies at 110 years of age (24:29), at a merely estimated date"},{"label":"Amarna letters","from":-1360,"to":-1330,"kind":"evento","note":"correspondence of the kings of Canaan with Pharaoh; complaints against groups called habiru"},{"label":"Entry into Canaan, 13th-century dating","from":-1250,"to":-1200,"kind":"evento","note":"for those who place the Exodus in the time of Ramesses II"},{"label":"Destruction of Hazor","from":-1300,"to":-1200,"kind":"evento","note":"burning of the Canaanite city (cf. 11:10–13); who caused it is debated"},{"label":"Merneptah Stele","from":-1210,"to":-1205,"kind":"evento","note":"Egyptian inscription that names “Israel” as a people in Canaan"},{"label":"Villages in the hill country","from":-1200,"to":-1000,"kind":"evento","note":"hundreds of small settlements appear, linked by many scholars to Israel's origins"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1400,"to":-1370,"kind":"escrita","note":"Joshua, with the ending completed by Eleazar and Phinehas"},{"label":"Composition, according to scholarship","from":-640,"to":-550,"kind":"escrita","note":"Deuteronomistic work: reign of Josiah and Babylonian exile"}]},"context":"In the Late Bronze Age (c. 1550–1200 BC), Canaan was made up of city-states ruled by local kings, generally under Egyptian rule; the Amarna letters, from the 14th century BC, record these kings' requests to Pharaoh for help. The book's route follows the geography: the people set out from Shittim, on the plains of Moab, cross the Jordan across from Jericho, go up into the central hill country, where Ai, Bethel and Gibeon lay, pursue their enemies down the descent of Beth-horon toward the south and then face the northern kings at the waters of Merom. The text itself admits that many areas remained outside the tribes' control, such as the Philistine coast and Jerusalem (13:2–3; 15:63), a picture that reappears at the beginning of Judges. The practice of devoting to destruction (herem), the complete destruction of a city consecrated to the deity, also appears on the stele of King Mesha of Moab, from the 9th century BC, and is among the most debated points of the book for Jewish and Christian readers.","places":["Shittim, on the plains of Moab","the Jordan River and Gilgal","Jericho and Ai","Gibeon and the Valley of Aijalon","Hazor and the waters of Merom","Shiloh","Shechem, between Mounts Ebal and Gerizim","Hebron"],"themes":["the land as gift and inheritance","faithfulness to the law of Moses","the LORD fights for Israel","devotion to destruction (herem)","covenant and choice","leadership and succession"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:8–9","text":"This Book of the Law must not depart from your mouth; meditate on it day and night, so that you may be careful to do everything written in it. For then you will prosper and succeed in all you do. Have I not commanded you to be strong and courageous? Do not be afraid; do not be discouraged, for the LORD your God is with you wherever you go.”"},{"ref":"21:45","text":"Not one of all the LORD’s good promises to the house of Israel had failed; everything was fulfilled."},{"ref":"24:14","text":"Now, therefore, fear the LORD and serve Him in sincerity and truth; cast aside the gods your fathers served beyond the Euphrates and in Egypt, and serve the LORD."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The Greek text of the Septuagint differs from the Hebrew in several passages; in it, the episode of the altar on Mount Ebal (8:30–35) comes after 9:2, and in a Qumran manuscript (4QJosha) it appears right after the crossing of the Jordan. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"JDG":{"t":{"1":"The tribes do not drive out the Canaanites","2":"Bochim and the cycle of unfaithfulness","3":"Othniel, Ehud and Shamgar","4":"Deborah, Barak and the death of Sisera","5":"The song of Deborah","6":"Gideon is called; the test of the fleece","7":"Gideon's three hundred defeat Midian","8":"The Midianite kings and Gideon's ephod","9":"Abimelech at Shechem; Jotham's fable","10":"Tola, Jair and the Ammonite oppression","11":"Jephthah defeats Ammon; the vow and the daughter","12":"Ephraim against Jephthah; Ibzan, Elon and Abdon","13":"The birth of Samson announced","14":"Samson's marriage and the riddle","15":"Samson takes revenge on the Philistines","16":"Samson and Delilah; death at Gaza","17":"Micah and his household shrine","18":"The Danites take the idol and seize Laish","19":"The Levite and the crime of Gibeah","20":"The war against Benjamin","21":"Wives for the survivors of Benjamin"},"about":{"title_orig":"שֹׁפְטִים","title_orig_xlit":"Shofetim","summary":"Judges narrates the life of the tribes of Israel in Canaan between the death of Joshua and the rise of the monarchy. The book begins by showing that the conquest remained incomplete and presents a cycle that repeats itself: the people turn to other gods, fall into the hands of an oppressor, cry out to the LORD and receive a deliverer, until it all begins again. Into this pattern come the stories of Othniel, Ehud, Deborah and Barak, Gideon, Jephthah and Samson, besides judges mentioned in a few lines. With each turn the situation worsens, and the final chapters, marked by the phrase “there was no king in Israel,” tell episodes of idolatry, violence and a war among the tribes themselves.","name":"The Hebrew title, Shofetim, comes from the leaders the book calls judges (2:16–19). The verb shafat covers judging, but also governing and doing justice on someone's behalf; for this reason, most of these judges appear as military chiefs and deliverers raised up in times of crisis, and only Deborah is shown deciding cases (4:4–5). The English name comes from the Latin of the Vulgate, Liber Iudicum, which follows the Greek Kritai of the Septuagint.","facts":[["Position","7th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, the second of the Former Prophets (Nevi’im Rishonim)"],["Chapters","21"],["Verses","618"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative, with hero stories and an ancient poem (ch. 5)"]],"structure":[{"title":"An unfinished conquest","from":1,"to":3,"text":"The tribes advance, but many Canaanites remain (1:1–2:5); after Joshua's death, a new generation turns away from the LORD, and the text presents the cycle that will repeat (2:6–3:6)."},{"title":"The first deliverers","from":3,"to":5,"text":"Othniel, Ehud and Shamgar; Deborah and Barak defeat Sisera's army, and a song celebrates the victory."},{"title":"Gideon and Abimelech","from":6,"to":9,"text":"Gideon defeats the Midianites with three hundred men and refuses the crown; his son Abimelech makes himself king in Shechem and ends up destroyed together with the city."},{"title":"Jephthah and other judges","from":10,"to":12,"text":"Tola and Jair, the oppression of the Ammonites, Jephthah's victory and vow, the conflict with Ephraim and the judges Ibzan, Elon and Abdon."},{"title":"Samson","from":13,"to":16,"text":"The Nazirite from the tribe of Dan faces the Philistines alone until he is betrayed by Delilah and dies bringing down the temple of Dagon, in Gaza."},{"title":"When there was no king","from":17,"to":21,"text":"Two final episodes: Micah's shrine and the migration of the tribe of Dan; the crime at Gibeah and the war that almost wipes out the tribe of Benjamin."}],"extra":{"title":"The cycle and the refrain","text":"The book's frame (2:11–19) describes a circular movement: Israel forsakes the LORD, is handed over to an oppressor, cries out, and God raises up a deliverer; when the deliverer dies, everything begins again, each time worse. The episodes almost always open with the remark that the Israelites did evil in the sight of the LORD (3:7; 3:12; 4:1; 6:1; 10:6; 13:1). In the final chapters the cycle gives way to a refrain: “there was no king in Israel” (17:6; 18:1; 19:1; 21:25), completed twice with the remark that everyone did as they saw fit. The major judges come from different tribes, and the episodes are told with humor and irony, as in the assassination of Eglon (ch. 3) or in the password “Shibboleth,” which the Ephraimites could not pronounce (12:6)."},"people":[{"name":"Othniel","chapters":"1; 3","ch":3,"note":"from Caleb's family; the first deliverer, against Cushan-rishathaim"},{"name":"Ehud","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"left-handed Benjamite who kills Eglon, king of Moab"},{"name":"Deborah","chapters":"4–5","ch":4,"note":"prophetess who judges Israel under a palm tree; summons Barak and sings of the victory"},{"name":"Barak","chapters":"4–5","ch":4,"note":"from Kedesh in Naphtali; commands the battle against Sisera's army"},{"name":"Sisera and Jael","chapters":"4–5","ch":4,"note":"the commander of Jabin's army and the wife of Heber the Kenite, who kills him in her tent"},{"name":"Gideon (Jerubbaal)","chapters":"6–8","ch":6,"note":"of Manasseh; defeats the Midianites and refuses to be king (8:23)"},{"name":"Abimelech","chapters":"9","ch":9,"note":"son of Gideon; kills his brothers and reigns three years in Shechem"},{"name":"Jotham","chapters":"9","ch":9,"note":"Gideon's youngest son, the only one to escape; tells the fable of the trees that seek a king"},{"name":"Jephthah","chapters":"11–12","ch":11,"note":"Gileadite driven out by his brothers; defeats the Ammonites and fulfills a vow that strikes his own daughter"},{"name":"Samson","chapters":"13–16","ch":13,"note":"Nazirite from the tribe of Dan, of extraordinary strength, who fights the Philistines alone"},{"name":"Delilah","chapters":"16","ch":16,"note":"woman from the Valley of Sorek who discovers Samson's secret and hands him over to the Philistines"},{"name":"Micah","chapters":"17–18","ch":17,"note":"Ephraimite who sets up a shrine with images and hires a Levite as priest"},{"name":"The Levite and his concubine","chapters":"19–20","ch":19,"note":"the violence she suffers at Gibeah sets off the war against Benjamin"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 14b) attributes to Samuel the writing of Judges, of Ruth and of the book that bears his name. For this reading, the phrase “there was no king in Israel” (17:6; 21:25) shows that the author already lived under the monarchy, and the note that the Jebusites remained in Jerusalem “to this day” (1:21) would indicate a date before David's conquest of the city. The book would then have been written in the time of Saul or at the beginning of David's reign, around 1050–1000 BC.","academic":"Most researchers see in Judges a collection of stories of tribal heroes, largely of northern Israelite origin, passed on orally and later gathered and framed by the pattern of unfaithfulness, oppression, outcry and deliverance (2:11–19). This frame is usually attributed to the Deuteronomistic History, composed between the end of the 7th century and the exile, c. 620–550 BC; some propose that before it there was a collection of deliverer stories in the Northern Kingdom. The Song of Deborah (ch. 5) is considered by many one of the oldest poems in the Bible. Chapters 17 to 21, which argue for the monarchy and portray Gibeah, Saul's city, badly, are often seen as additions, and the mention of the “captivity of the land” (18:30) is generally linked to the Assyrian deportations of the 8th century BC."},"events":{"intro":"Judges covers the period between the death of Joshua and the beginning of the monarchy. The years of oppression, rest and rule given in the book add up to about 390, more than fits in the 480 years of 1 Kings 6:1 together with the wilderness, the time of Samuel and the reigns of Saul and David; for this reason, traditional chronologies assume that some judges acted at the same time, in different regions, and place the period around 1380–1050 BC. Jephthah, for his part, speaks of three hundred years of Israelite presence east of the Jordan (11:26). Scholarship usually places the setting of the book in Iron Age I (c. 1200–1000 BC), a time of small hill-country villages and of the settlement of the Philistines on the coast.","axis":[-1450,-500],"band":{"label":"Iron Age I","from":-1200,"to":-1000},"timeline":[{"label":"Period of the judges, traditional chronology","from":-1380,"to":-1050,"kind":"evento","note":"from the death of Joshua to the beginning of Saul's reign, with judges partly contemporary"},{"label":"Period of the judges, according to scholarship","from":-1200,"to":-1025,"kind":"evento","note":"tribal villages in the hill country, before the monarchy"},{"label":"Ramesses III confronts the Sea Peoples","from":-1180,"to":-1175,"kind":"evento","note":"after the clash, the Philistines settle on the southern coast of Canaan"},{"label":"Song of Deborah","from":-1150,"to":-1000,"kind":"escrita","note":"date proposed by many scholars for the poem of ch. 5"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1050,"to":-1000,"kind":"escrita","note":"Samuel, at the beginning of the monarchy"},{"label":"Deportations from the Northern Kingdom","from":-732,"to":-722,"kind":"evento","note":"Assyria carries off part of Israel's population; perhaps the “captivity of the land” of 18:30"},{"label":"Composition, according to scholarship","from":-620,"to":-550,"kind":"escrita","note":"Deuteronomistic frame, over older traditions"}]},"context":"In the period portrayed, the tribes of Israel had no central government or standing army: each lived in its own territory, and alliances formed in the face of common threats, not always without friction, as Ephraim's complaints show (8:1; 12:1). The neighbors change from one episode to another: kings of Moab and Ammon east of the Jordan, Midianite nomads who arrive with camels at harvest time (6:3–5), Canaanite cities in the valleys, with iron chariots (4:3), and, in the southwest, the Philistines, one of the Sea Peoples who settled on the coast at the beginning of the 12th century BC. The geography runs from the Valley of Jezreel and Mount Tabor, in the north, to the hill country of Ephraim, to Gilead, east of the Jordan, and to the strip between Zorah and Gaza, where Samson's story takes place. The sanctuary at Shiloh appears as the religious center of the tribes (18:31; 21:19).","places":["Bochim","Mount Tabor and the Kishon River","Ophrah and the Valley of Jezreel","Shechem","Mizpah of Gilead","Zorah, Timnah and Gaza","Laish, later called Dan","Gibeah of Benjamin","Shiloh"],"themes":["unfaithfulness and repentance","deliverers raised up by God","the Spirit of the LORD","leadership and monarchy","moral and religious decline","unity and conflict among the tribes"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:10","text":"After that whole generation had also been gathered to their fathers, another generation rose up who did not know the LORD or the works that He had done for Israel."},{"ref":"2:16","text":"Then the LORD raised up judges, who saved them from the hands of those who plundered them."},{"ref":"8:23","text":"But Gideon replied, “I will not rule over you, nor will my son. The LORD shall rule over you.”"},{"ref":"21:25","text":"In those days there was no king in Israel; everyone did what was right in his own eyes."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. In 18:30, the traditional Hebrew text writes the name of the priest Jonathan's grandfather with a suspended letter, which turns Moses into Manasseh; many scholars understand that the original form was Moses. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"RUT":{"t":{"1":"Naomi and Ruth return from Moab","2":"Ruth gleans in the field of Boaz","3":"Ruth at the threshing floor of Boaz","4":"Boaz redeems the inheritance; Obed is born"},"about":{"title_orig":"רוּת","title_orig_xlit":"Rut","summary":"Ruth is a short narrative set “in the days when the judges ruled.” After losing her husband and her two sons in Moab, Naomi returns to Bethlehem accompanied by her Moabite daughter-in-law Ruth, who refuses to leave her. Ruth gleans grain in the field of Boaz, a relative of Naomi, and, at her mother-in-law's initiative, asks him for protection as the family's kinsman-redeemer. Boaz settles the matter before the elders, marries Ruth, and the couple's son, Obed, appears in a genealogy that ends with David.","name":"The book bears the name of its protagonist, Ruth the Moabite. The meaning of the name is uncertain; among the proposals is a connection with the Hebrew word for “friend” or “companion.”","facts":[["Position","8th book of the Bible, after Judges; in the Hebrew Bible, among the Writings (Ketuvim), one of the five Scrolls (Megillot)"],["Chapters","4"],["Verses","85"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","short narrative, with a closing genealogy"]],"structure":[{"title":"Loss and return","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Elimelech's family in Moab, the deaths and the return of Naomi and Ruth to Bethlehem."},{"title":"In the field of Boaz","from":2,"to":2,"text":"Ruth gleans grain and meets the generosity of Boaz."},{"title":"At the threshing floor","from":3,"to":3,"text":"At night, Ruth asks Boaz to take on the role of kinsman-redeemer."},{"title":"At the city gate","from":4,"to":4,"text":"The redemption before the elders, the marriage, the birth of Obed and the genealogy down to David."}],"extra":{"title":"The kinsman-redeemer (go’el)","text":"The plot turns on the go’el, the close relative who had the duty of protecting the family: recovering land that had been sold, redeeming relatives and keeping alive the name of the dead (Leviticus 25:25; Deuteronomy 25:5–10). In the BSB, the word appears as “kinsman-redeemer” (2:20; 3:9; 4:1). The book combines these practices in its own way, and scholars discuss how they worked in reality. Another key word is hesed, the loyal kindness that Naomi, Ruth and Boaz show one another, rendered in the BSB as “loving devotion” or “kindness” (1:8; 2:20; 3:10)."},"people":[{"name":"Naomi","chapters":"1–4","ch":1,"note":"returns to Bethlehem without husband or sons and asks to be called Mara, “bitter”"},{"name":"Ruth","chapters":"1–4","ch":1,"note":"Moabite, widow of Mahlon; goes with her mother-in-law and marries Boaz"},{"name":"Orpah","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"the other daughter-in-law, who goes back to her people"},{"name":"Elimelech, Mahlon and Chilion","chapters":"1; 4","ch":1,"note":"Naomi's husband and sons, who die in Moab"},{"name":"Boaz","chapters":"2–4","ch":2,"note":"wealthy relative of Elimelech in Bethlehem; redeems the inheritance and marries Ruth"},{"name":"Obed","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"son of Ruth and Boaz, grandfather of David"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 14b) attributes the writing of Ruth to Samuel, as well as that of Judges and of the book that bears his name. On this reading, the book would have been written at the beginning of the monarchy, around 1050–1000 BC, or a little later, since the final genealogy reaches David.","academic":"There is no consensus on the date. Some scholars argue for the time of the monarchy (10th to 8th centuries BC), relying on the classical-style Hebrew and on the interest in David's family. Others prefer the period after the exile (5th and 4th centuries BC), citing some late forms of language, the explanation of a custom already old (4:7) and the welcome given to a foreign woman, which many read as a counterpoint to the measures of Ezra and Nehemiah against marriages with foreign women. The genealogy of 4:18–22 is often seen as an addition."},"events":{"intro":"The book gives no dates, only placing the story in the time of the judges (1:1). Since Boaz is David's great-grandfather (4:17, 21–22), those who reckon by generations usually put the events in the 12th or early 11th century BC; the exact number of generations, however, is uncertain, and the timeline shows only an approximate setting. The proposals for the writing appear alongside.","axis":[-1400,-300],"band":{"label":"Iron Age I","from":-1200,"to":-1000},"timeline":[{"label":"Period of the judges, traditional chronology","from":-1380,"to":-1050,"kind":"evento","note":"from the death of Joshua to the beginning of Saul's reign"},{"label":"The story of Ruth, approximate setting","from":-1150,"to":-1100,"kind":"evento","note":"three generations before David, by the genealogy of 4:17–22"},{"label":"David","from":-1040,"to":-970,"kind":"pessoa","note":"great-grandson of Ruth and Boaz; reigns c. 1010–970 BC"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1050,"to":-1000,"kind":"escrita","note":"Samuel, according to the Talmud"},{"label":"Writing, according to some scholars","from":-950,"to":-700,"kind":"escrita","note":"time of the monarchy"},{"label":"Writing, according to other scholars","from":-500,"to":-350,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian period"}]},"context":"The story begins with a famine that takes a family from Bethlehem in Judah to the plains of Moab, east of the Dead Sea, and returns to Bethlehem at the beginning of the barley harvest, in spring. The book shows customs of rural life: the right of the poor to glean the grain left behind (Leviticus 19:9–10; Deuteronomy 24:19), work on the threshing floor and the settling of legal matters before the elders, at the city gate. The relationship between Israel and Moab was tense in other texts (Deuteronomy 23:3), which makes it significant that David's great-grandmother is a Moabite. In Jewish tradition, Ruth is read at the feast of Shavuot (Pentecost), which coincides with the harvest.","places":["Bethlehem in Judah","the fields of Moab","the field of Boaz","the threshing floor","the city gate"],"themes":["loyalty (hesed)","redemption","the foreigner welcomed","loss and restoration","quiet providence","the family of David"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:16","text":"But Ruth replied: “Do not urge me to leave you or to turn from following you. For wherever you go, I will go, and wherever you live, I will live; your people will be my people, and your God will be my God."},{"ref":"2:12","text":"May the LORD repay your work, and may you receive a rich reward from the LORD, the God of Israel, under whose wings you have taken refuge.”"},{"ref":"4:17","text":"The neighbor women said, “A son has been born to Naomi,” and they named him Obed. He became the father of Jesse, the father of David."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"1SA":{"t":{"1":"Hannah asks for a son; Samuel is born","2":"Hannah's song; the sons of Eli","3":"The call of the boy Samuel","4":"The ark is captured by the Philistines","5":"The ark among the Philistines","6":"The return of the ark","7":"Samuel judges Israel; victory at Mizpah","8":"The people ask for a king","9":"Saul looks for the donkeys and finds Samuel","10":"Saul is anointed and proclaimed king","11":"Saul rescues Jabesh-gilead","12":"Samuel's farewell","13":"Saul offers the burnt offering at Gilgal","14":"Jonathan at Michmash; Saul's oath","15":"Amalek and the rejection of Saul","16":"David is anointed; the harp before Saul","17":"David and Goliath","18":"Jonathan bonds with David; Saul fears him","19":"Saul tries to kill David","20":"Jonathan protects David","21":"David at Nob and at Gath","22":"The cave of Adullam; the priests of Nob","23":"David saves Keilah and flees from Saul","24":"David spares Saul in the cave","25":"The death of Samuel; Nabal and Abigail","26":"David spares Saul again","27":"David among the Philistines, at Ziklag","28":"Saul and the medium of Endor","29":"The Philistines send David away","30":"David rescues the captives of Ziklag","31":"The deaths of Saul and his sons"},"about":{"title_orig":"שְׁמוּאֵל א","title_orig_xlit":"Shemuel Alef","summary":"1 Samuel narrates Israel's passage from the time of the judges to the monarchy. The book begins with the birth of Samuel, son of Hannah, and with the fall of the house of the priest Eli, when the ark of God is captured by the Philistines and later returned. Samuel leads the people as judge and prophet until the elders ask for a king; Saul is anointed, wins the first battles, but disobeys and is rejected. The second half follows David: anointed in secret, he enters Saul's court, defeats Goliath, becomes Jonathan's friend and the target of the king's jealousy, and spends years as a fugitive. The book ends with the death of Saul and his sons on Mount Gilboa.","name":"In the Hebrew Bible, 1 and 2 Samuel form a single book, called Shemuel, the name of the prophet who anoints the first two kings, although he dies before the end of the first part. The division into two comes from the Septuagint, which joined Samuel and Kings into four “Books of Kingdoms”; the Latin Vulgate called them 1 to 4 Kings, and for this reason some older Catholic Bibles in English, such as the Douay-Rheims, call 1 Samuel “1 Kings.” The division into two books passed into printed Hebrew Bibles only in the 16th century.","facts":[["Position","9th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, the first part of Samuel, the third of the Former Prophets (Nevi’im Rishonim)"],["Chapters","31"],["Verses","810"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative, with poems (2:1–10)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Samuel and the end of the house of Eli","from":1,"to":7,"text":"Samuel's birth and call, the loss and return of the ark, and the victory at Mizpah."},{"title":"The request for a king and Saul's reign","from":8,"to":15,"text":"The people ask for a king, Saul is chosen and consolidates power, but ends up rejected after Gilgal and Amalek."},{"title":"David at Saul's court","from":16,"to":20,"text":"David's anointing, the victory over Goliath, the friendship with Jonathan and Saul's attempts to kill him."},{"title":"David a fugitive and the end of Saul","from":21,"to":31,"text":"David lives in the wilderness and among the Philistines and spares Saul's life twice; Saul dies at Gilboa."}],"extra":{"title":"Two songs that frame Samuel","text":"The work of Samuel, read as a single book, is opened and closed by poems. At the beginning, Hannah's song (1 Samuel 2:1–10) celebrates a God who brings down the strong and raises up the weak and ends by speaking of the “king” and the “anointed,” even before there was a monarchy in Israel. At the end, David's song and his last words (2 Samuel 22:1–23:7) take up the same vocabulary of deliverance and kingship."},"people":[{"name":"Hannah and Elkanah","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"Samuel's parents; Hannah asks for a son at Shiloh and dedicates him to the LORD"},{"name":"Eli","chapters":"1–4","ch":2,"note":"priest at Shiloh; his sons Hophni and Phinehas die in the battle in which the ark is captured"},{"name":"Samuel","chapters":"1–16; 19; 25; 28","ch":3,"note":"prophet and last of the judges; anoints Saul and then David"},{"name":"Saul","chapters":"9–31","ch":9,"note":"of the tribe of Benjamin; the first king of Israel"},{"name":"Jonathan","chapters":"13–31","ch":14,"note":"Saul's son, a warrior and David's faithful friend"},{"name":"David","chapters":"16–31","ch":16,"note":"the youngest son of Jesse, of Bethlehem; anointed to succeed Saul"},{"name":"Goliath","chapters":"17","ch":17,"note":"the Philistine warrior of Gath defeated by David"},{"name":"Michal","chapters":"18–19; 25","ch":18,"note":"Saul's daughter and David's wife; helps him escape"},{"name":"Abigail","chapters":"25","ch":25,"note":"wife of Nabal, keeps David from taking revenge and later marries him"},{"name":"Achish","chapters":"21; 27–29","ch":27,"note":"Philistine king of Gath, who gives David the town of Ziklag"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 14b–15a) attributes to Samuel the writing of the book that bears his name. Since Samuel's death is narrated in the first book (1 Samuel 25:1), the same tradition says that the work was completed by the prophets Gad and Nathan, relying on 1 Chronicles 29:29, which mentions records of Samuel, Nathan and Gad about David. On this reading, the book would have been written between the end of the 11th century and the 10th century BC, around 1020–970 BC.","academic":"Most researchers understand that Samuel brings together older narrative blocks: a story of the ark (1 Samuel 4–6; 2 Samuel 6), a story of David's rise (1 Samuel 16 to 2 Samuel 5) and the account of David's court (2 Samuel 9–20 and 1 Kings 1–2). These blocks were incorporated into the so-called Deuteronomistic History (Joshua to Kings), composed between the end of the 7th century and the exile, c. 620–550 BC. The date of the older blocks is debated: some place them close to the events, in the 10th century BC, others much later. The traditional Hebrew text of Samuel suffered losses in copying; the fragments found at Qumran and the Septuagint preserve different readings, and in some passages, such as the story of Goliath, the Septuagint is considerably shorter."},"events":{"intro":"1 Samuel covers the end of the period of the judges and the reign of Saul, until his death, around 1010 BC. The length of Saul's reign is uncertain: in 13:1 the Hebrew text has gaps in the numbers, Acts 13:21 speaks of forty years, and many historians propose a much shorter reign. Scholarship places these events in the transition from Iron Age I to Iron Age II, when the Philistines dominated the coastal plain and the tribes lived in villages in the hill country. There is debate about the size and organization of these first kingdoms. The chronologies of the kings vary among authors: the numbers of the Hebrew text do not always fit together, and each reconstruction resolves the differences in its own way, with co-regencies and different ways of counting the first year of a reign. The dates of this timeline are approximate and follow one of the most widely used reconstructions.","axis":[-1200,-500],"band":{"label":"Iron Age I","from":-1200,"to":-1000},"timeline":[{"label":"Samuel, judge and prophet","from":-1070,"to":-1015,"kind":"pessoa","note":"a very approximate period; the text gives no dates for Samuel"},{"label":"Saul, king of Israel","from":-1050,"to":-1010,"kind":"pessoa","note":"according to the traditional count of 40 years (Acts 13:21); many propose a shorter reign"},{"label":"David, from birth to his anointing at Hebron","from":-1040,"to":-1010,"kind":"pessoa","note":"he was 30 years old when he began to reign (2 Samuel 5:4)"},{"label":"Battle of Gilboa","from":-1010,"to":-1010,"kind":"evento","note":"death of Saul and three of his sons (ch. 31)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1020,"to":-970,"kind":"escrita","note":"Samuel, completed by Gad and Nathan"},{"label":"Composition, according to scholarship","from":-620,"to":-550,"kind":"escrita","note":"Deuteronomistic History, drawing on older sources"}]},"context":"The events take place mainly in the central hill country of Canaan, in the territory of Benjamin and Ephraim: Shiloh, where the ark was kept; Ramah, Samuel's town; and Gibeah, Saul's base. On the coastal plain were the Philistines, organized in five cities (Gaza, Ashdod, Ashkelon, Gath and Ekron, 6:17), who according to 13:19–22 controlled metalworking. David's flight takes him south, to the lands of Judah, to the Wilderness of Ziph and to En-gedi, near the Dead Sea, and then to Ziklag, under the protection of Gath. Excavations in the Valley of Elah, such as those at Khirbet Qeiyafa, a fortified town of c. 1000 BC, feed the debate about who ruled the region at that time.","places":["Shiloh and Ramah","Mizpah, Gilgal and Gibeah","Bethlehem","the Valley of Elah","Nob, Adullam, En-gedi and the Wilderness of Ziph","Philistine cities: Ashdod, Gath, Ekron; Ziklag","Mount Gilboa and Beth-shan"],"themes":["monarchy and its risks","choice and rejection","obedience","the ark of God","prophet and king","friendship and loyalty"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"3:10","text":"Then the LORD came and stood there, calling as before, “Samuel! Samuel!” And Samuel answered, “Speak, for Your servant is listening.”"},{"ref":"8:7","text":"And the LORD said to Samuel, “Listen to the voice of the people in all that they say to you. For it is not you they have rejected, but they have rejected Me as their king."},{"ref":"16:7","text":"But the LORD said to Samuel, “Do not consider his appearance or height, for I have rejected him; the LORD does not see as man does. For man sees the outward appearance, but the LORD sees the heart.”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and the chronologies of the kings vary among authors. In 13:1, the traditional Hebrew text does not give Saul's age when he began to reign and gives a number too short for his reign; translations fill the gap in different ways. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"2SA":{"t":{"1":"David mourns Saul and Jonathan","2":"David king at Hebron; Ish-bosheth in Israel","3":"Abner goes over to David and is killed","4":"The murder of Ish-bosheth","5":"David king of all Israel; Jerusalem taken","6":"The ark goes up to Jerusalem","7":"The promise of a house for David","8":"David's victories and officials","9":"David takes in Mephibosheth","10":"War against the Ammonites and Arameans","11":"David, Bathsheba and Uriah","12":"Nathan rebukes David; Solomon is born","13":"Amnon, Tamar and Absalom's revenge","14":"The woman of Tekoa; Absalom returns","15":"Absalom's revolt; David leaves Jerusalem","16":"Ziba, Shimei and the counsel of Ahithophel","17":"Hushai thwarts the counsel of Ahithophel","18":"The defeat and death of Absalom","19":"David's grief and his return","20":"The revolt of Sheba","21":"The Gibeonites; wars against the Philistines","22":"David's song of deliverance","23":"David's last words; his mighty men","24":"The census and the plague"},"about":{"title_orig":"שְׁמוּאֵל ב","title_orig_xlit":"Shemuel Bet","summary":"2 Samuel is the story of David's reign. After lamenting Saul and Jonathan, David reigns over Judah at Hebron, while Ish-bosheth, Saul's son, rules the other tribes; after a war between the two houses, David is recognized as king of all Israel, conquers Jerusalem and brings the ark there. Through the prophet Nathan, he receives the promise of a lasting dynasty. The adultery with Bathsheba and the death of Uriah mark a turning point: there follow crises in his own family, such as Amnon's violence against Tamar and Absalom's revolt, which forces David to flee Jerusalem. The last chapters gather episodes, lists and poems from various periods of the reign.","name":"In the Hebrew Bible, 1 and 2 Samuel form a single book, Shemuel, which bears the name of the prophet who anointed Saul and David, although Samuel has already died when this second part begins. The division into two comes from the Septuagint, which called this book 2 Kingdoms; in the Latin Vulgate it is 2 Kings, a name also used in some older Catholic Bibles in English, such as the Douay-Rheims.","facts":[["Position","10th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, the second part of Samuel, the third of the Former Prophets (Nevi’im Rishonim)"],["Chapters","24"],["Verses","695"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative, with poems (1:19–27; 22; 23:1–7)"]],"structure":[{"title":"David king at Hebron","from":1,"to":4,"text":"The lament for Saul and Jonathan, the reign over Judah and the war with the house of Saul."},{"title":"David in Jerusalem","from":5,"to":10,"text":"King of all Israel, David takes Jerusalem, brings up the ark, receives Nathan's promise and defeats his neighbors."},{"title":"Bathsheba and the crises of the reign","from":11,"to":20,"text":"David's sin, Nathan's rebuke, the tragedy of Amnon and Tamar and the revolts of Absalom and Sheba."},{"title":"Appendices","from":21,"to":24,"text":"Episodes, lists of warriors and two poems of David, outside chronological order."}],"extra":{"title":"The mirror-shaped appendices","text":"Chapters 21 to 24 interrupt the narrative, which continues in 1 Kings 1, and are arranged in pairs: two accounts of national calamity and how it ends (21:1–14 and 24), two lists of warriors and their exploits (21:15–22 and 23:8–39) and, at the center, two poems attributed to David (22 and 23:1–7). The poem of chapter 22 also appears, with small differences, as Psalm 18."},"people":[{"name":"David","chapters":"1–24","ch":2,"note":"king of Judah at Hebron and then of all Israel, in Jerusalem"},{"name":"Joab","chapters":"2–24","ch":2,"note":"David's nephew and commander of the army; kills Abner and Absalom"},{"name":"Abner","chapters":"2–3","ch":3,"note":"Saul's general who props up Ish-bosheth and later negotiates with David"},{"name":"Ish-bosheth","chapters":"2–4","ch":4,"note":"Saul's son, king over the northern tribes for two years"},{"name":"Michal","chapters":"3; 6","ch":6,"note":"Saul's daughter, returned to David; criticizes his dancing before the ark"},{"name":"Nathan","chapters":"7; 12","ch":7,"note":"prophet who announces David's dynasty and later confronts him"},{"name":"Mephibosheth","chapters":"4; 9; 16; 19","ch":9,"note":"Jonathan's son, welcomed at the king's table"},{"name":"Bathsheba and Uriah","chapters":"11–12","ch":11,"note":"the wife of Uriah the Hittite; David takes her and has her husband killed"},{"name":"Amnon and Tamar","chapters":"13","ch":13,"note":"children of David; Amnon violates his half-sister and is killed by Absalom"},{"name":"Absalom","chapters":"13–19","ch":15,"note":"son of David who rebels and has himself proclaimed king at Hebron"},{"name":"Ahithophel and Hushai","chapters":"15–17","ch":17,"note":"counselors who compete to guide Absalom"},{"name":"Shimei","chapters":"16; 19","ch":16,"note":"Benjamite of the house of Saul who curses David during his flight"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 14b–15a) attributes to Samuel the writing of the book that bears his name. Since Samuel's death is narrated in the first book (1 Samuel 25:1), the same tradition says that the work was completed by the prophets Gad and Nathan, relying on 1 Chronicles 29:29, which mentions records of Samuel, Nathan and Gad about David. On this reading, this second part would have been completed in David's time or shortly afterward, in the 10th century BC, around 1000–930 BC.","academic":"For most researchers, 2 Samuel combines the end of the story of David's rise (chs. 1–5), other traditions about the reign and the account of David's court (chs. 9–20, which continues in 1 Kings 1–2), often called the “Succession Narrative” because it revolves around who will inherit the throne. This account is admired for its realism and the absence of miracles; some date it to Solomon's time, others centuries later. The appendices (chs. 21–24) gather material of diverse origins. The whole was integrated into the Deuteronomistic History, composed between the end of the 7th century and the exile, c. 620–550 BC."},"events":{"intro":"According to 2 Samuel 5:4–5, David reigned forty years: seven and a half at Hebron and thirty-three in Jerusalem. The most widely used chronologies place this reign around 1010–970 BC, counting backward from firmer dates in the period of the kings. Outside the Bible, the oldest reference to the dynasty is the Tel Dan Stele, from the 9th century BC, which mentions the “house of David.” Researchers debate the size and wealth of David's kingdom: some think of an organized state, others of a more modest regional chiefdom, with Jerusalem still small. The chronologies of the kings vary among authors: the numbers of the Hebrew text do not always fit together, and each reconstruction resolves the differences in its own way, with co-regencies and different ways of counting the first year of a reign. The dates of this timeline are approximate and follow one of the most widely used reconstructions.","axis":[-1050,-500],"timeline":[{"label":"David","from":-1040,"to":-970,"kind":"pessoa","note":"lives 70 years: begins to reign at 30 and reigns 40 (5:4)"},{"label":"David king of Judah, at Hebron","from":-1010,"to":-1003,"kind":"evento","note":"seven years and six months (2:11; 5:5)"},{"label":"Ish-bosheth, king over Israel","from":-1010,"to":-1008,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reigns two years from Mahanaim (2:8–10)"},{"label":"David king of all Israel, in Jerusalem","from":-1003,"to":-970,"kind":"evento","note":"thirty-three years (5:5)"},{"label":"Tel Dan Stele","from":-850,"to":-800,"kind":"evento","note":"Aramaic inscription that mentions the “house of David”"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1000,"to":-930,"kind":"escrita","note":"work completed by the prophets Gad and Nathan"},{"label":"Composition, according to scholarship","from":-620,"to":-550,"kind":"escrita","note":"Deuteronomistic History, drawing on older sources"}]},"context":"David begins at Hebron, the main city of Judah, in the south of the hill country. Jerusalem, until then a Jebusite city between Judah and Benjamin, becomes the capital: because it belonged to no tribe, it served as a point of union between south and north. The campaigns of chapter 8 and chapters 10 to 12 involve the neighboring peoples: Philistines to the west; Moabites, Ammonites and Edomites east and south of the Jordan and the Dead Sea; and the Aramean kingdoms (Aram, “Syria” in some translations) to the north. In his flight from Absalom, David crosses the Jordan and takes refuge at Mahanaim, in Gilead.","places":["Hebron","Jerusalem (Zion, the City of David)","Mahanaim, in Gilead","Rabbah, capital of the Ammonites","the Mount of Olives and the Jordan valley","the threshing floor of Araunah"],"themes":["covenant with David","power and responsibility","sin and consequence","family and succession","Jerusalem and the ark","loyalty and betrayal"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:27","text":"How the mighty have fallen and the weapons of war have perished!”"},{"ref":"5:4","text":"David was thirty years old when he became king, and he reigned forty years."},{"ref":"7:16","text":"Your house and kingdom will endure forever before Me, and your throne will be established forever.”"},{"ref":"22:2","text":"He said: “The LORD is my rock, my fortress, and my deliverer."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and the chronologies of the kings vary among authors. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"1KI":{"t":{"1":"David's old age; Solomon is anointed","2":"David's instructions and death","3":"Solomon asks for wisdom; the two mothers","4":"Solomon's officials and wisdom","5":"The agreement with Hiram of Tyre","6":"Building the temple","7":"The palace and the bronze furnishings","8":"The dedication of the temple","9":"The second appearance; Solomon's works","10":"The queen of Sheba; Solomon's riches","11":"Solomon's foreign wives and adversaries","12":"The kingdom divides; Jeroboam's calves","13":"The man of God from Judah","14":"Ahijah condemns Jeroboam; Rehoboam in Judah","15":"Abijah and Asa in Judah; Nadab and Baasha","16":"From Baasha to Ahab in Israel","17":"Elijah, the drought and the widow of Zarephath","18":"Elijah and the prophets of Baal on Carmel","19":"Elijah at Horeb; the call of Elisha","20":"Ahab's wars against Ben-hadad","21":"Naboth's vineyard","22":"Micaiah and the death of Ahab"},"about":{"title_orig":"מְלָכִים א","title_orig_xlit":"Melakhim Alef","summary":"1 Kings begins with David's old age and the dispute over the succession, won by Solomon. Solomon's reign is presented as a time of wisdom, wealth and great works, with the building and dedication of the temple in Jerusalem, but also of marriages to foreign women and the worship of other gods. After his death the kingdom divides: Rehoboam keeps Judah, in the south, and Jeroboam founds the kingdom of Israel, in the north, with sanctuaries at Bethel and Dan. From then on the book alternates between the kings of the two kingdoms, assessing each one by faithfulness to the LORD. The final chapters revolve around the prophet Elijah and his confrontation with King Ahab and Queen Jezebel, who promoted the worship of Baal.","name":"In the Hebrew Bible, 1 and 2 Kings form a single book, Melakhim, “Kings,” which narrates the history of the monarchy from Solomon to the exile. The Septuagint divided the work in two and called it 3 and 4 Kingdoms, continuing the numbering of Samuel; the Latin Vulgate used 3 and 4 Kings, names that appear in some older Catholic Bibles in English, such as the Douay-Rheims. The division into two books passed into printed Hebrew Bibles only in the 16th century.","facts":[["Position","11th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, the first part of Kings, the last of the Former Prophets (Nevi’im Rishonim)"],["Chapters","22"],["Verses","816"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative, with prophetic accounts"]],"structure":[{"title":"David's succession","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Adonijah tries to take the throne, Solomon is anointed and David dies."},{"title":"Solomon's reign","from":3,"to":11,"text":"The king's wisdom, the temple and the palace, the riches and, at the end, the idolatry and the adversaries."},{"title":"The division and the first kings","from":12,"to":16,"text":"The kingdom splits in two; kings of Judah and Israel down to Ahab."},{"title":"Elijah and Ahab","from":17,"to":22,"text":"The drought, Carmel, Horeb, Naboth's vineyard and Ahab's death at Ramoth-gilead."}],"extra":{"title":"The regnal formulas","text":"Each king is introduced with a fixed pattern: the year in which he began to reign, counted by the reign of the king of the other kingdom; the length of his rule; in Judah, the king's age and his mother's name; and an assessment, he “did evil” or “did what was right in the eyes of the LORD.” At the end comes the mention of the chronicles where his other deeds would be found, the notice of his death and the name of his successor. These synchronisms are the basis of the reconstructions of the chronology of the kings."},"people":[{"name":"David","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"now old, settles the succession and leaves instructions to Solomon"},{"name":"Bathsheba and Nathan","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"Solomon's mother and the prophet, who secure his anointing"},{"name":"Adonijah","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"son of David who tries to make himself king"},{"name":"Solomon","chapters":"1–11","ch":3,"note":"son of David and Bathsheba; builds the temple"},{"name":"Hiram, king of Tyre","chapters":"5; 9–10","ch":5,"note":"supplies timber and craftsmen for Solomon's works"},{"name":"The queen of Sheba","chapters":"10","ch":10,"note":"visits Solomon to test his wisdom"},{"name":"Jeroboam","chapters":"11–14","ch":12,"note":"first king of Israel, the northern kingdom"},{"name":"Rehoboam","chapters":"12; 14","ch":12,"note":"son of Solomon, king of Judah"},{"name":"Asa","chapters":"15","ch":15,"note":"king of Judah, praised by the text"},{"name":"Ahab and Jezebel","chapters":"16–22","ch":21,"note":"king of Israel and his Phoenician wife, who promotes the worship of Baal"},{"name":"Elijah","chapters":"17–21","ch":17,"note":"the Tishbite prophet, from Gilead, Ahab's adversary"},{"name":"Elisha","chapters":"19","ch":19,"note":"called by Elijah to follow him"},{"name":"Micaiah","chapters":"22","ch":22,"note":"prophet who announces Ahab's defeat"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) attributes the writing of Kings to the prophet Jeremiah, who lived through the fall of Jerusalem. On this reading, the work would have been composed at the beginning of the exile, in the 6th century BC; the last event narrated, the release of King Jehoiachin in Babylon (2 Kings 25:27), took place around 562 BC.","academic":"Most researchers see 1 and 2 Kings as the final part of the so-called Deuteronomistic History, which runs from Joshua to Kings and assesses Israel's past in light of Deuteronomy. Many follow the proposal of two editions: one in the reign of Josiah, c. 620 BC, which culminated in his reform, and another in the exile, around 560 BC, which added the fall of Jerusalem; others think of a single composition in the exile. The text itself cites sources now lost: the “Book of the Acts of Solomon” (1 Kings 11:41) and the chronicles of the kings of Israel and Judah (1 Kings 14:19, 29). The accounts of Elijah and Elisha seem to come from collections of prophetic stories that circulated in the Northern Kingdom."},"events":{"intro":"1 Kings runs from the end of David's reign, around 970 BC, to the beginning of Ahaziah's reign in Israel, around 853 BC. The first points of support outside the Bible appear in this period: the campaign of Pharaoh Shishak (Sheshonq I) in Canaan, in Rehoboam's fifth year (14:25), recorded on a relief at Karnak and placed around 925 BC; and the participation of “Ahab the Israelite” in the battle of Qarqar against the Assyrian king Shalmaneser III, in 853 BC, recorded in an Assyrian inscription. From these anchors the earlier dates are calculated, such as the division of the kingdom, which most place between 931 and 922 BC. According to 6:1, the building of the temple began 480 years after the departure from Egypt, a number that serves as the basis for the traditional date of the Exodus, c. 1446 BC. The chronologies of the kings vary among authors: the numbers of 1 and 2 Kings do not always fit together, and each reconstruction resolves the differences in its own way, assuming co-regencies (father and son reigning together) and different ways of counting the first year of a reign. For this reason the same date may appear a few years apart in different works. The dates of this timeline are approximate and follow one of the most widely used reconstructions.","axis":[-1010,-530],"timeline":[{"label":"David, king","from":-1010,"to":-970,"kind":"pessoa","note":"the last days of his reign open the book"},{"label":"Solomon, king","from":-970,"to":-931,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reigns 40 years (11:42)"},{"label":"Start of the building of the temple","from":-966,"to":-966,"kind":"evento","note":"Solomon's fourth year (6:1); the work lasts seven years (6:38)"},{"label":"Division of the kingdom","from":-931,"to":-931,"kind":"evento","note":"other reconstructions propose c. 928 or 922 BC"},{"label":"Jeroboam I, king of Israel","from":-931,"to":-910,"kind":"pessoa","note":"22 years (14:20)"},{"label":"Rehoboam, king of Judah","from":-931,"to":-913,"kind":"pessoa","note":"17 years (14:21)"},{"label":"Shishak's campaign","from":-925,"to":-925,"kind":"evento","note":"the pharaoh plunders Jerusalem (14:25–26)"},{"label":"Asa, king of Judah","from":-911,"to":-870,"kind":"pessoa","note":"41 years (15:10)"},{"label":"Omri, king of Israel","from":-885,"to":-874,"kind":"pessoa","note":"founds Samaria (16:24); Assyria comes to call Israel the “house of Omri”"},{"label":"Ahab, king of Israel","from":-874,"to":-853,"kind":"pessoa","note":"22 years (16:29–30)"},{"label":"Jehoshaphat, king of Judah","from":-872,"to":-848,"kind":"pessoa","note":"25 years (22:42), partly together with his father, according to many reconstructions"},{"label":"Elijah","from":-865,"to":-850,"kind":"pessoa","note":"active in the reigns of Ahab and Ahaziah; approximate dates"},{"label":"Battle of Qarqar","from":-853,"to":-853,"kind":"evento","note":"Ahab among the kings who face Shalmaneser III"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-586,"to":-560,"kind":"escrita","note":"Jeremiah, at the beginning of the exile"},{"label":"Composition, according to scholarship","from":-620,"to":-550,"kind":"escrita","note":"Deuteronomistic History: Josianic edition and exilic edition"}]},"context":"In Solomon's time, Jerusalem gains the temple and the palace, and the kingdom appears linked to the region's trade: timber from Lebanon by way of Tyre, in Phoenicia, ships setting out from Ezion-geber, on the Gulf of Aqaba, and caravans coming from Arabia, like that of the queen of Sheba. After the division, Judah keeps Jerusalem and the dynasty of David; Israel, larger and richer, has successive capitals until Omri founds Samaria. Jeroboam sets up sanctuaries at Bethel and at Dan, at the edges of his territory. In the 9th century BC Israel contends for land with the Arameans of Damascus and draws close to Phoenicia through Ahab's marriage to Jezebel, daughter of the king of Sidon, while Assyria begins to advance westward.","places":["Jerusalem: the temple and the palace","Gibeon","Shechem, Bethel and Dan","Samaria and Jezreel","Mount Carmel and Horeb","Zarephath, in Phoenicia","Ramoth-gilead","Tyre, Damascus and Ezion-geber"],"themes":["temple and worship","wisdom","faithfulness and idolatry","the division of the kingdom","prophet and king","Elijah and Baal"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"3:9","text":"Therefore give Your servant an understanding heart to judge Your people and to discern between good and evil. For who is able to govern this great people of Yours?”"},{"ref":"8:27","text":"But will God indeed dwell upon the earth? Even heaven, the highest heaven, cannot contain You, much less this temple I have built."},{"ref":"18:21","text":"Then Elijah approached all the people and said, “How long will you waver between two opinions? If the LORD is God, follow Him. But if Baal is God, follow him.” But the people did not answer a word."},{"ref":"19:11–12","text":"Then the LORD said, “Go out and stand on the mountain before the LORD. Behold, the LORD is about to pass by.” And a great and mighty wind tore into the mountains and shattered the rocks before the LORD, but the LORD was not in the wind. After the wind there was an earthquake, but the LORD was not in the earthquake. After the earthquake there was a fire, but the LORD was not in the fire. And after the fire came a still, small voice."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and the chronologies of the kings vary among authors; those given here broadly follow the reconstruction of E. R. Thiele, which is widely used, and other works may differ by a few years. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"2KI":{"t":{"1":"Elijah and King Ahaziah","2":"Elijah is taken to heaven; Elisha succeeds him","3":"The campaign against Moab","4":"Elisha, the widow and the Shunammite","5":"The healing of Naaman","6":"The floating axe head; Samaria besieged","7":"The end of the siege of Samaria","8":"The Shunammite returns; Hazael; kings of Judah","9":"Jehu is anointed and kills Joram and Jezebel","10":"Jehu wipes out the house of Ahab and Baal","11":"Athaliah seizes the throne; Joash is crowned","12":"Joash repairs the temple","13":"Jehoahaz and Jehoash; the death of Elisha","14":"Amaziah in Judah; Jeroboam II in Israel","15":"From Azariah to Jotham; kings in Israel","16":"Ahaz and the altar from Damascus","17":"The fall of Samaria and its causes","18":"Hezekiah and the threat of Sennacherib","19":"Hezekiah's prayer; Jerusalem is spared","20":"Hezekiah's illness; the envoys from Babylon","21":"The reigns of Manasseh and Amon","22":"Josiah and the Book of the Law","23":"Josiah's reform and his successors","24":"Babylon rules Judah; the first deportation","25":"The fall of Jerusalem and the exile"},"about":{"title_orig":"מְלָכִים ב","title_orig_xlit":"Melakhim Bet","summary":"2 Kings continues the history of the two kingdoms to the end of both. Elijah is taken up to heaven and Elisha takes his place, with a series of miracles and interventions in the politics of Israel and Aram. Jehu's coup eliminates Ahab's dynasty and the worship of Baal in the north, while in Judah Queen Athaliah usurps the throne until the crowning of the boy Joash. With the advance of Assyria, Samaria falls and much of Israel's population is deported, which chapter 17 interprets as a consequence of the people's unfaithfulness. Judah survives Sennacherib's siege in the time of Hezekiah, passes through the long reign of Manasseh and the reform of Josiah, but ends up subject to Babylon: Jerusalem and the temple are destroyed and the people go into exile. The book ends with King Jehoiachin, a prisoner in Babylon, being released and received at the king's table.","name":"In the Hebrew Bible, 1 and 2 Kings form a single book, Melakhim, “Kings.” The Septuagint divided the work in two and called this second part 4 Kingdoms; the Latin Vulgate called it 4 Kings, a name also used in some older Catholic Bibles in English, such as the Douay-Rheims.","facts":[["Position","12th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, the second part of Kings, the last of the Former Prophets (Nevi’im Rishonim)"],["Chapters","25"],["Verses","719"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative, with prophetic accounts"]],"structure":[{"title":"Elisha and the end of the house of Ahab","from":1,"to":8,"text":"Elijah is taken up to heaven; Elisha's miracles, the wars with Moab and with Aram."},{"title":"From Jehu to the fall of Samaria","from":9,"to":17,"text":"Jehu's coup, Athaliah and Joash, the kings of the two kingdoms and the end of the kingdom of Israel."},{"title":"Judah, from Hezekiah to Josiah","from":18,"to":23,"text":"Sennacherib's siege, the reign of Manasseh and the reform of Josiah."},{"title":"The last kings and the exile","from":24,"to":25,"text":"Babylon dominates Judah, deports King Jehoiachin and destroys Jerusalem and the temple."}],"extra":{"title":"The stories of Elisha","text":"Chapters 2 to 8 and 13:14–21 gather a set of short accounts about Elisha, many of them set among the “sons of the prophets,” groups that lived around a master. They are stories of miracles tied to ordinary life (the widow's oil, the pot of stew, the floating axe head), alongside interventions in wars and in the choice of kings, such as the anointing of Jehu and the announcement of Hazael as king of Aram."},"people":[{"name":"Elijah","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"announces Ahaziah's death and is taken up to heaven in a whirlwind"},{"name":"Elisha","chapters":"2–9; 13","ch":2,"note":"Elijah's successor, a prophet in Israel for about half a century"},{"name":"The Shunammite woman","chapters":"4; 8","ch":4,"note":"woman who gives Elisha lodging and has her son brought back to life"},{"name":"Naaman","chapters":"5","ch":5,"note":"Aramean general healed in the Jordan River"},{"name":"Hazael","chapters":"8–13","ch":8,"note":"seizes the throne of Damascus and presses Israel and Judah"},{"name":"Jehu","chapters":"9–10","ch":9,"note":"commander anointed king, who wipes out the house of Ahab"},{"name":"Athaliah","chapters":"8; 11","ch":11,"note":"daughter of the house of Ahab; the only queen to rule Judah alone"},{"name":"Joash and Jehoiada","chapters":"11–12","ch":11,"note":"the boy king of Judah and the priest who protects and guides him"},{"name":"Hezekiah","chapters":"18–20","ch":18,"note":"king of Judah who resists Assyria"},{"name":"Isaiah","chapters":"19–20","ch":19,"note":"prophet who counsels Hezekiah"},{"name":"Sennacherib","chapters":"18–19","ch":18,"note":"king of Assyria who besieges Jerusalem"},{"name":"Manasseh","chapters":"21","ch":21,"note":"king of Judah with the longest and most criticized reign"},{"name":"Josiah","chapters":"22–23","ch":22,"note":"king who reforms worship after the Book of the Law is found"},{"name":"Huldah","chapters":"22","ch":22,"note":"prophetess consulted about the book found in the temple"},{"name":"Nebuchadnezzar","chapters":"24–25","ch":24,"note":"king of Babylon who conquers Jerusalem"},{"name":"Jehoiachin and Zedekiah","chapters":"24–25","ch":24,"note":"the last kings of Judah; one is deported, the other sees the city fall"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) attributes the writing of Kings to the prophet Jeremiah, who lived through the fall of Jerusalem. On this reading, the work would have been composed at the beginning of the exile, in the 6th century BC; the last event narrated, the release of King Jehoiachin in Babylon (2 Kings 25:27), took place around 562 BC.","academic":"Most researchers see 1 and 2 Kings as the final part of the so-called Deuteronomistic History, which runs from Joshua to Kings and assesses Israel's past in light of Deuteronomy. Many follow the proposal of two editions: one in the reign of Josiah, c. 620 BC, which culminated in his reform, and another in the exile, around 560 BC, which added the fall of Jerusalem; others think of a single composition in the exile. The text itself cites sources now lost: the “Book of the Acts of Solomon” (1 Kings 11:41) and the chronicles of the kings of Israel and Judah (1 Kings 14:19, 29). The accounts of Elijah and Elisha seem to come from collections of prophetic stories that circulated in the Northern Kingdom."},"events":{"intro":"2 Kings covers about three centuries, from Ahaziah's reign in Israel, around 853 BC, to Jehoiachin's release in Babylon, around 562 BC. It is the biblical book with the most points of contact with outside sources: the Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III shows Jehu paying tribute (841 BC); the stele of Mesha, king of Moab, speaks of the rule of the house of Omri over Moab; the Assyrian annals record the fall of Samaria (722 BC) and Sennacherib's campaign against Judah (701 BC); and the Babylonian Chronicle dates the first capture of Jerusalem to March 597 BC. The destruction of the city is placed in 587 or 586 BC, depending on how the years are counted. The chronologies of the kings vary among authors: the numbers of 1 and 2 Kings do not always fit together, and each reconstruction resolves the differences in its own way, assuming co-regencies (father and son reigning together) and different ways of counting the first year of a reign. For this reason the same date may appear a few years apart in different works. The dates of this timeline are approximate and follow one of the most widely used reconstructions.","axis":[-870,-550],"band":{"label":"Assyrian rule in the Levant","from":-740,"to":-612},"timeline":[{"label":"Elisha","from":-852,"to":-795,"kind":"pessoa","note":"from the end of Elijah to the reign of Joash of Israel (13:14–20); approximate dates"},{"label":"Joram, king of Israel","from":-852,"to":-841,"kind":"pessoa","note":"12 years (3:1); last king of the house of Omri"},{"label":"Mesha Stele","from":-840,"to":-830,"kind":"evento","note":"the king of Moab celebrates his release from Israel's rule (cf. 3:4–5)"},{"label":"Jehu, king of Israel","from":-841,"to":-814,"kind":"pessoa","note":"28 years (10:36); pays tribute to Assyria in 841 BC"},{"label":"Athaliah, queen of Judah","from":-841,"to":-835,"kind":"pessoa","note":"six years (11:3)"},{"label":"Joash, king of Judah","from":-835,"to":-796,"kind":"pessoa","note":"40 years (12:1)"},{"label":"Jeroboam II, king of Israel","from":-793,"to":-753,"kind":"pessoa","note":"41 years (14:23), partly together with his father; a time of prosperity in the north"},{"label":"Azariah (Uzziah), king of Judah","from":-792,"to":-740,"kind":"pessoa","note":"52 years (15:2), counting co-regencies"},{"label":"Ahaz, king of Judah","from":-735,"to":-715,"kind":"pessoa","note":"asks Tiglath-pileser III for help (16:7)"},{"label":"Hoshea, last king of Israel","from":-732,"to":-722,"kind":"pessoa","note":"nine years (17:1)"},{"label":"Fall of Samaria","from":-722,"to":-720,"kind":"evento","note":"end of the kingdom of Israel and deportations to Assyria (17:6)"},{"label":"Hezekiah, king of Judah","from":-715,"to":-686,"kind":"pessoa","note":"29 years (18:2); some proposals begin the reign c. 729 BC"},{"label":"Sennacherib's campaign","from":-701,"to":-701,"kind":"evento","note":"siege of Lachish and threat to Jerusalem (18–19)"},{"label":"Manasseh, king of Judah","from":-697,"to":-642,"kind":"pessoa","note":"55 years (21:1), partly together with his father"},{"label":"Josiah, king of Judah","from":-640,"to":-609,"kind":"pessoa","note":"31 years (22:1); dies at Megiddo before Pharaoh Neco"},{"label":"Josiah's reform","from":-622,"to":-622,"kind":"evento","note":"in the king's 18th year, after the Book of the Law is found (22:3)"},{"label":"Jehoiakim, king of Judah","from":-609,"to":-598,"kind":"pessoa","note":"11 years (23:36)"},{"label":"Jehoiachin, king of Judah","from":-598,"to":-597,"kind":"pessoa","note":"three months (24:8); taken to Babylon"},{"label":"First deportation","from":-597,"to":-597,"kind":"evento","note":"Nebuchadnezzar takes Jerusalem (24:10–16)"},{"label":"Zedekiah, king of Judah","from":-597,"to":-586,"kind":"pessoa","note":"11 years (24:18)"},{"label":"Destruction of Jerusalem and the temple","from":-587,"to":-586,"kind":"evento","note":"the people are taken into exile (25:8–21)"},{"label":"Release of Jehoiachin","from":-562,"to":-562,"kind":"evento","note":"Evil-merodach takes him out of prison (25:27)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-586,"to":-560,"kind":"escrita","note":"Jeremiah, at the beginning of the exile"},{"label":"Composition, according to scholarship","from":-620,"to":-550,"kind":"escrita","note":"Deuteronomistic History: Josianic edition and exilic edition"}]},"context":"The background of 2 Kings is the succession of empires of the ancient Near East. In the 9th century BC Israel contends for territory with the Arameans of Damascus. In the 8th century BC Assyria, from Tiglath-pileser III on, turns the small kingdoms of the Levant into vassals or provinces: Damascus falls in 732 BC and Samaria ten years later, and Assyria brings peoples from other lands into the region (17:24). Judah remains a vassal, resists Sennacherib in 701 BC and, with Assyria's weakening at the end of the 7th century BC, lives through a period of greater autonomy under Josiah. After Nineveh falls (612 BC), Egypt and Babylon contend for the region; the Babylonian victory places Judah under Nebuchadnezzar, and the revolts against him end with the destruction of Jerusalem.","places":["Samaria and Jezreel","the Jordan River and Jericho","Damascus, in Aram","Moab","Jerusalem: the temple","Lachish","Megiddo","Nineveh and Babylon","Riblah, in Hamath"],"themes":["prophecy and power","faithfulness and idolatry","reform of worship","Assyria and Babylon","judgment and exile","the promise to David"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:11","text":"As they were walking along and talking together, suddenly a chariot of fire with horses of fire appeared and separated the two of them, and Elijah went up into heaven in a whirlwind."},{"ref":"17:6","text":"In the ninth year of Hoshea, the king of Assyria captured Samaria and carried away the Israelites to Assyria, where he settled them in Halah, in Gozan by the Habor River, and in the cities of the Medes."},{"ref":"23:25","text":"Neither before nor after Josiah was there any king like him, who turned to the LORD with all his heart and with all his soul and with all his strength, according to all the Law of Moses."},{"ref":"25:21","text":"There at Riblah in the land of Hamath, the king of Babylon struck them down and put them to death. So Judah was taken into exile, away from its own land."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and the chronologies of the kings vary among authors; those given here broadly follow the reconstruction of E. R. Thiele, which is widely used, and other works may differ by a few years, especially for Hezekiah and the kings of the 8th century BC. The BSB spells Jehoiakim for the king who reigned from 609 to 598 BC and Jehoiachin for his son, deported in 597 BC. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"1CH":{"t":{"1":"From Adam to Israel and Edom","2":"The descendants of Judah","3":"The family of David and the royal line","4":"Clans of Judah and Simeon","5":"The tribes east of the Jordan","6":"The Levites and their cities","7":"Issachar, Benjamin, Naphtali, Manasseh, Ephraim and Asher","8":"The tribe of Benjamin and the family of Saul","9":"The people of Jerusalem after the exile","10":"The death of Saul","11":"David king; the taking of Jerusalem","12":"The warriors who joined David","13":"The first attempt to bring the ark","14":"David in Jerusalem and victory over the Philistines","15":"The Levites carry the ark to Jerusalem","16":"The song of praise before the ark","17":"The promise of a house for David","18":"David's victories","19":"War against the Ammonites and Arameans","20":"The capture of Rabbah and the giants of Gath","21":"The census and the threshing floor of Ornan","22":"David prepares for the temple and charges Solomon","23":"The Levites and their duties","24":"The divisions of the priests","25":"The singers and musicians","26":"Gatekeepers, treasurers and officials","27":"The commanders of the army and the administration","28":"David gives Solomon the plan of the temple","29":"The offerings, the prayer and the death of David"},"about":{"title_orig":"דִּבְרֵי הַיָּמִים","title_orig_xlit":"Divrei ha-Yamim","summary":"1 Chronicles retells the history of Israel from the standpoint of Jerusalem and the temple. Chapters 1 to 9 are genealogies running from Adam to the community that returned from exile, with emphasis on the tribe of Judah, the family of David and the tribe of Levi. After a brief account of Saul's death (ch. 10), the book concentrates on David: his acclamation as king, the conquest of Jerusalem, the arrival of the ark in the city and the promise of a lasting dynasty. The final part deals with the preparations for the temple and the organization of priests, Levites, singers and gatekeepers, which David leaves ready for Solomon before he dies.","name":"The Hebrew title, Divrei ha-Yamim, means “the events of the days” (literally, “the words of the days”), an expression used for annals and official records. The Septuagint called the book Paraleipomena, “things left out,” understanding it as a supplement to Samuel and Kings. The name “Chronicles” comes from Jerome, who suggested calling it a “chronicle of the whole of sacred history.” In Hebrew tradition it was a single book; the division into two comes from the Septuagint and later passed into printed Hebrew editions.","facts":[["Position","13th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, 1 and 2 Chronicles form a single book, which generally closes the Writings (Ketuvim)"],["Chapters","29"],["Verses","942"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative, with long genealogies and lists"]],"structure":[{"title":"Genealogies","from":1,"to":9,"text":"From Adam to the twelve tribes, with emphasis on Judah, the house of David and Levi, down to the inhabitants of Jerusalem after the exile."},{"title":"David becomes king","from":10,"to":12,"text":"The death of Saul, David's anointing at Hebron, the conquest of Jerusalem and the warriors who supported him."},{"title":"The ark in Jerusalem","from":13,"to":16,"text":"The first failed attempt, the transfer carried out by the Levites and the worship of praise before the ark."},{"title":"The promise and the wars","from":17,"to":21,"text":"The promise of a dynasty, the victories over the neighboring peoples and the census that leads to the choice of the site of the temple."},{"title":"Preparations for the temple","from":22,"to":29,"text":"Materials, divisions of priests and Levites, musicians, gatekeepers and officials; David's last words and Solomon's succession."}],"extra":{"title":"A new reading of Samuel and Kings","text":"Much of the narrative takes up, sometimes word for word, passages from 2 Samuel and 1 Kings, but with its own choices: the book does not tell of David's affair with Bathsheba or of Absalom's revolt, and it adds long lists about worship. At some points the difference is theological: in 2 Samuel 24:1 it is the LORD's anger that moves David to count the people, while in 1 Chronicles 21:1 the one who incites him is Satan. The author also cites sources that have not come down to us, such as the chronicles of Samuel, Nathan and Gad (29:29)."},"people":[{"name":"Jabez","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"a figure in the genealogies of Judah remembered for his prayer in 4:9–10"},{"name":"Saul","chapters":"8–10","ch":10,"note":"the first king; his death on Mount Gilboa opens the narrative"},{"name":"David","chapters":"2–3; 10–29","ch":11,"note":"king at Hebron and in Jerusalem, the central figure of the book"},{"name":"Joab","chapters":"11–21; 27","ch":11,"note":"commander of David's army"},{"name":"Michal","chapters":"15","ch":15,"note":"Saul's daughter; despises David when he dances before the ark"},{"name":"Asaph, Heman and Jeduthun","chapters":"6; 15–16; 25","ch":25,"note":"leaders of the Levite singers and musicians"},{"name":"Nathan","chapters":"17; 29","ch":17,"note":"prophet who announces to David the promise of a house"},{"name":"Gad","chapters":"21; 29","ch":21,"note":"seer who brings David the message after the census"},{"name":"Ornan the Jebusite","chapters":"21","ch":21,"note":"owner of the threshing floor where David builds an altar"},{"name":"Solomon","chapters":"3; 22–29","ch":22,"note":"son of David, chosen to build the temple"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) attributes to Ezra the writing of the genealogies of Chronicles “down to himself” and says the work was completed by Nehemiah. On this reading, the book would have been written in Jerusalem in the 5th century BC, around 450–400 BC.","academic":"The author is anonymous and is usually called “the Chronicler”; the interest in the Levites, the singers and the gatekeepers suggests that he belonged to, or was close to, temple circles. The genealogy of 3:17–24 follows David's family for several generations after Zerubbabel, and the “darics” of 29:7 (in Hebrew, adarkonim) are generally understood as Persian coins; for this reason most scholars date the book to the end of the Persian period or the beginning of the Hellenistic period, between c. 400 and 250 BC, many around 350–300 BC. For a long time it was thought that the same author wrote Ezra and Nehemiah, since the end of 2 Chronicles and the beginning of Ezra coincide; today many researchers treat the two works as independent, and the question remains under debate."},"events":{"intro":"Chapters 1 to 9 cover from Adam to after the exile, without dates. The narrative runs from the death of Saul to the death of David, who reigned forty years, seven at Hebron and thirty-three in Jerusalem (29:27). The most widely used chronology places David's reign around 1010–970 BC, with variations of a few years depending on the system adopted. The extent and organization of David's kingdom are debated by historians; an Aramaic inscription of the 9th century BC found at Tel Dan mentions the “house of David.” The book, however, was written centuries later, for the community of Judah under Persian rule.","axis":[-1100,-200],"band":{"label":"Persian period","from":-539,"to":-332},"timeline":[{"label":"Death of Saul","from":-1010,"to":-1010,"kind":"evento","note":"battle of Mount Gilboa (ch. 10); approximate date"},{"label":"David","from":-1010,"to":-970,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reigns seven years at Hebron and thirty-three in Jerusalem (29:27)"},{"label":"Solomon begins to reign","from":-970,"to":-970,"kind":"evento","note":"anointed while David was still alive (29:22)"},{"label":"Fall of Jerusalem and exile","from":-586,"to":-538,"kind":"evento","note":"background of the genealogies of ch. 9"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-450,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"Ezra, completed by Nehemiah"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-400,"to":-250,"kind":"escrita","note":"the Chronicler, at the end of the Persian period or the beginning of the Hellenistic"}]},"context":"The book was written for the small province of Judah (Yehud) under the Persian Empire, a community that had returned from exile, rebuilt the temple and lived without a king of its own. In retelling the past, the Chronicler puts at the center what gave this group its identity: the link with the tribes of Israel, the promise made to David and, above all, the temple in Jerusalem and its worship. The genealogies work as a map of the people, and the expression “all Israel” appears often as a reminder of the unity of the tribes.","places":["Jerusalem (the City of David)","Hebron","Mount Gilboa","Kiriath-jearim","the threshing floor of Ornan","Rabbah of the Ammonites"],"themes":["genealogy and identity","David and his dynasty","the temple and worship","Levites and music","all Israel","seeking God"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"17:11–14","text":"And when your days are fulfilled and you go to be with your fathers, I will raise up your descendant after you, one of your own sons, and I will establish his kingdom. He will build a house for Me, and I will establish his throne forever. I will be his Father, and he will be My son. And I will never remove My loving devotion from him as I removed it from your predecessor. But I will set him over My house and My kingdom forever, and his throne will be established forever.”"},{"ref":"28:9","text":"As for you, Solomon my son, know the God of your father and serve Him wholeheartedly and with a willing mind, for the LORD searches every heart and understands the intent of every thought. If you seek Him, He will be found by you; but if you forsake Him, He will reject you forever."},{"ref":"29:14","text":"But who am I, and who are my people, that we should be able to give as generously as this? For everything comes from You, and from Your own hand we have given to You."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The BSB follows the numbering of English Bibles: in the Hebrew text, 6:1–15 corresponds to 5:27–41, and the rest of chapter 6 has shifted numbering. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"2CH":{"t":{"1":"Solomon asks for wisdom at Gibeon","2":"The agreement with Hiram for the building","3":"Building the temple","4":"The furnishings of the temple","5":"The ark is brought into the temple","6":"Solomon's prayer at the dedication","7":"Fire from heaven and God's answer","8":"Solomon's works and reign","9":"The queen of Sheba and the death of Solomon","10":"The kingdom divides","11":"Rehoboam fortifies Judah","12":"The invasion of Shishak, king of Egypt","13":"Abijah defeats Jeroboam","14":"Asa reforms worship and defeats Zerah","15":"The covenant renewed in Asa's time","16":"Asa seeks help from Aram","17":"Jehoshaphat sends teachers of the law","18":"Jehoshaphat, Ahab and the prophet Micaiah","19":"Jehoshaphat appoints judges","20":"Victory over Moab and Ammon","21":"The reign of Jehoram","22":"Ahaziah and Athaliah's seizure of power","23":"Joash is crowned and Athaliah falls","24":"Joash restores the temple and then turns away","25":"The reign of Amaziah","26":"Uzziah: prosperity, pride and leprosy","27":"The reign of Jotham","28":"Ahaz and idolatry in Judah","29":"Hezekiah cleanses the temple","30":"Hezekiah's great Passover","31":"Organizing the offerings and the Levites","32":"Sennacherib besieges Jerusalem","33":"Manasseh repents; the reign of Amon","34":"Josiah and the Book of the Law","35":"Josiah's Passover and his death","36":"The last kings, the exile and Cyrus"},"about":{"title_orig":"דִּבְרֵי הַיָּמִים","title_orig_xlit":"Divrei ha-Yamim","summary":"2 Chronicles continues the history begun in 1 Chronicles. Chapters 1 to 9 deal with the reign of Solomon, almost entirely devoted to the building and dedication of the temple. After the division of the kingdom, the book follows only the kings of Judah, from Rehoboam to the destruction of Jerusalem by the Babylonians, and assesses each one by his faithfulness to God and his care for the temple. Reforming kings, such as Asa, Jehoshaphat, Hezekiah and Josiah, receive a good deal of space. The book ends with the exile and with the decree of Cyrus, which authorizes the return to Jerusalem.","name":"The Hebrew title, Divrei ha-Yamim, means “the events of the days,” that is, the annals. The Septuagint used the name Paraleipomena, “things left out,” and the name “Chronicles” comes from a suggestion of Jerome. In Hebrew tradition, 1 and 2 Chronicles form a single book; the division comes from the Septuagint.","facts":[["Position","14th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, the second half of the book of Chronicles, which generally closes the Writings (Ketuvim)"],["Chapters","36"],["Verses","822"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative"]],"structure":[{"title":"Solomon and the temple","from":1,"to":9,"text":"The wisdom asked for at Gibeon, the building and dedication of the temple, the riches of the kingdom and the visit of the queen of Sheba."},{"title":"The division and the first kings of Judah","from":10,"to":16,"text":"Rehoboam, the separation of the northern tribes, Abijah and the long reign of Asa."},{"title":"Jehoshaphat","from":17,"to":20,"text":"Teaching of the law, alliance with Ahab, organization of justice and the victory over Moab and Ammon."},{"title":"From Jehoram to Ahaz","from":21,"to":28,"text":"A period of crises: the usurpation of Athaliah, the ambiguous reigns of Joash, Amaziah and Uzziah, and the idolatry of Ahaz."},{"title":"Hezekiah","from":29,"to":32,"text":"The purification of the temple, the great Passover and the deliverance of Jerusalem from Sennacherib."},{"title":"From Manasseh to the exile","from":33,"to":36,"text":"Manasseh and his repentance, Josiah's reform, the last kings, the destruction of Jerusalem and the decree of Cyrus."}],"extra":{"title":"Conduct and consequence","text":"More than Kings, the Chronicler links each king's destiny to his conduct in the same generation: faithfulness brings peace and victory, and forsaking God brings defeat or disease, as in Uzziah's leprosy (26:16–21). At the same time, the book insists on the possibility of turning back: only it tells that Manasseh, taken captive, humbled himself and was restored (33:12–13). Words such as “seek,” “humble oneself” and “forsake” appear often and help us read these stories."},"people":[{"name":"Solomon","chapters":"1–9","ch":1,"note":"builds and dedicates the temple"},{"name":"Hiram, king of Tyre","chapters":"2; 8–9","ch":2,"note":"supplies timber and craftsmen for the work"},{"name":"The queen of Sheba","chapters":"9","ch":9,"note":"visits Solomon to learn of his wisdom"},{"name":"Rehoboam and Jeroboam","chapters":"10–13","ch":10,"note":"Solomon's son and the leader of the northern tribes, in the division of the kingdom"},{"name":"Asa","chapters":"14–16","ch":14,"note":"reforming king who, in the end, seeks support from Aram"},{"name":"Jehoshaphat","chapters":"17–20","ch":17,"note":"promotes the teaching of the law and organizes the judges"},{"name":"Micaiah","chapters":"18","ch":18,"note":"prophet who announces Ahab's defeat at Ramoth-gilead"},{"name":"Athaliah and Jehoiada","chapters":"22–24","ch":23,"note":"the queen who usurps the throne and the priest who protects and crowns Joash"},{"name":"Uzziah","chapters":"26","ch":26,"note":"prosperous king who becomes leprous after burning incense in the temple"},{"name":"Hezekiah","chapters":"29–32","ch":29,"note":"restores worship and resists the Assyrian siege"},{"name":"Manasseh","chapters":"33","ch":33,"note":"the most idolatrous king, who repents in captivity"},{"name":"Josiah and Huldah","chapters":"34–35","ch":34,"note":"the king of the great reform and the prophetess consulted about the Book of the Law"},{"name":"Cyrus","chapters":"36","ch":36,"note":"Persian king whose decree closes the book"}],"writing":{"tradition":"For the Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 15a), Ezra wrote the genealogies of Chronicles and Nehemiah completed the work. On this reading, the book would have been written in Jerusalem in the 5th century BC, around 450–400 BC.","academic":"1 and 2 Chronicles are the work of the same anonymous author, called “the Chronicler,” linked to the circles of the temple in Jerusalem. The book uses Samuel and Kings as its main source and cites other records, such as those of prophets and seers (for example, 9:29; 33:19). Most scholars date it to the end of the Persian period or the beginning of the Hellenistic period, between c. 400 and 250 BC. The end of the book (36:22–23) repeats the beginning of Ezra; for a long time this was read as a sign of a common author, a hypothesis many now question."},"events":{"intro":"The book covers about four centuries, from the beginning of Solomon's reign, around 970 BC, to the decree of Cyrus, in 538 BC. The dates of the kings of Judah come from calculations based on the numbers of the text itself and on synchronisms with Assyrian and Babylonian records; different systems diverge by a few years, especially before the 8th century BC. Some episodes have outside confirmation: the campaign of Pharaoh Shishak (Sheshonq I) appears in a list of cities at Karnak, and Sennacherib's siege is narrated in the Assyrian annals.","axis":[-1000,-200],"band":{"label":"Kingdom of Judah after the division","from":-931,"to":-586},"timeline":[{"label":"Solomon","from":-970,"to":-931,"kind":"pessoa","note":"forty years of reign (9:30)"},{"label":"Division of the kingdom","from":-931,"to":-931,"kind":"evento","note":"Rehoboam in Judah, Jeroboam in the north (ch. 10); approximate date"},{"label":"Shishak's campaign","from":-926,"to":-925,"kind":"evento","note":"in Rehoboam's fifth year (12:2)"},{"label":"Hezekiah","from":-715,"to":-686,"kind":"pessoa","note":"some prefer to begin the reign c. 727 BC"},{"label":"Sennacherib's siege","from":-701,"to":-701,"kind":"evento","note":"Assyrian campaign against Judah (ch. 32)"},{"label":"Josiah","from":-640,"to":-609,"kind":"pessoa","note":"dies at Megiddo before Pharaoh Neco (35:20–24)"},{"label":"Destruction of Jerusalem","from":-587,"to":-586,"kind":"evento","note":"the temple is burned by the Babylonians (36:19)"},{"label":"Decree of Cyrus","from":-538,"to":-538,"kind":"evento","note":"authorizes the return and the rebuilding of the temple (36:22–23)"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-400,"to":-250,"kind":"escrita","note":"the Chronicler; tradition prefers c. 450–400 BC, with Ezra"}]},"context":"Judah was a small kingdom, with its capital in Jerusalem, surrounded by larger powers: Egypt to the south, the Aramean kingdoms and the Northern Kingdom (Israel), then Assyria and, finally, Babylon, which destroyed the city in 587/586 BC. The author, however, writes for the community that returned from exile and lives under the Persians, with the temple already rebuilt. For this reason he reads the history of the kings from the standpoint of the temple and its worship and ends the book not with ruin but with the proclamation of Cyrus, which opens the way for the return.","places":["Jerusalem and the temple","Gibeon","Shechem","Samaria","Ramoth-gilead","Megiddo","Babylon"],"themes":["the temple","the dynasty of David","faithfulness and forsaking","religious reforms","humbling and repentance","exile and return"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"6:18","text":"But will God indeed dwell with man upon the earth? Even heaven, the highest heaven, cannot contain You, much less this temple I have built."},{"ref":"16:9","text":"For the eyes of the LORD roam to and fro over all the earth, to show Himself strong on behalf of those whose hearts are fully devoted to Him. You have acted foolishly in this matter. From now on, therefore, you will be at war.”"},{"ref":"36:22–23","text":"In the first year of Cyrus king of Persia, to fulfill the word of the LORD spoken through Jeremiah, the LORD stirred the spirit of Cyrus king of Persia to send a proclamation throughout his kingdom and to put it in writing as follows: “This is what Cyrus king of Persia says: ‘The LORD, the God of heaven, who has given me all the kingdoms of the earth, has appointed me to build a house for Him at Jerusalem in Judah. Whoever among you belongs to His people, may the LORD his God be with him, and may he go up.’”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The BSB follows the numbering of English Bibles: in the Hebrew text, 2:1 corresponds to 1:18 and 2:2–18 to 2:1–17; 14:1 corresponds to 13:23 and 14:2–15 to 14:1–14. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"EZR":{"t":{"1":"The decree of Cyrus","2":"The list of those who returned from exile","3":"The altar and the foundation of the temple","4":"Opposition halts the work","5":"The work resumes; Tattenai's letter","6":"Darius's decree and the temple completed","7":"Ezra and the letter of Artaxerxes","8":"Ezra's journey to Jerusalem","9":"Ezra's prayer over the mixed marriages","10":"The sending away of the foreign wives"},"about":{"title_orig":"עֶזְרָא","title_orig_xlit":"Ezra","summary":"Ezra tells of the return of the Jews exiled in Babylon and the rebuilding of religious life in Jerusalem. Chapters 1 to 6 run from the decree of Cyrus, in 538 BC, to the completion of the temple, in 515 BC, passing through the opposition of the neighboring peoples, which halts the work for some years. After an interval of several decades, chapters 7 to 10 narrate the arrival of Ezra, priest and scribe, with a letter from King Artaxerxes authorizing him to teach and apply the law of Moses. The book ends with the crisis of the marriages with foreign women and the decision to dissolve them.","name":"The book bears the name of Ezra, the main character of chapters 7 to 10. Ezra is a shortened form of names such as Azariah and means “help” (of God). In Hebrew tradition, Ezra and Nehemiah formed a single book; the separation appears among Christian authors such as Origen and Jerome, and printed Hebrew editions adopted it later. In the Septuagint, the book is called Esdras B, to distinguish it from another Greek book, Esdras A.","facts":[["Position","15th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, it forms a single book with Nehemiah, among the Writings (Ketuvim)"],["Chapters","10"],["Verses","280"],["Original language","Hebrew, with passages in Aramaic (4:8–6:18; 7:12–26)"],["Genre","historical narrative, with lists and official documents"]],"structure":[{"title":"The return from exile","from":1,"to":2,"text":"The decree of Cyrus, the return of the temple articles and the list of those who came back."},{"title":"The rebuilding of the temple","from":3,"to":6,"text":"The altar and the foundations, the opposition and the halt, the resumption with Haggai and Zechariah and the dedication of the temple."},{"title":"Ezra's mission","from":7,"to":8,"text":"Ezra's genealogy, Artaxerxes' letter and the journey from Babylon to Jerusalem."},{"title":"The mixed marriages","from":9,"to":10,"text":"Ezra's prayer, the assembly of the people and the list of those who separated from their foreign wives."}],"extra":{"title":"Hebrew and Aramaic","text":"Two parts of the book are in Aramaic, the language used in the administration of the Persian Empire: 4:8–6:18, which gathers the correspondence between local authorities and the Persian court and the narrative around it, and 7:12–26, Artaxerxes' letter to Ezra. The text itself notes, in 4:7, that the letter was written in Aramaic. Another mark of the book is the shift to the first person: from 7:27 to 9:15, the one speaking is Ezra himself."},"people":[{"name":"Cyrus","chapters":"1; 3–6","ch":1,"note":"Persian king who authorizes the return and the rebuilding of the temple"},{"name":"Sheshbazzar","chapters":"1; 5","ch":1,"note":"leader who brings back the temple articles"},{"name":"Zerubbabel and Jeshua","chapters":"2–5","ch":3,"note":"the leader from the family of David and the priest who lead the rebuilding"},{"name":"Haggai and Zechariah","chapters":"5–6","ch":5,"note":"prophets who encourage the resumption of the work"},{"name":"Tattenai","chapters":"5–6","ch":5,"note":"governor of the region “west of the Euphrates,” who consults the king about the work"},{"name":"Darius","chapters":"4–6","ch":6,"note":"Persian king who confirms the decree of Cyrus"},{"name":"Artaxerxes","chapters":"4; 7–8","ch":7,"note":"Persian king who sends Ezra to Jerusalem"},{"name":"Ezra","chapters":"7–10","ch":7,"note":"priest and scribe skilled in the law of Moses"},{"name":"Shecaniah","chapters":"10","ch":10,"note":"proposes the commitment to dissolve the mixed marriages"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) attributes the book to Ezra, and the first-person passages have been read as written by him. On this reading, Ezra–Nehemiah would have been written in Jerusalem in the second half of the 5th century BC, around 440–400 BC.","academic":"Most scholars see the book as the work of a compiler who brought together diverse sources: the memoirs of Ezra, lists of people and objects and official documents in Aramaic, whose authenticity is accepted by many, at least in substance, and debated by others. The final form of Ezra–Nehemiah is usually dated c. 400–300 BC. The book was formerly attributed to the author of Chronicles; today many consider it an independent work. The date of Ezra's mission is also debated: the seventh year of Artaxerxes (7:7–8) is generally understood as 458 BC, under Artaxerxes I, but some propose 398 BC, under Artaxerxes II, which would place Ezra after Nehemiah."},"events":{"intro":"The book covers two periods separated by about sixty years: from 538 BC, Cyrus's first year as king of Babylon, to 515 BC, the sixth year of Darius I, when the temple was completed (6:15); and Ezra's mission, which begins in the seventh year of Artaxerxes, generally placed in 458 BC. The section 4:6–23 interrupts the chronological order: it gathers complaints made in the reigns of Xerxes and Artaxerxes, later than Darius, to show how the opposition kept recurring.","axis":[-600,-300],"band":{"label":"Persian period","from":-539,"to":-332},"timeline":[{"label":"Exile in Babylon","from":-597,"to":-538,"kind":"evento","note":"from the deportations under Nebuchadnezzar to the fall of Babylon"},{"label":"Decree of Cyrus and first return","from":-538,"to":-537,"kind":"evento","note":"with Sheshbazzar; then Zerubbabel and Jeshua (chs. 1–3)"},{"label":"Rebuilding of the temple","from":-520,"to":-515,"kind":"evento","note":"from the second to the sixth year of Darius I (4:24; 6:15)"},{"label":"Complaints to Xerxes and Artaxerxes","from":-486,"to":-445,"kind":"evento","note":"correspondence gathered in 4:6–23"},{"label":"Ezra arrives in Jerusalem","from":-458,"to":-458,"kind":"pessoa","note":"most accepted date; another proposal is 398 BC"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-440,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"Ezra"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-400,"to":-300,"kind":"escrita","note":"compilation of memoirs, lists and documents"}]},"context":"In 539 BC, Cyrus, king of Persia, took Babylon and came to rule the whole former Babylonian empire. Persian policy generally allowed deported peoples to return and restore their sanctuaries, and the so-called Cyrus Cylinder describes measures of this kind, although it does not speak of the Jews. Judah became a small province within the satrapy the book calls “west of the Euphrates” (in Aramaic, “beyond the River”). The return was gradual and met resistance from groups who had stayed in the land or came from other regions, such as the people of Samaria (4:10). The archive found at Ecbatana (6:2) recalls that this city in Media was one of the residences of the Persian court.","places":["Babylon","Jerusalem and the temple","Samaria and the region “west of the Euphrates”","Ecbatana, in Media","the Ahava Canal"],"themes":["return from exile","rebuilding of the temple","the law of Moses","identity and separation","opposition","God acts through foreign kings"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:2–3","text":"“This is what Cyrus king of Persia says: ‘The LORD, the God of heaven, who has given me all the kingdoms of the earth, has appointed me to build a house for Him at Jerusalem in Judah. Whoever among you belongs to His people, may his God be with him, and may he go to Jerusalem in Judah and build the house of the LORD, the God of Israel; He is the God who is in Jerusalem."},{"ref":"6:15–16","text":"And this temple was completed on the third day of the month of Adar, in the sixth year of the reign of King Darius. Then the people of Israel—the priests, the Levites, and the rest of the exiles—celebrated the dedication of the house of God with joy."},{"ref":"7:10","text":"For Ezra had set his heart to study the Law of the LORD, to practice it, and to teach its statutes and ordinances in Israel."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"NEH":{"t":{"1":"Nehemiah's prayer in Susa","2":"Nehemiah goes to Jerusalem and inspects the wall","3":"The builders of the wall and the gates","4":"Armed work in the face of opposition","5":"Nehemiah defends the indebted poor","6":"Threats, plots and the wall completed","7":"Guards for the city and the list of returnees","8":"The reading of the law and the Feast of Booths","9":"The people's confession","10":"The pledge signed by the community","11":"The new residents of Jerusalem","12":"Priests, Levites and the dedication of the wall","13":"Nehemiah's last reforms"},"about":{"title_orig":"נְחֶמְיָה","title_orig_xlit":"Nechemyah","summary":"Nehemiah, cupbearer to the Persian king Artaxerxes in Susa, receives news of Jerusalem's precarious situation and obtains permission to rebuild the city's walls. Despite the opposition of Sanballat, Tobiah and Geshem, the work is completed in fifty-two days. The book also narrates the public reading of the law by Ezra, the people's confession and the commitment signed by the community, the repopulation of Jerusalem and the dedication of the walls. It ends with Nehemiah's reforms in a second term as governor: the Sabbath, the tithes, the temple service and marriages with foreigners.","name":"The book bears the name of Nehemiah, who speaks in the first person in much of the text; the name means “the LORD comforts.” In Hebrew tradition, Ezra and Nehemiah formed a single book; the Septuagint includes Nehemiah in Esdras B, and the Vulgate called it 2 Esdras. The separation into two books, common in Christian Bibles, was adopted later in printed Hebrew editions.","facts":[["Position","16th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, the second part of the book of Ezra–Nehemiah, among the Writings (Ketuvim)"],["Chapters","13"],["Verses","406"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","historical narrative, largely first-person memoirs, with lists"]],"structure":[{"title":"Nehemiah's call","from":1,"to":2,"text":"The news about Jerusalem, the prayer, the request to the king and the night inspection of the walls."},{"title":"Rebuilding the wall","from":3,"to":7,"text":"The groups of builders, the armed opposition and the intrigues, the debt crisis, the completion of the wall and the list of the returned exiles."},{"title":"The law and the covenant","from":8,"to":10,"text":"Ezra reads the law before the people, the Feast of Tabernacles, the great confession and the written commitment."},{"title":"Jerusalem repopulated","from":11,"to":12,"text":"The city's new residents, the lists of priests and Levites and the celebration of the dedication of the wall."},{"title":"The final reforms","from":13,"to":13,"text":"On his return to Jerusalem, Nehemiah corrects abuses in the temple, on the Sabbath and in marriages."}],"extra":{"title":"The memoirs of Nehemiah","text":"A large part of the book (especially 1–7 and 12:31–13:31) is written in the first person and has the tone of a personal account. In it appear short prayers in which Nehemiah asks God to remember him “with favor” (5:19; 13:14, 22, 31) or to remember the deeds of his adversaries (4:4–5; 6:14; 13:29). Many historians consider these memoirs one of the most direct sources on Judah in the Persian period."},"people":[{"name":"Nehemiah","chapters":"1–13","ch":1,"note":"the king's cupbearer and later governor of Judah"},{"name":"Hanani","chapters":"1; 7","ch":1,"note":"Nehemiah's brother, who brings news from Jerusalem"},{"name":"Artaxerxes","chapters":"2; 5; 13","ch":2,"note":"Persian king who authorizes the work"},{"name":"Eliashib","chapters":"3; 12–13","ch":3,"note":"high priest; related to Tobiah (13:4)"},{"name":"Sanballat","chapters":"2; 4; 6; 13","ch":4,"note":"leader linked to Samaria, the main opponent of the work"},{"name":"Tobiah the Ammonite","chapters":"2; 4; 6; 13","ch":6,"note":"Sanballat's ally, with connections among the nobles of Jerusalem"},{"name":"Geshem the Arab","chapters":"2; 6","ch":6,"note":"the third opponent"},{"name":"Noadiah","chapters":"6","ch":6,"note":"prophetess who tried to intimidate Nehemiah (6:14)"},{"name":"Ezra","chapters":"8; 12","ch":8,"note":"reads the law of Moses before the people"}],"writing":{"tradition":"In Jewish tradition, Ezra and Nehemiah formed a single book, attributed to Ezra by the Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 15a); the first-person passages have been read as written by Nehemiah himself. On this reading, the book would date from the end of the 5th century BC, around 430–400 BC.","academic":"For most scholars, Nehemiah's memoirs are an authentic text, written by him around 430 BC, perhaps as an accounting of his work or as a votive inscription. A compiler joined them to the material about Ezra and to lists (the one in 7:6–73 repeats that of Ezra 2). The list in 12:10–11, 22 reaches the high priest Jaddua, whom the historian Josephus places in the time of Alexander the Great; for this reason the final form is usually dated c. 400–300 BC, although some identify Jaddua with an earlier priest. The chronological relationship between Ezra and Nehemiah, who appear together in 8:9 and 12:26, 36, is also debated."},"events":{"intro":"Nehemiah receives the news about Jerusalem in the twentieth year of Artaxerxes (1:1; 2:1), which corresponds to 445/444 BC, under Artaxerxes I. The wall is completed in fifty-two days (6:15), and Nehemiah governs Judah for twelve years, until the king's thirty-second year, c. 433 BC (5:14); he then returns to the court and comes back to Jerusalem some years later (13:6–7). Aramaic papyri from Elephantine, in Egypt, dated to 407 BC, mention the sons of Sanballat, governor of Samaria, and the high priest Johanan, named in 12:22.","axis":[-500,-300],"band":{"label":"Persian period","from":-539,"to":-332},"timeline":[{"label":"Ezra arrives in Jerusalem","from":-458,"to":-458,"kind":"pessoa","note":"most accepted date (Ezra 7:7–8); another proposal is 398 BC"},{"label":"Nehemiah, first term","from":-445,"to":-433,"kind":"pessoa","note":"from the twentieth to the thirty-second year of Artaxerxes (5:14)"},{"label":"Wall rebuilt","from":-445,"to":-445,"kind":"evento","note":"in fifty-two days, finished in Elul (6:15)"},{"label":"Nehemiah's second stay","from":-430,"to":-425,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate date of the reforms of ch. 13"},{"label":"Memoirs of Nehemiah","from":-430,"to":-425,"kind":"escrita","note":"according to most scholars"},{"label":"Elephantine papyri","from":-407,"to":-407,"kind":"evento","note":"mention the sons of Sanballat and the high priest Johanan"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-400,"to":-300,"kind":"escrita","note":"compilation of Ezra–Nehemiah"}]},"context":"In the 5th century BC, Judah was a small province of the Persian Empire, with a sparsely populated Jerusalem still without complete walls. Susa, where Nehemiah served, was one of the capitals of the empire. Nehemiah's opponents represented regional forces: Sanballat was linked to the government of Samaria, to the north; Tobiah had connections with the region of Ammon, east of the Jordan; and Geshem led Arab groups of the south, and an inscription on a silver bowl from Egypt mentions a “Geshem, king of Kedar,” perhaps the same person. The crisis of chapter 5 shows the economic difficulties of the province, with debts, mortgages and taxes to the king.","places":["Susa","Jerusalem: walls and gates","the square before the Water Gate","Samaria","the plain of Ono","the towns of Judah and Benjamin"],"themes":["rebuilding","prayer","opposition and perseverance","social justice","the law read and explained","covenant","Sabbath and purity of worship"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:17–18","text":"Then I said to them, “You see the trouble we are in. Jerusalem lies in ruins, and its gates have been burned down. Come, let us rebuild the wall of Jerusalem, so that we will no longer be a disgrace.” I also told them about the gracious hand of my God upon me, and what the king had said to me. “Let us start rebuilding,” they replied, and they set their hands to this good work."},{"ref":"6:15–16","text":"So the wall was completed in fifty-two days, on the twenty-fifth of Elul. When all our enemies heard about this, all the surrounding nations were afraid and disheartened, for they realized that this task had been accomplished by our God."},{"ref":"8:10","text":"Then Nehemiah told them, “Go and eat what is rich, drink what is sweet, and send out portions to those who have nothing prepared, since today is holy to our Lord. Do not grieve, for the joy of the LORD is your strength.”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The BSB follows the numbering of English Bibles: in the Hebrew text, 4:1–6 corresponds to 3:33–38 and 4:7–23 to 4:1–17; 9:38 corresponds to 10:1, and 10:1–39 to 10:2–40. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"EST":{"t":{"1":"Xerxes' banquet and Vashti's refusal","2":"Esther becomes queen","3":"Haman's decree against the Jews","4":"Mordecai asks Esther to intercede","5":"Esther's first banquet","6":"The king honors Mordecai","7":"Haman is exposed and executed","8":"The new decree in favor of the Jews","9":"The Jews defend themselves; the feast of Purim","10":"The greatness of Mordecai"},"about":{"title_orig":"אֶסְתֵּר","title_orig_xlit":"Ester","summary":"At the court of the Persian king Xerxes (Ahasuerus in Hebrew), in Susa, the young Jewish woman Esther becomes queen without revealing her origin. When Haman, the most powerful official in the kingdom, obtains a decree to exterminate the Jews of the empire, Mordecai, Esther's cousin and guardian, persuades her to risk her life and intercede with the king. In a sequence of banquets and reversals, Haman is unmasked and executed, and a new decree allows the Jews to defend themselves. The book ends by explaining the origin of the feast of Purim.","name":"The book bears the name of its protagonist. Esther was the name by which she was known at court; her Hebrew name, Hadassah (2:7), means “myrtle.” Esther is usually explained from Persian (“star”) or linked to the name of the Babylonian goddess Ishtar. In Jewish tradition, the book is called Megillat Esther, “the scroll of Esther,” read publicly at the feast of Purim.","facts":[["Position","17th book of the Bible, among the historical books; in the Hebrew Bible, one of the five Scrolls (Megillot), among the Writings (Ketuvim)"],["Chapters","10"],["Verses","167"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","narrative"]],"structure":[{"title":"Esther comes to the throne","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Queen Vashti is deposed, Esther is chosen, and Mordecai uncovers a conspiracy against the king."},{"title":"The threat","from":3,"to":4,"text":"Mordecai does not bow before Haman, who obtains a decree against all the Jews; Esther decides to go to the king."},{"title":"The reversal","from":5,"to":7,"text":"Esther's banquets, the king's sleepless night, the honoring of Mordecai and Haman's fall."},{"title":"Deliverance and Purim","from":8,"to":10,"text":"The counter-decree, the Jews' defense, the institution of the feast and the greatness of Mordecai."}],"extra":{"title":"A book in which God is not named","text":"The Hebrew text of Esther does not mention God even once, nor does it speak explicitly of prayer, although there is fasting (4:16) and many readers see divine action behind the coincidences of the story, as in Mordecai's question in 4:14. The Greek translation (Septuagint) has six additions, with about a hundred verses, which include prayers of Mordecai and Esther and mention God; they are part of Catholic and Orthodox Bibles as deuterocanonical text. The BSB translates the Hebrew text. The name of the feast comes from pur, “lot,” which Haman casts to choose the date of the massacre (3:7; 9:24–26)."},"people":[{"name":"Xerxes (Ahasuerus)","chapters":"1–10","ch":1,"note":"the Persian king, generally identified with Xerxes I"},{"name":"Vashti","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"the queen who refuses to appear at the banquet and is deposed"},{"name":"Esther (Hadassah)","chapters":"2–9","ch":2,"note":"young Jewish woman who becomes queen and intercedes for her people"},{"name":"Mordecai","chapters":"2–10","ch":2,"note":"Esther's cousin and guardian, of the tribe of Benjamin"},{"name":"Bigthan and Teresh","chapters":"2; 6","ch":2,"note":"eunuchs whose conspiracy Mordecai reports"},{"name":"Haman","chapters":"3–9","ch":3,"note":"the Agagite, a high official who plots to destroy the Jews"},{"name":"Hathach","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"eunuch who carries the messages between Esther and Mordecai"},{"name":"Zeresh","chapters":"5–6","ch":5,"note":"Haman's wife, who suggests the gallows for Mordecai"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Babylonian Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) attributes the writing of Esther to the “men of the Great Assembly,” the council that tradition places after the exile. Other Jewish authors, based on 9:20 (“Mordecai recorded these events”), attribute the book to Mordecai. On this reading, it would have been written in the Persian period, shortly after the events, in the 5th century BC.","academic":"The author is anonymous. The book knows the Persian court and the city of Susa well and shows no evident Greek influence, which leads many to date it to the end of the Persian period; others prefer the beginning of the Hellenistic period. The most common proposals fall between c. 400 and 200 BC. Its historical character is debated: the queen of Xerxes known to the Greek historians was named Amestris, and certain details, such as the hundred and twenty-seven provinces, do not match what is known of Persian administration; for this reason many classify the book as a fictionalized historical narrative, while others argue that it preserves a core of facts. A cuneiform document from the time of Xerxes mentions an official named Marduka in Susa, but it is not known whether he is the same Mordecai. Esther is the only book of the Hebrew Bible of which no copy has been found among the Dead Sea Scrolls."},"events":{"intro":"If Ahasuerus is Xerxes I (486–465 BC), as most scholars think and as the BSB translates, the book's own dates allow the events to be placed: the banquet of the third year (1:3) in 483 BC, Esther's coming to the king in the seventh year (2:16) in 479/478 BC, and Haman's casting of lots in the twelfth year (3:7) in 474 BC, with the day set for the thirteenth day of Adar of the following year. Between the third and the seventh year, Xerxes waged his campaign against Greece (480–479 BC). The Septuagint and the historian Josephus identify the king with Artaxerxes.","axis":[-520,-150],"band":{"label":"Reign of Xerxes I","from":-486,"to":-465},"timeline":[{"label":"Banquet of Xerxes","from":-483,"to":-483,"kind":"evento","note":"third year of the reign (1:3)"},{"label":"Xerxes' campaign against Greece","from":-480,"to":-479,"kind":"evento","note":"battles of Thermopylae, Salamis and Plataea"},{"label":"Esther becomes queen","from":-479,"to":-478,"kind":"pessoa","note":"in the month of Tebeth of the seventh year (2:16)"},{"label":"Haman's decree and the deliverance","from":-474,"to":-473,"kind":"evento","note":"lots cast in the twelfth year (3:7); the 13th of Adar (9:1)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-470,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"Mordecai or the men of the Great Assembly"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-400,"to":-200,"kind":"escrita","note":"end of the Persian period or beginning of the Hellenistic"}]},"context":"The story takes place in the diaspora, among the Jews who did not return to Judah and lived scattered across the Persian Empire, the largest of its time, which stretched from India to Ethiopia (Cush, 1:1). Susa, in the southwest of present-day Iran, was one of the capitals of the Persian kings, and excavations there have revealed the great palace built by Darius I and used by Xerxes. The book portrays a court of banquets, decrees written in all the languages of the empire and royal couriers, and shows the vulnerability of a minority that depends on the king's favor.","places":["Susa: the citadel and the city","the king's palace and garden","the provinces of the empire, from India to Cush"],"themes":["hidden providence","courage","Jewish identity in the diaspora","reversal of fortunes","fasting and solidarity","the feast of Purim"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"4:14","text":"For if you remain silent at this time, relief and deliverance for the Jews will arise from another place, but you and your father’s house will perish. And who knows if perhaps you have come to the kingdom for such a time as this?”"},{"ref":"4:16","text":"“Go and assemble all the Jews who can be found in Susa, and fast for me. Do not eat or drink for three days, night or day, and I and my maidens will fast as you do. After that, I will go to the king, even though it is against the law. And if I perish, I perish!”"},{"ref":"9:22","text":"as the days on which the Jews gained rest from their enemies and the month in which their sorrow turned to joy and their mourning into a holiday. He wrote that these were to be days of feasting and joy, of sending gifts to one another and to the poor."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and depend on identifying Ahasuerus with Xerxes I. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"JOB":{"t":{"1":"Job loses his possessions and his children","2":"Job is struck; the three friends arrive","3":"Job curses the day he was born","4":"Eliphaz: the innocent do not perish","5":"Eliphaz advises Job to seek God","6":"Job laments his friends' lack of support","7":"Life is brief; a complaint to God","8":"Bildad defends the justice of God","9":"How can anyone be righteous before God","10":"Job questions the Creator","11":"Zophar calls Job to repentance","12":"Job answers: divine wisdom and power","13":"Job wants to argue his case with God","14":"Human frailty and the hope of a tree","15":"Eliphaz describes the fate of the wicked","16":"Miserable comforters; a witness in heaven","17":"Job without hope before the grave","18":"Bildad and the ruin of the wicked","19":"Abandoned by all, Job awaits a redeemer","20":"Zophar: the joy of the wicked is brief","21":"Job observes that the wicked prosper","22":"Eliphaz accuses Job of injustice","23":"Job seeks God and does not find Him","24":"The injustice suffered by the poor","25":"Bildad: no one is pure before God","26":"The greatness of God in creation","27":"Job holds on to his integrity","28":"Where wisdom is found","29":"Job remembers his days of honor","30":"Job's present humiliation","31":"Job's oath of innocence","32":"Elihu enters the debate","33":"Elihu: God speaks through dreams and pain","34":"Elihu defends the righteousness of God","35":"Elihu: God does not depend on man","36":"Elihu: affliction as instruction","37":"God in the storm, the snow and the rain","38":"The LORD answers from the whirlwind","39":"God and the wild animals","40":"Job falls silent; Behemoth","41":"Leviathan","42":"Job repents and is restored"},"about":{"title_orig":"אִיּוֹב","title_orig_xlit":"Iyyov","summary":"Job deals with the suffering of a righteous man and the question of how it relates to God's justice. A prose prologue introduces Job, rich and upright, who in a short time loses his possessions, his children and his health, after a conversation in heaven in which the accuser questions the reason for his faithfulness. Three friends come to comfort him, and the meeting turns into a long debate in poetry: they maintain that suffering is the consequence of sin, and Job insists on his own innocence and asks to present his case before God. After the speeches of a fourth character, Elihu, the LORD Himself speaks out of a whirlwind, showing Job creation and its mysteries. Job yields, the friends are rebuked, and a prose epilogue tells of the restoration of his life.","name":"The book bears the name of its main character, Job, in Hebrew Iyyov. The origin of the name is debated: some link it to the Hebrew root meaning “to be an enemy,” and others compare it to ancient Semitic names meaning “where is the father?”. In the Septuagint the name appears as Iōb, from which come the Latin Iob and the English Job.","facts":[["Position","the first of the poetic and wisdom books, after Esther; in the Hebrew Bible, among the Writings (Ketuvim)"],["Chapters","42"],["Verses","1,070"],["Original language","Hebrew, with many rare words and some Aramaic features"],["Genre","wisdom: a narrative frame in prose and dialogues in poetry"]],"structure":[{"title":"Prologue","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Job is introduced as upright and prosperous; in two scenes in heaven, the accuser questions his faithfulness, and Job loses possessions, children and health. Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar arrive."},{"title":"Job's lament","from":3,"to":3,"text":"After seven days of silence, Job curses the day of his birth and asks why life is given to those who suffer."},{"title":"First cycle of dialogues","from":4,"to":14,"text":"Each friend speaks and Job answers each one: the friends argue that God rewards the righteous and punishes the wicked; Job rejects this explanation for his own case."},{"title":"Second cycle of dialogues","from":15,"to":21,"text":"The friends insist on the fate reserved for the wicked; Job feels abandoned, but affirms that he has a witness in heaven and that he will see God."},{"title":"Third cycle of dialogues","from":22,"to":27,"text":"Eliphaz accuses Job of concrete wrongs; Bildad says little, and Zophar does not speak again. Job describes the injustice in the world and maintains his integrity."},{"title":"Poem of wisdom","from":28,"to":28,"text":"Human beings know how to extract metals from the depths, but cannot find the way of wisdom, which only God knows."},{"title":"Job's final defense","from":29,"to":31,"text":"Job compares the honor of former days with his present humiliation and ends with an oath of innocence, challenging God to answer."},{"title":"The speeches of Elihu","from":32,"to":37,"text":"A young man, not mentioned until now, criticizes Job and the friends and presents suffering as a way God warns and instructs."},{"title":"The speeches of the LORD","from":38,"to":42,"text":"Out of a whirlwind, God questions Job about the order of the cosmos, the wild animals, Behemoth and Leviathan; Job answers twice, the second time in 42:1–6."},{"title":"Epilogue","from":42,"to":42,"text":"God rebukes the three friends, Job intercedes for them and receives double what he had lost (42:7–17)."}],"extra":{"title":"Prose, poetry and dialogue","text":"The book has the shape of a sandwich: the beginning and the end (1–2 and 42:7–17) are prose narrative, and the whole middle is poetry, written in parallelism, with some of the most difficult vocabulary in the Hebrew Bible. The dialogues follow a regular pattern, in which each friend speaks and Job answers, until the third cycle comes apart: Bildad's speech is short (ch. 25) and Zophar does not appear. Many scholars think part of this cycle became disordered in transmission; others see the disorder as a sign that the debate has run its course."},"people":[{"name":"Job","chapters":"1–42","ch":1,"note":"a man from the land of Uz, “blameless and upright, who feared God” (1:1), who loses everything and argues with his friends and with God"},{"name":"Job's wife","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"appears only once and tells Job to curse God and die (2:9)"},{"name":"Satan, the accuser","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"in Hebrew ha-satan, “the adversary,” with the article; questions before the LORD the motive of Job's faithfulness and does not appear again"},{"name":"Eliphaz","chapters":"2; 4–5; 15; 22; 42","ch":4,"note":"the Temanite, the first and longest of the friends to speak; appeals to a night vision (4:12–21)"},{"name":"Bildad","chapters":"2; 8; 18; 25","ch":8,"note":"the Shuhite; relies on the wisdom of the ancients"},{"name":"Zophar","chapters":"2; 11; 20","ch":11,"note":"the Naamathite; the harshest of the three, speaks only twice"},{"name":"Elihu","chapters":"32–37","ch":32,"note":"son of Barachel the Buzite; a young man who intervenes, angry with Job and with the friends"},{"name":"Jemimah, Keziah and Keren-happuch","chapters":"42","ch":42,"note":"Job's daughters born after the restoration, who receive an inheritance along with their brothers (42:14–15)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book does not say who wrote it. A tradition recorded in the Talmud attributes the book to Moses, and other rabbinic opinions place Job in very different periods, from the patriarchs to the exile; one of them even says that Job was a parable and not a historical person. Many Christian and Jewish readers have placed the story in the time of the patriarchs, because of the way of life described: wealth measured in flocks, the head of the family offering sacrifices, very long life. The prophet Ezekiel mentions Job alongside Noah and Daniel as an example of righteousness (Ezekiel 14:14, 20).","academic":"Most scholars date the composition of the book between the 6th and 4th centuries BC, around 600–400 BC, based on the language, the themes and the dialogue with other biblical texts; some propose slightly earlier or later dates. A common hypothesis is that the prose frame preserves an old story about a righteous man put to the test, on which a poet built the dialogues. Elihu's speeches, absent from the prologue and the epilogue, are seen by many as a later addition, and the poem of chapter 28 is also debated. At Qumran, Hebrew fragments of Job were found, together with an Aramaic translation (a targum), copied around the 1st century BC."},"events":{"intro":"Job gives no dates, names of kings or events that can be placed: the story takes place at an indefinite time, in a setting that recalls the age of the patriarchs. The timeline shows the two proposals for the writing, the mention of Job in Ezekiel, the Qumran fragments and two Babylonian works on the suffering of the righteous, with which the book is often compared. All dates are approximate.","axis":[-1500,0],"band":{"label":"Exile and Persian period","from":-586,"to":-332},"timeline":[{"label":"Ludlul bel nemeqi","from":-1400,"to":-1150,"kind":"evento","note":"Babylonian poem about a righteous man who suffers and is restored; approximate date"},{"label":"Writing, according to one tradition","from":-1446,"to":-1406,"kind":"escrita","note":"Moses, according to the Talmud"},{"label":"Babylonian Theodicy","from":-1000,"to":-800,"kind":"evento","note":"acrostic dialogue between a sufferer and a friend; approximate date"},{"label":"Job cited by Ezekiel","from":-593,"to":-571,"kind":"evento","note":"alongside Noah and Daniel (Ezekiel 14:14, 20)"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-600,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"most accepted range; there are earlier and later proposals"},{"label":"Targum of Job at Qumran","from":-150,"to":-1,"kind":"evento","note":"Aramaic translation found in Cave 11; approximate date of the copy"}]},"context":"The story takes place outside Israel, in the “land of Uz,” whose location is uncertain: the book of Lamentations links Uz to Edom (Lamentations 4:21), and other proposals point to northern Arabia or to the region east of the Jordan. The friends also come from places east and southeast of Canaan: Teman is a region of Edom, and the locations of Shuah and Naamah are debated. Job is not presented as an Israelite, and the name of the LORD appears mainly in the prologue, the epilogue and the divine speeches; in the dialogues names such as God and the Almighty predominate. The theme of the righteous sufferer was already known in the ancient Near East, in Babylonian works such as the Ludlul bel nemeqi and the so-called Babylonian Theodicy, and the book engages with Israel's traditional wisdom, which associated uprightness with prosperity.","places":["Uz, the land of Job","Teman, in Edom, the land of Eliphaz","Shuah and Naamah, the lands of Bildad and Zophar","heaven, where the sons of God assemble (1–2)","the ash heap where Job sits (2:8)"],"themes":["the suffering of the righteous","God's justice","integrity","the limits of human wisdom","creation","friendship and comfort"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:21","text":"saying: “Naked I came from my mother’s womb, and naked I will return. The LORD gave, and the LORD has taken away. Blessed be the name of the LORD.”"},{"ref":"19:25","text":"But I know that my Redeemer lives, and in the end He will stand upon the earth."},{"ref":"28:28","text":"And He said to man, ‘Behold, the fear of the Lord, that is wisdom, and to turn away from evil is understanding.’”"},{"ref":"42:5","text":"My ears had heard of You, but now my eyes have seen You."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. In chapters 40 and 41, the BSB follows the numbering of English Bibles: 40:1–24 corresponds to 40:1–24 of the Hebrew, and 41:1–34 corresponds to 40:25–41:26. The Septuagint has a text of Job considerably shorter than the Hebrew and adds an ending about Job's origin. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"PSA":{"t":{"1":"The two ways: the righteous and the wicked","2":"The anointed king and the rebellious nations","3":"Trust while fleeing from Absalom","4":"Peace to sleep in the midst of distress","5":"Morning prayer against deceivers","6":"The plea of a sick and weary man","7":"An appeal to God the judge against persecutors","8":"The glory of God and human dignity","9":"Praise to the God who judges the nations","10":"A complaint against the arrogance of the wicked","11":"Refuge in the LORD when the foundations shake","12":"False words and the pure word of God","13":"From long complaint to trust","14":"The folly of those who deny God","15":"Who may dwell in the tent of the LORD","16":"God as inheritance and joy","17":"The appeal of an innocent man pursued","18":"The king's thanksgiving for victory","19":"Creation and the Law reveal God","20":"A prayer for the king before battle","21":"Gratitude for the king's victories","22":"From forsakenness to praise in the assembly","23":"The LORD as shepherd and host","24":"The entrance of the King of glory","25":"An acrostic prayer for guidance and forgiveness","26":"A man of integrity asks to be examined","27":"Trust and longing for the house of the LORD","28":"A cry heard and thanksgiving","29":"The voice of the LORD in the storm","30":"Praise for healing and restoration","31":"A life committed into God's hands","32":"The blessedness of the forgiven","33":"Praise to the Creator and Lord of history","34":"An acrostic testimony of deliverance","35":"A plea for defense against ungrateful accusers","36":"Human wickedness and the goodness of God","37":"Patience before the prosperity of the wicked","38":"Sickness, guilt and abandonment","39":"The brevity of human life","40":"Deliverance, praise and a new cry for help","41":"Sick and betrayed, trusting in God","42":"Longing for God far from the sanctuary","43":"A plea for light to return to the altar","44":"The people's lament after defeat","45":"A song for the king's wedding","46":"God, the refuge of the city amid chaos","47":"God, king of all the earth","48":"Zion, the city of the great King","49":"Wealth does not rescue from death","50":"God judges the worship of His people","51":"Confession and a plea for a pure heart","52":"The end of the mighty who trusts in deceit","53":"A new version of the psalm on the fool","54":"Help in the face of the Ziphites' betrayal","55":"Betrayed by a close companion","56":"Trust in God in the midst of fear","57":"Refuge under the wings of God","58":"Against unjust judges","59":"Enemies prowling the city at night","60":"Lament after defeat and a divine promise","61":"A prayer from the ends of the earth","62":"In God alone the soul finds rest","63":"Thirsting for God in the wilderness","64":"Against conspirators with sharp tongues","65":"God forgives and fills the earth with harvests","66":"All the earth invited to praise","67":"A blessing so that the peoples praise","68":"God's victorious march to the sanctuary","69":"Sinking in waters and insults","70":"An urgent plea for help","71":"The prayer of one who has trusted since youth","72":"The righteous reign of the king","73":"Envy of the wicked and the sanctuary","74":"Lament for the ruined sanctuary","75":"God judges at the appointed time","76":"God victorious in Zion","77":"Remembering the exodus in a night of distress","78":"Lessons from the history of Israel","79":"Lament for Jerusalem invaded","80":"A plea to restore the vine of Israel","81":"Call to the feast and appeal to obedience","82":"God judges the gods","83":"Against the alliance of enemy nations","84":"Longing for the courts of the LORD","85":"Restoration: righteousness and peace meet","86":"The prayer of a poor and needy servant","87":"Zion, the birthplace of the peoples","88":"Lament without comfort in the face of death","89":"The covenant with David in crisis","90":"The eternal God and fleeting life","91":"Protection in the shadow of the Most High","92":"Praise for the Sabbath day","93":"The LORD reigns above the waters","94":"An appeal to the Judge of the earth","95":"A call to worship and obedience","96":"A new song for all the earth","97":"God's reign makes the earth rejoice","98":"The King comes to judge with justice","99":"The holy King of Israel","100":"All the earth serving with gladness","101":"The king's commitment to integrity","102":"The lament of the afflicted and hope for Zion","103":"The mercy and forgiveness of God","104":"A hymn to the Creator who sustains life","105":"God's faithfulness from the patriarchs to the exodus","106":"Israel's unfaithfulness and divine patience","107":"Redeemed from four dangers","108":"Praise and confidence in victory","109":"A cry against unjust accusers","110":"The priest-king at God's right hand","111":"An acrostic of the works of the LORD","112":"An acrostic of the life of one who fears God","113":"God lifts up the humble","114":"Nature trembles before the exodus","115":"The living God and lifeless idols","116":"Thanksgiving of one who escaped death","117":"An invitation to all nations","118":"Thanksgiving and the rejected stone","119":"An acrostic meditation on the Law of God","120":"Living among those who hate peace","121":"The LORD keeps the pilgrim","122":"The joy of arriving in Jerusalem","123":"Eyes turned to God amid contempt","124":"The people escape like a bird from the snare","125":"Firm as Mount Zion","126":"The joy of Zion's restoration","127":"House and children as a gift from God","128":"The blessing of a God-fearing home","129":"Israel afflicted but not defeated","130":"Out of the depths, waiting for forgiveness","131":"The soul quieted like a child","132":"David, the ark and the choice of Zion","133":"Living together in unity","134":"A night blessing in the temple","135":"Praise to the God who chose Israel","136":"A litany of thanks for creation and history","137":"The lament of the exiles in Babylon","138":"Thanksgiving before the kings of the earth","139":"God knows and surrounds the psalmist","140":"Protection against the violent","141":"A prayer not to give in to evil","142":"A cry from the cave","143":"No one is righteous before God","144":"The king asks for victory and prosperity","145":"An acrostic hymn to the greatness of the King","146":"Trust in God, not in princes","147":"God rebuilds Jerusalem and rules nature","148":"Let all creation praise the LORD","149":"The praise and victory of the faithful","150":"Final praise with every instrument"},"about":{"title_orig":"תְּהִלִּים","title_orig_xlit":"Tehillim","summary":"Psalms is Israel's great collection of poems of prayer and song: 150 texts that speak to God and speak of God. There are hymns that celebrate the Creator and the King of all the earth, laments of individuals and of the people in danger, thanksgivings for deliverance, psalms about Israel's king and about Zion, meditations on the Law and reflections of wisdom. Many voices cross: the complaint of one who feels abandoned, the confession of guilt, trust, the joy of the festival and even the plea for vengeance against oppressors. The collection is divided into five books and moves, as a whole, from lament to praise, ending in a series of psalms that begin and end with “Hallelujah.”","name":"In Hebrew the book is called Tehillim, “praises,” a word from the same root as “hallelujah” (“praise Yah”). The English name comes from the Greek Psalmoi, the title used in the Septuagint: a psalmos was a song accompanied by a stringed instrument. Another Greek form, Psaltērion, the name of a stringed instrument, gave rise to “Psalter,” the traditional name of the collection.","facts":[["Position","among the poetic and wisdom books, after Job; in the Hebrew Bible, among the Writings (Ketuvim), which it usually opens"],["Chapters","150"],["Verses","2,461"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","poetry: hymns, laments, thanksgivings, prayers and wisdom psalms"]],"structure":[{"title":"Book I","from":1,"to":41,"text":"Opens with two introductory psalms, on the two ways (1) and on the anointed king (2). Almost all the others bear David's name in the title, and individual pleas predominate. It ends with a blessing (41:13)."},{"title":"Book II","from":42,"to":72,"text":"Begins with psalms of the sons of Korah (42–49), continues with one of Asaph (50) and another group attributed to David (51–71). It ends with a psalm for the king, a blessing and the note “the prayers of David son of Jesse are concluded” (72:18–20)."},{"title":"Book III","from":73,"to":89,"text":"Gathers psalms of Asaph (73–83) and of the sons of Korah (84–88), with many laments over the destruction of the sanctuary and the crisis of the people. It ends with Psalm 89, which asks about the covenant with David, and a blessing (89:52)."},{"title":"Book IV","from":90,"to":106,"text":"Opens with the prayer attributed to Moses (90) and gathers the psalms that proclaim that the LORD reigns (93–99). Psalms 105 and 106 retell Israel's history; the closing blessing is in 106:48."},{"title":"Book V","from":107,"to":150,"text":"Includes the long psalm of the Law (119), the Songs of Ascents (120–134), a last group attributed to David (138–145) and the five “Hallelujah” psalms that close the collection (146–150)."}],"extra":{"title":"Parallelism and acrostics","text":"The poetry of the Psalms does not rely on rhyme, but on parallelism: one line is followed by another that repeats the idea in other words (“God is our refuge and strength, an ever-present help in times of trouble,” 46:1), contrasts with it or develops it. Several psalms are acrostics, in which lines or stanzas begin with the letters of the Hebrew alphabet in order: 9–10 (incompletely), 25, 34, 37, 111, 112, 119 and 145. The most elaborate case is Psalm 119, the longest in the Bible: there are 22 stanzas, one for each letter, and the eight verses of each stanza all begin with the same letter, which adds up to 176 verses; the app shows the name of the letter at the start of each stanza (Aleph, Beth…). Other devices are refrains (42–43; 46; 107; 136) and the word Selah, of uncertain meaning, perhaps a musical direction."},"people":[{"name":"David","chapters":"3–41; 51–70; 138–145 and others","ch":3,"note":"king of Israel, named in the title of 73 psalms in the Hebrew text; several titles link the psalm to episodes of his life (3; 18; 34; 51; 52; 54; 56; 57; 59; 60; 63; 142)"},{"name":"Asaph","chapters":"50; 73–83","ch":73,"note":"Levite musician of David's time, according to Chronicles; his name is in the title of 12 psalms, perhaps indicating a family or group of singers"},{"name":"The sons of Korah","chapters":"42–49; 84–85; 87–88","ch":42,"note":"a group of Levite singers, descendants of Korah; 11 psalms bear their name"},{"name":"Solomon","chapters":"72; 127","ch":72,"note":"David's son and successor; the expression in the title can mean “of Solomon” or “for Solomon”"},{"name":"Moses","chapters":"90","ch":90,"note":"called “the man of God” in the title of the only psalm attributed to him"},{"name":"Heman and Ethan the Ezrahites","chapters":"88–89","ch":88,"note":"wise men and musicians also mentioned in 1 Kings 4:31 and in Chronicles; each gives his name to a psalm"},{"name":"Jeduthun","chapters":"39; 62; 77","ch":39,"note":"Levite musician, according to Chronicles; in the titles, the expression “according to Jeduthun” may indicate the author, a style or a group of singers"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Following the titles, Jewish and Christian traditions attribute most of the Psalms to David, remembered as “the sweet psalmist of Israel” (2 Samuel 23:1) and organizer of the music of worship (1 Chronicles 16; 25). The book itself acknowledges other authors: Asaph, the sons of Korah, Solomon, Moses, Heman and Ethan. According to the Talmud, David composed the book with the contribution of ten earlier authors, among them Adam, Melchizedek, Abraham and Moses. The New Testament quotes psalms as words of David, for example Psalms 2, 16 and 110. On this reading, the collection began to be composed around 1000 BC and was completed with later psalms, such as those of the exile.","academic":"Scholars see the Psalms as a collection formed over many centuries, from smaller collections: those of David, of Asaph, of the sons of Korah, the Songs of Ascents and the “Hallelujah” psalms. Many consider the titles later additions to the poems, and the Hebrew expression translated “of David” may indicate authorship, dedication or a link with a royal collection. Some psalms may come from the time of the monarchy, such as the psalms about the king, and Psalm 29 has parallels with the Canaanite poetry of Ugarit; others presuppose the exile (137) or the return to Zion (126). In Psalms 42 to 83 the name “God” (Elohim) predominates instead of “the LORD,” a sign of a distinct edition of this block. The final form is usually placed between the 5th and 2nd centuries BC; Qumran manuscripts show that the order of Books IV and V still varied in some places in the 1st century."},"events":{"intro":"The Psalms do not tell a story, but several titles refer to episodes in David's life, and some poems allude to events in the life of the people, such as the destruction of Jerusalem and the exile (74; 79; 137) and the return to Zion (126). The timeline shows the kings named in the titles, according to the most widely used chronology, these events, the proposals for the composition of the collection and the Qumran manuscripts. The dates are approximate.","axis":[-1100,100],"band":{"label":"First temple","from":-960,"to":-586},"timeline":[{"label":"David","from":-1010,"to":-970,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign; 73 psalms bear his name"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-1010,"to":-931,"kind":"escrita","note":"David composes most of them; other psalms are attributed to Moses, Solomon, Asaph and the sons of Korah"},{"label":"Solomon","from":-970,"to":-931,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign; builds the temple in Jerusalem (Ps 72; 127)"},{"label":"Composition of the psalms, according to scholarship","from":-1000,"to":-300,"kind":"escrita","note":"poems from various periods, from the monarchy to the Persian period"},{"label":"Destruction of Jerusalem and exile","from":-586,"to":-539,"kind":"evento","note":"background of psalms such as 74, 79 and 137"},{"label":"Return from exile and second temple","from":-538,"to":-515,"kind":"evento","note":"the period to which Psalm 126 is usually linked"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-400,"to":-150,"kind":"escrita","note":"gathered into five books; the final order of the last third still varied"},{"label":"Qumran manuscripts","from":-150,"to":68,"kind":"evento","note":"dozens of copies of psalms, some with a different order in Books IV and V"}]},"context":"Much of the Psalms was born and used in the worship of the temple in Jerusalem, where, according to Chronicles, Levite singers and musicians accompanied the sacrifices and feasts. The titles keep traces of this use: mentions of the “choirmaster,” of stringed and wind instruments, of known tunes (“The Doe of the Dawn,” “The Lilies”) and of occasions such as the dedication of the house (30) and the Sabbath (92). The Songs of Ascents (120–134) are usually linked to the pilgrimages to Jerusalem, and Psalms 113 to 118, the Hallel, were sung at the great feasts. The poetry of the Psalms shares forms and images with the hymns and prayers of the ancient Near East, such as the texts of Ugarit and the Egyptian hymns to the sun, compared with Psalm 104. After the exile, the collection also came to serve personal prayer and study, and became the prayer book of the synagogue and the church.","places":["Jerusalem and Mount Zion","the temple and its courts","the Wilderness of Judah (63)","the Jordan and Mount Hermon (42:6; 133:3)","Egypt and the Red Sea (78; 106; 114)","the rivers of Babylon (137)"],"themes":["praise","lament and supplication","trust","the reign of God","the king and Zion","the Law","the righteous and the wicked"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:1–2","text":"Blessed is the man who does not walk in the counsel of the wicked, or set foot on the path of sinners, or sit in the seat of mockers. But his delight is in the Law of the LORD, and on His law he meditates day and night."},{"ref":"23:1","text":"A Psalm of David. The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want."},{"ref":"46:1","text":"God is our refuge and strength, an ever-present help in times of trouble."},{"ref":"150:6","text":"Let everything that has breath praise the LORD! Hallelujah!"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. In this app, the title of many psalms appears as verse 0, separate from the poem; in others, the title comes at the start of verse 1. The Hebrew Bible counts the title as verse 1 (sometimes 1–2), so the verse numbering there can be one or two higher. The Septuagint and the Vulgate join Psalms 9 and 10 and Psalms 114 and 115 and divide Psalms 116 and 147; for this reason, between Psalms 10 and 147, their numbering is usually one lower, and the Septuagint also has a Psalm 151. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"PRO":{"t":{"1":"The purpose of the book; Wisdom calls in the streets","2":"Seeking wisdom like hidden treasure","3":"Trust in the LORD and the worth of wisdom","4":"A father teaches to guard the heart","5":"Faithfulness to the wife of your youth","6":"Pledges, laziness and what the LORD hates","7":"A young man seduced at nightfall","8":"Wisdom speaks and recalls the creation","9":"Two banquets: Wisdom and Folly","10":"Proverbs of Solomon: the righteous and the wicked","11":"Honest scales, generosity and integrity","12":"Truthful words and diligent work","13":"Correction, honest wealth and good company","14":"The wise and the foolish; righteousness exalts a nation","15":"The gentle answer and the eyes of the LORD","16":"Man plans, the LORD directs","17":"Peace at home, friendship and silence","18":"The power of the tongue; the name of the LORD","19":"The honest poor and the counsel of the LORD","20":"Wine, honest weights and a man's steps","21":"The king's heart in the hand of the LORD","22":"A good name; the words of the wise begin","23":"At the table of the powerful; the evils of wine","24":"More sayings of the wise; the sluggard's field","25":"Proverbs copied by the men of Hezekiah","26":"The fool, the sluggard and the deceiver","27":"Sincere friendship and care for the flock","28":"Those who keep the law and those who forsake it","29":"Just rule, discipline and trust in the LORD","30":"Agur: the limits of knowledge and numerical sayings","31":"Lemuel and the praise of the noble wife"},"about":{"title_orig":"מִשְׁלֵי","title_orig_xlit":"Mishlei","summary":"Proverbs is a collection of teachings on how to live wisely in everyday life: in the family, at work, in the use of words, in dealing with money, friends and authorities. Chapters 1 to 9 gather longer speeches, in which a father instructs his son and Wisdom herself, portrayed as a woman, calls to people in the streets and squares. From chapter 10 on, short sayings predominate, almost always of two lines, that contrast the wise and the fool, the righteous and the wicked, the hard worker and the sluggard. What unites the whole is the idea that the fear of the LORD is the starting point of knowledge and wisdom (1:7; 9:10). The book ends with the words of Agur and Lemuel and with a poem in praise of the wife of noble character.","name":"The Hebrew title, Mishlei, comes from the first words of the book: “The proverbs of Solomon” (1:1). The word mashal, translated “proverb,” is broader than a popular saying and covers comparisons, maxims and teachings in poetic form. The English name comes from the Latin of the Vulgate, Liber Proverbiorum, “Book of Proverbs”; in the Septuagint, the title is Paroimiai.","facts":[["Position","among the poetic and wisdom books, after Psalms; in the Hebrew Bible, among the Writings (Ketuvim)"],["Chapters","31"],["Verses","915"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","wisdom: instructions and sayings in poetry"]],"structure":[{"title":"Prologue and instructions to the son","from":1,"to":9,"text":"The purpose of the book (1:1–7), a father's advice to his son and the speeches of personified Wisdom. The part ends with two rival invitations: that of Wisdom and that of the woman Folly (ch. 9)."},{"title":"Proverbs of Solomon","from":10,"to":22,"text":"The first great collection (10:1–22:16), with 375 verses, almost all independent sayings of two lines."},{"title":"Sayings of the wise","from":22,"to":24,"text":"Two smaller collections (22:17–24:22 and 24:23–34), with longer counsels, partly similar to an Egyptian wisdom work."},{"title":"Collection of the men of Hezekiah","from":25,"to":29,"text":"Proverbs of Solomon “copied by the men of Hezekiah king of Judah” (25:1), with many comparisons drawn from nature and daily life."},{"title":"Words of Agur","from":30,"to":30,"text":"Reflections on the limits of human knowledge, a short prayer and numerical sayings of the “three things… and four” kind."},{"title":"Lemuel and the wife of noble character","from":31,"to":31,"text":"The counsel King Lemuel's mother taught him (31:1–9) and the alphabetic poem of the wife of noble character (31:10–31)."}],"extra":{"title":"Sayings in two lines","text":"Most of the book is written in parallelism, the typical device of Hebrew poetry: the second line repeats the first in other words, contrasts with it or completes it. In chapters 10 to 15 contrast predominates, almost always marked by “but,” as in 10:1. The book closes with an acrostic: each of the 22 verses of 31:10–31 begins with a letter of the Hebrew alphabet, in order. Some scholars have also observed that the collection of 10:1–22:16 has 375 verses, the same number as the sum of the values of the letters of Solomon's name (Shelomoh) in Hebrew."},"people":[{"name":"Solomon","chapters":"1; 10; 25","ch":1,"note":"king of Israel, to whom the titles attribute the main collections"},{"name":"The father and the son","chapters":"1–7; 23–24","ch":1,"note":"the teacher who instructs (“my son”) and the young man who must choose between two ways; the mother also teaches (1:8; 6:20)"},{"name":"Wisdom","chapters":"1; 8–9","ch":8,"note":"personified as a woman who calls out in the squares and who was with God at creation (8:22–31)"},{"name":"The adulteress and Folly","chapters":"2; 5–7; 9","ch":7,"note":"figures of the seduction that leads to death, in contrast with Wisdom"},{"name":"Hezekiah","chapters":"25","ch":25,"note":"king of Judah; his men copied a collection of Solomon's proverbs"},{"name":"Agur","chapters":"30","ch":30,"note":"son of Jakeh, author of sayings on the limits of human knowledge; not known from other sources"},{"name":"Lemuel and his mother","chapters":"31","ch":31,"note":"a king mentioned only here; records his mother's counsel about women, wine and justice for the poor"},{"name":"The wife of noble character","chapters":"31","ch":31,"note":"in Hebrew, eshet chayil, “woman of valor”: she cares for the house and for business and is praised for fearing the LORD"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Following the book's own titles (1:1; 10:1; 25:1), Jewish and Christian traditions attribute most of Proverbs to Solomon, who reigned around 970–931 BC and of whom it is said that he “composed three thousand proverbs” (1 Kings 4:32). The book also acknowledges other voices: the “wise” (22:17; 24:23), Agur and Lemuel. According to the Talmud, Hezekiah and his companions gave the book its written form, in line with 25:1; some rabbinic readings identified Agur and Lemuel with Solomon himself.","academic":"Scholars see Proverbs as an anthology formed over centuries. Some of the sayings may come from the time of the monarchy, and many consider plausible the note about the men of Hezekiah (25:1), around 700 BC, as a sign of scribal activity at the court of Judah. The passage 22:17–24:22 has close parallels with the Instruction of Amenemope, an older Egyptian work. Chapters 1 to 9 are usually considered the most recent part, and the final form of the book is generally placed in the Persian or early Hellenistic period, around 500–300 BC, although some propose older dates."},"events":{"intro":"Proverbs narrates no events: its sayings speak of everyday life and can almost never be dated. The timeline shows the kings named in the titles, according to the most widely used chronology (authors vary by a few years), the Egyptian work compared with 22:17–24:22 and the two main proposals for the composition of the book.","axis":[-1350,-250],"band":{"label":"Monarchy in Israel and Judah","from":-1030,"to":-586},"timeline":[{"label":"Instruction of Amenemope","from":-1300,"to":-1075,"kind":"evento","note":"Egyptian wisdom work, of approximate date; parallels in 22:17–24:22"},{"label":"Solomon","from":-970,"to":-931,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign (1:1; 10:1; 25:1)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-970,"to":-686,"kind":"escrita","note":"Solomon composes most of it; the compilation is completed in Hezekiah's time"},{"label":"Hezekiah","from":-715,"to":-686,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign; his men copy proverbs of Solomon (25:1)"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-500,"to":-300,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian and early Hellenistic periods"}]},"context":"Proverbs belongs to a tradition of wisdom common to the whole ancient Near East. In Egypt, scribes and officials learned from “instructions” attributed to kings and high officials, such as those of Ptahhotep and Amenemope; in Mesopotamia collections of proverbs circulated and, in Aramaic, the words of the sage Ahiqar. The biblical account itself compares Solomon's wisdom to that “of all the men of the East” and “of Egypt” (1 Kings 4:30). The settings of the book are the house, the field, the market, the city gate, where cases were judged, and the king's court. The Hebrew word massa, in the titles of 30:1 and 31:1, can be read as “oracle” or “burden,” or as the name of a tribe of northern Arabia (Genesis 25:14); in that case, Agur and Lemuel would be foreign sages.","places":["Israel and Judah (1:1; 25:1)","the king's court","the city gate","squares, streets and crossroads","the house and the field","Massa, perhaps the land of Agur and Lemuel"],"themes":["the fear of the LORD","wisdom and folly","the power of words","work and laziness","wealth, poverty and justice","family and education"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"4:23","text":"Guard your heart with all diligence, for from it flow springs of life."},{"ref":"9:10","text":"The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom, and knowledge of the Holy One is understanding."},{"ref":"16:9","text":"A man’s heart plans his course, but the LORD determines his steps."},{"ref":"31:30","text":"Charm is deceptive and beauty is fleeting, but a woman who fears the LORD is to be praised."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. In the Septuagint, the collections of chapters 24 to 31 appear in a different order, and there are sayings that are not in the Hebrew text. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"ECC":{"t":{"1":"Nothing new under the sun","2":"The king tries pleasure, works and wisdom","3":"A time for everything","4":"The oppressed; two are better than one","5":"Vows to God and the love of money","6":"Goods that cannot be enjoyed","7":"Sayings on what is better","8":"The king's power; justice delayed","9":"The same fate for all; enjoy life","10":"A little folly spoils wisdom","11":"Acting without knowing the future; youth","12":"Old age, death and the conclusion"},"about":{"title_orig":"קֹהֶלֶת","title_orig_xlit":"Qohelet","summary":"Ecclesiastes records the reflection of a sage called Qohelet, the Teacher, who presents himself as “the son of David, king in Jerusalem” and investigates what profit human beings gain from all their labor “under the sun.” He puts to the test wisdom, pleasure, great works and wealth, observes injustice and death, which comes equally to the wise and the fool, and concludes again and again that everything is “futile.” At the same time, he recommends enjoying with gladness bread, wine, work and life with the one you love, as gifts of God. The book ends with an epilogue that sums up the teaching: fear God and keep His commandments (12:13).","name":"In Hebrew, the book is called Qohelet, the name or title of the one who speaks; the word comes from a root meaning “to gather” and indicates someone who convenes an assembly or addresses it. The Septuagint translated the term as Ekklesiastes, from ekklesia, “assembly,” a form that passed into Latin and English. The BSB translates it as “the Teacher.”","facts":[["Position","among the poetic and wisdom books, after Proverbs; in the Hebrew Bible, one of the five Scrolls (Megillot)"],["Chapters","12"],["Verses","222"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","wisdom: reflection in prose and poetry"]],"structure":[{"title":"Opening","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The title, the motto “everything is futile” and a poem on the endless cycle of nature and of the generations (1:1–11)."},{"title":"The king's experience","from":1,"to":2,"text":"In the role of a king, the Teacher puts to the test wisdom, pleasure, building and wealth, and runs up against death (1:12–2:26)."},{"title":"Time, injustice and work","from":3,"to":6,"text":"The time for everything, oppression, loneliness, vows, money and possessions that bring no satisfaction (3:1–6:9)."},{"title":"What cannot be known","from":6,"to":11,"text":"Counsels and reflections on the limits of wisdom: no one knows for sure what is good or what is to come, but life should be lived with joy (6:10–11:6)."},{"title":"Youth, old age and conclusion","from":11,"to":12,"text":"A poem about enjoying youth before old age and death, the return of the motto and the epilogue (11:7–12:14)."}],"extra":{"title":"“Futility” and “under the sun”","text":"The Hebrew word hevel, which the BSB translates as “futility,” literally means “vapor” or “breath” and appears 38 times in the book: it points to what is fleeting and cannot be fully grasped or understood. The expression “under the sun” appears 29 times and marks out the field of the investigation: life as it can be observed in this world. The motto “futility of futilities” opens and closes the words of the Teacher (1:2; 12:8), and in 1:1–2, 7:27 and 12:8–14 another voice speaks of him in the third person, like a frame. Ecclesiastes always calls God Elohim, “God,” and never uses the name YHWH, which the BSB renders as “the LORD.”"},"people":[{"name":"Qohelet, the Teacher","chapters":"1–12","ch":1,"note":"the main voice of the book, who reports in the first person what he saw and concluded"},{"name":"The king, son of David","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"a role the Teacher takes on; the name is not given, but the riches and works of 2:4–9 recall Solomon"},{"name":"The narrator of the epilogue","chapters":"1; 7; 12","ch":12,"note":"speaks of the Teacher in the third person (1:1–2; 7:27; 12:8–14) and sums up his teaching"},{"name":"The poor wise man","chapters":"9","ch":9,"note":"delivers a small besieged city by his wisdom and is then forgotten (9:13–16)"},{"name":"The young man","chapters":"11–12","ch":11,"note":"recipient of the final counsels (“rejoice, O young man,” 11:9; “my son,” 12:12)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Based on 1:1 and 1:12, Jewish and Christian traditions attributed the book to Solomon, who reigned around 970–931 BC. A rabbinic tradition says he wrote the Song of Songs in his youth, Proverbs in his mature years and Ecclesiastes in his old age. The Talmud attributes the written form of the book to Hezekiah and his companions.","academic":"Most scholars understand that the author uses the figure of Solomon as a literary device and place the work between the Persian and Hellenistic periods, around 450–200 BC; many prefer the 3rd century BC. The arguments are the language, close to the late Hebrew of the Mishnah and influenced by Aramaic, words of Persian origin (such as pardes, “park,” in 2:5) and the social setting of trade, money and administration. Fragments found at Qumran, from the mid-2nd century BC, set a limit for the date. A minority argues for older dates, based on another analysis of the language."},"events":{"intro":"Ecclesiastes narrates no datable events: its observations hold for life in any age. The timeline shows the reign of Solomon, to whom the book alludes, the two main proposals for the period of composition and the oldest known manuscripts.","axis":[-1000,-50],"band":{"label":"Hellenistic period","from":-332,"to":-63},"timeline":[{"label":"Solomon","from":-970,"to":-931,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign; the “son of David, king in Jerusalem” (1:1)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-950,"to":-931,"kind":"escrita","note":"Solomon, in his old age, according to a rabbinic tradition"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-450,"to":-200,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian or Hellenistic periods; many prefer the 3rd century BC"},{"label":"Qumran fragments","from":-175,"to":-150,"kind":"evento","note":"the oldest known copies of the book, of approximate date"}]},"context":"If the date proposed by most scholars is correct, Ecclesiastes was written when Judah was a small province of foreign empires: first the Persian, then that of the Hellenistic kings of Egypt, the Ptolemies. The book speaks of money, profit, inheritance and a chain of officials who watch over one another (5:8–9). The brevity of life and the advice to enjoy the present also appear in other ancient literatures: in the Epic of Gilgamesh, the alewife Siduri advises the hero to eat, be merry, wear clean clothes and make his wife happy, in terms similar to 9:7–9. In Judaism, there was an ancient debate about the book's place in Scripture, recorded in the Mishnah; in many communities, it is read at the Feast of Tabernacles (Sukkot).","places":["Jerusalem (1:1, 12, 16)","the house of God (5:1)","the king's gardens, parks and pools (2:4–6)","a small besieged city (9:14)"],"themes":["futility (hevel)","work and profit","time and death","the limits of wisdom","joy as a gift of God","the fear of God"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:2","text":"“Futility of futilities,” says the Teacher, “futility of futilities! Everything is futile!”"},{"ref":"3:1","text":"To everything there is a season, and a time for every purpose under heaven:"},{"ref":"9:7","text":"Go, eat your bread with joy, and drink your wine with a cheerful heart, for God has already approved your works:"},{"ref":"12:13","text":"When all has been heard, the conclusion of the matter is this: Fear God and keep His commandments, because this is the whole duty of man."}],"note":"Dates are approximate. In the Hebrew numbering, the first verse of chapter 5 in English Bibles is counted as the last verse of chapter 4, and the other verses of chapter 5 are one number lower. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"SNG":{"t":{"1":"Desire and praise between the lovers","2":"Spring arrives and the beloved calls","3":"A search by night and Solomon's procession","4":"The beauty of the bride, a garden locked","5":"Missed at the door; a portrait of the beloved","6":"The beloved in the garden; the only one among all","7":"The beauty of the Shulammite; an invitation to the fields","8":"Love is as strong as death"},"about":{"title_orig":"שִׁיר הַשִּׁירִים","title_orig_xlit":"Shir ha-Shirim","summary":"The Song of Songs is a collection of love poems in which a woman and a man speak of each other and to each other, with the participation of a chorus, the “daughters of Jerusalem.” The poems celebrate desire, the beauty of the body and the joy of meeting, with images drawn from gardens, vineyards, spices, animals and landscapes of the land of Israel. There are also moments of absence and searching, such as the two night scenes in which the beloved woman looks for her beloved through the city (3:1–4; 5:2–8). The sentence “My beloved is mine and I am his” (2:16), taken up with variations in 6:3 and 7:10, sums up the mutuality of love, and the high point of the book affirms that “love is as strong as death” (8:6). God is not mentioned explicitly, but Jews and Christians have for centuries also read the book as an image of the love between God and His people.","name":"The Hebrew title, Shir ha-Shirim, “Song of Songs,” is a form of superlative, like “Holy of Holies,” and means “the most beautiful of songs.” The Septuagint and the Vulgate kept the expression (Asma Asmaton; Canticum Canticorum). In English, the book is also called the Song of Solomon or Canticles, because of the title in 1:1.","facts":[["Position","among the poetic and wisdom books, after Ecclesiastes; in the Hebrew Bible, one of the five Scrolls (Megillot)"],["Chapters","8"],["Verses","117"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","love poetry"]],"structure":[{"title":"Desire and first meetings","from":1,"to":2,"text":"The beloved woman longs for her beloved and speaks to the daughters of Jerusalem; the two exchange praises, until the first plea not to awaken love before its time (1:1–2:7)."},{"title":"Spring and search","from":2,"to":3,"text":"At the end of winter, the beloved calls her to come away; at night, she searches for him through the city and finds him (2:8–3:5)."},{"title":"The procession and the garden","from":3,"to":5,"text":"Solomon's carriage on his wedding day, the description of the beloved woman's beauty and the image of her as a locked garden (3:6–5:1)."},{"title":"Missed and found again","from":5,"to":6,"text":"The beloved knocks at the door and goes away; she searches for him, describes him to the daughters of Jerusalem and says he has gone down to his garden (5:2–6:3)."},{"title":"The beauty of the Shulammite","from":6,"to":8,"text":"New praises of the beloved woman, unique among all, and her invitation to go together to the countryside (6:4–8:4)."},{"title":"As strong as death","from":8,"to":8,"text":"The affirmation of the strength of love, the words of the brothers, Solomon's vineyard and the final call (8:5–14)."}],"extra":{"title":"Who is speaking?","text":"The Hebrew text does not indicate who speaks in each passage; the reader is guided by the masculine and feminine forms of verbs and pronouns, which Hebrew distinguishes. The speaker headings that many translations add, such as “She,” “He” and “Friends,” are additions: they help the reading, but reflect an interpretation. Some lines return as refrains and mark the rhythm of the book: the plea to the daughters of Jerusalem not to awaken love before its time (2:7; 3:5; 8:4), the formula of mutual belonging (2:16; 6:3; 7:10) and the question “Who is this…?” (3:6; 6:10; 8:5)."},"people":[{"name":"The beloved woman","chapters":"1–8","ch":1,"note":"the voice that speaks most in the book; in 6:13 she is called the Shulammite, a name that may indicate her origin (Shunem) or echo the name of Solomon"},{"name":"The beloved","chapters":"1–8","ch":1,"note":"presented sometimes as a shepherd, sometimes as a king; praises the beloved woman's beauty and is described by her (5:10–16)"},{"name":"The daughters of Jerusalem","chapters":"1–3; 5–6; 8","ch":5,"note":"the chorus of women with whom the beloved woman converses"},{"name":"Solomon","chapters":"1; 3; 8","ch":3,"note":"named in the title, in the wedding procession (3:6–11) and in the vineyard at Baal-hamon (8:11–12)"},{"name":"The beloved woman's brothers","chapters":"1; 8","ch":8,"note":"made her keeper of the vineyards (1:6) and discuss her future (8:8–9)"},{"name":"The watchmen of the city","chapters":"3; 5","ch":5,"note":"find the beloved woman in her night searches; the second time, they beat her (5:7)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The title (1:1) links the book to Solomon, to whom 1 Kings 4:32 attributes a thousand and five songs, and Jewish and Christian traditions considered him the author, placing the work in the 10th century BC. A rabbinic tradition says he wrote it in his youth; the Talmud attributes the written form to Hezekiah and his companions. The Hebrew expression of the title, however, can mean “of Solomon,” “for Solomon” or “concerning Solomon.”","academic":"Many scholars see the book as an anthology of love poems, perhaps sung at wedding celebrations, gathered and edited in a later period. The language is one of the arguments: the short form of the relative pronoun, typical of late Hebrew, appears throughout the book except in the title, and there are words of Persian origin (pardes, “orchard,” in 4:13) and perhaps Greek (the “carriage” of 3:9). For this reason the final form is usually dated to the Persian or Hellenistic periods, between about 500 and 250 BC. Other researchers argue that some of the poems are older, recalling, for example, the mention of Tirzah (6:4), capital of the northern kingdom in the 10th and early 9th centuries BC."},"events":{"intro":"The Song narrates no datable historical events. The timeline shows the reign of Solomon, named in the title, the period when Tirzah, mentioned in 6:4, was capital of the northern kingdom, and the two main proposals for the period of composition.","axis":[-1000,-200],"band":{"label":"Persian period","from":-539,"to":-332},"timeline":[{"label":"Solomon","from":-970,"to":-931,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign (1:1; 3:7–11; 8:11–12)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-970,"to":-950,"kind":"escrita","note":"Solomon, in his youth, according to a rabbinic tradition"},{"label":"Tirzah, capital of the northern kingdom","from":-930,"to":-880,"kind":"evento","note":"compared to Jerusalem in 6:4"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-500,"to":-250,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian or Hellenistic periods; parts may be older"}]},"context":"The images of the Song come from the landscape of the land of Israel and its neighbors: the vineyards of En-gedi, by the Dead Sea, the plain of Sharon, the mountains of Gilead, Carmel, Lebanon and Hermon, as well as cities such as Jerusalem, Tirzah, Heshbon and Damascus. Love poetry was common in the ancient Near East: Egyptian songs of the New Kingdom (c. 1300–1100 BC) also call the lovers “brother” and “sister,” as in 4:9–12, and use gardens, perfumes and flowers as images of desire. For many centuries the allegorical reading predominated: the Targum and the Midrash saw in the book the love between God and Israel, and Rabbi Akiva called it the “holy of holies” among the Writings; Christian authors such as Origen and Bernard of Clairvaux read it as an image of the love between Christ and the Church or the soul. Among scholars today the reading of the book as poetry about human love prevails, which does not exclude the traditional religious readings. In Judaism, it is read at Passover (Pesach).","places":["Jerusalem and Zion","En-gedi","Sharon","Lebanon, Hermon and Senir","Gilead and Carmel","Tirzah, Heshbon and Damascus"],"themes":["love and desire","beauty","searching and finding","mutual belonging","nature and the garden","the strength of love"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:7","text":"O daughters of Jerusalem, I adjure you by the gazelles and does of the field: Do not arouse or awaken love until the time is right."},{"ref":"2:16","text":"My beloved is mine and I am his; he pastures his flock among the lilies."},{"ref":"8:6–7","text":"Set me as a seal over your heart, as a seal upon your arm. For love is as strong as death, its jealousy as unrelenting as Sheol. Its sparks are fiery flames, the fiercest blaze of all. Mighty waters cannot quench love; rivers cannot sweep it away. If a man were to give all the wealth of his house for love, his offer would be utterly scorned."}],"note":"Dates are approximate. In the Hebrew numbering, the last verse of chapter 6 in English Bibles (6:13) opens chapter 7, and the verses of that chapter are one number higher. The speaker headings found in many translations are not part of the Hebrew text. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"ISA":{"t":{"1":"Charges against Judah and a call to cleansing","2":"The mountain of the LORD and pride brought low","3":"Judgment on the leaders and women of Jerusalem","4":"The Branch of the LORD and Zion cleansed","5":"The song of the vineyard and the woes","6":"The vision of the throne; Isaiah is sent","7":"Ahaz, the threat of Aram and Ephraim; Immanuel","8":"Maher-shalal-hash-baz and the Assyrian invasion","9":"Light to a people in darkness; wrath against Israel","10":"Assyria, the rod of anger; the remnant","11":"The shoot of Jesse and the scattered gathered","12":"A song of thanks for salvation","13":"Oracle against Babylon","14":"The fall of the king of Babylon; Assyria and Philistia","15":"Lament over Moab","16":"The fugitives of Moab and a new lament","17":"Against Damascus and Ephraim","18":"A message to the land of Cush","19":"Judgment and future healing of Egypt","20":"Isaiah stripped, a sign against Egypt and Cush","21":"The fall of Babylon; Edom and Arabia","22":"The valley of vision; Shebna and Eliakim","23":"Oracle against Tyre and Sidon","24":"Judgment on the whole earth","25":"The banquet on the mountain and death swallowed up","26":"A song of trust of the strong city","27":"Leviathan defeated and the vineyard restored","28":"The drunkards of Ephraim; the stone in Zion","29":"Ariel besieged; a people with lips but no heart","30":"Against the alliance with Egypt","31":"Trust God, not the horses of Egypt","32":"The righteous king and the outpouring of the Spirit","33":"Woe to the destroyer; Zion secure","34":"Judgment on the nations and on Edom","35":"The desert blooms; the way of holiness","36":"Sennacherib invades Judah; the Rabshakeh's speech","37":"Hezekiah's prayer and the Assyrian retreat","38":"Hezekiah's illness and recovery","39":"The envoys from Babylon","40":"Comfort for Jerusalem and the greatness of God","41":"The LORD summons the nations to judgment","42":"The servant of the LORD and a new song","43":"Israel redeemed, the LORD's witness","44":"The only God and the folly of idols","45":"Cyrus, the LORD's anointed","46":"The gods of Babylon carried as burdens","47":"The fall of the daughter of Babylon","48":"Israel refined and called out of Babylon","49":"The servant, a light to the nations; Zion remembered","50":"The servant who does not turn back from his attackers","51":"Awakening and comfort for Zion","52":"Zion awakes; the feet of the messenger","53":"The suffering servant","54":"The barren woman rejoices; the covenant of peace","55":"An invitation to the waters and the effective word","56":"Foreigners and eunuchs in the house of prayer","57":"Against idolatry; peace for the humble","58":"The fast that pleases God","59":"The sin that separates; the Redeemer comes","60":"The glory of Zion among the nations","61":"Good news to the afflicted","62":"A new name for Zion","63":"The victor from Edom; mercies remembered","64":"A plea for God to rend the heavens","65":"Answer to the rebels; new heavens and new earth","66":"Final judgment and the gathering of the nations"},"about":{"title_orig":"יְשַׁעְיָהוּ","title_orig_xlit":"Yeshayahu","summary":"Isaiah gathers oracles linked to the prophet Isaiah son of Amoz, who was active in Jerusalem in the second half of the 8th century BC, when Assyria dominated the region. Chapters 1 to 39 denounce Judah's social injustice and empty worship, warn the kings against alliances with foreign powers, announce judgment on the neighboring nations and end with accounts of King Hezekiah and Sennacherib's invasion. From chapter 40 on, the setting is the exile in Babylon and the return: God comforts Jerusalem, calls Cyrus, king of Persia, His anointed and presents a “servant” whose suffering brings restoration to others. The final chapters speak of a rebuilt community, open to foreigners, and end with the vision of new heavens and a new earth. Throughout the book, God is often called “the Holy One of Israel.”","name":"The Hebrew name Yeshayahu means “the LORD saves” or “salvation of the LORD.” Through the Greek form Esaias, used in the Septuagint, and the Latin Isaias, it came into English as Isaiah.","facts":[["Position","1st of the Major Prophets; in the Hebrew Bible, among the Latter Prophets (Nevi’im)"],["Chapters","66"],["Verses","1,292"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (oracles in poetry, with narrative passages)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Judah and Jerusalem before God","from":1,"to":12,"text":"Denunciations and promises about Jerusalem, the vision of the call (ch. 6) and the oracles of the time of King Ahaz, with the signs of Immanuel and of the shoot of Jesse."},{"title":"Oracles about the nations","from":13,"to":23,"text":"Messages about Babylon, Assyria, Philistia, Moab, Damascus, Cush, Egypt, Edom, Arabia, Tyre and Jerusalem itself."},{"title":"Judgment and feast for the whole earth","from":24,"to":27,"text":"Poems of universal scope: the devastated earth, the banquet prepared on the mountain and the defeat of Leviathan."},{"title":"Woes and promises","from":28,"to":35,"text":"Criticism of the leaders of Ephraim and Judah and of seeking help from Egypt, followed by promises of a righteous king and of the desert that blooms."},{"title":"Narratives about Hezekiah","from":36,"to":39,"text":"The Assyrian siege of Jerusalem, the king's illness and healing and the visit of the envoys from Babylon; the passage closely parallels 2 Kings 18–20."},{"title":"Comfort and deliverance","from":40,"to":55,"text":"Announcement of the end of the exile, polemic against idols, the mission of Cyrus and the poems about the servant of the LORD."},{"title":"Zion restored","from":56,"to":66,"text":"Exhortations to a community that has returned to the land, criticism of worship and injustice, the future glory of Jerusalem and the promise of new heavens and a new earth."}],"extra":{"title":"The servant poems","text":"In 1892, the German scholar Bernhard Duhm singled out four passages of Isaiah 40–55 that speak of a “servant” of the LORD: 42:1–4, 49:1–6, 50:4–9 and 52:13–53:12. The exact limits vary among authors. The servant's identity has been debated since antiquity: the text itself calls him Israel in 49:3, and many Jewish interpreters understand him as the people or a faithful group within it; others think of the prophet himself or of a historical figure; Christian tradition, already in the New Testament (Acts 8:32–35), reads these poems as referring to Jesus."},"people":[{"name":"Isaiah","chapters":"1–39","ch":6,"note":"son of Amoz; prophet in Jerusalem, adviser to the kings of Judah"},{"name":"Isaiah's sons","chapters":"7–8","ch":8,"note":"Shear-jashub and Maher-shalal-hash-baz, whose names work as signs"},{"name":"Ahaz","chapters":"7","ch":7,"note":"king of Judah who, threatened by Aram and Ephraim, refuses to ask for a sign"},{"name":"Hezekiah","chapters":"36–39","ch":37,"note":"king of Judah during the Assyrian invasion of 701 BC"},{"name":"Sennacherib and the Rabshakeh","chapters":"36–37","ch":36,"note":"the king of Assyria and the high official who speaks before the walls of Jerusalem"},{"name":"Shebna and Eliakim","chapters":"22; 36–37","ch":22,"note":"administrators of the royal palace"},{"name":"Merodach-baladan","chapters":"39","ch":39,"note":"king of Babylon who sends envoys to Hezekiah"},{"name":"Cyrus","chapters":"44–45","ch":45,"note":"Persian king called the LORD's shepherd and anointed"},{"name":"The servant of the LORD","chapters":"42–53","ch":53,"note":"figure of the servant poems; his identity is debated"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Jewish and Christian tradition attribute the whole book to Isaiah son of Amoz, active from about 740 to 700 BC or a little later. On this reading, chapters 40 to 66 are prophecies about a distant future, and the mention of Cyrus (44:28; 45:1), about a century and a half before him, is understood as prediction. The Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) says that Hezekiah and his companions “wrote” Isaiah, which is usually understood as gathering and editing the oracles. Ben Sira, around 180 BC, already links to Isaiah the comfort of Zion (Sirach 48:24), and the New Testament quotes passages from both halves of the book under the prophet's name. Those who defend its unity also point to vocabulary common to both parts, such as the title “the Holy One of Israel.”","academic":"Since the end of the 18th century, many scholars have understood that chapters 40 to 66 do not come from the 8th-century prophet. The main argument is the setting: these chapters speak to people already living in exile, treat the destruction of Jerusalem as past and name Cyrus. Hence the hypothesis of an anonymous prophet, conventionally called “Second Isaiah” (Deutero-Isaiah), author of chapters 40–55, around 550–539 BC, and, from Duhm (1892) on, the attribution of chapters 56–66 to one or more authors of the period after the return (“Third Isaiah”), between about 538 and 450 BC. More recent research stresses both the differences and the links between the parts and tends to see the book as the work of a tradition that gathered, expanded and edited Isaiah's message over centuries; passages of chapters 1–39, such as 13–14 and 24–27, are also usually dated after the 8th century. Conservative scholars continue to defend Isaiah's authorship of the whole book."},"events":{"intro":"Chapters 1 to 39 are set in the reigns of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah, when Assyria was expanding toward the Mediterranean; chapters 40 to 66 have as their background the exile in Babylon, the victory of Cyrus and the return to Jerusalem. The timeline shows these two settings and the dates proposed for the writing, according to tradition and according to scholarship. The dates are approximate.","axis":[-760,-420],"band":{"label":"Exile in Babylon","from":-586,"to":-538},"timeline":[{"label":"Isaiah's ministry","from":-740,"to":-700,"kind":"pessoa","note":"from the year of Uzziah's death (6:1), c. 740 BC, until after 701 BC"},{"label":"War of Aram and Ephraim against Judah","from":-734,"to":-732,"kind":"evento","note":"Ahaz asks Assyria for help (chs. 7–8)"},{"label":"Fall of Samaria","from":-722,"to":-720,"kind":"evento","note":"end of the kingdom of Israel, c. 722/721 BC"},{"label":"Assyria takes Ashdod","from":-711,"to":-711,"kind":"evento","note":"campaign of Sargon II (ch. 20)"},{"label":"Sennacherib's campaign","from":-701,"to":-701,"kind":"evento","note":"cities of Judah taken and Jerusalem besieged (chs. 36–37)"},{"label":"Destruction of Jerusalem","from":-587,"to":-586,"kind":"evento","note":"by Nebuchadnezzar's Babylonians"},{"label":"Cyrus takes Babylon","from":-539,"to":-539,"kind":"evento","note":"soon after, the exiles can return (chs. 44–45)"},{"label":"Rebuilding of the temple","from":-520,"to":-515,"kind":"evento","note":"in the reign of the Persian Darius I"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-740,"to":-686,"kind":"escrita","note":"Isaiah, with the oracles gathered in Hezekiah's time"},{"label":"Chs. 40–55, according to many scholars","from":-550,"to":-539,"kind":"escrita","note":"end of the exile in Babylon"},{"label":"Chs. 56–66, according to many scholars","from":-538,"to":-450,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian period; dates debated"}]},"context":"The book spans three empires. In the 8th century BC, the Assyria of Tiglath-pileser III, Sargon II and Sennacherib subjugated the kingdoms of Syria and Palestine: Damascus fell in 732, Samaria around 722, and in 701 Judah was devastated and Jerusalem besieged. Sennacherib's annals say he shut Hezekiah up in Jerusalem “like a bird in a cage,” but do not claim to have taken the city, and the reliefs of his palace in Nineveh show the conquest of Lachish. In the 6th century BC, Nebuchadnezzar's Babylon destroyed Jerusalem and deported part of the population; chapters 40 to 55 speak to these exiles, mock the Babylonian gods Bel and Nebo and announce the coming of Cyrus of Persia, who took Babylon in 539 BC and allowed the deported peoples to return, a policy recalled in the so-called Cyrus Cylinder. The last chapters reflect the life of the community under Persian rule, between rebuilding and disappointments. The Great Isaiah Scroll, found at Qumran (1QIsaᵃ) and copied around 150–100 BC, contains all 66 chapters and is one of the oldest complete biblical manuscripts known.","places":["Jerusalem (Zion)","Judah and Ephraim (northern Israel)","Damascus and Aram","Assyria: Nineveh","Babylon","Egypt and Cush","Moab, Edom, Tyre and Sidon"],"themes":["God's holiness","social justice","trust in God in the face of empires","the remnant","comfort and return from exile","the servant of the LORD","the one God and the critique of idols","salvation for the nations"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:4","text":"Then He will judge between the nations and arbitrate for many peoples. They will beat their swords into plowshares and their spears into pruning hooks. Nation will no longer take up the sword against nation, nor train anymore for war."},{"ref":"6:3","text":"And they were calling out to one another: “Holy, holy, holy is the LORD of Hosts; all the earth is full of His glory.”"},{"ref":"40:31","text":"But those who wait upon the LORD will renew their strength; they will mount up with wings like eagles; they will run and not grow weary, they will walk and not faint."},{"ref":"53:5","text":"But He was pierced for our transgressions, He was crushed for our iniquities; the punishment that brought us peace was upon Him, and by His stripes we are healed."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. At two points the numbering of English Bibles differs from the Hebrew: 9:1 corresponds to 8:23 in the Hebrew text, and 64:1 to the second half of 63:19, so that the following verses of those chapters are shifted by one. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"JER":{"t":{"1":"The call of Jeremiah; the almond branch and the pot","2":"Israel's unfaithfulness since the wilderness","3":"The unfaithful wife invited to return","4":"A call to repentance; the enemy from the north","5":"No one does justice in Jerusalem","6":"The siege of Jerusalem announced","7":"The sermon at the temple gate","8":"A people that does not repent; lament","9":"Tears for Zion; boasting in knowing God","10":"Idols and the true God","11":"The broken covenant; a plot in Anathoth","12":"Jeremiah's complaint and God's answer","13":"The ruined linen belt","14":"The drought and the false prophets","15":"An irrevocable sentence; Jeremiah complains again","16":"Jeremiah forbidden to marry; the future return","17":"Sin engraved on the heart; the Sabbath","18":"At the potter's house","19":"The jar broken in the Valley of Hinnom","20":"Jeremiah in the stocks; his bitter complaint","21":"An answer to Zedekiah: the city will fall","22":"Messages to the kings of Judah","23":"Bad shepherds, the Branch and false prophets","24":"The two baskets of figs","25":"Seventy years and the cup of wrath","26":"Jeremiah tried for speaking against the temple","27":"The yoke of the king of Babylon","28":"Hananiah breaks the wooden yoke","29":"A letter to the exiles in Babylon","30":"The restoration of Israel and Judah","31":"The return of the exiles and the new covenant","32":"Jeremiah buys the field at Anathoth","33":"A promise of restoration and of the Branch of David","34":"The release of the slaves revoked","35":"The faithfulness of the Rechabites","36":"The scroll burned by Jehoiakim","37":"Jeremiah arrested as a deserter","38":"Jeremiah in the cistern; a talk with Zedekiah","39":"The fall of Jerusalem","40":"Jeremiah freed; Gedaliah governor","41":"The murder of Gedaliah","42":"Jeremiah consulted: do not go to Egypt","43":"The flight to Egypt","44":"Against the idolatry of the Jews in Egypt","45":"A word to Baruch","46":"Oracle against Egypt","47":"Oracle against the Philistines","48":"Oracle against Moab","49":"Ammon, Edom, Damascus, Kedar and Elam","50":"The fall of Babylon announced","51":"Judgment on Babylon; the scroll in the Euphrates","52":"The fall of Jerusalem; Jehoiachin released"},"about":{"title_orig":"יִרְמְיָהוּ","title_orig_xlit":"Yirmeyahu","summary":"Jeremiah gathers the messages and the story of a prophet who was active in Jerusalem during the last four decades of the kingdom of Judah, from about 627 BC until after the destruction of the city by the Babylonians, in 587/586 BC. He denounces idolatry and injustice, attacks blind trust in the temple and insists that Judah must submit to Babylon, which makes him seen as a traitor, imprisoned and threatened with death. The book also preserves prayers in which the prophet complains to God about the weight of his mission, the accounts of his imprisonments and of the fall of Jerusalem, and the forced flight of a group of Jews, with Jeremiah, to Egypt. Amid the announcements of judgment, there are promises of return from exile and of a new covenant written on the heart (chs. 30–33). The book ends with oracles against the nations and a historical appendix on the fall of Jerusalem.","name":"The Hebrew name Yirmeyahu is generally explained as “the LORD exalts” or “the LORD establishes”; the exact meaning is debated. Through the Greek form Ieremias and the Latin Ieremias (or Hieremias), it came into English as Jeremiah.","facts":[["Position","2nd of the Major Prophets; in the Hebrew Bible, among the Latter Prophets (Nevi’im)"],["Chapters","52"],["Verses","1,364"],["Original language","Hebrew; 10:11 in Aramaic"],["Genre","prophecy (oracles in poetry, prose sermons and biographical narratives)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Call and warnings to Judah","from":1,"to":25,"text":"The prophet's call, denunciations of idolatry and injustice, the temple sermon, symbolic acts, Jeremiah's personal complaints and the announcement of the seventy years of Babylonian rule."},{"title":"Conflicts with priests, prophets and kings","from":26,"to":29,"text":"Jeremiah is put on trial, confronts the prophet Hananiah over the yoke of Babylon and writes to the exiles."},{"title":"Promises of restoration","from":30,"to":33,"text":"Poems of comfort, the new covenant and the purchase of a field at Anathoth as a sign that life will return to the land."},{"title":"Jerusalem's last years","from":34,"to":45,"text":"The scroll burned by the king, the prophet's imprisonments, the fall of the city, the murder of the governor Gedaliah and the flight to Egypt."},{"title":"Oracles against the nations","from":46,"to":51,"text":"Egypt, Philistia, Moab, Ammon, Edom, Damascus, Kedar, Elam and, last and at greatest length, Babylon."},{"title":"Historical appendix","from":52,"to":52,"text":"The fall of Jerusalem and the release of King Jehoiachin in Babylon, in a text close to 2 Kings 24–25."}],"extra":{"title":"A verse in Aramaic","text":"In the middle of a Hebrew poem against idols, Jeremiah 10:11 is in Aramaic, the international language of the time, used in the diplomacy and trade of the Near East. It is the only such verse in the book; outside it, the Hebrew Bible has Aramaic passages only in Ezra (4:8–6:18; 7:12–26), in Daniel (from 2:4 to 7:28) and two words in Genesis 31:47. The sentence seems designed to be spoken to the nations in their own language, and the text itself plays on two Aramaic terms for “earth.” Some scholars consider it an early addition to the poem and others see it as an original part of it."},"people":[{"name":"Jeremiah","chapters":"1–45","ch":1,"note":"from a priestly family of Anathoth; prophet of Judah until after the fall of Jerusalem"},{"name":"Baruch","chapters":"32; 36; 43; 45","ch":36,"note":"son of Neriah, the scribe who writes down the words dictated by Jeremiah"},{"name":"Josiah","chapters":"1; 3; 22","ch":1,"note":"king of Judah at the beginning of the prophet's ministry"},{"name":"Jehoiakim","chapters":"22; 26; 36","ch":36,"note":"king who burns the scroll of the prophecies"},{"name":"Zedekiah","chapters":"21; 27; 32–39","ch":38,"note":"last king of Judah; consults Jeremiah in secret"},{"name":"Hananiah","chapters":"28","ch":28,"note":"prophet who announces a quick end to Babylonian rule"},{"name":"Ebed-melech","chapters":"38–39","ch":38,"note":"Cushite official who pulls Jeremiah out of the cistern"},{"name":"Nebuchadnezzar","chapters":"21–52","ch":39,"note":"king of Babylon who takes Jerusalem"},{"name":"Gedaliah","chapters":"39–41","ch":40,"note":"governor of Judah appointed by the Babylonians, murdered by Ishmael"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition attributes the book to Jeremiah himself, with the help of Baruch. Chapter 36 tells that the prophet dictated his messages to the scribe in the fourth year of Jehoiakim (c. 605 BC) and that, after the king burned the scroll, he dictated another, expanded one. The Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) says Jeremiah wrote his book, as well as Kings and Lamentations. On this reading, the book was composed between c. 627 and c. 580 BC, with chapter 52 added after 562 BC, the date of Jehoiachin's release.","academic":"Scholars agree that the book contains much material linked to the prophet, especially in the poems, but describe its formation as long. They distinguish the poetic oracles, the accounts about Jeremiah, often attributed to Baruch's circle, and prose sermons in a style similar to Deuteronomy and Kings, which many attribute to editors of the exilic period. An important fact is that the book circulated in two forms: the Greek text of the Septuagint is about one-seventh shorter and places the oracles against the nations right after 25:13, in a different order; fragments found at Qumran confirm that both forms existed in Hebrew. Many understand that the Greek form reflects an older edition and that the traditional Hebrew text, followed by English Bibles, received additions down to the Persian period."},"events":{"intro":"Jeremiah dates many of his messages by the years of the kings of Judah, which makes it possible to follow his ministry through the transition from Assyrian to Babylonian rule, until after the destruction of Jerusalem. The dates of the timeline are approximate; when the Hebrew year does not coincide with ours, they appear as ranges.","axis":[-650,-400],"band":{"label":"Babylonian Empire","from":-626,"to":-539},"timeline":[{"label":"Jeremiah's ministry","from":-627,"to":-582,"kind":"pessoa","note":"from Josiah's 13th year (1:2) to the stay in Egypt; the end of his life is not known"},{"label":"Josiah's reform","from":-622,"to":-622,"kind":"evento","note":"the Book of the Law found in the temple (2 Kings 22–23)"},{"label":"Fall of Nineveh","from":-612,"to":-612,"kind":"evento","note":"Babylonians and Medes defeat Assyria"},{"label":"Death of Josiah at Megiddo","from":-609,"to":-609,"kind":"evento","note":"Judah comes to depend on Egypt"},{"label":"Battle of Carchemish","from":-605,"to":-605,"kind":"evento","note":"Nebuchadnezzar defeats Pharaoh Neco (46:2)"},{"label":"The scroll dictated to Baruch","from":-605,"to":-604,"kind":"escrita","note":"read in the temple and burned by the king (ch. 36)"},{"label":"First deportation","from":-597,"to":-597,"kind":"evento","note":"King Jehoiachin and part of the elite taken to Babylon"},{"label":"Siege and destruction of Jerusalem","from":-588,"to":-586,"kind":"evento","note":"the city falls in 587 or 586 BC (ch. 39; 52)"},{"label":"Murder of Gedaliah and flight to Egypt","from":-586,"to":-582,"kind":"evento","note":"the exact year is debated (chs. 41–43)"},{"label":"Jehoiachin released in Babylon","from":-562,"to":-562,"kind":"evento","note":"in the first year of Evil-merodach (52:31)"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-560,"to":-400,"kind":"escrita","note":"editions in the exile and the Persian period"}]},"context":"Jeremiah lived through the passage from one empire to another. In Josiah's years, Assyria was collapsing; Nineveh fell in 612 BC, and Egypt tried to fill the gap, until it was defeated by the Babylonians at Carchemish, in 605 BC. From then on, Judah was caught between submission to Babylon and the hope of Egyptian help, and the kings wavered between the two sides. The Babylonian Chronicle records the capture of Jerusalem in 597 BC, and administrative tablets from Babylon mention food rations for Jehoiachin, “king of Judah.” From the Lachish letters, written on ostraca shortly before the final fall, comes a picture of the military tension of those months. After the destruction of 587/586 BC, part of the population remained in the land under the government of Gedaliah, at Mizpah, and another part went to Egypt, where the book places the prophet's last words. The horizon of Persia appears indirectly, in the announcement that Babylonian rule would come to an end.","places":["Jerusalem and the temple","Anathoth, the prophet's hometown","Mizpah","Egypt: Tahpanhes, Migdol, Memphis","Babylon","Carchemish, by the Euphrates"],"themes":["covenant and unfaithfulness","false security in the temple","true and false prophets","the prophet's suffering","submission to Babylon","the new covenant","hope beyond the exile"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:5","text":"“Before I formed you in the womb I knew you, and before you were born I set you apart and appointed you as a prophet to the nations.”"},{"ref":"10:11","text":"Thus you are to tell them: “These gods, who have made neither the heavens nor the earth, will perish from this earth and from under these heavens.”"},{"ref":"20:9","text":"If I say, “I will not mention Him or speak any more in His name,” His message becomes a fire burning in my heart, shut up in my bones, and I become weary of holding it in, and I cannot prevail."},{"ref":"29:7","text":"Seek the prosperity of the city to which I have sent you as exiles. Pray to the LORD on its behalf, for if it prospers, you too will prosper.”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source; the fall of Jerusalem is dated to 587 or 586 BC, depending on how the years of the kings are counted. English Bibles follow the traditional Hebrew text, longer than that of the Septuagint. In 9:1 the English numbering corresponds to 8:23 in the Hebrew text, and the following verses of chapter 9 are shifted by one. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"LAM":{"t":{"1":"Jerusalem, the widowed city without comfort","2":"The Lord acted like an enemy","3":"From affliction to hope in His mercy","4":"Hunger and ruin in the besieged city","5":"The people's prayer for restoration"},"about":{"title_orig":"אֵיכָה","title_orig_xlit":"Eikhah","summary":"Lamentations gathers five poems of mourning for the destruction of Jerusalem and the temple by the Babylonians, in 587 or 586 BC. The poems describe the siege, the famine, the death of children, humiliation before the enemies and the exile, and give a voice to the city itself, portrayed as a widow who weeps with no one to comfort her. The catastrophe is understood as the consequence of the sins of the people and their leaders, but the text does not hide the pain or the questions addressed to God. At the center of the book (3:21–33), the complaint opens into hope in the loving devotion and faithfulness of the LORD, and the last poem ends with a prayer for restoration.","name":"In Hebrew, the book is called by its first word, Eikhah, “How!”, the cry of lament that opens chapters 1, 2 and 4. Rabbinic tradition also calls it Qinot, “laments,” a meaning that passed into Greek (Threnoi) and Latin (Lamentationes), from which the English name comes.","facts":[["Position","after Jeremiah, among the prophetic books; in the Hebrew Bible, one of the five Scrolls (Megillot)"],["Chapters","5"],["Verses","154"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","poetry of lament"]],"structure":[{"title":"The widowed city","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The poet describes Jerusalem abandoned, and the city itself speaks to ask the LORD to see its pain."},{"title":"The Lord's anger","from":2,"to":2,"text":"God is described acting like an enemy: He tears down walls, palaces and the sanctuary, while children faint from hunger in the streets."},{"title":"The man who has seen affliction","from":3,"to":3,"text":"A sufferer speaks in the first person; from complaint he passes to hope in the LORD's mercies and to a call to repentance."},{"title":"Before and after the siege","from":4,"to":4,"text":"The contrast between past glory and present misery, the guilt of prophets and priests, the capture of the king and the announcement of punishment on Edom."},{"title":"The community's prayer","from":5,"to":5,"text":"The people, in the first person plural, describe their humiliation and ask the LORD to restore them and renew their days."}],"extra":{"title":"Poems in alphabetical order","text":"Chapters 1, 2 and 4 are acrostics: each has 22 verses, and each verse begins with a letter of the Hebrew alphabet, in order. Chapter 3 is a triple acrostic, with 66 verses: each letter opens three verses in a row. In chapters 2 to 4, the letters pe and ayin appear in reverse order compared with the usual alphabet. Chapter 5 is not an acrostic, but it also has 22 verses. The first four poems often use the rhythm of lament (qinah), in which the second half of the line is shorter than the first, like a voice that breaks off."},"people":[{"name":"Jerusalem, the Daughter of Zion","chapters":"1–2; 4","ch":1,"note":"the city personified as a widow and a grieving mother; speaks in the second half of chapter 1"},{"name":"The poet","chapters":"1–2; 4","ch":2,"note":"the narrator who describes the ruin and weeps with the city (2:11)"},{"name":"The man who has seen affliction","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"the sufferer of chapter 3 (in Hebrew, gever), who moves from complaint to hope"},{"name":"Prophets and priests","chapters":"2; 4","ch":4,"note":"accused of not exposing the people's sin and of shedding the blood of the righteous (2:14; 4:13)"},{"name":"The LORD's anointed","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"the king of Judah captured by the enemies (4:20), generally identified with Zedekiah"},{"name":"The Daughter of Edom","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"the neighboring people who rejoiced at Jerusalem's fall and receive an announcement of punishment (4:21–22)"},{"name":"The surviving people","chapters":"5","ch":5,"note":"the “we” of the last poem, who tell of life under foreign rule and plead for restoration"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The Hebrew text does not give the author's name. Jewish and Christian traditions attributed the book to Jeremiah, the prophet who lived through the fall of Jerusalem: the Septuagint opens it with a sentence that presents Jeremiah weeping over the city, and the Talmud includes it among the prophet's writings. On this reading, the poems were composed soon after the destruction, around 586 BC. The link also rests on 2 Chronicles 35:25, which mentions laments of Jeremiah, there for the death of King Josiah.","academic":"Many scholars doubt Jeremiah's authorship, because of differences of style and point of view: the hope for help from another nation (4:17) and the esteem for the king (4:20), for example, contrast with the prophet's preaching. The poems are usually dated to the period of the exile, between the destruction of 587/586 BC and the rebuilding of the temple, in 520–515 BC. The vividness of the descriptions suggests witnesses close to the events, and some attribute the five poems to more than one author."},"events":{"intro":"The poems respond to events also narrated in 2 Kings 24–25 and Jeremiah 39 and 52; the first capture of Jerusalem, in 597 BC, is also recorded in a Babylonian chronicle. The fall of the city is dated to 587 or 586 BC, depending on how the regnal years are counted, and the timeline uses ranges that cover both possibilities.","axis":[-600,-510],"band":{"label":"Exile in Babylon","from":-586,"to":-538},"timeline":[{"label":"First capture of Jerusalem","from":-598,"to":-597,"kind":"evento","note":"Nebuchadnezzar takes King Jehoiachin and part of Judah's elite to Babylon"},{"label":"Zedekiah","from":-597,"to":-586,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign; the last king of Judah, captured while fleeing the city (2 Kings 25:4–7)"},{"label":"Siege of Jerusalem","from":-588,"to":-586,"kind":"evento","note":"about a year and a half of siege and famine (2 Kings 25:1–3)"},{"label":"Fall of Jerusalem","from":-587,"to":-586,"kind":"evento","note":"destruction of the city and the temple by the Babylonians"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-587,"to":-580,"kind":"escrita","note":"Jeremiah, in the years following the destruction"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-587,"to":-520,"kind":"escrita","note":"during the exile, before the rebuilding of the temple; perhaps by more than one author"},{"label":"End of the exile","from":-539,"to":-538,"kind":"evento","note":"Cyrus, king of Persia, takes Babylon and allows the Jews to return"},{"label":"Rebuilding of the temple","from":-520,"to":-515,"kind":"evento","note":"the second temple is completed in Jerusalem"}]},"context":"At the end of the 7th century BC, Judah passed from Egyptian to Babylonian rule. After a revolt by King Jehoiakim, Nebuchadnezzar II took Jerusalem in 597 BC and deported his son and successor, Jehoiachin; the next king, Zedekiah, rebelled again, trusting in Egypt's support. Then came the siege, the famine, the breach in the walls and, about a month later, the burning of the temple, the palace and the houses of the city (2 Kings 25:8–10), followed by a new deportation. Edom, Judah's neighbor, is remembered in other biblical texts for having rejoiced at the fall (Obadiah 1:11–14; Psalm 137:7). Laments for the destruction of cities were an ancient genre in Mesopotamia, such as the Lament for the Destruction of Ur, from the beginning of the second millennium BC. In Judaism, Lamentations is read on the fast of the 9th of Av, which recalls the destruction of the first and second temples.","places":["Jerusalem (Zion)","the temple","the cities of Judah","Edom and the land of Uz (4:21)","Egypt and Assyria (5:6)"],"themes":["mourning and lament","judgment and guilt","the wrath of God","the LORD's faithfulness and mercy","hope","prayer"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:1","text":"How lonely lies the city, once so full of people! She who was great among the nations has become a widow. The princess of the provinces has become a slave."},{"ref":"3:22–23","text":"Because of the loving devotion of the LORD we are not consumed, for His mercies never fail. They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness!"},{"ref":"5:19","text":"You, O LORD, reign forever; Your throne endures from generation to generation."}],"note":"Dates are approximate; for the fall of Jerusalem, historians are divided between 587 and 586 BC. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"EZK":{"t":{"1":"The vision of glory by the river Chebar","2":"Ezekiel sent to the rebellious house","3":"The scroll eaten; the watchman of Israel","4":"The brick and the days of the siege","5":"The hair cut off, a sign of judgment","6":"Against the mountains of Israel","7":"The end has come","8":"Abominations in the temple","9":"The executioners and the mark on the forehead","10":"The glory leaves the temple","11":"Judgment on the leaders; a new spirit","12":"The exile's baggage","13":"Against false prophets and prophetesses","14":"Idols in the heart; Noah, Daniel and Job","15":"The useless wood of the vine","16":"Jerusalem, the unfaithful wife","17":"The two eagles and the cedar","18":"Each one answers for his own sin","19":"A lament for the princes of Israel","20":"The history of Israel's rebellion","21":"The sharpened sword of the LORD","22":"The crimes of the bloody city","23":"Oholah and Oholibah","24":"The boiling pot; the death of the prophet's wife","25":"Against Ammon, Moab, Edom and the Philistines","26":"The destruction of Tyre","27":"A lament over Tyre, the ship","28":"The prince of Tyre; Sidon; a promise to Israel","29":"Against Pharaoh and Egypt","30":"The day of Egypt","31":"Pharaoh compared to a great cedar","32":"A lament for Pharaoh and the descent to the Pit","33":"The watchman and the news of the fall","34":"The shepherds of Israel and the good shepherd","35":"Against Mount Seir","36":"Renewal of the mountains and a new heart","37":"The dry bones; the two sticks joined","38":"Gog, of the land of Magog","39":"The defeat of Gog","40":"Vision of the new temple: gates and courts","41":"The sanctuary and its measurements","42":"The priests' chambers and the wall","43":"The glory returns to the temple; the altar","44":"The closed gate; Levites and priests","45":"The holy portion and the prince's duties","46":"Rules of worship for the prince and the people","47":"The river flowing from the temple; the borders","48":"The division of the land and the city"},"about":{"title_orig":"יְחֶזְקֵאל","title_orig_xlit":"Yechezqel","summary":"Ezekiel is the book of a priest taken to Babylon in the deportation of 597 BC, who received his prophetic call among the exiles, by the Chebar River. Until the fall of Jerusalem, in 587/586 BC, his message is mainly one of judgment: in visions, symbolic acts and allegories, he shows that the city and the temple will be destroyed because of idolatry and violence, and that the glory of God leaves the sanctuary. Then come oracles against neighboring nations, especially Tyre and Egypt. After the news of the fall arrives, the tone changes to hope: God promises to be the shepherd of the people Himself, to give a new heart, to bring the dry bones to life and to gather Israel and Judah. The book ends with a long vision of a renewed temple, of a river that flows from it and gives life, and of a new division of the land.","name":"The Hebrew name Yechezqel means “God strengthens” or “God makes strong.” Through the Greek form Iezekiel, of the Septuagint, and the Latin Ezechiel, it came into English as Ezekiel.","facts":[["Position","3rd of the Major Prophets; in the Hebrew Bible, among the Latter Prophets (Nevi’im)"],["Chapters","48"],["Verses","1,273"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (visions, symbolic acts, allegories and oracles, largely in prose)"]],"structure":[{"title":"The prophet's call","from":1,"to":3,"text":"The vision of God's glory above a chariot of living creatures and wheels, the scroll the prophet eats and the mission of watchman."},{"title":"Judgment on Jerusalem","from":4,"to":24,"text":"Acts that enact the siege, the vision of the abominations in the temple and of the glory that departs, allegories of Israel's history and the announcement of the start of the siege."},{"title":"Oracles against the nations","from":25,"to":32,"text":"Ammon, Moab, Edom and the Philistines, and long poems about Tyre and Egypt."},{"title":"Israel's restoration","from":33,"to":39,"text":"After the news of the fall: the watchman, the good shepherd, the new heart, the valley of dry bones and the defeat of Gog."},{"title":"The new temple and the new land","from":40,"to":48,"text":"A detailed vision of the temple, the return of the glory, rules for worship, the river that flows from the sanctuary and the division of the land among the tribes."}],"extra":{"title":"The glory that leaves and returns","text":"A thread links the beginning of the book to its end. In chapters 8 to 11, the glory of the LORD gradually leaves the temple in Jerusalem and stops above the mountain east of the city, a sign that the sanctuary is handed over to destruction. In chapter 43, in the vision of the renewed temple, it returns by the same eastern way and fills the house. The last verse gives the city the name “THE LORD IS THERE” (48:35)."},"people":[{"name":"Ezekiel","chapters":"1–48","ch":1,"note":"priest, son of Buzi, deported to Babylon; called “son of man”"},{"name":"The elders of Judah and Israel","chapters":"8; 14; 20","ch":14,"note":"leaders of the exiles who come to consult the prophet"},{"name":"Pelatiah","chapters":"11","ch":11,"note":"one of the leaders of Jerusalem, who dies during the vision"},{"name":"Oholah and Oholibah","chapters":"23","ch":23,"note":"two sisters who represent Samaria and Jerusalem"},{"name":"Ezekiel's wife","chapters":"24","ch":24,"note":"her death, without public mourning, becomes a sign for the people"},{"name":"The ruler of Tyre","chapters":"28","ch":28,"note":"a king who thinks himself a god"},{"name":"Pharaoh","chapters":"29–32","ch":29,"note":"the king of Egypt, compared to a great monster of the waters and to a cedar"},{"name":"Gog","chapters":"38–39","ch":38,"note":"chief of a coalition coming from the north, defeated on the mountains of Israel"},{"name":"The prince","chapters":"44–48","ch":45,"note":"ruler of the restored community in the final vision"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book speaks almost always in the first person and gives dates counted from the deportation of King Jehoiachin, which tradition understands as a sign that it was written by Ezekiel himself, between c. 593 and c. 571 BC, in Babylon. The Talmud (Bava Batra 15a) says that the men of the Great Assembly “wrote” Ezekiel, which is read as the work of copying and editing. The rabbis also discussed the difference between the rules of chapters 40 to 48 and those of the Torah; according to the Talmud (Shabbat 13b), a sage named Hananiah ben Hezekiah managed to reconcile them, and the book remained in use.","academic":"Most scholars accept that the book preserves a broad core linked to Ezekiel, because of the unified style, the use of dates and the setting consistent with the exile. At the beginning of the 20th century, some authors went so far as to attribute almost everything to later writers, a position little followed today. It is debated how much comes from the prophet himself and how much from disciples who gathered and expanded his texts, especially in chapters 38–39 and 40–48. The oldest Greek text, Papyrus 967 (c. AD 200), lacks most of 36:23–38 and places chapter 37 after chapters 38–39, a sign that the book circulated in more than one form. The final form is usually dated to the end of the exile and the beginning of the Persian period, around 570–500 BC."},"events":{"intro":"Ezekiel is the prophet who dates his messages most: about thirteen dates, almost always counted from the deportation of King Jehoiachin, in 597 BC. They run from the fifth year (1:2), around 593 BC, to the twenty-seventh (29:17), around 571 BC. The conversion to our calendar can vary by a year. The “thirtieth year” of 1:1 is understood by many as the prophet's age, but the meaning is debated.","axis":[-610,-490],"band":{"label":"Exile (counted from 597 BC)","from":-597,"to":-538},"timeline":[{"label":"Ezekiel's ministry","from":-593,"to":-571,"kind":"pessoa","note":"from the first vision (1:2) to the latest dated oracle (29:17)"},{"label":"Deportation of Jehoiachin","from":-597,"to":-597,"kind":"evento","note":"Ezekiel goes to Babylon with the king and part of the elite"},{"label":"Start of the siege of Jerusalem","from":-588,"to":-587,"kind":"evento","note":"a date recorded by the prophet (24:1–2)"},{"label":"Fall of Jerusalem","from":-587,"to":-586,"kind":"evento","note":"the temple is destroyed by the Babylonians"},{"label":"The news reaches the exiles","from":-586,"to":-585,"kind":"evento","note":"a fugitive announces: “The city has been taken!” (33:21)"},{"label":"Nebuchadnezzar's siege of Tyre","from":-586,"to":-573,"kind":"evento","note":"about thirteen years, according to Flavius Josephus; see 29:18"},{"label":"Vision of the new temple","from":-573,"to":-573,"kind":"evento","note":"in the 25th year of the exile (40:1)"},{"label":"Cyrus takes Babylon","from":-539,"to":-539,"kind":"evento","note":"end of Babylonian rule"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-593,"to":-571,"kind":"escrita","note":"Ezekiel himself, in Babylon"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-570,"to":-500,"kind":"escrita","note":"end of the exile and beginning of the Persian period"}]},"context":"Ezekiel lived among the deportees from Judah settled in southern Mesopotamia, at Tel-abib, by the Chebar River (or canal), probably near Nippur. The exiles could live in houses, gather and keep their elders, and tablets of the time show Jews living in villages of the region. Meanwhile, in Jerusalem, King Zedekiah wavered between loyalty to Babylon and the hope of help from Egypt, which led to the siege and destruction of the city. The oracles against Tyre and Egypt reflect Nebuchadnezzar's campaigns in the west. The vision of the temple, the rules for priests and Levites and the interest in purity show the prophet's priestly background and influenced the hopes of the community that, already under Persian rule, returned to Jerusalem and rebuilt the temple.","places":["the Chebar River and Tel-abib, in Babylonia","Jerusalem and the temple","the mountains of Israel","Tyre and Sidon","Egypt","Ammon, Moab, Edom (Mount Seir) and Philistia"],"themes":["the glory and holiness of God","judgment and personal responsibility","the prophet as watchman","God as shepherd of the people","a new heart and a new spirit","temple and purity","restoration after the exile"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"18:20","text":"The soul who sins is the one who will die. A son will not bear the iniquity of his father, and a father will not bear the iniquity of his son. The righteousness of the righteous man will fall upon him, and the wickedness of the wicked man will fall upon him."},{"ref":"36:26","text":"I will give you a new heart and put a new spirit within you; I will remove your heart of stone and give you a heart of flesh."},{"ref":"37:4–5","text":"And He said to me, “Prophesy concerning these bones and tell them, ‘Dry bones, hear the word of the LORD! This is what the Lord GOD says to these bones: I will cause breath to enter you, and you will come to life."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source; the fall of Jerusalem is dated to 587 or 586 BC. In the numbering of English Bibles, 20:45–49 corresponds to 21:1–5 in the Hebrew text, and chapter 21 runs from 21:6 to 21:37 in the Hebrew. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"DAN":{"t":{"1":"Daniel and his friends at the court of Babylon","2":"Nebuchadnezzar's dream of the statue","3":"The golden statue and the fiery furnace","4":"The dream of the tree and the king humbled","5":"Belshazzar's feast and the writing on the wall","6":"Daniel in the lions' den","7":"The four beasts and the son of man","8":"The ram and the goat","9":"Daniel's prayer and the seventy weeks","10":"The man dressed in linen by the Tigris","11":"The kings of the north and the south","12":"The time of the end and the resurrection"},"about":{"title_orig":"דָּנִיֵּאל","title_orig_xlit":"Daniyyel","summary":"Daniel brings together two kinds of text. Chapters 1 to 6 tell stories of Daniel and three Jewish companions taken to Babylon, who serve at the court of foreign kings without abandoning faithfulness to their God: the refusal of the king's food, the fiery furnace, the lions' den. Chapters 7 to 12 contain visions in which successive empires appear as beasts and kings at war, until God establishes a kingdom that will have no end. The book affirms that the God of Israel rules over human kingdoms and ends with one of the most explicit references in the Hebrew Bible to the resurrection of the dead (12:2).","name":"The book bears the name of its main character. Daniel, in Hebrew Daniyyel, is usually understood as “God is my judge” or “God has judged.” At the Babylonian court he receives the name Belteshazzar (1:7).","facts":[["Position","4th of the Major Prophets, after Ezekiel; in the Hebrew Bible, among the Writings (Ketuvim)"],["Chapters","12"],["Verses","357"],["Original language","Hebrew and Aramaic (2:4b–7:28)"],["Genre","court narratives and apocalyptic literature"]],"structure":[{"title":"Stories at the foreign court","from":1,"to":6,"text":"Daniel and his companions serve Nebuchadnezzar, Belshazzar and Darius and are put to the test for keeping their faith: dreams interpreted, the furnace and the lions' den."},{"title":"Visions of the kingdoms and of the end","from":7,"to":12,"text":"The four beasts, the ram and the goat, the seventy weeks and the long revelation about the kings of the north and the south, which ends with the promise of resurrection."}],"extra":{"title":"Two languages and two kinds of text","text":"From 2:4b to 7:28 the book is written in Aramaic, the international language of the empire; the rest is in Hebrew. The division of languages does not coincide with that of the subjects: chapter 1 is narrative in Hebrew and chapter 7, already a vision, is still in Aramaic, which links the two halves. The Septuagint also has passages in Greek that are not in the Hebrew-Aramaic text (the Prayer of Azariah and the Song of the Three Young Men, the story of Susanna and that of Bel and the Dragon); they are part of the book in Catholic and Orthodox Bibles and do not appear in this translation."},"people":[{"name":"Daniel (Belteshazzar)","chapters":"1–12","ch":1,"note":"a young man from Judah taken to Babylon; interpreter of dreams and recipient of the visions"},{"name":"Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah","chapters":"1–3","ch":3,"note":"Daniel's companions, called Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego; thrown into the furnace"},{"name":"Nebuchadnezzar","chapters":"1–4","ch":4,"note":"king of Babylon; dreams of the statue and of the great tree"},{"name":"Belshazzar","chapters":"5; 7–8","ch":5,"note":"ruler of Babylon on the night of the writing on the wall"},{"name":"Darius the Mede","chapters":"5:31–6; 9; 11","ch":6,"note":"king who throws Daniel into the lions' den"},{"name":"Gabriel and Michael","chapters":"8–12","ch":9,"note":"heavenly beings who explain the visions and defend the people"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Ancient Jewish and Christian traditions attribute the book to Daniel himself, who speaks in the first person in the visions (7–12). On this reading, it was written in the 6th century BC, at the end of the exile, around 540–530 BC. Defenders of this date point out that the book knows the Babylonian and Persian court well, that fragments of Daniel appear among the Dead Sea Scrolls a short time after the date proposed by critical scholarship and that the visions present themselves as prediction.","academic":"Most critical scholars date the final form of the book to c. 167–164 BC, during the persecution of Antiochus IV Epiphanes, who desecrated the temple in Jerusalem. It is argued that the vision of chapter 11 describes in great detail the wars between the Seleucids and the Ptolemies up to that time and becomes vague after it, and that there are inaccuracies about the Babylonian and Persian period (such as “Darius the Mede”). The stories of chapters 1 to 6 are seen as older, perhaps from the Persian or early Hellenistic periods. The question of the date remains under debate and usually also depends on assumptions about the possibility of predictive prophecy."},"events":{"intro":"The stories are set between the first deportation from Judah, at the end of the 7th century BC, and the first years of Persian rule. The visions point to the succession of empires that culminates in the Hellenistic period; in the critical reading, chapter 11 reaches the crisis under Antiochus IV, in the 2nd century BC. The dates are approximate, and the timeline shows the two proposals for the writing.","axis":[-610,-160],"band":{"label":"Exile in Babylon","from":-586,"to":-538},"timeline":[{"label":"Daniel taken to Babylon","from":-605,"to":-605,"kind":"evento","note":"“in the third year” of Jehoiakim (1:1), by the book's reckoning"},{"label":"Nebuchadnezzar","from":-605,"to":-562,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign"},{"label":"Daniel","from":-605,"to":-536,"kind":"pessoa","note":"service at court, until the third year of Cyrus (10:1); approximate dates"},{"label":"Fall of Babylon","from":-539,"to":-539,"kind":"evento","note":"Cyrus, king of Persia, takes the city; end of the Babylonian kingdom (5:30–31)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-540,"to":-530,"kind":"escrita","note":"Daniel, at the end of the exile"},{"label":"Alexander and the Hellenistic kingdoms","from":-333,"to":-301,"kind":"evento","note":"the “goat” and its four horns (8:21–22), according to the book's own explanation"},{"label":"Antiochus IV Epiphanes","from":-175,"to":-164,"kind":"pessoa","note":"Seleucid king; desecrates the temple in 167 BC"},{"label":"Final form, according to scholarship","from":-167,"to":-164,"kind":"escrita","note":"during the persecution of Antiochus IV, before the rededication of the temple"}]},"context":"The narratives take place in Nebuchadnezzar's Babylon, the empire that destroyed Jerusalem in 587/586 BC, and in the first years of Persian rule, after Cyrus took the city in 539 BC. Babylonian sources show that the last king, Nabonidus, spent years away from the capital and left the government to his son Belshazzar, who appears in chapter 5. The visions use the symbolic language typical of Jewish apocalyptic literature (beasts, horns, numbers and angels) to speak of empires and of their end, and the book had great influence on this genre and, later, on the New Testament.","places":["Jerusalem","Babylon and the province of Babylon","the plain of Dura","Susa, by the Ulai Canal","the Tigris River"],"themes":["God's sovereignty over the kingdoms","faithfulness in exile","dreams and visions","wisdom","the everlasting kingdom","resurrection"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:44","text":"In the days of those kings, the God of heaven will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed, nor will it be left to another people. It will shatter all these kingdoms and bring them to an end, but will itself stand forever."},{"ref":"7:13–14","text":"In my vision in the night I continued to watch, and I saw One like the Son of Man coming with the clouds of heaven. He approached the Ancient of Days and was led into His presence. And He was given dominion, glory, and kingship, that the people of every nation and language should serve Him. His dominion is an everlasting dominion that will not pass away, and His kingdom is one that will never be destroyed."},{"ref":"12:2–3","text":"And many who sleep in the dust of the earth will awake, some to everlasting life, but others to shame and everlasting contempt. Then the wise will shine like the brightness of the heavens, and those who lead many to righteousness will shine like the stars forever and ever."}],"note":"Dates vary according to the source; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB). This edition follows the numbering of English Bibles: 4:1–3 corresponds to 3:31–33 in the Aramaic text, and 5:31 corresponds to 6:1, so that chapter 6 has one verse less there."}},"HOS":{"t":{"1":"Hosea's marriage and his children","2":"The unfaithful wife and the new betrothal","3":"Hosea buys his wife back","4":"The charge against the people and the priests","5":"Judgment on Ephraim and Judah","6":"A repentance that does not last","7":"Ephraim, a senseless dove","8":"Sow the wind and reap the whirlwind","9":"Exile announced to Ephraim","10":"Israel, a vine that multiplied altars","11":"God's love for the rebellious son","12":"Jacob and the unfaithfulness of Ephraim","13":"The fall of Ephraim and Samaria","14":"A call to return and a promise of healing"},"about":{"title_orig":"הוֹשֵׁעַ","title_orig_xlit":"Hoshea","summary":"Hosea prophesied in the Northern Kingdom, Israel, in the last decades before its fall to Assyria. Chapters 1 to 3 use the prophet's own marriage to an unfaithful woman as an image of the relationship between God and Israel, which turns to Baal and other gods. Chapters 4 to 14 gather oracles against idolatry, the corruption of priests and kings and the alliances with Egypt and Assyria. Despite the accusations, the book insists on God's persistent love, which calls the people back and promises to heal them.","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. Hoshea comes from the Hebrew root meaning “to save” and can be understood as “salvation”; it is the same name Joshua had before Moses changed it (Numbers 13:16) and that of the last king of Israel.","facts":[["Position","1st of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","14"],["Verses","197"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (oracles in poetry, with narrative in chapters 1 and 3)"]],"structure":[{"title":"The prophet's marriage","from":1,"to":3,"text":"Hosea marries Gomer, gives his children names that announce judgment and later buys back his wife, in an image of Israel's unfaithfulness and God's faithfulness."},{"title":"The charge against Israel","from":4,"to":10,"text":"Lack of knowledge of God, idolatry, kings made without consulting God and alliances with foreign powers."},{"title":"The love that does not give up","from":11,"to":14,"text":"God recalls His care for Israel since Egypt, announces the fall of Samaria and ends with a call to repentance and a promise of healing."}],"people":[{"name":"Hosea","chapters":"1–14","ch":1,"note":"prophet, son of Beeri"},{"name":"Gomer","chapters":"1; 3","ch":1,"note":"the prophet's wife, daughter of Diblaim"},{"name":"Jezreel, Lo-ruhamah and Lo-ammi","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"the children, whose symbolic names (“No Compassion,” “Not My People”) are reversed in 2:23"},{"name":"Ephraim","chapters":"4–14","ch":5,"note":"name of the leading tribe, used for the Northern Kingdom"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition attributes the book to the prophet Hosea son of Beeri, who was active in the days of Jeroboam II, king of Israel, and of the kings of Judah Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah (1:1), roughly between 755 and 725 BC.","academic":"Scholars generally consider that most of the oracles come from Hosea and reflect the crisis of the Northern Kingdom before 722 BC. After the fall of Samaria, the material would have been taken to Judah and edited there: the mentions of Judah and some promises of restoration are, for many, additions from this phase, although others attribute them to the prophet himself. The Hebrew of the book is difficult and the text has several obscure passages."},"events":{"intro":"The title places the prophet in the reigns of Jeroboam II in Israel and of Uzziah to Hezekiah in Judah. The regnal years of this period are reconstructed somewhat differently by historians; the dates below are approximate.","axis":[-800,-690],"timeline":[{"label":"Jeroboam II","from":-786,"to":-746,"kind":"pessoa","note":"king of Israel; a period of prosperity (approximate dates)"},{"label":"Hosea, the prophet","from":-755,"to":-725,"kind":"pessoa","note":"ministry in the Northern Kingdom, approximate"},{"label":"Tiglath-pileser III","from":-745,"to":-727,"kind":"pessoa","note":"king of Assyria; advance on Aram and Israel"},{"label":"Syro-Ephraimite War","from":-735,"to":-732,"kind":"evento","note":"Israel and Damascus against Judah; Judah asks Assyria for help"},{"label":"Fall of Samaria","from":-722,"to":-720,"kind":"evento","note":"end of the Northern Kingdom; deportation to Assyria"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-755,"to":-720,"kind":"escrita","note":"Hosea, over the course of his ministry"},{"label":"Edition in Judah, according to scholarship","from":-720,"to":-700,"kind":"escrita","note":"first collection edited after the fall of the North; later touches possible"}]},"context":"Hosea began his ministry in a period of prosperity under Jeroboam II, but most of his oracles reflect the chaotic years that came after: kings assassinated one after another, parties favoring Egypt or Assyria and the growing pressure of the Assyrian empire, which took Samaria in 722 BC. The worship of Baal, a Canaanite deity linked to rain and fertility, and the sanctuaries of Bethel and Gilgal are frequent targets. The prophet draws heavily on Israel's ancient history (Jacob, Egypt, the wilderness) to expose the unfaithfulness of the present.","places":["Samaria","Bethel (called Beth-aven)","Gilgal","Jezreel","Egypt and Assyria"],"themes":["covenant as marriage","unfaithfulness and idolatry","knowledge of God","persistent love (hesed)","repentance and healing"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:19–20","text":"So I will betroth you to Me forever; I will betroth you in righteousness and justice, in loving devotion and compassion. And I will betroth you in faithfulness, and you will know the LORD.”"},{"ref":"6:6","text":"For I desire mercy, not sacrifice, and the knowledge of God rather than burnt offerings."},{"ref":"11:1","text":"When Israel was a child, I loved him, and out of Egypt I called My son."}],"note":"Dates are approximate; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB), which follows the numbering of English Bibles: 1:10–11 corresponds to 2:1–2 in the Hebrew text, and there are similar one-verse differences between chapters 11 and 14."}},"JOL":{"t":{"1":"The plague of locusts and the drought","2":"The day of the LORD and the Spirit poured out","3":"The nations judged in the Valley of Jehoshaphat"},"about":{"title_orig":"יוֹאֵל","title_orig_xlit":"Yoel","summary":"Joel starts from a concrete calamity, a plague of locusts accompanied by drought, and calls priests and people to fasting and mourning. The catastrophe becomes a sign of the “day of the LORD,” described as the invasion of an army. In response to the people's repentance, God promises to restore the harvests, pour out His Spirit on all people and judge the nations that mistreated Judah.","name":"The book bears the name of the prophet, Joel son of Pethuel (1:1). In Hebrew, Yoel joins two forms of the divine name and means “the LORD is God.”","facts":[["Position","2nd of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","3"],["Verses","73"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (poetry)"]],"structure":[{"title":"The plague and the lament","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Locusts and drought devastate the land; the prophet summons everyone to fasting and prayer."},{"title":"The day of the LORD and God's answer","from":2,"to":2,"text":"The alarm in Zion, the call to return with all one's heart, the promise to restore the land and to pour out the Spirit."},{"title":"The judgment of the nations","from":3,"to":3,"text":"The nations are gathered in the Valley of Jehoshaphat, and Judah and Jerusalem are restored."}],"people":[{"name":"Joel","chapters":"1–3","ch":1,"note":"prophet, son of Pethuel; nothing more is known of him"},{"name":"Priests","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"ministers of the altar, called to lead the fasting and weeping"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book is attributed to the prophet Joel, but the title mentions no kings or dates. Its position between Hosea and Amos led many traditional interpreters to place it in the pre-exilic period; an old proposal puts it in the 9th century BC, during the minority of King Joash of Judah, since the book names no king.","academic":"Today many scholars prefer a date after the exile, between c. 500 and 350 BC: the temple is functioning, there is no king and the leaders are elders and priests, the mention of the Greeks as buyers of slaves (3:6) fits that period and the book seems to quote other prophets. Others defend a pre-exilic date. The unity between the first part (the plague) and the second (the day of the LORD) is also debated, but many read the book as a cohesive composition."},"events":{"intro":"Joel gives no data that allow it to be dated securely, and the proposals range from the 9th to the 4th century BC. The timeline shows the two most cited positions.","axis":[-850,-330],"timeline":[{"label":"Joash, king of Judah","from":-835,"to":-796,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign; an old traditional proposal for the book (approximate dates)"},{"label":"Writing, according to a traditional proposal","from":-835,"to":-800,"kind":"escrita","note":"the period of the priest Jehoiada, when Joash was still young"},{"label":"Rebuilding of the temple","from":-520,"to":-515,"kind":"evento","note":"the second temple functions again in Jerusalem"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-500,"to":-350,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian period; the date preferred by many scholars"}]},"context":"Locust plagues were a real threat in the Near East: swarms could destroy in a few days wheat, barley, vines and fig trees, and with them the temple offerings. Joel reads this crisis in the language of the “day of the LORD,” a theme that also appears in Amos, Zephaniah and other prophets. The passage about the Spirit poured out (2:28–32) is quoted in Peter's speech at Pentecost, in Acts 2.","places":["Jerusalem and Mount Zion","Judah","the Valley of Jehoshaphat","Tyre, Sidon and Philistia","Egypt and Edom"],"themes":["the day of the LORD","fasting and repentance","restoration of the land","the outpouring of the Spirit","judgment of the nations"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:12","text":"“Yet even now,” declares the LORD, “return to Me with all your heart, with fasting, weeping, and mourning.”"},{"ref":"2:28–29","text":"And afterward, I will pour out My Spirit on all people. Your sons and daughters will prophesy, your old men will dream dreams, your young men will see visions. Even on My menservants and maidservants, I will pour out My Spirit in those days."},{"ref":"2:32","text":"And everyone who calls on the name of the LORD will be saved; for on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem there will be deliverance, as the LORD has promised, among the remnant called by the LORD."}],"note":"The BSB follows the numbering of English Bibles, with three chapters. In the Hebrew text the book has four: 2:28–32 corresponds to 3:1–5, and chapter 3 corresponds to chapter 4."}},"AMO":{"t":{"1":"Oracles against the neighboring peoples","2":"Moab, Judah and Israel facing judgment","3":"The responsibility of the chosen people","4":"The cows of Bashan and the ignored warnings","5":"Lament and a call to justice","6":"Woe to those at ease","7":"Three visions and the clash with Amaziah","8":"The basket of summer fruit","9":"The end of Israel and the promised restoration"},"about":{"title_orig":"עָמוֹס","title_orig_xlit":"Amos","summary":"Amos, a herdsman from Judah, was sent to the Northern Kingdom in a time of prosperity. The book begins with oracles against the neighboring peoples and ends by pointing at Israel, accused of exploiting the poor, corrupting the courts and keeping an abundant and empty worship. A series of visions announces the end of the kingdom, and the confrontation with the priest of Bethel shows the resistance to the message. The final verses promise the restoration of the “tent of David.”","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. The meaning of Amos is uncertain; it is usually linked to the Hebrew verb meaning “to carry,” perhaps in the sense of “carried” or “upheld” by God.","facts":[["Position","3rd of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","9"],["Verses","146"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (oracles in poetry, visions and a narrative)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Oracles against the nations","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Damascus, Gaza, Tyre, Edom, Ammon, Moab and Judah are judged, and the circle closes on Israel."},{"title":"Words against Israel","from":3,"to":6,"text":"Being chosen brings responsibility; the luxury of Samaria, the oppression of the poor and worship without justice are denounced."},{"title":"Visions and restoration","from":7,"to":9,"text":"Locusts, fire, plumb line, basket of fruit and the Lord beside the altar; the confrontation with Amaziah and the final promise."}],"extra":{"title":"“For three transgressions, even four”","text":"The oracles of chapters 1 and 2 follow the same numerical formula, repeated for each people. An Israelite hearer could agree with the condemnation of the neighbors, until the formula reaches Judah and, finally, Israel itself, which receives the longest accusation."},"people":[{"name":"Amos","chapters":"1–9","ch":1,"note":"shepherd and herdsman from Tekoa, in Judah"},{"name":"Jeroboam II","chapters":"1; 7","ch":7,"note":"king of Israel"},{"name":"Amaziah","chapters":"7","ch":7,"note":"priest of Bethel, who orders Amos to leave the kingdom"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition attributes the book to Amos, who was active “two years before the earthquake” in the days of Uzziah, king of Judah, and of Jeroboam II, king of Israel (1:1), around 760–750 BC. He is considered one of the first prophets whose words were gathered into a book.","academic":"Scholars generally accept that most of the oracles go back to Amos in the mid-8th century BC. Many consider later additions the oracle against Judah (2:4–5), the hymnic passages (4:13; 5:8–9; 9:5–6) and the final promise (9:11–15), which presupposes the fall of the Davidic dynasty; others attribute them to the prophet. The book would have been edited in Judah, after 722 BC and again in the exile."},"events":{"intro":"The book is set at the height of Jeroboam II's reign. The “earthquake” of 1:1 seems to have been memorable, since it is recalled in Zechariah 14:5, and archaeological traces of a strong quake in the mid-8th century BC have been identified at several sites. The dates are approximate.","axis":[-800,-540],"timeline":[{"label":"Uzziah, king of Judah","from":-783,"to":-742,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign, approximate dates"},{"label":"Jeroboam II","from":-786,"to":-746,"kind":"pessoa","note":"king of Israel; expansion and prosperity"},{"label":"Ministry of Amos","from":-760,"to":-750,"kind":"pessoa","note":"at Bethel and Samaria; approximate date"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-760,"to":-750,"kind":"escrita","note":"the words of Amos gathered"},{"label":"Fall of Samaria","from":-722,"to":-720,"kind":"evento","note":"fulfillment of the threats against the Northern Kingdom"},{"label":"Final edition, according to scholarship","from":-720,"to":-550,"kind":"escrita","note":"editions in Judah, down to the period of the exile"}]},"context":"Under Jeroboam II, Israel recovered territories and lived through a phase of trade and wealth, while Assyria was going through a period of weakness. Excavations at Samaria revealed ivory plaques that recall the “houses of ivory” of 3:15. The wealth, however, was concentrated in an elite, and Amos denounces the selling of the poor for debts, false weights and corruption at the city gates, where trials were held. A few decades later, Assyria expanded again and destroyed the kingdom.","places":["Tekoa","Bethel","Samaria","Gilgal and Beersheba","Damascus, Gaza, Tyre, Edom, Ammon and Moab"],"themes":["social justice","worship and ethics","election and responsibility","the day of the LORD","restoration"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"5:24","text":"But let justice roll on like a river, and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream."},{"ref":"7:14–15","text":"“I was not a prophet,” Amos replied, “nor was I the son of a prophet; rather, I was a herdsman and a tender of sycamore-fig trees. But the LORD took me from following the flock and said to me, ‘Go, prophesy to My people Israel.’"},{"ref":"8:11","text":"Behold, the days are coming, declares the Lord GOD, when I will send a famine on the land— not a famine of bread or a thirst for water, but a famine of hearing the words of the LORD."}],"note":"Dates vary according to the source; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"OBA":{"t":{"1":"The judgment on Edom"},"about":{"title_orig":"עֹבַדְיָה","title_orig_xlit":"Ovadyah","summary":"Obadiah, the shortest book of the Hebrew Bible, is an oracle against Edom, a neighboring people descended from Esau. Edom is accused of pride and of having taken advantage of the fall of Jerusalem, plundering and handing over fugitives. The prophet announces that the day of the LORD will come upon all the nations and that the kingdom will belong to the LORD.","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. Obadiah, in Hebrew Ovadyah, means “servant of the LORD”; it was a common name, used by several people in the Old Testament.","facts":[["Position","4th of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","1"],["Verses","21"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (oracle against a nation)"]],"structure":[{"title":"The fall of Edom","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Verses 1–14: Edom's pride, its announced defeat and the accusation for what it did on the day of Jerusalem's disaster."},{"title":"The day of the LORD","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Verses 15–21: judgment on the nations and the restoration of the house of Jacob on Mount Zion."}],"people":[{"name":"Obadiah","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"a prophet of whom nothing more is known"},{"name":"Edom (Esau)","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"people descended from Esau, Jacob's brother"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book is attributed to the prophet Obadiah. A tradition recorded in the Talmud identifies him with Obadiah, the steward of King Ahab's palace (1 Kings 18), in the 9th century BC. Other traditional interpreters link the text to an attack on Jerusalem in the reign of Jehoram of Judah, c. 848–841 BC, when Edom rebelled.","academic":"Most scholars relate the book to the destruction of Jerusalem by the Babylonians in 587/586 BC, when, according to other biblical texts (Psalm 137:7; Lamentations 4:21–22), the Edomites sided with the victors. The book would have been written shortly afterward, in the 6th century BC. Verses 1–9 strongly parallel Jeremiah 49:7–22, which suggests the use of a common tradition."},"events":{"intro":"The text gives no date. The timeline shows the oldest traditional proposal and that of most scholars, which links it to the fall of Jerusalem; the dates are approximate.","axis":[-870,-500],"band":{"label":"Exile in Babylon","from":-586,"to":-538},"timeline":[{"label":"Jehoram, king of Judah","from":-848,"to":-841,"kind":"pessoa","note":"Edom rebels against Judah (2 Kings 8:20–22)"},{"label":"Writing, according to one tradition","from":-848,"to":-841,"kind":"escrita","note":"a proposal that links the book to this period"},{"label":"Fall of Jerusalem","from":-587,"to":-586,"kind":"evento","note":"destruction by the Babylonians; Edom takes advantage of the defeat"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-586,"to":-550,"kind":"escrita","note":"shortly after the destruction of Jerusalem"}]},"context":"Edom occupied the mountainous region southeast of the Dead Sea, with cities in high, rocky places, which explains the image of the nest among the rocks (verses 3–4). The Bible describes the Edomites as descendants of Esau, Jacob's brother, and the rivalry between the two peoples runs through the whole history of Israel. In the period of the exile and afterward, Edomite groups settled in the south of Judah, in the region that would later be called Idumea.","places":["Edom (Mount Esau)","Teman","Jerusalem and Mount Zion","the Negev and the Shephelah"],"themes":["pride and fall","solidarity between brothers","the day of the LORD","the kingdom of God"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:15","text":"For the Day of the LORD is near for all the nations. As you have done, it will be done to you; your recompense will return upon your own head."},{"ref":"1:21","text":"The deliverers will ascend Mount Zion to rule over the mountains of Esau. And the kingdom will belong to the LORD."}],"note":"Dates are approximate; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"JON":{"t":{"1":"Jonah's flight and the storm","2":"The prayer in the belly of the fish","3":"The preaching in Nineveh and repentance","4":"Jonah's anger and the plant"},"about":{"title_orig":"יוֹנָה","title_orig_xlit":"Yonah","summary":"Unlike the other prophets, Jonah is almost entirely narrative. Sent to preach against Nineveh, the Assyrian capital and Israel's enemy, the prophet flees by ship, is thrown into the sea and swallowed by a great fish. After praying and being returned to land, he preaches in the city, which repents and is spared. The book ends with Jonah angry at God's mercy and with a question that goes unanswered.","name":"The book bears the name of its protagonist. Jonah, in Hebrew Yonah, means “dove.” He is identified with Jonah son of Amittai, a prophet mentioned in 2 Kings 14:25.","facts":[["Position","5th of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","4"],["Verses","48"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophetic narrative, with a psalm (2:2–9)"]],"structure":[{"title":"The flight","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Jonah flees to Tarshish, the storm overtakes him, the sailors throw him into the sea and he prays from the belly of the fish."},{"title":"The mission and the reaction","from":3,"to":4,"text":"Nineveh hears the message and repents; God spares the city, and Jonah argues with God because of it."}],"people":[{"name":"Jonah","chapters":"1–4","ch":1,"note":"prophet, son of Amittai, from Gath-hepher (2 Kings 14:25)"},{"name":"The sailors","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"foreigners who fear the LORD and offer sacrifices"},{"name":"The king of Nineveh","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"decrees fasting and repentance for the whole city"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Jewish and Christian tradition see the book as an account of the mission of Jonah son of Amittai, who prophesied in the days of Jeroboam II (2 Kings 14:25), in the 8th century BC, and many attribute it to the prophet himself, around 780–750 BC. On this reading, the book narrates historical events.","academic":"The book speaks of Jonah in the third person and names no author. Many scholars date it to the Persian period, between c. 500 and 300 BC, based on features of the language and on the way Nineveh is described, as a city of the past. The genre is debated: historical account, didactic narrative, parable or prophetic satire. There is also discussion about whether the psalm of chapter 2 was composed together with the narrative or inserted later."},"events":{"intro":"The character belongs to the 8th century BC, but the book gives no date of its own. The dates below are approximate and show Jonah's time and the two proposals for the writing.","axis":[-800,-280],"timeline":[{"label":"Jeroboam II","from":-786,"to":-746,"kind":"pessoa","note":"king of Israel in whose days Jonah son of Amittai was active"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-780,"to":-750,"kind":"escrita","note":"account of Jonah's mission"},{"label":"Nineveh, Assyrian capital","from":-705,"to":-612,"kind":"evento","note":"Sennacherib makes Nineveh the capital; the city falls in 612 BC"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-500,"to":-300,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian or early Hellenistic period"}]},"context":"Nineveh, on the banks of the Tigris, was one of the great cities of Assyria and, from Sennacherib on, around 700 BC, became the capital of the empire that destroyed the Northern Kingdom in 722 BC. For readers in Israel, the city symbolized the enemy's cruelty, as the book of Nahum shows. Tarshish is usually placed at the far western end of the Mediterranean, perhaps in the Iberian Peninsula, the opposite direction from Nineveh. Joppa was the port on Israel's coast.","places":["Joppa","Tarshish","the sea","Nineveh"],"themes":["God's mercy","repentance","God and the nations","obedience and flight","compassion"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:9","text":"But I, with the voice of thanksgiving, will sacrifice to You. I will fulfill what I have vowed. Salvation is from the LORD!”"},{"ref":"3:10","text":"When God saw their actions—that they had turned from their evil ways—He relented from the disaster He had threatened to bring upon them."},{"ref":"4:2","text":"So he prayed to the LORD, saying, “O LORD, is this not what I said while I was still in my own country? This is why I was so quick to flee toward Tarshish. I knew that You are a gracious and compassionate God, slow to anger, abounding in loving devotion—One who relents from sending disaster."}],"note":"The BSB follows the numbering of English Bibles: 1:17 corresponds to 2:1 in the Hebrew text, and 2:1 to 2:2, and so on to the end of chapter 2. Dates are approximate."}},"MIC":{"t":{"1":"Judgment on Samaria and Jerusalem","2":"Against those who covet fields and houses","3":"Against leaders, prophets and priests","4":"The mountain of the LORD in the last days","5":"The ruler who will come from Bethlehem","6":"God's case against His people","7":"Lament, trust and forgiveness"},"about":{"title_orig":"מִיכָה","title_orig_xlit":"Mikhah","summary":"Micah, a prophet from the countryside of Judah, announces judgment on Samaria and Jerusalem because of the greed of the powerful, who seize fields and houses, and the corruption of leaders, priests and paid prophets. Alternating threats and promises, the book announces the ruin of Zion, but also a time when the nations will go up to the mountain of the LORD and will no longer train for war, and a ruler who will come forth from Bethlehem. The book sums up what God asks: to act justly, to love mercy and to walk humbly with God (6:8).","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. Micah is a shortened form of a name that asks “Who is like the LORD?”, a question echoed in 7:18.","facts":[["Position","6th of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","7"],["Verses","105"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (poetry)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Judgment on the two kingdoms","from":1,"to":3,"text":"Samaria and Jerusalem are condemned; denunciation of those who steal land and of leaders, prophets and priests who act for money."},{"title":"Hope for Zion","from":4,"to":5,"text":"The mountain of the LORD above the others, peace among the nations, the remnant gathered and the ruler coming from Bethlehem."},{"title":"The lawsuit and trust","from":6,"to":7,"text":"God brings His people to trial and reminds them of what is good; the book ends with lament, trust and praise to the God who forgives."}],"people":[{"name":"Micah","chapters":"1–7","ch":1,"note":"prophet from Moresheth, in the countryside of Judah"},{"name":"The leaders of Jacob","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"rulers, judges, priests and prophets accused of corruption"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition attributes the book to Micah of Moresheth, who prophesied in the days of Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah, kings of Judah (1:1), roughly between 740 and 700 BC. Jeremiah 26:18 quotes Micah 3:12 and places him in the reign of Hezekiah.","academic":"Many scholars attribute to the prophet mainly chapters 1 to 3, with their denunciations, and consider that much of chapters 4 to 7 was added in the exile or after it, since it mentions Babylon (4:10) and the return of the scattered. Others see the book as substantially from the 8th century BC. The oracle of 4:1–3 appears almost identically in Isaiah 2:2–4, and it is debated which text depends on the other or whether both use a common source."},"events":{"intro":"Micah was active at the time when Assyria destroyed the Northern Kingdom and invaded Judah. The dates are approximate.","axis":[-760,-490],"band":{"label":"Exile in Babylon","from":-586,"to":-538},"timeline":[{"label":"Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah","from":-750,"to":-686,"kind":"pessoa","note":"kings of Judah named in 1:1 (approximate dates)"},{"label":"Micah","from":-740,"to":-700,"kind":"pessoa","note":"approximate ministry"},{"label":"Fall of Samaria","from":-722,"to":-720,"kind":"evento","note":"announced in 1:6–7"},{"label":"Sennacherib's campaign","from":-701,"to":-701,"kind":"evento","note":"cities of the Shephelah, such as Lachish, are taken; Jerusalem is besieged"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-740,"to":-700,"kind":"escrita","note":"Micah of Moresheth"},{"label":"Additions, according to scholarship","from":-586,"to":-500,"kind":"escrita","note":"part of chapters 4–7, in the exile or shortly after"}]},"context":"Moresheth-gath lay in the Shephelah, the foothills between the coastal plain and the mountains of Judah, an agricultural border region. Micah speaks from the point of view of the small farmers of the countryside, threatened by creditors and by powerful families of the capital. The towns named in chapter 1, such as Lachish, lay in the path of the Assyrian army, which in 701 BC devastated the region, as recorded in Sennacherib's annals and the Lachish reliefs at Nineveh.","places":["Moresheth-gath and the towns of the Shephelah","Lachish","Samaria","Jerusalem and Zion","Bethlehem Ephrathah"],"themes":["social justice","corruption of the leaders","peace among the nations","the ruler from Bethlehem","God's forgiveness"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"4:3","text":"Then He will judge between many peoples and arbitrate for strong nations far and wide. Then they will beat their swords into plowshares and their spears into pruning hooks. Nation will no longer take up the sword against nation, nor will they train anymore for war."},{"ref":"5:2","text":"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, who are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come forth for Me One to be ruler over Israel— One whose origins are of old, from the days of eternity."},{"ref":"6:8","text":"He has shown you, O man, what is good. And what does the LORD require of you but to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God?"},{"ref":"7:18","text":"Who is a God like You, who pardons iniquity and passes over the transgression of the remnant of His inheritance— who does not retain His anger forever, because He delights in loving devotion?"}],"note":"Dates are approximate; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB), which follows the numbering of English Bibles: 5:1 corresponds to 4:14 in the Hebrew text, and 5:2 to 5:1, and so on to the end of the chapter."}},"NAM":{"t":{"1":"The wrath and the goodness of the LORD","2":"The attack and fall of Nineveh","3":"Woe to the city of blood"},"about":{"title_orig":"נַחוּם","title_orig_xlit":"Nachum","summary":"Nahum announces the fall of Nineveh, capital of Assyria, the empire that dominated the Near East for more than a century and oppressed Israel and Judah. The book begins with a poem about the LORD, slow to anger, but who does not leave the guilty unpunished, and then describes, in vivid images, the attack on the city and its ruin. For Judah, the news is proclaimed as good news of peace.","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. Nahum, in Hebrew Nachum, means “comfort” or “consolation.” He is called “the Elkoshite” (1:1), from a place called Elkosh whose location is unknown.","facts":[["Position","7th of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","3"],["Verses","47"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (oracle against a nation, in poetry)"]],"structure":[{"title":"The God who judges","from":1,"to":1,"text":"A poem about the LORD's power, followed by the announcement of deliverance for Judah."},{"title":"The fall of Nineveh","from":2,"to":3,"text":"The attack, the plunder and the ruin of the city; the comparison with Thebes and the final lament with no healing."}],"extra":{"title":"An incomplete acrostic","text":"The poem of 1:2–8 seems to follow the order of the Hebrew alphabet partially, with lines that begin with successive letters, from the first to about the middle of the alphabet. The form is lost in translation."},"people":[{"name":"Nahum","chapters":"1–3","ch":1,"note":"prophet, called “the Elkoshite”"},{"name":"The king of Assyria","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"to whom the final words are addressed"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book is attributed to the prophet Nahum. Since he speaks of the capture of Thebes (No-amon, in Egypt) as a past event (3:8–10) and of the fall of Nineveh as future, tradition and many interpreters place the prophecy between 663 and 612 BC.","academic":"Scholars generally accept the same interval, between the destruction of Thebes by the Assyrians in 663 BC and the fall of Nineveh in 612 BC, often near its end, when Assyria's decline was visible. Some think that the book, or parts of it, was composed or re-edited soon after the fall, as a celebration. The opening poem is sometimes seen as an editor's addition."},"events":{"intro":"The two dates that frame the book are known from Assyrian and Babylonian sources: the capture of Thebes and the fall of Nineveh.","axis":[-700,-600],"timeline":[{"label":"Ashurbanipal","from":-668,"to":-631,"kind":"pessoa","note":"king of Assyria; reign of approximate dates"},{"label":"Capture of Thebes (No-amon)","from":-663,"to":-663,"kind":"evento","note":"the Assyrians plunder the Egyptian city (3:8–10)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition and scholarship","from":-663,"to":-612,"kind":"escrita","note":"accepted interval; many prefer near 615 BC"},{"label":"Fall of Nineveh","from":-612,"to":-612,"kind":"evento","note":"Babylonians and Medes take and destroy the city"}]},"context":"Assyria dominated the region from the 9th century BC, destroyed Samaria in 722 BC, invaded Judah in 701 BC and exacted tribute from the kings of Jerusalem for decades. Its own reliefs and annals extol deportations and cruel punishments of rebels. After the death of Ashurbanipal, the empire weakened rapidly, and in 612 BC a coalition of Babylonians and Medes took Nineveh. For the subject peoples, the news was a relief, and the book expresses that reaction.","places":["Nineveh","Thebes (No-amon, in Egypt)","Judah","Bashan, Carmel and Lebanon"],"themes":["God's justice","the fall of the oppressor","God's wrath and goodness","good news of peace"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:3","text":"The LORD is slow to anger and great in power; the LORD will by no means leave the guilty unpunished. His path is in the whirlwind and storm, and clouds are the dust beneath His feet."},{"ref":"1:7","text":"The LORD is good, a stronghold in the day of distress; He cares for those who trust in Him."},{"ref":"1:15","text":"Look to the mountains— the feet of one who brings good news, who proclaims peace! Celebrate your feasts, O Judah; fulfill your vows. For the wicked will never again march through you; they will be utterly cut off."}],"note":"The BSB follows the numbering of English Bibles: 1:15 corresponds to 2:1 in the Hebrew text, and 2:1 to 2:2, and so on to the end of chapter 2."}},"HAB":{"t":{"1":"The prophet's complaint and the Chaldeans","2":"God's answer and the five woes","3":"The prayer of Habakkuk"},"about":{"title_orig":"חֲבַקּוּק","title_orig_xlit":"Chavaqquq","summary":"Habakkuk is a dialogue between the prophet and God. The prophet complains of the violence and injustice in Judah; God answers that He will raise up the Chaldeans (Babylonians) as an instrument of judgment, which provokes a second complaint: how can He use a people even more violent? The answer includes the affirmation that the righteous will live by faith and a series of woes against the oppressor. The book ends with a prayer in the form of a psalm, which trusts in God even when everything is lacking.","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. The meaning of Habakkuk is uncertain: some link it to the Hebrew verb “to embrace,” others to an Akkadian word that designates a plant.","facts":[["Position","8th of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","3"],["Verses","56"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy in the form of a dialogue, with a psalm (chapter 3)"]],"structure":[{"title":"The dialogue with God","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Two complaints of the prophet and two answers: the Chaldeans as an instrument of judgment and the vision that must be written down; then, five woes against the oppressor."},{"title":"The prophet's prayer","from":3,"to":3,"text":"A psalm that describes God's coming as a warrior and ends with a declaration of trust."}],"people":[{"name":"Habakkuk","chapters":"1–3","ch":1,"note":"prophet; nothing is known of his life beyond the book"},{"name":"The Chaldeans","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"the Babylonians, described as a swift and violent nation"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book is attributed to the prophet Habakkuk. Since it speaks of the rise of the Chaldeans as a power, it is usually placed at the end of the 7th century BC, around 609–598 BC, during the reign of Jehoiakim in Judah.","academic":"Scholars generally accept a date at the end of the 7th or beginning of the 6th century BC, linked to the rise of Babylon after the battle of Carchemish (605 BC). It is debated whether chapter 3, which has its own title and musical directions, was composed together with the rest; among the Dead Sea Scrolls, the Qumran commentary on Habakkuk covers only chapters 1 and 2, although other ancient manuscripts include chapter 3."},"events":{"intro":"The book reflects the passage from Assyrian to Babylonian rule. The dates are approximate.","axis":[-630,-580],"timeline":[{"label":"Fall of Nineveh","from":-612,"to":-612,"kind":"evento","note":"end of Assyrian power"},{"label":"Jehoiakim, king of Judah","from":-609,"to":-598,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign"},{"label":"Battle of Carchemish","from":-605,"to":-605,"kind":"evento","note":"Nebuchadnezzar defeats Egypt; Babylon comes to dominate the region"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition and scholarship","from":-609,"to":-598,"kind":"escrita","note":"the most cited interval"},{"label":"First capture of Jerusalem","from":-597,"to":-597,"kind":"evento","note":"the Babylonians deport King Jehoiachin and part of the elite"}]},"context":"At the end of the 7th century BC, Assyria collapsed and Babylon, under Nabopolassar and then Nebuchadnezzar, took its place. Judah, which had lived through Josiah's reform, again faced internal injustice and external dependence, first on Egypt and then on Babylon. The statement of 2:4 about the righteous who live by faith was much commented on in Jewish tradition and is quoted by Paul in Romans 1:17 and Galatians 3:11, and in the Letter to the Hebrews.","places":["Judah and Jerusalem","Babylon (the Chaldeans)","Teman and Mount Paran"],"themes":["the problem of evil","God's justice","faith and faithfulness","judgment of the oppressor","trust in adversity"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:2","text":"How long, O LORD, must I call for help but You do not hear, or cry out to You, “Violence!” but You do not save?"},{"ref":"2:4","text":"Look at the proud one; his soul is not upright— but the righteous will live by faith—"},{"ref":"2:14","text":"For the earth will be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD as the waters cover the sea."},{"ref":"3:17–18","text":"Though the fig tree does not bud and no fruit is on the vines, though the olive crop fails and the fields produce no food, though the sheep are cut off from the fold and no cattle are in the stalls, yet I will exult in the LORD; I will rejoice in the God of my salvation!"}],"note":"Dates are approximate; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"ZEP":{"t":{"1":"The great day of the LORD on Judah","2":"A call to humility and judgment on the nations","3":"Jerusalem rebuked and the remnant restored"},"about":{"title_orig":"צְפַנְיָה","title_orig_xlit":"Tsefanyah","summary":"Zephaniah announces the “great day of the LORD” as a day of wrath on Judah and Jerusalem, because of idolatry, violence and the indifference of those who think God does neither good nor evil. The judgment extends to the neighboring nations and to Nineveh itself. At the end, the tone changes: God will leave a humble and lowly people who trust in Him, and He will rejoice over a restored Jerusalem.","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. Zephaniah, in Hebrew Tsefanyah, is usually understood as “the LORD has hidden” or “the LORD has protected.”","facts":[["Position","9th of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","3"],["Verses","53"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (poetry)"]],"structure":[{"title":"The day of the LORD on Judah","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Judgment against foreign cults and indifference in Jerusalem; the day of wrath described in strong images."},{"title":"Call and judgment of the nations","from":2,"to":3,"text":"An invitation to seek the LORD and oracles against Philistia, Moab, Ammon, Cush and Assyria; then, against Jerusalem."},{"title":"Restoration","from":3,"to":3,"text":"The peoples are purified, a humble remnant remains and Zion is called to sing."}],"people":[{"name":"Zephaniah","chapters":"1–3","ch":1,"note":"prophet, a descendant of a Hezekiah, perhaps the king"},{"name":"Josiah","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"king of Judah in whose days the prophet was active"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book is attributed to Zephaniah, who prophesied in the days of Josiah, king of Judah (1:1), between 640 and 609 BC. His four-generation genealogy, back to a certain Hezekiah, is traditionally read as a sign that he descended from King Hezekiah.","academic":"Many scholars place the core of the book at the beginning of Josiah's reign, before the religious reform of 622 BC, since the practices denounced in chapter 1 are those the reform combated. Parts of the book, especially the oracles of restoration in chapter 3, are seen by many as additions from the exile or later; others attribute them to the prophet himself."},"events":{"intro":"The title dates the prophet to the reign of Josiah. The dates are approximate.","axis":[-650,-500],"band":{"label":"Exile in Babylon","from":-586,"to":-538},"timeline":[{"label":"Josiah, king of Judah","from":-640,"to":-609,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-640,"to":-622,"kind":"escrita","note":"Zephaniah, before Josiah's reform"},{"label":"Josiah's reform","from":-622,"to":-622,"kind":"evento","note":"the Book of the Law is found in the temple (2 Kings 22–23)"},{"label":"Fall of Nineveh","from":-612,"to":-612,"kind":"evento","note":"announced in 2:13–15"},{"label":"Additions, according to scholarship","from":-586,"to":-520,"kind":"escrita","note":"part of the oracles of restoration"}]},"context":"Josiah came to the throne as a child, after long reigns (Manasseh and Amon) in which Judah was under Assyrian rule and foreign cults were accepted in Jerusalem, such as those of Baal and of the “host of heaven.” With Assyria's decline, Josiah carried out a religious and political reform. Zephaniah reflects this setting, and his description of the day of the LORD inspired the medieval Latin hymn Dies irae.","places":["Jerusalem","Philistia: Gaza, Ashkelon, Ashdod and Ekron","Moab and Ammon","Cush","Nineveh"],"themes":["the day of the LORD","idolatry and indifference","humility","the remnant","God's joy over His people"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:14","text":"The great Day of the LORD is near— near and coming quickly. Listen, the Day of the LORD! Then the cry of the mighty will be bitter."},{"ref":"2:3","text":"Seek the LORD, all you humble of the earth who carry out His justice. Seek righteousness; seek humility. Perhaps you will be sheltered on the day of the LORD’s anger."},{"ref":"3:17","text":"The LORD your God is among you; He is mighty to save. He will rejoice over you with gladness; He will quiet you with His love; He will rejoice over you with singing.”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"HAG":{"t":{"1":"The call to rebuild the temple","2":"The glory of the new temple and Zerubbabel"},"about":{"title_orig":"חַגַּי","title_orig_xlit":"Chaggai","summary":"Haggai gathers four messages, all dated to the second year of the Persian king Darius, in 520 BC. The Jews who had returned from exile had built their houses, but left the temple in ruins. The prophet links the poor harvests to this neglect, and the governor Zerubbabel, the high priest Joshua and the people resume the work. Haggai announces that the glory of the new temple will be greater than that of the first and ends with a promise to Zerubbabel.","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. Haggai, in Hebrew Chaggai, comes from the word for “feast” and may mean “festive,” perhaps because he was born on a feast day.","facts":[["Position","10th of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","2"],["Verses","38"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy (dated messages, in prose)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Call to rebuild","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The house of God lies in ruins; the leaders and the people listen and begin the work."},{"title":"Promises for the new temple","from":2,"to":2,"text":"The future glory of the temple, a lesson about clean and unclean and the promise to Zerubbabel, called a signet ring."}],"people":[{"name":"Haggai","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"prophet; also mentioned in Ezra 5:1 and 6:14"},{"name":"Zerubbabel","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, a descendant of David"},{"name":"Joshua","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"son of Jehozadak, high priest"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book is attributed to the prophet Haggai, and the messages are precisely dated between the sixth and the ninth month of the second year of Darius, from August to December 520 BC, in the modern calendar.","academic":"Scholars generally accept the book's dates. Many note that the prophet is mentioned in the third person and that the dates form an editorial frame, which suggests that the messages were gathered by a disciple or editor shortly afterward, perhaps before the completion of the temple, in 515 BC, together with Zechariah 1–8."},"events":{"intro":"Haggai is one of the most precisely dated prophetic books. The messages cover about four months of 520 BC.","axis":[-545,-480],"timeline":[{"label":"Decree of Cyrus and first return","from":-539,"to":-538,"kind":"evento","note":"the exiles receive permission to return to Judah"},{"label":"Darius I, king of Persia","from":-522,"to":-486,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign"},{"label":"Haggai's messages","from":-520,"to":-520,"kind":"escrita","note":"from the 6th to the 9th month of the second year of Darius"},{"label":"Rebuilding of the temple","from":-520,"to":-515,"kind":"evento","note":"the second temple is completed in the sixth year of Darius (Ezra 6:15)"}]},"context":"Cyrus allowed the exiles to return after 539 BC, but the rebuilding of the temple stalled in the face of poverty, poor harvests and the opposition of the neighbors (Ezra 4). When Darius I took the throne, in 522 BC, there were revolts in several parts of the empire, which may explain the expectation in 2:6–7 and 2:21–22 that God would shake the kingdoms. Judah was then a small Persian province around Jerusalem.","places":["Jerusalem","the temple (the house of the LORD)","Judah, a Persian province"],"themes":["rebuilding of the temple","priorities","God's presence","purity","messianic hope"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:4","text":"“Is it a time for you yourselves to live in your paneled houses, while this house lies in ruins?”"},{"ref":"2:9","text":"The latter glory of this house will be greater than the former, says the LORD of Hosts. And in this place I will provide peace, declares the LORD of Hosts.”"}],"note":"The book's dates follow the Babylonian calendar used by the Jews; the conversion to modern months is approximate. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"ZEC":{"t":{"1":"A call to return and the horsemen among the myrtles","2":"The man with the measuring line","3":"Joshua the high priest cleansed","4":"The lampstand and the two olive trees","5":"The flying scroll and the woman in the basket","6":"The four chariots and Joshua's crown","7":"Fasting, justice and the fathers' disobedience","8":"Promises of restoration for Jerusalem","9":"The king who comes riding on a colt","10":"The LORD gathers and strengthens His people","11":"The two staffs and the worthless shepherds","12":"Jerusalem besieged and mourning for the pierced one","13":"The fountain of cleansing and the stricken shepherd","14":"The day of the LORD and the universal reign"},"about":{"title_orig":"זְכַרְיָה","title_orig_xlit":"Zekharyah","summary":"Zechariah was active together with Haggai, when the Jews who had returned from exile were rebuilding the temple. Chapters 1 to 8 contain eight night visions, full of symbols (horsemen, horns, a lampstand, a flying scroll), that announce the purification of the people and the return of God's presence to Jerusalem, as well as answers about fasting and promises of restoration. Chapters 9 to 14, undated, speak of a humble king who comes riding on a donkey's colt, of faithful and unfaithful shepherds and of the day when the LORD will be king over all the earth.","name":"The book bears the prophet's name. Zechariah, in Hebrew Zekharyah, means “the LORD has remembered.” It was one of the most common names in the Old Testament.","facts":[["Position","11th of the Twelve Minor Prophets"],["Chapters","14"],["Verses","211"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy, with visions of an apocalyptic tone"]],"structure":[{"title":"Night visions","from":1,"to":6,"text":"A call to repentance and eight visions about the judgment of the nations, the purification of Jerusalem and its leaders; the crown of the high priest Joshua."},{"title":"Fasting and the promises","from":7,"to":8,"text":"A question about the fasts leads to recalling the justice required and announcing days of joy for Jerusalem."},{"title":"First oracle","from":9,"to":11,"text":"The king who comes, the people gathered and the parable of the two staffs and the bad shepherds."},{"title":"Second oracle","from":12,"to":14,"text":"Jerusalem attacked and defended, the mourning for the one who was pierced, the fountain of cleansing and the day of the LORD."}],"people":[{"name":"Zechariah","chapters":"1–14","ch":1,"note":"prophet, son of Berechiah, son of Iddo"},{"name":"Joshua","chapters":"3; 6","ch":3,"note":"high priest, cleansed in the vision and crowned as a sign"},{"name":"Zerubbabel","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"governor of Judah, who will complete the building of the temple"},{"name":"The angel who was speaking with me","chapters":"1–6","ch":1,"note":"interpreter of the visions"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition attributes the whole book to Zechariah son of Berechiah, a prophet contemporary with Haggai (Ezra 5:1; 6:14). The dated visions and messages run from 520 to 518 BC; chapters 9 to 14 would be by the same prophet, perhaps from a later phase of his life.","academic":"Scholars generally attribute chapters 1 to 8 to Zechariah, between 520 and 518 BC. Chapters 9 to 14 have a different style and subject matter, give no dates or the prophet's name and begin with their own titles (9:1; 12:1); many attribute them to one or more later authors, called “Second Zechariah,” and date them between the end of the Persian period and the beginning of the Hellenistic (5th to 4th centuries BC), partly because of the mention of Greece (9:13). Some argue for an older date for these chapters, and others maintain the unity of the book."},"events":{"intro":"The dates of chapters 1 to 8 follow the reign of Darius I; chapters 9 to 14 are undated. The dates are approximate.","axis":[-545,-300],"timeline":[{"label":"Decree of Cyrus and first return","from":-539,"to":-538,"kind":"evento","note":"the exiles receive permission to return"},{"label":"Darius I, king of Persia","from":-522,"to":-486,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign"},{"label":"Messages of Zechariah 1–8","from":-520,"to":-518,"kind":"escrita","note":"from the 8th month of the 2nd year to the 9th month of the 4th year of Darius (1:1; 7:1)"},{"label":"Rebuilding of the temple","from":-520,"to":-515,"kind":"evento","note":"completed in 515 BC"},{"label":"Chapters 9–14, according to scholarship","from":-480,"to":-330,"kind":"escrita","note":"“Second Zechariah,” at much-debated dates"},{"label":"Alexander the Great","from":-336,"to":-323,"kind":"pessoa","note":"conquers the Persian empire"}]},"context":"Zechariah was active in the small Persian province of Judah, where a community that had returned from exile was trying to rebuild the temple and reorganize religious life, sharing leadership between the governor Zerubbabel, of the line of David, and the high priest Joshua. The visions use images that anticipate apocalyptic literature, with interpreting angels and numerical symbols. Chapters 9 to 14 were much used in the New Testament, especially in the accounts of Jesus' entry into Jerusalem and of the passion.","places":["Jerusalem and Zion","the temple","Babylon (the land of Shinar and the land of the north)","the Mount of Olives","Hadrach, Damascus, Tyre and Sidon"],"themes":["rebuilding and return","purification","leadership: king and priest","faithful and unfaithful shepherds","the day of the LORD","God's universal reign"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"8:23","text":"This is what the LORD of Hosts says: “In those days ten men from the nations of every tongue will tightly grasp the robe of a Jew, saying, ‘Let us go with you, for we have heard that God is with you.’”"},{"ref":"9:9","text":"Rejoice greatly, O Daughter of Zion! Shout in triumph, O Daughter of Jerusalem! See, your King comes to you, righteous and victorious, humble and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey."},{"ref":"14:9","text":"On that day the LORD will become King over all the earth—the LORD alone, and His name alone."}],"note":"Dates are approximate; the verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB), which follows the numbering of English Bibles: 1:18–21 corresponds to 2:1–4 in the Hebrew text, and 2:1 to 2:5, and so on to the end of chapter 2."}},"MAL":{"t":{"1":"God's love and the blemished offerings","2":"A warning to the priests and marital faithfulness","3":"The messenger of the covenant and the tithes","4":"The coming day and the prophet Elijah"},"about":{"title_orig":"מַלְאָכִי","title_orig_xlit":"Malakhi","summary":"Malachi addresses the community of Judah some decades after the rebuilding of the temple, when the enthusiasm had cooled. The book is organized in dialogues: God states something, the people answer with a question (“How…?”) and God explains. The accusations deal with sacrifices of defective animals, negligent priests, broken marriages, injustice and withheld tithes. The book announces a messenger who will prepare the way and ends with the promise of the sending of the prophet Elijah before the day of the LORD.","name":"Malachi, in Hebrew Malakhi, means “my messenger.” Because of 3:1 (“I will send My messenger”), some understand the word in 1:1 as a title and not a proper name; the Septuagint translates it “his messenger.”","facts":[["Position","12th of the Twelve Minor Prophets; the last book of the Old Testament in Christian Bibles"],["Chapters","4"],["Verses","55"],["Original language","Hebrew"],["Genre","prophecy in the form of dialogues (disputations)"]],"structure":[{"title":"God's love and the despised worship","from":1,"to":2,"text":"God affirms His love; priests and people offer the worst; the covenant with Levi and faithfulness in marriage."},{"title":"The coming day","from":2,"to":4,"text":"The complaint that God does not do justice, the messenger of the covenant, the tithes, the scroll of remembrance and the promise of Elijah."}],"extra":{"title":"Six disputations","text":"The book is organized in about six disputations that follow the same pattern: a statement by God, an objection by the people introduced by “But you ask…” or “How…?”, and an answer. The format recalls a debate and gives the text a direct tone."},"people":[{"name":"Malachi","chapters":"1–4","ch":1,"note":"prophet, or “my messenger”"},{"name":"The priests","chapters":"1–2","ch":2,"note":"accused of despising the altar and of teaching badly"},{"name":"Elijah","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"ancient prophet whose return is announced before the day of the LORD"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition considers him the last of the Old Testament prophets. A Jewish tradition recorded in the Targum and the Talmud identifies Malachi with Ezra the scribe. In general, he is placed in the time of Ezra and Nehemiah, in the 5th century BC.","academic":"Scholars date the book to the Persian period, after the rebuilding of the temple (515 BC), since worship is functioning and the province has a governor (1:8). The faults denounced, such as mixed marriages and tithes not delivered, recall those Ezra and Nehemiah combat, and the most cited date is c. 500–450 BC. Many consider the final verses (4:4–6) an addition that closes the collection of the prophets."},"events":{"intro":"Malachi gives no dates. The references to the functioning temple and to the governor place the book in the Persian period; the dates are approximate.","axis":[-530,-420],"timeline":[{"label":"Rebuilding of the temple","from":-520,"to":-515,"kind":"evento","note":"the second temple is completed"},{"label":"Writing, according to scholarship","from":-500,"to":-450,"kind":"escrita","note":"Persian period, before or near the time of Ezra and Nehemiah"},{"label":"Ezra","from":-458,"to":-440,"kind":"pessoa","note":"arrival in Jerusalem c. 458 BC according to the most common dating, which is debated"},{"label":"Nehemiah","from":-445,"to":-430,"kind":"pessoa","note":"governor of Judah; rebuilding of the walls"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":-445,"to":-430,"kind":"escrita","note":"in the time of Ezra and Nehemiah"}]},"context":"Judah was then a small province of the Persian empire, with a rebuilt but modest temple and a population facing droughts, pests and poverty. The promises of Haggai and Zechariah seemed slow in coming, and the book gives voice to this discouragement: many wondered whether it was worth serving God. In Christian Bibles, Malachi closes the Old Testament, and the figure of the messenger and of Elijah is taken up in the Gospels in relation to John the Baptist; in the Hebrew Bible, the book closes the Prophets.","places":["Jerusalem and the temple","Judah","Edom"],"themes":["God's love","sincere worship","covenant and priesthood","marital faithfulness","God's justice","the day of the LORD"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"3:6","text":"“Because I, the LORD, do not change, you descendants of Jacob have not been destroyed."},{"ref":"4:5–6","text":"Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and awesome Day of the LORD. And he will turn the hearts of the fathers to their children, and the hearts of the children to their fathers. Otherwise, I will come and strike the land with a curse.”"}],"note":"The BSB follows the numbering of English Bibles, with four chapters. In the Hebrew text the book has three: chapter 4 corresponds to 3:19–24. Dates are approximate."}},"MAT":{"t":{"1":"The genealogy and birth of Jesus","2":"The Magi, the flight to Egypt and Nazareth","3":"John the Baptist and the baptism of Jesus","4":"The temptation and the beginning in Galilee","5":"Sermon on the Mount: the Beatitudes and the Law","6":"Giving, prayer, fasting and trust in the Father","7":"Do not judge; the house on the rock","8":"Healings, the storm and the Gadarene demoniacs","9":"The call of Matthew and more healings","10":"The Twelve are sent on mission","11":"John's doubt; the invitation to the weary","12":"Disputes over the Sabbath and Beelzebul","13":"Parables of the kingdom; rejection at Nazareth","14":"Death of John; five thousand fed","15":"Clean and unclean; the Canaanite woman; the four thousand","16":"Peter confesses the Christ; the cross announced","17":"The transfiguration and the possessed boy","18":"Humility, correction and forgiveness in the community","19":"Divorce, the children and the rich young man","20":"The workers in the vineyard; to serve, not be served","21":"Entry into Jerusalem and the cleansing of the temple","22":"The wedding banquet and debates in the temple","23":"The woes against scribes and Pharisees","24":"The discourse on the Mount of Olives","25":"The ten virgins, the talents and the judgment","26":"Supper, Gethsemane, arrest and Peter's denial","27":"Pilate, the crucifixion and the burial","28":"The resurrection and the sending of the disciples"},"about":{"title_orig":"Κατὰ Ματθαῖον","title_orig_xlit":"Kata Matthaion","summary":"Matthew narrates the life of Jesus of Nazareth, from the genealogy and the birth in Bethlehem to the death in Jerusalem, the resurrection and the sending of the disciples to all nations. The book insists that Jesus is the Messiah awaited by Israel, “the son of David, the son of Abraham” (1:1), and shows again and again that His actions fulfill what the prophets had said. Much of the gospel is taken up by teachings, gathered into five long discourses, the first of which is the Sermon on the Mount (chs. 5–7). Prominent are the proclamation of the “kingdom of heaven,” the debates with Pharisees and scribes about the interpretation of the Law and the life of the community of disciples, which only this gospel calls a “church” (16:18; 18:17).","name":"“Gospel” translates the Greek euangelion, “good news,” a word Christians came to use for the proclamation about Jesus and, later, for the books that tell His story. The title “According to Matthew” (Kata Matthaion) is not part of the text: it was given when the four gospels began to circulate together and was already known at the end of the 2nd century. It links the work to Matthew, the tax collector called by Jesus (9:9; 10:3); in the Greek text used in the app, the name appears with the spelling Maththaios (Μαθθαῖος).","facts":[["Position","1st book of the New Testament and the first of the four Gospels"],["Chapters","28"],["Verses","1,071"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","gospel: an account of the life, teaching, death and resurrection of Jesus, close to ancient biographies"]],"structure":[{"title":"Jesus' origin and childhood","from":1,"to":2,"text":"The genealogy from Abraham to Jesus, the birth in Bethlehem, the visit of the Magi, the flight to Egypt and the move to Nazareth."},{"title":"Preparation","from":3,"to":4,"text":"The preaching of John the Baptist, the baptism of Jesus, the temptation in the wilderness and the call of the first disciples by the Sea of Galilee."},{"title":"The Sermon on the Mount","from":5,"to":7,"text":"First discourse: the Beatitudes, Jesus and the Law, almsgiving, prayer and fasting done in secret, and the comparison between those who build on the rock and those who build on the sand."},{"title":"Miracles and mission","from":8,"to":10,"text":"A series of healings and other miracles, the call of Matthew and the second discourse, with the instructions to the Twelve sent out to the towns of Israel."},{"title":"Opposition and parables","from":11,"to":13,"text":"John the Baptist's question, the arguments about the Sabbath and the charge that Jesus acted by the power of Beelzebul; in the third discourse, the parables of the Kingdom."},{"title":"Jesus forms His disciples","from":14,"to":18,"text":"The feedings of the crowds, Peter's confession, the first announcements of the passion, the transfiguration and the fourth discourse, on life together in the community."},{"title":"Toward Jerusalem and debates in the temple","from":19,"to":23,"text":"Teachings on the way to Judea, the entry into Jerusalem, the cleansing of the temple, parables of judgment and the controversies with the leaders, which end in the woes against the scribes and Pharisees."},{"title":"The discourse on the end","from":24,"to":25,"text":"Fifth discourse, on the Mount of Olives: the destruction of the temple, the coming of the Son of Man and parables about keeping watch and giving account."},{"title":"Passion and resurrection","from":26,"to":28,"text":"The anointing at Bethany, the Last Supper, the arrest in Gethsemane, the trials, the crucifixion, the empty tomb and the final sending of the disciples."}],"extra":{"title":"Five discourses and the fulfillment of the Scriptures","text":"Matthew gathers Jesus' teachings into five great blocks (chs. 5–7, 10, 13, 18 and 24–25), and each one ends with a similar formula: “When Jesus had finished saying these things…” (7:28; 11:1; 13:53; 19:1; 26:1). Some scholars relate the number to the five books of Moses; others see in it only a device of organization. Another striking feature are the quotations introduced by “to fulfill what was spoken through the prophet,” about ten in all, which link episodes of Jesus' life to texts of Israel's Scriptures. The book also opens and closes with the same idea: the child will be called Immanuel, “God with us” (1:23), and the Risen One promises to be with the disciples “always” (28:20)."},"people":[{"name":"Jesus","chapters":"1–28","ch":1,"note":"born in Bethlehem and raised in Nazareth; the book presents Him as Messiah, son of David and Son of God"},{"name":"Joseph and Mary","chapters":"1–2","ch":1,"note":"Joseph, a descendant of David, receives in dreams the instructions about the child; Mary is the mother of Jesus"},{"name":"Herod the Great","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"king of Judea; on learning of the birth, orders the killing of the boys of Bethlehem"},{"name":"John the Baptist","chapters":"3; 11; 14","ch":3,"note":"prophet who preaches in the wilderness of Judea and baptizes Jesus; executed by order of Herod Antipas"},{"name":"Simon Peter","chapters":"4–26","ch":16,"note":"fisherman from Galilee and spokesman of the Twelve; recognizes Jesus as the Christ and later denies Him three times"},{"name":"Matthew","chapters":"9–10","ch":9,"note":"tax collector called by Jesus; tradition holds him to be the author of the book"},{"name":"The Twelve","chapters":"10; 26–28","ch":10,"note":"the disciples chosen and sent on mission; at the end, the eleven are sent to all nations"},{"name":"Herod Antipas","chapters":"14","ch":14,"note":"tetrarch of Galilee, son of Herod the Great; has John beheaded"},{"name":"Pharisees, Sadducees and scribes","chapters":"9–23","ch":23,"note":"Jewish groups and teachers with whom Jesus debates the Law, the Sabbath, purity and the resurrection"},{"name":"Judas Iscariot","chapters":"10; 26–27","ch":26,"note":"one of the Twelve; betrays Jesus to the chief priests for thirty pieces of silver"},{"name":"Caiaphas","chapters":"26","ch":26,"note":"high priest in whose house Jesus is questioned"},{"name":"Pilate","chapters":"27","ch":27,"note":"Roman governor of Judea, who hands Jesus over to be crucified"},{"name":"Mary Magdalene","chapters":"27–28","ch":28,"note":"witnesses the crucifixion and the burial and is among the first to find the empty tomb"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Since the 2nd century, Christian tradition has attributed this gospel to the apostle Matthew, the tax collector of 9:9. Papias of Hierapolis, quoted by Eusebius, says that Matthew put together the sayings (logia) of Jesus “in the Hebrew dialect” and that each one translated them as best he could; Irenaeus, around 180, adds that Matthew wrote among the Hebrews, in their language, while Peter and Paul were preaching in Rome. For this reason the early Church considered it the first gospel to be written, and this is the place it holds in the New Testament. Those who follow this reading usually date it before the destruction of Jerusalem, in 70, between the 50s and the 60s.","academic":"The text that has come down to us was written in Greek and does not bear the marks of a translation; Papias's expression may refer to another work, to a version in Hebrew or Aramaic, or to a Jewish style of writing. To explain the similarities between Matthew, Mark and Luke (the so-called Synoptic Problem), most scholars adopt the hypothesis that Mark came first and was used by Matthew and Luke, who would also have used a collection of Jesus' sayings now lost, called Q. On this view, the author would be a Christian of Jewish origin, deeply versed in the Scriptures, and many consider him anonymous; the most cited date is c. 80–90, after the destruction of the temple, to which some see an allusion in 22:7, and Antioch in Syria is the most suggested place of origin. There are also those who do without the Q source, those who argue that Matthew was the first gospel and those who maintain apostolic authorship, with a date before 70."},"events":{"intro":"The gospels give no years, and the dates of Jesus' life are calculated from historical references. Matthew places the birth “in the days of King Herod” (2:1), who died around 4 BC; hence the approximate date of 6 to 4 BC. The public ministry is usually placed between c. AD 27/28 and 30/33, and the crucifixion, under Pilate's governorship, in 30 or 33. The timeline also shows the destruction of the temple, in 70, a point of reference for dating the book, and the two main proposals for its writing.","axis":[-40,100],"band":{"label":"Pilate's governorship of Judea","from":26,"to":36},"timeline":[{"label":"Herod the Great","from":-37,"to":-4,"kind":"pessoa","note":"reign over Judea; Jesus is born in his last years (2:1)"},{"label":"Birth of Jesus","from":-6,"to":-4,"kind":"evento","note":"in Bethlehem of Judea; approximate date"},{"label":"Archelaus","from":-4,"to":6,"kind":"pessoa","note":"rules Judea; for this reason Joseph takes the family to Galilee (2:22)"},{"label":"Herod Antipas","from":-4,"to":39,"kind":"pessoa","note":"tetrarch of Galilee; has John the Baptist killed (14:1–12)"},{"label":"Ministry of Jesus","from":27,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 27/28 to 30/33, depending on the chronology adopted"},{"label":"Crucifixion","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"in Jerusalem, under Pilate; the most defended dates are 30 and 33"},{"label":"Destruction of Jerusalem","from":70,"to":70,"kind":"evento","note":"the Roman army takes the city and burns the temple"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":50,"to":66,"kind":"escrita","note":"the apostle Matthew, before the war against Rome"},{"label":"Writing, according to most researchers","from":80,"to":90,"kind":"escrita","note":"after Mark and the destruction of the temple"}]},"context":"The story takes place in the land of Israel at the beginning of the 1st century, under Roman rule. Galilee was ruled by Herod Antipas, and Judea, from the year 6, by Roman prefects such as Pilate, who left the administration of the temple in Jerusalem to the high priests. In the Judaism of this period, groups with different readings of the Law lived side by side, such as Pharisees and Sadducees, and hopes for a Messiah of David's line were alive. For most scholars, the gospel was written after 70 for communities of Christians largely of Jewish origin, at a time when Judaism, without the temple, was reorganizing around the synagogues and the teachers of the Law; this would help explain the harshness of the debates with the Pharisees and expressions such as “their synagogues” (4:23; 10:17), alongside the final opening to all peoples (28:19).","places":["Bethlehem of Judea","Egypt","Nazareth","Galilee: Capernaum and the Sea of Galilee","Caesarea Philippi","Jericho","Jerusalem: the temple, the Mount of Olives, Gethsemane and Golgotha"],"themes":["the kingdom of heaven","fulfillment of the Scriptures","Jesus, Messiah and son of David","the Law and righteousness","discipleship and the church","mission to all nations"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"5:17","text":"Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish them, but to fulfill them."},{"ref":"16:15–16","text":"“But what about you?” Jesus asked. “Who do you say I am?” Simon Peter answered, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.”"},{"ref":"28:18–20","text":"Then Jesus came to them and said, “All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to Me. Therefore go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit, and teaching them to obey all that I have commanded you. And surely I am with you always, even to the end of the age.”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The count of years from the birth of Christ was calculated in the 6th century with a small error; that is why Jesus' birth falls a few years “before Christ.” Verses 17:21, 18:11 and 23:14 appear in many later manuscripts, but not in the oldest, in the SBLGNT Greek text used in the app or in the main text of the BSB, which mentions them only in a footnote; so they appear here without text. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"MRK":{"t":{"1":"Baptism, first disciples and healings in Capernaum","2":"The paralytic, Levi and the Sabbath question","3":"The choosing of the Twelve; the Beelzebul charge","4":"Parables of the seed and the storm calmed","5":"The Gerasene demoniac and Jairus's daughter","6":"Nazareth, the mission of the Twelve, the five thousand","7":"Tradition and purity; the Syrophoenician woman","8":"The four thousand; Peter confesses the Christ","9":"The transfiguration and the possessed boy","10":"Divorce, the rich man and blind Bartimaeus","11":"Entry into Jerusalem, the fig tree and the temple","12":"The tenants of the vineyard and debates in the temple","13":"Discourse on the end and watchfulness","14":"Anointing, supper, Gethsemane, Sanhedrin and Peter's denial","15":"Pilate, the crucifixion and the burial","16":"The empty tomb; the longer ending"},"about":{"title_orig":"Κατὰ Μᾶρκον","title_orig_xlit":"Kata Markon","summary":"Mark is the shortest of the gospels and tells the story of Jesus at a fast pace, marked by the repetition of “immediately” (in Greek, euthys). It does not narrate the birth: it begins with the preaching of John the Baptist and the baptism of Jesus and follows His ministry in Galilee, with healings, exorcisms, parables and conflicts with the religious leaders. At the center of the book, Peter recognizes Jesus as the Christ (8:29), and from then on Jesus announces three times that He will be handed over, killed and raised, while the disciples struggle to understand. The last week in Jerusalem takes up more than a third of the text (chs. 11–16). In the oldest manuscripts, the book ends at 16:8, with the women fleeing the empty tomb, seized with fear.","name":"The title “According to Mark” (Kata Markon) was given to the work when the gospels began to circulate together; the text itself does not say who wrote it. Mark (Marcus) was a very common Latin name, and tradition identifies him with John Mark, of Jerusalem, companion of Barnabas and Paul in Acts. The word “gospel” (euangelion, “good news”) appears already in the first sentence (1:1), and many scholars think it was from this use that it came to designate books like this one.","facts":[["Position","2nd book of the New Testament and the second of the four Gospels"],["Chapters","16"],["Verses","678"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","gospel: a brief and lively account of the activity, death and resurrection of Jesus"]],"structure":[{"title":"Beginning in Galilee","from":1,"to":3,"text":"John the Baptist, the baptism and the temptation; the call of the first disciples, healings in Capernaum, the first conflicts with the Pharisees and the choice of the Twelve."},{"title":"Parables and miracles","from":4,"to":5,"text":"Parables about the seed and the Kingdom, the calming of the storm, the demon-possessed man of Gerasa, the woman who touches Jesus' cloak and Jairus's daughter."},{"title":"Mission and misunderstanding","from":6,"to":8,"text":"The rejection at Nazareth, the sending of the Twelve, the death of John the Baptist, two feedings of the crowds and journeys to regions of Gentile majority, while the disciples are slow to understand (to 8:26)."},{"title":"On the way","from":8,"to":10,"text":"Peter's confession at Caesarea Philippi opens the central part: three announcements of the passion, the transfiguration and teachings on serving, up to the healing of blind Bartimaeus at Jericho."},{"title":"In Jerusalem","from":11,"to":13,"text":"The entry into the city, the fruitless fig tree, the cleansing of the temple, the debates with the leaders and the discourse on the destruction of the temple and the end."},{"title":"Passion and empty tomb","from":14,"to":16,"text":"The anointing at Bethany, the Last Supper, the arrest, the trials, the crucifixion, the burial and the announcement of the resurrection to the women; then follows the longer ending (16:9–20)."}],"extra":{"title":"The ending of Mark","text":"In the two oldest complete manuscripts of the New Testament, Sinaiticus and Vaticanus, both from the 4th century, Mark ends at 16:8: the women find the empty tomb, hear that Jesus has risen and flee without saying anything to anyone, “because they were afraid.” Most of the other manuscripts have a longer ending (16:9–20), with appearances of Jesus, the sending of the disciples and the ascension; some have a short ending, of one or two sentences, and others combine the two. The longer ending was already known in the 2nd century, since Irenaeus quotes it, and was read in the churches over the centuries; but most scholars consider it a later addition, because of the differences in vocabulary and style and because it summarizes episodes narrated in other gospels. Some think the author meant to end at 16:8, some suppose the original ending was lost and some defend the authenticity of the longer ending. The BSB includes 16:9–20 with a note on the manuscripts, and the SBLGNT Greek text prints it in double brackets."},"people":[{"name":"Jesus","chapters":"1–16","ch":1,"note":"the Nazarene who proclaims the coming of the Kingdom of God; called Son by the voice from heaven at the baptism (1:11) and, before the cross, by the Roman centurion (15:39)"},{"name":"John the Baptist","chapters":"1; 6","ch":1,"note":"preaches in the wilderness a baptism of repentance and baptizes Jesus in the Jordan; beheaded by order of Herod"},{"name":"Simon Peter","chapters":"1–16","ch":8,"note":"fisherman called among the first; confesses Jesus as the Christ and later denies Him in the high priest's courtyard; is remembered by name in 16:7"},{"name":"James and John","chapters":"1; 3; 9–10; 14","ch":10,"note":"sons of Zebedee, called Boanerges, “Sons of Thunder”; ask for the first places in glory (10:35–45)"},{"name":"The Twelve","chapters":"3; 6; 14","ch":3,"note":"chosen to be with Jesus and to be sent out to preach; in the account, they often do not understand their master"},{"name":"Herod Antipas and Herodias","chapters":"6","ch":6,"note":"the ruler of Galilee, called “king,” and the woman who asks for the head of John the Baptist"},{"name":"Jairus","chapters":"5","ch":5,"note":"synagogue leader who pleads with Jesus for his dying daughter"},{"name":"Bartimaeus","chapters":"10","ch":10,"note":"blind beggar of Jericho; after being healed, follows Jesus along the road"},{"name":"Judas Iscariot","chapters":"3; 14","ch":14,"note":"one of the Twelve; betrays Jesus in Gethsemane with a kiss"},{"name":"Pilate","chapters":"15","ch":15,"note":"Roman governor who questions Jesus as “King of the Jews” and hands Him over to be crucified"},{"name":"Joseph of Arimathea","chapters":"15","ch":15,"note":"member of the Council who asks for the body of Jesus and buries it"},{"name":"Mary Magdalene, Mary and Salome","chapters":"15–16","ch":16,"note":"watch the crucifixion from a distance, see where Jesus is buried and find the empty tomb"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book does not give the author's name. Papias of Hierapolis, at the beginning of the 2nd century, quoted by Eusebius, records what he had heard from an “elder”: Mark, who had been Peter's interpreter, wrote down carefully, though not in order, what he remembered of the Lord's words and deeds as Peter taught them. Irenaeus, Clement of Alexandria and other ancient writers repeat this link with Peter and place the work in Rome: for some, while the apostle was still alive; for others, soon after his death, in the 60s. This Mark is usually identified with the John Mark of Acts (12:12, 25) and with the “Mark” mentioned in 1 Peter 5:13.","academic":"Since the 19th century, most scholars have considered Mark the oldest of the gospels and one of the sources of Matthew and Luke, the so-called Markan priority: it is the shortest, has the simplest Greek, and almost all of its content reappears in the other two, often with polished wording. The most defended dates revolve around 65–75, near the Jewish war against Rome; chapter 13, with the warning “let the reader understand” (13:14), is read by some as reflecting the years before the destruction of the temple and by others as later than it. Rome remains the most cited place, but some propose Syria or Galilee. The link with Peter is accepted by some researchers and seen by others as a way of giving authority to the text; a minority, following the Griesbach hypothesis, considers Mark a summary of Matthew and Luke."},"events":{"intro":"Mark begins with Jesus already an adult and gives no years; the dates of His life are calculated from other texts and historical references. The public ministry is usually placed between c. AD 27/28 and 30/33, and the crucifixion, under Pilate, in 30 or 33. Since the book is generally dated near the Jewish war against Rome, the timeline highlights that period, together with Nero's persecution and the two main proposals for the writing.","axis":[-10,100],"band":{"label":"Jewish war against Rome","from":66,"to":73},"timeline":[{"label":"Herod Antipas","from":-4,"to":39,"kind":"pessoa","note":"rules Galilee; has John the Baptist beheaded (6:14–29)"},{"label":"Ministry of Jesus","from":27,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 27/28 to 30/33, depending on the chronology adopted"},{"label":"Crucifixion","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"in Jerusalem, under Pilate; the most defended dates are 30 and 33"},{"label":"Nero's persecution in Rome","from":64,"to":68,"kind":"evento","note":"after the fire of Rome, in 64; tradition places Peter's death in this period"},{"label":"Destruction of Jerusalem","from":70,"to":70,"kind":"evento","note":"the Roman army takes the city and burns the temple (cf. 13:1–2)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":60,"to":68,"kind":"escrita","note":"Mark, Peter's interpreter, in Rome"},{"label":"Writing, according to most researchers","from":65,"to":75,"kind":"escrita","note":"the first gospel, near the war and the fall of the temple"}]},"context":"Mark seems to write for readers unfamiliar with Jewish customs and with Aramaic: he explains the purity rules of the Pharisees (7:3–4) and translates expressions such as “Talitha koum” (5:41), “Ephphatha” (7:34) and “Abba” (14:36). He also uses several Latin words transcribed into Greek, linked to the army, coins and administration, such as “legion” and “centurion.” The action concentrates on Galilee and around its sea, with visits to lands of Gentile majority, such as the region of Tyre and Sidon and the Decapolis, before the going up to Jerusalem. If the book was written in Rome, as tradition says, its first readers had vivid memories of Nero's persecution and were receiving news of the war in Judea, which would help explain the insistence on suffering and on the disciples' faithfulness.","places":["the wilderness of Judea and the Jordan River","Galilee: Capernaum, Nazareth and the Sea of Galilee","the region of the Gerasenes and the Decapolis","the region of Tyre and Sidon","Caesarea Philippi","Jericho","Jerusalem: the temple, Bethany, Gethsemane and Golgotha"],"themes":["the Kingdom of God","the messianic secret","the suffering Son of Man","faith and the disciples' misunderstanding","following on the way of the cross","Jesus' authority"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:15","text":"“The time is fulfilled,” He said, “and the kingdom of God is near. Repent and believe in the gospel!”"},{"ref":"8:29","text":"“But what about you?” Jesus asked. “Who do you say I am?” Peter answered, “You are the Christ.”"},{"ref":"10:45","text":"For even the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.”"},{"ref":"15:39","text":"When the centurion standing there in front of Jesus saw how He had breathed His last, he said, “Truly this man was the Son of God!”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. In 1:1, the words “the Son of God” are missing in some ancient manuscripts; the BSB includes them, and the SBLGNT omits them. Verses 7:16, 9:44, 9:46, 11:26 and 15:28 appear in later manuscripts, but not in the oldest, in the SBLGNT or in the main text of the BSB, which mentions them only in a footnote; so they appear here without text. On 16:9–20, see “The ending of Mark.” The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"LUK":{"t":{"1":"Zechariah, Mary and the birth of John","2":"Birth of Jesus; the boy in the temple","3":"John's preaching, the baptism and the genealogy of Jesus","4":"The temptation and rejection at Nazareth","5":"The great catch, the paralytic and Levi","6":"The Twelve and the sermon on the plain","7":"The centurion, the widow of Nain and the forgiven woman","8":"The sower, the storm and Jairus's daughter","9":"Mission of the Twelve, transfiguration and departure for Jerusalem","10":"The seventy-two, the Samaritan, Martha and Mary","11":"Prayer, Beelzebul and woes against the Pharisees","12":"Against greed and worry; watchfulness","13":"Repentance, the narrow door and Jerusalem","14":"At table with Pharisees; the great banquet","15":"The lost sheep, coin and son","16":"The shrewd manager; the rich man and Lazarus","17":"Forgiveness, faith, the ten lepers and the kingdom","18":"Parables on prayer and the rich ruler","19":"Zacchaeus, the minas and the entry into Jerusalem","20":"Debates in the temple on authority and resurrection","21":"The poor widow; signs of the end","22":"Supper, arrest and Peter's denial","23":"Pilate, Herod, the cross and the burial","24":"The resurrection, Emmaus and the ascension"},"about":{"title_orig":"Κατὰ Λουκᾶν","title_orig_xlit":"Kata Loukan","summary":"Luke is the first part of a two-volume work that continues in the Acts of the Apostles, both dedicated to a certain Theophilus. The author says he has investigated everything carefully in order to write an orderly account (1:1–4) and places the story of Jesus within the framework of the Roman Empire, with mentions of emperors, governors and high priests. The book tells side by side the births of John the Baptist and of Jesus, follows the ministry in Galilee and devotes a long section to the journey to Jerusalem (9:51–19:27), where parables that only Luke records are concentrated, such as the Good Samaritan and the Prodigal Son. The poor, women, foreigners and those considered sinners are given prominence, as are prayer, joy and the work of the Holy Spirit. The gospel begins and ends in the temple in Jerusalem, after the appearances of the Risen One and the farewell at Bethany.","name":"The title “According to Luke” (Kata Loukan) was given to the work when the gospels began to circulate together and already appears in the oldest papyri that preserve titles. The author does not give his name, but addresses the book to “most excellent Theophilus” (1:3). Luke (Loukas) is the Greek form of a Latin name; Paul mentions a Luke among his co-workers and calls him “the beloved physician” (Colossians 4:14; see also Philemon 24 and 2 Timothy 4:11).","facts":[["Position","3rd book of the New Testament and the third of the four Gospels; first part of the work Luke–Acts"],["Chapters","24"],["Verses","1,151"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","gospel: an orderly account of the life and teachings of Jesus, opened with a prologue in the manner of Greek historians"]],"structure":[{"title":"Prologue and childhood","from":1,"to":2,"text":"The dedication to Theophilus (1:1–4); the announcements and births of John and of Jesus, narrated in parallel, the songs of Mary, Zechariah and Simeon, and Jesus at twelve years old in the temple."},{"title":"Preparation","from":3,"to":4,"text":"John's preaching, the baptism, Jesus' genealogy back to Adam, the temptation and the reading of Isaiah in the synagogue at Nazareth (4:16–30), which announces the program of the ministry."},{"title":"Ministry in Galilee","from":4,"to":9,"text":"Healings and calls, the Sermon on the Plain (6:20–49), the forgiven woman, the women who accompany Jesus, the mission of the Twelve, Peter's confession and the transfiguration (to 9:50)."},{"title":"The journey to Jerusalem","from":9,"to":19,"text":"From 9:51 on, Jesus resolves to go up to Jerusalem; in this long section are most of Luke's own parables, teachings on prayer, wealth and discipleship and encounters such as that with Zacchaeus (to 19:27)."},{"title":"In Jerusalem","from":19,"to":21,"text":"The entry into the city, the weeping over Jerusalem, the cleansing of the temple, the debates with the leaders and the discourse on the destruction of the city and the coming of the Son of Man."},{"title":"The passion","from":22,"to":23,"text":"The Last Supper, the prayer on the Mount of Olives, the arrest, the hearings before the Council, Pilate and Herod, the crucifixion and the burial."},{"title":"Resurrection and farewell","from":24,"to":24,"text":"The empty tomb, the encounter on the road to Emmaus, the appearance to the eleven in Jerusalem and the farewell at Bethany, which prepares the beginning of Acts."}],"extra":{"title":"What only Luke tells","text":"A considerable part of the gospel has no parallel in Matthew or Mark. Only Luke narrates, for example, the birth of John the Baptist, the visit of the shepherds, the boy Jesus in the temple, the son of the widow of Nain (7:11–17), the encounter with Zacchaeus (19:1–10) and the road to Emmaus (24:13–35), and only he has parables such as the Good Samaritan (10:25–37), the Prodigal Son (15:11–32) and the rich man and Lazarus (16:19–31). The first two chapters also preserve three songs that entered Christian prayer under Latin names: Mary's Magnificat (1:46–55), Zechariah's Benedictus (1:68–79) and Simeon's Nunc dimittis (2:29–32)."},"people":[{"name":"Theophilus","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"recipient of the work; perhaps a patron of high standing, perhaps a symbolic name, since it means “friend of God”"},{"name":"Zechariah and Elizabeth","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"an elderly couple of priestly family; parents of John the Baptist"},{"name":"Mary","chapters":"1–2; 8","ch":1,"note":"receives the announcement of the angel Gabriel, visits Elizabeth and treasures in her heart what happens to her son (2:19, 51)"},{"name":"Joseph","chapters":"1–2","ch":2,"note":"of the house of David; goes with Mary from Nazareth to Bethlehem because of the census"},{"name":"Simeon and Anna","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"a righteous man and an elderly prophetess who recognize the child in the temple"},{"name":"John the Baptist","chapters":"1; 3; 7","ch":3,"note":"son of Zechariah; preaches repentance at the Jordan and baptizes Jesus"},{"name":"Jesus","chapters":"1–24","ch":2,"note":"born in Bethlehem and raised in Nazareth; presented as Savior, Christ and Lord (2:11)"},{"name":"Simon Peter","chapters":"4–24","ch":5,"note":"fisherman called after the great catch; denies Jesus in the high priest's courtyard and receives an appearance of the Risen One (24:34)"},{"name":"Mary Magdalene, Joanna and Susanna","chapters":"8; 23–24","ch":8,"note":"women who follow Jesus and support Him out of their means; they are at the tomb on the morning of the resurrection"},{"name":"Martha and Mary","chapters":"10","ch":10,"note":"sisters who welcome Jesus into their home"},{"name":"Zacchaeus","chapters":"19","ch":19,"note":"chief tax collector of Jericho, who welcomes Jesus and gives away his goods"},{"name":"Herod Antipas","chapters":"3; 9; 13; 23","ch":23,"note":"tetrarch of Galilee; wants to see Jesus and questions Him during the trial"},{"name":"Pilate","chapters":"3; 13; 23","ch":23,"note":"Roman governor of Judea; declares he finds no guilt in Jesus, but hands Him over to be crucified"},{"name":"Cleopas","chapters":"24","ch":24,"note":"one of the two disciples who meet the Risen One on the road to Emmaus"}],"writing":{"tradition":"From the end of the 2nd century, with Irenaeus and the so-called Muratorian Canon, tradition attributes the third gospel and Acts to Luke, physician and traveling companion of Paul. He would have been a Christian of Gentile origin, since Colossians 4:10–14 mentions him apart from Paul's Jewish co-workers, and an ancient prologue says he was a native of Antioch in Syria. In the passages of Acts narrated in the first person plural (“we”), tradition sees the author himself alongside the apostle. Those who follow this reading usually date the gospel shortly before Acts, which ends with Paul under arrest in Rome around 62, that is, in the early 60s.","academic":"There is broad consensus that the same author wrote Luke and Acts, because of the style, the common dedication to Theophilus and the mention in Acts 1:1 of the “first book.” He is an educated writer, who moves through several registers of Greek. On the Synoptic Problem, the most accepted hypothesis is that he used Mark and the sayings collection Q, as well as material of his own; others think he also knew Matthew. As to the author, some researchers accept Luke, Paul's companion, and some see in him a Christian of a later generation, pointing to differences between the Paul of Acts and the Paul of the letters. The most cited date is c. 80–90, because the text seems to presuppose the siege and destruction of Jerusalem (19:43–44; 21:20–24); some argue for the 60s, and a minority places Luke–Acts in the early 2nd century."},"events":{"intro":"Luke is the evangelist who most ties the narrative to dates of Roman history. He places the announcement to Zechariah “in the time of Herod king of Judea” (1:5), associates the birth of Jesus with a census ordered by Caesar Augustus while Quirinius was governor of Syria (2:1–2) and dates the beginning of John's preaching “in the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar” (3:1), around 28/29. The census of Quirinius recorded by the historian Flavius Josephus is from AD 6, about ten years after Herod's death; the difference is explained in various ways, such as an earlier registration, another translation of 2:2 or an inaccuracy of the author. Jesus' ministry is usually placed between c. 27/28 and 30/33, and the crucifixion in 30 or 33.","axis":[-40,100],"band":{"label":"Reign of Tiberius","from":14,"to":37},"timeline":[{"label":"Herod the Great","from":-37,"to":-4,"kind":"pessoa","note":"king of Judea when John and Jesus are born (1:5)"},{"label":"Caesar Augustus","from":-27,"to":14,"kind":"pessoa","note":"emperor who orders the census (2:1)"},{"label":"Birth of Jesus","from":-6,"to":-4,"kind":"evento","note":"in Bethlehem; approximate date, still under Herod"},{"label":"Census of Quirinius","from":6,"to":6,"kind":"evento","note":"census of Judea reported by Josephus; compare 2:2"},{"label":"Preaching of John the Baptist","from":28,"to":29,"kind":"evento","note":"“in the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius” (3:1)"},{"label":"Ministry of Jesus","from":27,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 27/28 to 30/33, depending on the chronology adopted"},{"label":"Crucifixion","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"in Jerusalem, under Pilate; the most defended dates are 30 and 33"},{"label":"Destruction of Jerusalem","from":70,"to":70,"kind":"evento","note":"the Roman army surrounds and takes the city (cf. 21:20)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":59,"to":63,"kind":"escrita","note":"Luke, Paul's companion, shortly before Acts"},{"label":"Writing, according to most researchers","from":80,"to":90,"kind":"escrita","note":"after Mark and the destruction of Jerusalem"}]},"context":"Luke writes for readers of the Greco-Roman world and presents the story of Jesus as part of the history of the Empire: he mentions Augustus and Tiberius, the governors Quirinius and Pilate, the tetrarchs of Herod's family and the high priests Annas and Caiaphas (2:1–2; 3:1–2). The prologue (1:1–4) follows the conventions of Greek historians and technical writers, and the dedication to Theophilus recalls the custom of offering a work to a patron. The author knows the Septuagint well, and the first two chapters imitate the style of the narratives of the Hebrew Bible. The action moves from Galilee to Jerusalem, where the book begins and ends, and prepares the expansion narrated in Acts, with the message carried “to all nations, beginning at Jerusalem” (24:47).","places":["Jerusalem and the temple","Nazareth","Bethlehem","Galilee: Capernaum, Nain and the Lake of Gennesaret","Samaria","Jericho","Emmaus","Bethany"],"themes":["salvation for all","poor and rich","mercy and forgiveness","prayer","the Holy Spirit","joy and praise","women among Jesus' followers"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:10–11","text":"But the angel said to them, “Do not be afraid! For behold, I bring you good news of great joy that will be for all the people: Today in the city of David a Savior has been born to you. He is Christ the Lord!"},{"ref":"4:18–19","text":"“The Spirit of the Lord is on Me, because He has anointed Me to preach good news to the poor. He has sent Me to proclaim liberty to the captives and recovery of sight to the blind, to release the oppressed, to proclaim the year of the Lord’s favor.”"},{"ref":"15:7","text":"In the same way, I tell you that there will be more joy in heaven over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine righteous ones who do not need to repent."},{"ref":"19:10","text":"For the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. Verses 17:36 and 23:17 appear in later manuscripts, but not in the oldest, in the SBLGNT Greek text used in the app or in the main text of the BSB, which mentions them only in a footnote; so they appear here without text. Verses 22:43–44 (the angel and the sweat like drops of blood) and the words “Father, forgive them” in 23:34 are also missing in some important ancient manuscripts; the SBLGNT and the BSB keep them, noting the doubt. In 10:1 and 10:17, the BSB, like the SBLGNT, speaks of seventy-two sent out, and other manuscripts and translations of seventy. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"JHN":{"t":{"1":"The Word; John the Baptist and the first disciples","2":"The wedding at Cana and the temple","3":"Nicodemus and the new birth","4":"The Samaritan woman and the official's son","5":"Healing at Bethesda; the work of the Son","6":"The five thousand and the bread of life","7":"Jesus at the Feast of Tabernacles","8":"The adulterous woman; the light of the world","9":"The healing of the man born blind","10":"The good shepherd and the Feast of Dedication","11":"The raising of Lazarus","12":"Anointing, entry into Jerusalem and the hour","13":"The foot washing and the betrayal foretold","14":"The way to the Father and the Advocate","15":"The true vine; the hatred of the world","16":"The work of the Spirit; sorrow turned to joy","17":"Jesus prays for His disciples","18":"Arrest, Annas, Peter's denial and Pilate","19":"The condemnation, the crucifixion and the burial","20":"The empty tomb, Mary Magdalene and Thomas","21":"Appearance at Tiberias; Peter and the beloved disciple"},"about":{"title_orig":"Κατὰ Ἰωάννην","title_orig_xlit":"Kata Ioannen","summary":"John tells the story of Jesus in a way quite different from the other three gospels. It opens with a poetic prologue about the Word (in Greek, Logos), who was with God from the beginning and “became flesh” (1:14). The first half of the book is organized around signs, such as water turned into wine at Cana and the raising of Lazarus, followed by long dialogues and discourses in which Jesus speaks of Himself with images introduced by “I am.” The second half concentrates on the last night with the disciples, with the foot washing and the farewell discourses (chs. 13–17), and then on the passion and the resurrection. The book itself states its purpose: that its readers may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and have life in His name (20:31).","name":"The title “According to John” (Kata Ioannen) was given to the book when the gospels began to circulate together and already appears, in the form “Gospel according to John,” in a papyrus from around the year 200 (P66). The text does not give the author's name, but the last chapter links it to “the disciple whom Jesus loved,” who “testifies to these things and who has written them down” (21:20, 24). Tradition identified this disciple with John son of Zebedee, one of the Twelve.","facts":[["Position","4th book of the New Testament and the last of the four Gospels"],["Chapters","21"],["Verses","879"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","gospel: a narrative of Jesus' signs and discourses, with strong theological reflection"]],"structure":[{"title":"Prologue and first disciples","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The hymn to the Word (1:1–18), the testimony of John the Baptist, who points to Jesus as the Lamb of God, and the call of Andrew, Peter, Philip and Nathanael."},{"title":"Signs and discourses","from":2,"to":12,"text":"Signs and dialogues in Cana, Jerusalem, Samaria and Galilee, across several Jewish feasts; opposition grows until the raising of Lazarus and the decision to kill Jesus, and the part ends with the announcement: “The hour has come” (12:23)."},{"title":"The farewell","from":13,"to":17,"text":"At the Last Supper, Jesus washes the disciples' feet, announces the betrayal and the denial, gives the commandment of love, promises the Advocate and prays for His own (ch. 17)."},{"title":"Passion and death","from":18,"to":19,"text":"The arrest in the garden beyond the Kidron, the hearings before Annas and Pilate, the crucifixion and the burial by Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus."},{"title":"Resurrection","from":20,"to":20,"text":"Mary Magdalene at the tomb, the appearances to the disciples and to Thomas and the book's first conclusion (20:30–31)."},{"title":"Epilogue","from":21,"to":21,"text":"The appearance by the Sea of Tiberias, Jesus' dialogue with Peter and the final note about the beloved disciple and his testimony."}],"extra":{"title":"John and the Synoptics","text":"Matthew, Mark and Luke are called Synoptics because, read side by side, they show the same overall view; John follows another path, and most of his content has no parallel in the other three. In John, Jesus goes up to Jerusalem several times and passes through at least three Passovers (2:13; 6:4; 11:55); the cleansing of the temple comes at the beginning (ch. 2), not in the last week; there are no exorcisms or parables in the style of the Synoptics; and, instead of short sayings, there are long discourses. Jesus speaks of Himself with seven images introduced by “I am”: the bread of life (6:35), the light of the world (8:12), the gate (10:9), the good shepherd (10:11), the resurrection and the life (11:25), the way, the truth and the life (14:6) and the true vine (15:1)."},"people":[{"name":"Jesus","chapters":"1–21","ch":1,"note":"the Word who became flesh; called Lamb of God, Son of God, King of Israel and, by Thomas, “My Lord and my God” (20:28)"},{"name":"John the Baptist","chapters":"1; 3; 5; 10","ch":1,"note":"a witness who points to Jesus as the Lamb of God (1:29) and says Jesus must increase and he must decrease (3:30)"},{"name":"Simon Peter","chapters":"1; 6; 13; 18; 20–21","ch":21,"note":"denies Jesus three times and, by the Sea of Tiberias, is questioned three times about his love"},{"name":"The mother of Jesus","chapters":"2; 19","ch":2,"note":"never called by name in John; she is at the wedding in Cana and by the cross"},{"name":"Nicodemus","chapters":"3; 7; 19","ch":3,"note":"Pharisee and leader of the Jews who seeks Jesus by night and later helps bury Him"},{"name":"The Samaritan woman","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"talks with Jesus by Jacob's well and leads her people to know Him"},{"name":"Martha, Mary and Lazarus","chapters":"11–12","ch":11,"note":"siblings from Bethany, friends of Jesus; Lazarus is called out of the tomb"},{"name":"The beloved disciple","chapters":"13; 19–21","ch":13,"note":"an unnamed disciple, close to Jesus at the supper, by the cross and at the tomb; the book presents him as its witness"},{"name":"Thomas","chapters":"11; 14; 20–21","ch":20,"note":"called Didymus; doubts the resurrection until he sees Jesus"},{"name":"Judas Iscariot","chapters":"6; 12–13; 18","ch":13,"note":"one of the Twelve; leaves the supper to betray Jesus"},{"name":"Annas and Caiaphas","chapters":"11; 18","ch":18,"note":"the former high priest and his son-in-law, high priest “that year”"},{"name":"Pilate","chapters":"18–19","ch":18,"note":"Roman governor; talks with Jesus about kingship and truth and hands Him over to be crucified"},{"name":"Mary Magdalene","chapters":"19–20","ch":20,"note":"stands by the cross and is the first to see the Risen One, in the garden"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book presents itself as the testimony of “the disciple whom Jesus loved” (21:20–24). From the end of the 2nd century, with Irenaeus, tradition identifies this disciple with the apostle John son of Zebedee and holds that he wrote the gospel in Ephesus, in his old age, after the other three; Irenaeus says he heard memories of him from Polycarp, a disciple of John. Clement of Alexandria called it a “spiritual gospel,” written to complete the others. On this reading, the work is dated to the end of the 1st century, around 90–100; some defenders of apostolic authorship propose dates before 70.","academic":"Johannine authorship is a much-debated question. The Greek of the book is simple, but with its own theological vocabulary, and many scholars see signs of more than one stage of composition: in 14:31, Jesus says “let us go on from here,” but the discourse continues to chapter 17, and chapter 21 seems to have been added after the conclusion of 20:30–31. A frequent hypothesis is that the book arose from the tradition linked to the beloved disciple and was written and revised in the circle of his followers, the so-called Johannine community. The beloved disciple is identified by some with John son of Zebedee; by others, with the “elder John” mentioned by Papias or with another disciple from Jerusalem. The most accepted date is c. 90–100, and papyrus P52, with verses of chapter 18, is usually dated to the first half of the 2nd century, although some propose later dates. It is also debated whether the author knew the Synoptics or used independent traditions."},"events":{"intro":"John gives no years, but mentions several Jewish feasts, among them three Passovers (2:13; 6:4; 11:55), which suggests a ministry of two to three years. The remark that the temple had been under construction for forty-six years (2:20) points to c. 27/28, since Herod's rebuilding began around 20/19 BC. John places the crucifixion on the day of Preparation for the Passover (19:14), which, combined with astronomical calculations of the Jewish calendar, favors the dates 30 or 33. All dates are approximate.","axis":[-25,150],"band":{"label":"Temple rebuilt by Herod","from":-20,"to":70},"timeline":[{"label":"Caiaphas","from":18,"to":36,"kind":"pessoa","note":"high priest; his father-in-law Annas had been before him (18:13)"},{"label":"Pilate","from":26,"to":36,"kind":"pessoa","note":"Roman governor of Judea (18–19)"},{"label":"Ministry of Jesus","from":27,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 27/28 to 30/33; in John, at least three Passovers"},{"label":"Crucifixion","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"on the day of Preparation for the Passover (19:14); the most defended dates are 30 and 33"},{"label":"Destruction of Jerusalem","from":70,"to":70,"kind":"evento","note":"the Roman army burns the temple"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":90,"to":100,"kind":"escrita","note":"the apostle John, in Ephesus, at the end of his life"},{"label":"Final form, according to most researchers","from":90,"to":110,"kind":"escrita","note":"perhaps after more than one stage of composition"},{"label":"Papyrus P52","from":100,"to":150,"kind":"evento","note":"fragment with 18:31–33, 37–38; paleographic date debated"}]},"context":"Much of the narrative takes place in Judea and Jerusalem, around the temple and the Jewish feasts: Passover, the Feast of Tabernacles (ch. 7) and the Feast of Dedication, in winter (10:22). The author knows the city closely and mentions places such as the Pool of Bethesda, with its five covered colonnades (5:2), whose remains have been found by archaeologists, and the Pool of Siloam (9:7). The book often calls Jesus' opponents “the Jews” and speaks of the risk of being put out of the synagogue (9:22; 16:2); many scholars read this as reflecting conflicts between the author's community and Jewish leaders at the end of the 1st century, and not as a reference to the Jewish people as a whole, to whom Jesus Himself and His disciples belong. Tradition links the gospel to Ephesus, a Greek city of Asia Minor where philosophical ideas about the Logos circulated, a term the prologue uses in its own sense and that also echoes the first words of Genesis.","places":["Bethany beyond the Jordan (in other manuscripts, Bethabara)","Cana in Galilee","Samaria: Sychar and Jacob's well","Capernaum and the Sea of Tiberias","Bethany, the village of Lazarus","Jerusalem: the temple, the Pools of Bethesda and Siloam, the garden beyond the Kidron, Gabbatha and Golgotha"],"themes":["the Word who became flesh","believing and having life","signs and glory","light and darkness","truth and testimony","love for one another","the Spirit, the Advocate"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:1","text":"In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God."},{"ref":"1:14","text":"The Word became flesh and made His dwelling among us. We have seen His glory, the glory of the one and only Son from the Father, full of grace and truth."},{"ref":"3:16","text":"For God so loved the world that He gave His one and only Son, that everyone who believes in Him shall not perish but have eternal life."},{"ref":"20:31","text":"But these are written so that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that by believing you may have life in His name."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The passage of the woman caught in adultery (7:53–8:11) is not in the oldest known manuscripts, and in later manuscripts it appears in different places, sometimes after Luke 21:38; most scholars consider it an addition to the text of John, although many see in it an ancient tradition about Jesus. The BSB includes it with a note, and the SBLGNT does not, so these verses have no Greek original in the app. The end of 5:3 and 5:4, about the angel who stirred the water of the pool, are not in the SBLGNT or in the main text of the BSB. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"ACT":{"t":{"1":"The ascension and the choice of Matthias","2":"Pentecost: the Spirit and Peter's sermon","3":"Healing of the lame man and the temple sermon","4":"Peter and John before the council; everything in common","5":"Ananias and Sapphira; the apostles jailed and freed","6":"The Seven chosen; Stephen is arrested","7":"Stephen's speech and death","8":"Scattering; Philip, Samaria and the Ethiopian eunuch","9":"Conversion of Saul; Peter at Lydda and Joppa","10":"Peter and the centurion Cornelius","11":"Peter explains himself; the church in Antioch","12":"James is killed; Peter is freed; Herod dies","13":"Barnabas and Saul sent out; Pisidian Antioch","14":"Iconium, Lystra, Derbe and the return to Antioch","15":"The council in Jerusalem on the Gentiles","16":"Timothy, the Macedonian call and Philippi","17":"Thessalonica, Berea and the speech in Athens","18":"Corinth, Aquila and Priscilla; Apollos","19":"Paul in Ephesus and the riot of the silversmiths","20":"Eutychus at Troas; farewell at Miletus","21":"Journey to Jerusalem and arrest in the temple","22":"Paul tells the people of his conversion","23":"Before the council; transfer to Caesarea","24":"Paul before the governor Felix","25":"Paul appeals to Caesar before Festus","26":"Paul's defense before Agrippa","27":"The voyage to Rome and the shipwreck","28":"Malta and the arrival in Rome"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πράξεις Ἀποστόλων","title_orig_xlit":"Praxeis Apostolon","summary":"The Acts of the Apostles continues the Gospel of Luke and narrates about thirty years of the Christian movement, from Jesus' departure in Jerusalem to the arrival of Paul, a prisoner, in Rome. The plan appears right at the beginning: the disciples would be witnesses “in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth” (1:8). The first part follows the community in Jerusalem, led by Peter, with Pentecost, the death of Stephen and the welcome of the first non-Jews, such as the centurion Cornelius. From chapter 13 on, the focus shifts to Paul and his journeys through Asia Minor and Greece, up to his arrest in Jerusalem, the trials before Roman authorities and the sea voyage to Rome. Throughout the book, the Holy Spirit drives the expansion, and speeches by Peter, Stephen and Paul present the message to different audiences.","name":"The title “Acts of the Apostles” (Praxeis Apostolon) is not part of the text and was already in circulation at the end of the 2nd century; praxeis, “acts” or “deeds,” was the name of a kind of ancient account of the actions of notable figures. In practice, the book deals mainly with two apostles, Peter and Paul, and Paul is called an apostle only in 14:4 and 14:14, together with Barnabas. The author again addresses Theophilus and calls the gospel “the first book” (1:1).","facts":[["Position","5th book of the New Testament, right after the Gospels; second part of the work Luke–Acts"],["Chapters","28"],["Verses","1,007"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","historical narrative, in the mold of ancient historiography, with many speeches"]],"structure":[{"title":"Jerusalem","from":1,"to":7,"text":"The ascension, the choice of Matthias, Pentecost and Peter's speech, the life of the community, the conflicts with the temple authorities, the choice of the Seven and the death of Stephen."},{"title":"Judea, Samaria and the first Gentiles","from":8,"to":12,"text":"With the scattering, Philip preaches in Samaria and baptizes an Ethiopian; Saul is converted on the road to Damascus; Peter baptizes Cornelius; in Antioch a church with many Gentiles arises, where the disciples are first called Christians (11:26)."},{"title":"First journey and the Jerusalem council","from":13,"to":15,"text":"Barnabas and Saul set out from Antioch for Cyprus and southern Asia Minor; back again, the demand to circumcise the Gentiles is debated by the apostles and elders in Jerusalem (ch. 15)."},{"title":"Second and third journeys","from":15,"to":21,"text":"Paul takes the message to Macedonia and Greece (Philippi, Thessalonica, Athens and Corinth), spends about three years in Ephesus, says farewell to the elders at Miletus and goes up to Jerusalem (15:36–21:16)."},{"title":"Paul a prisoner","from":21,"to":26,"text":"Arrested in the temple, Paul defends himself before the people, the Council, the governors Felix and Festus and King Agrippa, and appeals to Caesar."},{"title":"The voyage to Rome","from":27,"to":28,"text":"The crossing, the shipwreck at Malta and the arrival in Rome, where Paul spends two years in a rented house, preaching “with all boldness and without hindrance” (28:31)."}],"extra":{"title":"The “we” passages and the speeches","text":"In four passages, the narrative suddenly shifts from the third person to the first person plural, as in “we got ready to leave for Macedonia” (16:10–17; 20:5–15; 21:1–18; 27:1–28:16). Tradition sees in this the author himself traveling with Paul; other scholars think he drew on the diary of a traveling companion or used a literary device typical of sea-voyage accounts. Another mark of the book are the speeches, which take up nearly a third of the text: Peter's at Pentecost (2:14–40), Stephen's (7:2–53) and Paul's at the Areopagus in Athens (17:22–31) are among the best known. In the manner of ancient historians, they sum up the message addressed to each audience: Jews of Judea, Jews of the Diaspora and pagans."},"people":[{"name":"Peter","chapters":"1–12; 15","ch":2,"note":"spokesman of the apostles in Jerusalem; preaches at Pentecost, is arrested by the authorities and baptizes Cornelius and his household"},{"name":"John","chapters":"3–4; 8","ch":3,"note":"accompanies Peter in the temple, before the Council and in Samaria"},{"name":"Stephen","chapters":"6–7","ch":7,"note":"one of the Seven chosen to serve at tables; stoned to death after a long speech"},{"name":"Philip","chapters":"6; 8; 21","ch":8,"note":"another of the Seven; preaches in Samaria, baptizes the Ethiopian eunuch and later lives in Caesarea"},{"name":"Saul (Paul)","chapters":"7–28","ch":9,"note":"Pharisee from Tarsus who persecutes the Christians, is converted on the road to Damascus and becomes the main missionary among the Gentiles"},{"name":"Barnabas","chapters":"4; 9; 11–15","ch":13,"note":"Levite from Cyprus; introduces Saul to the apostles and accompanies him on the first journey"},{"name":"Cornelius","chapters":"10–11","ch":10,"note":"Roman centurion in Caesarea, a God-fearer; with his household, receives the Spirit and baptism"},{"name":"Herod Agrippa I","chapters":"12","ch":12,"note":"the “King Herod” who has James, John's brother, killed and Peter arrested; dies in Caesarea"},{"name":"James","chapters":"12; 15; 21","ch":15,"note":"leader of the church in Jerusalem, identified with the “Lord's brother” of Galatians 1:19"},{"name":"Silas and Timothy","chapters":"15–20","ch":16,"note":"Paul's companions on the second journey"},{"name":"Lydia","chapters":"16","ch":16,"note":"dealer in purple cloth from Thyatira; welcomes Paul in Philippi"},{"name":"Aquila and Priscilla","chapters":"18","ch":18,"note":"Jewish couple expelled from Rome; work with Paul in Corinth and instruct Apollos in Ephesus"},{"name":"Felix, Festus and Agrippa","chapters":"23–26","ch":24,"note":"the Roman governors of Judea and King Agrippa II, before whom Paul defends himself in Caesarea"}],"writing":{"tradition":"From the end of the 2nd century, with Irenaeus, the so-called Muratorian Canon and Clement of Alexandria, tradition attributes Acts to Luke, the physician and companion of Paul, author also of the third gospel. The passages narrated in “we” would be the author's own memories. Since the book ends with Paul two years in Rome, without telling the outcome of the trial or his death, many who follow this reading date the work around 62, when the final events would still be under way.","academic":"That Luke and Acts have the same author is practically a consensus. Most scholars date Acts after the gospel, c. 80–90, and understand the open ending as a literary choice: the message reaches Rome, and the program of 1:8 is fulfilled. Some researchers keep the date of c. 62, and a minority proposes c. 100–130, pointing to possible contacts with the works of Flavius Josephus. The historical value of the book is also debated: many details about cities, titles of officials and sea routes are confirmed by inscriptions and other sources, such as the proconsul Gallio in Corinth (18:12); on the other hand, there are differences from Paul's letters, for example in the number of visits to Jerusalem and in the account of the meeting of chapter 15 compared with Galatians 2. The speeches are usually seen as compositions of the author, as was common in ancient historiography, perhaps based on earlier traditions."},"events":{"intro":"Acts gives no years, but mentions people known from other sources, which makes it possible to build an approximate chronology. The key piece is Gallio, proconsul of Achaia, before whom Paul appears in Corinth (18:12): an inscription found at Delphi places his term c. 51–52. Other points of support are the death of Herod Agrippa I, in 44 (ch. 12), the expulsion of the Jews from Rome by Claudius, c. 49 (18:2), and the change of governors of Judea, from Felix to Festus, c. 59/60 (24:27). The dates of Paul's journeys vary by a few years among authors.","axis":[25,100],"band":{"label":"Reign of Claudius","from":41,"to":54},"timeline":[{"label":"Pentecost and the church in Jerusalem","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"shortly after the crucifixion, in 30 or 33 (chs. 1–5)"},{"label":"Death of Stephen and conversion of Paul","from":33,"to":36,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate dates (chs. 7–9)"},{"label":"Herod Agrippa I","from":41,"to":44,"kind":"pessoa","note":"king of Judea; dies in Caesarea in 44 (12:20–23)"},{"label":"Paul's first journey","from":46,"to":48,"kind":"evento","note":"Cyprus and southern Asia Minor (chs. 13–14)"},{"label":"Jerusalem council","from":48,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 48/49 (ch. 15)"},{"label":"Paul's second journey","from":49,"to":52,"kind":"evento","note":"Macedonia and Greece; in Corinth, before Gallio (15:36–18:22)"},{"label":"Gallio, proconsul of Achaia","from":51,"to":52,"kind":"pessoa","note":"date fixed by an inscription at Delphi (18:12)"},{"label":"Paul's third journey","from":53,"to":57,"kind":"evento","note":"about three years in Ephesus (18:23–21:16)"},{"label":"Imprisonment in Jerusalem and Caesarea","from":57,"to":59,"kind":"evento","note":"two years under the governor Felix (24:27)"},{"label":"Voyage and imprisonment in Rome","from":59,"to":62,"kind":"evento","note":"shipwreck at Malta; two years in a rented house (28:30)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":62,"to":63,"kind":"escrita","note":"Luke, right after the final events"},{"label":"Writing, according to most researchers","from":80,"to":90,"kind":"escrita","note":"after the Gospel of Luke"}]},"context":"Acts takes place in the eastern Mediterranean under the Roman Empire, at a time of patrolled roads and busy sea routes, which made journeys like Paul's possible. The Jewish communities scattered through the cities, with their synagogues and their non-Jewish sympathizers, the “God-fearers,” were the first point of contact for the preaching. The book shows the Christians before very different bodies: the Council and the high priests in Jerusalem, magistrates of the Roman colony of Philippi, Epicurean and Stoic philosophers in Athens, the city assembly of Ephesus, governors and kings in Caesarea. Paul appears as a Jew from Tarsus and a Roman citizen by birth (22:25–28), which weighs in his trial and in his appeal to Caesar. A recurring motif is that the Roman authorities find in the Christians no crime deserving death (18:14–15; 26:31–32).","places":["Jerusalem","Samaria","Damascus","Antioch in Syria","Cyprus and Asia Minor: Pisidian Antioch, Iconium, Lystra and Derbe","Macedonia: Philippi, Thessalonica and Berea","Greece: Athens and Corinth","Ephesus and Miletus","Caesarea","Malta","Rome"],"themes":["the Holy Spirit","witness to the ends of the earth","Jews and Gentiles","the word that grows","the resurrection of Jesus","persecution and courage","the life of the community"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:8","text":"But you will receive power when the Holy Spirit comes upon you, and you will be My witnesses in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.”"},{"ref":"2:42","text":"They devoted themselves to the apostles’ teaching and to the fellowship, to the breaking of bread and to prayer."},{"ref":"10:34–35","text":"Then Peter began to speak: “I now truly understand that God does not show favoritism, but welcomes those from every nation who fear Him and do what is right."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. Verses 8:37, 15:34, 24:7 and 28:29 appear in later manuscripts, but not in the oldest, in the SBLGNT Greek text used in the app or in the main text of the BSB, which mentions them only in a footnote; so they appear here without text. Acts also circulated in a longer textual form, called “Western” and known above all from Codex Bezae, from around the year 400, with about 8 to 10% more text; the SBLGNT follows the shorter form. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"ROM":{"t":{"1":"Greeting; the gospel and human idolatry","2":"God's impartial judgment; circumcision of the heart","3":"All have sinned; righteousness through faith in Christ","4":"The example of Abraham: faith and righteousness","5":"Peace with God; Adam and Christ","6":"Baptism, grace and serving righteousness","7":"The Law and the inner conflict","8":"The Spirit, hope and unconquerable love","9":"Paul's sorrow; promise and election","10":"Righteousness by faith, within everyone's reach","11":"Israel not rejected; the olive tree and the branches","12":"A renewed mind, gifts and sincere love","13":"The authorities and love of neighbor","14":"Food, holy days and the weaker brother","15":"Accept one another; travel plans","16":"Phoebe, greetings and final warnings"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Ῥωμαίους","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Rhomaious","summary":"Romans is Paul's longest letter and his most systematic. Written to a church he did not yet know, it presents his gospel as “the power of God for salvation to everyone who believes, first to the Jew, then to the Greek” (1:16). Chapters 1 to 8 develop the argument: Jews and non-Jews are equally under sin, God makes righteous those who believe, as He did with Abraham, and gives a new life, led by the Spirit. Chapters 9 to 11 face the question of Israel's place, since most of Paul's people had not accepted Jesus as Messiah. The final part deals with life in community, the relationship with the authorities and living together among those who think differently about food and holy days, and ends with Paul's travel plans and a long list of greetings.","name":"The title Pros Rhomaious, “To the Romans,” was given when Paul's letters began to circulate as a collection; in the text, Paul addresses “all in Rome” (1:7). The letter opens the collection not because it is the oldest, but because it is the longest: in the canon, the letters to churches come before the letters to individuals, and within each group the order follows, in general, the length, from longest to shortest.","facts":[["Position","6th book of the New Testament; 1st of Paul's letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","16"],["Verses","433"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter, with a long theological exposition"]],"structure":[{"title":"Opening and theme","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Paul introduces himself, speaks of his long-held desire to visit Rome and announces the subject: in the gospel the righteousness of God is revealed, received by faith (1:1–17)."},{"title":"All under sin","from":1,"to":3,"text":"The peoples exchanged the Creator for idols, and those who judge others do the same; Jews and Greeks stand equally before God's judgment (1:18–3:20)."},{"title":"Justified by faith","from":3,"to":5,"text":"God freely makes righteous those who believe in Jesus; Abraham is the example, and Christ undoes what began with Adam (3:21–5:21)."},{"title":"New life","from":6,"to":8,"text":"United with Christ through baptism, those who believe die to sin; the Law is good, but does not set free, and the one who sets free is the Spirit, who makes them children of God and sustains them in hope."},{"title":"Israel in God's plan","from":9,"to":11,"text":"The refusal of much of Israel does not cancel the promises: there is a remnant, the Gentiles have been grafted into the olive tree, and Paul hopes that “all Israel” will be saved."},{"title":"The life of the community","from":12,"to":15,"text":"Worship as the offering of one's whole life, the gifts, love without hypocrisy, the authorities and the duty to welcome those with different convictions (12:1–15:13)."},{"title":"Plans and greetings","from":15,"to":16,"text":"Paul explains his work among the Gentiles, the trip to Jerusalem with the collection and the plan to go on to Spain, and sends greetings to dozens of people (15:14–16:27)."}],"extra":{"title":"Dialogue with an imaginary opponent","text":"In several passages, Paul argues in the style of the diatribe, common among the teachers of philosophy of the time: he gives voice to an imaginary interlocutor, raises objections and answers them. Hence the recurring questions (“What then shall we say?”) followed by the emphatic denial “Certainly not!” (3:5–6; 6:1–2; 6:15; 7:7; 9:14). Romans is also the letter of Paul with the most explicit quotations of Israel's Scriptures, above all from Isaiah, the Psalms and Deuteronomy."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1; 15","ch":1,"note":"writes to a church he did not found, asking its support to take the gospel as far as Spain"},{"name":"Abraham","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"example of one who was counted righteous for having believed, even before being circumcised"},{"name":"Adam","chapters":"5","ch":5,"note":"set in parallel with Christ: through one man sin entered, through another came justification"},{"name":"Phoebe","chapters":"16","ch":16,"note":"servant (in Greek, diakonos) of the church in Cenchrea, the port of Corinth; commended to the Romans, she probably carried the letter"},{"name":"Prisca and Aquila","chapters":"16","ch":16,"note":"a couple of Paul's fellow workers, back in Rome; a church meets in their house"},{"name":"Andronicus and Junia","chapters":"16","ch":16,"note":"Paul's kinsmen and fellow prisoners, “outstanding among the apostles” (16:7)"},{"name":"Timothy, Tertius, Gaius and Erastus","chapters":"16","ch":16,"note":"send greetings from Corinth: Tertius is the one who wrote the letter (16:22), Gaius is Paul's host and Erastus is the city treasurer"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The early Church never doubted that Romans was by Paul. The letter itself indicates the occasion: Paul is about to take to Jerusalem the collection of the churches of Macedonia and Achaia (15:25–26), commends Phoebe, of Cenchrea (16:1), and sends greetings from Gaius and Erastus, linked to Corinth (16:23). For this reason it is understood that he wrote in Corinth, during the three months Acts places in Greece (Acts 20:2–3), around 56–57. Tertius, who identifies himself as the one who wrote the letter (16:22), would be the secretary to whom Paul dictated it.","academic":"Romans is among the seven letters that practically all scholars attribute to Paul, alongside 1 and 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon. The proposed dates generally fall between 55 and 58. The debates are about other points. Chapter 16, with more than twenty names in a church Paul had not visited, led some to think of a note originally sent to Ephesus, a hypothesis less followed today. The final doxology (16:25–27) appears in different positions in the manuscripts. And the purpose of the letter is debated: to present Paul's gospel to a church he did not found, to prepare the mission to Spain, to ask for prayers for the delivery of the collection in Jerusalem or to deal with life together between Christians of Jewish and Gentile origin; probably a little of each."},"events":{"intro":"The letter gives no dates, but fits into the itinerary of Acts: Paul writes it shortly before taking the collection to Jerusalem, where he would be arrested, and only reaches Rome some years later, as a prisoner. The chronology is the same as the one used for Acts; the dates are approximate and vary by a few years among authors.","axis":[30,70],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Conversion of Paul","from":33,"to":36,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate date (Acts 9)"},{"label":"Claudius expels Jews from Rome","from":49,"to":49,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 49; Aquila and Priscilla go to Corinth (Acts 18:2)"},{"label":"Paul's third journey","from":53,"to":57,"kind":"evento","note":"about three years in Ephesus; then Macedonia and Greece"},{"label":"Writing of the letter","from":56,"to":57,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Corinth, before the journey with the collection to Jerusalem"},{"label":"Imprisonment in Jerusalem and Caesarea","from":57,"to":59,"kind":"evento","note":"Acts 21–26"},{"label":"Paul a prisoner in Rome","from":60,"to":62,"kind":"evento","note":"two years in a rented house (Acts 28:30)"},{"label":"Fire of Rome and Nero's persecution","from":64,"to":64,"kind":"evento","note":"according to tradition, Peter and Paul die in Rome at this time"}]},"context":"Rome was the capital of the empire and the largest city of the Mediterranean, with a population estimated between a few hundred thousand and a million inhabitants. It had a large Jewish community, with several synagogues, and it was in this setting that faith in Jesus arrived early, brought by travelers and not by a known apostle; Paul did not found this church (15:20–22). The historian Suetonius tells that the emperor Claudius expelled from the city the Jews who were in constant tumult “at the instigation of Chrestus,” a report many link to disputes about Christ and place around 49. With the return of those expelled, after Claudius's death in 54, the churches of the city, which met in houses (16:5), may have come to have a majority of non-Jewish origin, a possible background for the counsel about the “strong” and the “weak” (chs. 14–15).","places":["Rome","Corinth and the port of Cenchrea","Macedonia and Achaia","Jerusalem","Illyricum","Spain"],"themes":["the righteousness of God","faith and justification","sin and grace","the Law","life in the Spirit","Israel and the Gentiles","life in community"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:16–17","text":"I am not ashamed of the gospel, because it is the power of God for salvation to everyone who believes, first to the Jew, then to the Greek. For the gospel reveals the righteousness of God that comes by faith from start to finish, just as it is written: “The righteous will live by faith.”"},{"ref":"3:23–24","text":"for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, and are justified freely by His grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus."},{"ref":"8:38–39","text":"For I am convinced that neither death nor life, neither angels nor principalities, neither the present nor the future, nor any powers, neither height nor depth, nor anything else in all creation, will be able to separate us from the love of God that is in Christ Jesus our Lord."},{"ref":"12:1–2","text":"Therefore I urge you, brothers, on account of God’s mercy, to offer your bodies as living sacrifices, holy and pleasing to God, which is your spiritual service of worship. Do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind. Then you will be able to test and approve what is the good, pleasing, and perfect will of God."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The final doxology (16:25–27) appears in different places in the manuscripts (after 14:23, after 15:33 or at the end of the letter) and is missing in some; the SBLGNT, the Greek text used in the app, leaves it out of the text, so these verses appear without the Greek original, while the BSB keeps them at the end of the letter. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"1CO":{"t":{"1":"Greeting; divisions and the message of the cross","2":"God's wisdom revealed by the Spirit","3":"Paul and Apollos; the temple of God","4":"Servants of Christ; Paul as a father","5":"Immorality in the church; the old leaven","6":"Lawsuits among believers; the body as a temple","7":"Marriage, singleness and widowhood","8":"Food offered to idols; knowledge and love","9":"Paul gives up his rights","10":"Lessons from the wilderness; the table of the Lord","11":"Head coverings in worship; the Lord's Supper","12":"Many gifts, one body","13":"Love, the most excellent way","14":"Prophecy, tongues and order in worship","15":"The resurrection of Christ and of the dead","16":"The collection, travel plans and greetings"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Κορινθίους Αʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Korinthious A","summary":"In 1 Corinthians, Paul writes from Ephesus to a church he himself had founded and that was going through many problems at once. Some said “I follow Paul,” others “I follow Apollos” or “I follow Cephas” (1:12); there was a case of incestuous union, lawsuits between brothers in the courts, doubts about marriage and about eating meat offered to idols, disorder at the Lord's Supper and disputes over spiritual gifts. Paul deals with each question from the standpoint of the cross of Christ, which contradicts the wisdom and prestige valued by the city, and of love, which puts the good of the other above one's own freedom. Chapter 13, on love, is among Paul's best-known texts, and chapter 15 is his longest exposition on the resurrection of Jesus and of the dead.","name":"The title “First to the Corinthians” (Pros Korinthious A) was given when Paul's letters were gathered, to distinguish this one from the other letter to the same church, which comes right after it in the canon. The number indicates the order in the collection, which coincides with the order in which the two were written. The letter itself shows that there was at least one earlier letter from Paul to the Corinthians, now lost (5:9).","facts":[["Position","7th book of the New Testament; 2nd of Paul's letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","16"],["Verses","437"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter, with answers to the problems and questions of a church"]],"structure":[{"title":"Divisions and the wisdom of the cross","from":1,"to":4,"text":"Paul combats the factions formed around leaders: the cross is “foolishness” to the world and the power of God to those who believe, and the apostles are only servants."},{"title":"Problems of conduct","from":5,"to":6,"text":"A man living with his father's wife, brothers suing each other in pagan courts and the use of the body with prostitutes."},{"title":"Marriage and celibacy","from":7,"to":7,"text":"Answers to the questions in the Corinthians' letter about married life, separation, virgins and widows."},{"title":"Meat offered to idols","from":8,"to":10,"text":"The knowledge that idols are nothing does not authorize wounding a brother's conscience; Paul gives his own example of one who gives up rights and recalls the history of Israel in the wilderness."},{"title":"The community's worship","from":11,"to":14,"text":"Head covering when praying and prophesying, the Lord's Supper, the gifts of the Spirit, love as the more excellent way and order between prophecy and tongues."},{"title":"The resurrection","from":15,"to":15,"text":"The tradition received about the death and the appearances of Christ and the answer to those who denied the resurrection of the dead."},{"title":"Collection and farewell","from":16,"to":16,"text":"The collection for the Christians in Jerusalem, travel plans, recommendations and greetings."}],"extra":{"title":"“Now about…”: the church's questions","text":"The Corinthians had written to Paul (7:1), and the letter answers point by point. Each new subject begins with the same Greek formula, peri de, “now about…”: the matters you wrote about (7:1), virgins (7:25), food sacrificed to idols (8:1), spiritual gifts (12:1), the collection (16:1) and Apollos (16:12). Other subjects, such as the divisions and the Supper, Paul knew of from reports that reached him (1:11; 11:18). At the end, he writes the greeting in his own hand (16:21) and preserves an Aramaic invocation of the first communities, marana tha, which the BSB translates as “Come, O Lord!” (16:22)."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1–4; 9; 15–16","ch":9,"note":"founder of the church; defends his way of exercising the apostleship, working with his own hands"},{"name":"Sosthenes","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"“our brother,” who appears with Paul as a sender (1:1)"},{"name":"Apollos","chapters":"1; 3–4; 16","ch":3,"note":"preacher who worked in Corinth after Paul: “I planted the seed and Apollos watered it” (3:6)"},{"name":"Cephas (Peter)","chapters":"1; 3; 9; 15","ch":15,"note":"one of the names used by the factions; the first in the list of appearances of the Risen One (15:5)"},{"name":"Chloe","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"people from her household tell Paul of the quarrels in the church (1:11)"},{"name":"Timothy","chapters":"4; 16","ch":4,"note":"sent by Paul to remind the Corinthians of his teachings (4:17; 16:10)"},{"name":"Stephanas, Fortunatus and Achaicus","chapters":"1; 16","ch":16,"note":"members of the church in Corinth who visit Paul in Ephesus; the household of Stephanas was the first to believe in Achaia"},{"name":"Aquila and Priscilla","chapters":"16","ch":16,"note":"the couple who had worked with Paul in Corinth; now in Ephesus, they send greetings with the church that meets at their house"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Paul's authorship is affirmed very early: around 96, Clement of Rome, writing precisely to the Corinthians, reminds them of the letter that “the blessed Paul” had sent them about the factions. Paul writes from Ephesus, where he intends to stay until Pentecost (16:8), near the end of the long stay in the city described in Acts 19, which leads to c. 54–55.","academic":"1 Corinthians is one of the seven letters that practically all scholars attribute to Paul, and the most common dating falls between 53 and 56, in Ephesus. Most read the text as a single letter, although some researchers have proposed that it combines two or more smaller letters. A debated passage is 14:34–35, on women keeping silent in the church: in some manuscripts it appears after 14:40, and some scholars consider it an early addition, while others keep it as Paul's and read it in light of the local situation. The correspondence with Corinth was larger than the two preserved letters: besides the earlier letter mentioned in 5:9, 2 Corinthians speaks of a letter written “with many tears” (2 Corinthians 2:4)."},"events":{"intro":"Paul arrived in Corinth on the second journey and stayed there a year and a half (Acts 18:11), part of that time during the term of the proconsul Gallio, dated c. 51–52 by an inscription at Delphi. Some years later, already in Ephesus, he receives news and a letter from the church and answers with 1 Corinthians. The chronology is the same as the one used for Acts, with a variation of a few years among authors.","axis":[45,60],"band":{"label":"Paul in Ephesus","from":53,"to":56},"timeline":[{"label":"Founding of the church in Corinth","from":50,"to":52,"kind":"evento","note":"a year and a half of Paul's work, with Aquila, Priscilla, Silas and Timothy (Acts 18)"},{"label":"Gallio, proconsul of Achaia","from":51,"to":52,"kind":"pessoa","note":"before him Paul is accused by the Jews of the city (Acts 18:12)"},{"label":"Apollos in Corinth","from":52,"to":53,"kind":"pessoa","note":"arrives in Achaia after Paul's departure (Acts 18:27–19:1); approximate date"},{"label":"Earlier letter, now lost","from":53,"to":54,"kind":"escrita","note":"mentioned in 5:9; date uncertain"},{"label":"Writing of 1 Corinthians","from":54,"to":55,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Ephesus, before Pentecost (16:8)"},{"label":"Paul in Macedonia and Greece","from":56,"to":57,"kind":"evento","note":"three months in Greece, probably in Corinth (Acts 20:1–3)"}]},"context":"Corinth lay on the isthmus that joins the Peloponnese to the rest of Greece, between two ports: Lechaeum, facing west, and Cenchrea, facing east. The old Greek city had been destroyed by the Romans in 146 BC and refounded by Julius Caesar in 44 BC as a Roman colony, populated by freedmen, veterans and merchants; in Paul's time it was the capital of the province of Achaia, rich, cosmopolitan and very attentive to social standing. The Isthmian Games were held there, which may lie behind the images of the race and the crown in 9:24–27. The church brought together people of different social levels (“not many of you were wise by human standards; not many were powerful,” 1:26, but there were householders able to host the community), and this mixture helps explain conflicts such as that at the Supper, in which “one remains hungry, another gets drunk” (11:21).","places":["Corinth","Ephesus","Achaia and Macedonia","Galatia","Jerusalem"],"themes":["the unity of the church","the cross and wisdom","the body and sexual ethics","freedom and conscience","the Lord's Supper","gifts of the Spirit and love","the resurrection"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:18","text":"For the message of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing, but to us who are being saved it is the power of God."},{"ref":"12:27","text":"Now you are the body of Christ, and each of you is a member of it."},{"ref":"13:4–7","text":"Love is patient, love is kind. It does not envy, it does not boast, it is not proud. It is not rude, it is not self-seeking, it is not easily angered, it keeps no account of wrongs. Love takes no pleasure in evil, but rejoices in the truth. It bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things."},{"ref":"15:3–4","text":"For what I received I passed on to you as of first importance: that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that He was buried, that He was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures,"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. Some older translations reproduce at the end of 16:24 an ancient scribal note according to which the letter was written at Philippi; that note is not part of the Greek text, and the letter itself points to Ephesus (16:8). The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"2CO":{"t":{"1":"The God of comfort; a change of plans","2":"The tearful letter; forgiving the offender","3":"The new covenant and the veil of Moses","4":"The light of Christ in fragile messengers","5":"Hope beyond death; ambassadors of reconciliation","6":"Hardships of the ministry; separation from idols","7":"Titus brings good news; sorrow that transforms","8":"The collection and the example of Macedonia","9":"Generosity and joy in giving","10":"Paul answers those who look down on him","11":"False apostles and Paul's sufferings","12":"Visions, the thorn and sufficient grace","13":"Final warnings and blessing"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Κορινθίους Βʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Korinthious B","summary":"2 Corinthians is the most personal of Paul's letters. After 1 Corinthians, the relationship with the church deteriorated: there was a painful visit, an offense against Paul and a letter written “with many tears” (2:4). In chapters 1 to 7, relieved by the good news brought by Titus, Paul explains the changes in his plans and reflects on the ministry of the new covenant, carried by frail people (“treasure in jars of clay,” 4:7) and devoted to reconciliation with God. Chapters 8 and 9 ask the Corinthians to complete the collection for the poor in Jerusalem. In chapters 10 to 13, the tone changes: Paul confronts rival preachers, whom he ironically calls “super-apostles” (12:11), and defends himself by speaking not of successes but of sufferings and weaknesses.","name":"The title “Second to the Corinthians” (Pros Korinthious B) distinguishes this one from the other letter to the same church and was given when Paul's letters were gathered. Despite the number, the text shows that Paul wrote to the Corinthians at least four times: a letter before 1 Corinthians (1 Corinthians 5:9), 1 Corinthians itself, the letter “with many tears” (2:4; 7:8) and this one.","facts":[["Position","8th book of the New Testament; 3rd of Paul's letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","13"],["Verses","257"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter, with a strong personal and defensive tone"]],"structure":[{"title":"Comfort and change of plans","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Paul gives thanks for God's comfort in the danger he went through in Asia, explains why he did not return to Corinth as he had promised and asks forgiveness for the one who offended him."},{"title":"Paul's ministry","from":2,"to":7,"text":"The new covenant, of the Spirit, compared with that of Moses; the message carried in frail bodies; hope in the face of death; the appeal for reconciliation; and the joy at the news from Titus (2:14–7:16)."},{"title":"The collection for Jerusalem","from":8,"to":9,"text":"The example of the poor churches of Macedonia, the sending of Titus and other brothers to Corinth and the invitation to give generously and cheerfully."},{"title":"Defense against the rivals","from":10,"to":13,"text":"Paul answers those who found him strong in his letters and weak in person, speaks “as a fool” of his own credentials and sufferings and announces the third visit."}],"extra":{"title":"The “fool's” speech","text":"In 11:1–12:13, Paul enters the game of his opponents, who boasted of credentials, and says he is forced to speak “as a fool.” But the list of merits turns into a list of sufferings: floggings, imprisonments, three shipwrecks, dangers of every kind and the escape from Damascus let down in a basket through the wall (11:23–33). When he speaks of an extraordinary experience, that of a man caught up “to the third heaven” fourteen years before, he speaks of himself in the third person (12:2–4) and then moves to the “thorn in the flesh” and the answer he received: “My grace is sufficient for you” (12:7–9)."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1–2; 10–13","ch":11,"note":"defends his way of acting and his apostleship before a church divided in its view of him"},{"name":"Titus","chapters":"2; 7–8; 12","ch":7,"note":"Paul's co-worker; carries news to and from Corinth and is sent to complete the collection"},{"name":"Timothy","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"appears with Paul as a sender (1:1) and preached in Corinth with him and Silvanus (1:19)"},{"name":"Silvanus","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"the Silas of Acts, Paul's companion in founding the church (1:19)"},{"name":"The offender","chapters":"2; 7","ch":2,"note":"an unidentified member of the church who caused Paul grief; after being rebuked, he is to be forgiven (2:5–11)"},{"name":"The “super-apostles”","chapters":"10–12","ch":11,"note":"preachers of Jewish origin (11:22) who sought the church's allegiance and disparaged Paul"},{"name":"Aretas","chapters":"11","ch":11,"note":"Aretas IV, king of the Nabateans; a governor under him tried to seize Paul in Damascus (11:32)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition has always attributed the letter to Paul, written with Timothy (1:1). From the text itself, Paul is in Macedonia, after leaving Ephesus and passing through Troas (2:12–13; 7:5), and is preparing to go to Corinth for the third time (12:14; 13:1). This leads to c. 55–56, shortly before the winter spent in Greece, when he would write Romans.","academic":"Paul's authorship is accepted by practically all scholars; the discussion is about the unity of the text. The abrupt change of tone in chapter 10 leads many to see in chapters 10–13 a separate letter: for some, part of the letter “with many tears,” earlier than chapters 1–9; for others, a later letter, written when the crisis returned. It is also debated whether chapters 8 and 9, both about the collection, were separate notes, and whether 6:14–7:1, which interrupts the thread of the text, came from another context. Other scholars argue that the letter was always a single one and explain the changes by new news or by rhetorical strategy. The proposed dates generally fall between 55 and 57."},"events":{"intro":"The letter allows part of Paul's relations with Corinth to be reconstructed between the founding of the church and his last visit, although the order of some events is debated. Two details help to place Paul's life: the escape from Damascus at the time of the Nabatean king Aretas IV, who died around the year 40 (11:32–33), and the vision he places “fourteen years ago” (12:2). The chronology is the same as the one used for Acts; the dates are approximate.","axis":[30,60],"band":{"label":"Paul in Ephesus","from":53,"to":56},"timeline":[{"label":"Escape from Damascus","from":36,"to":37,"kind":"evento","note":"under a governor of King Aretas IV (11:32–33; Acts 9:23–25)"},{"label":"The vision of the “third heaven”","from":41,"to":42,"kind":"evento","note":"date calculated from the “fourteen years” of 12:2"},{"label":"Founding of the church in Corinth","from":50,"to":52,"kind":"evento","note":"Paul, Silvanus and Timothy (1:19; Acts 18)"},{"label":"Writing of 1 Corinthians","from":54,"to":55,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Ephesus"},{"label":"Painful visit and the letter “with tears”","from":55,"to":55,"kind":"evento","note":"deduced from 2:1–4 and 13:2; dates and order debated"},{"label":"Writing of 2 Corinthians","from":55,"to":56,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Macedonia, after the meeting with Titus (7:5–7)"},{"label":"Third visit to Corinth","from":56,"to":57,"kind":"evento","note":"three months in Greece (Acts 20:2–3)"}]},"context":"Corinth, a Roman colony and the capital of Achaia, valued good oratory, appearance and the patronage of influential people. It is in this setting that the criticisms of Paul are understood: they said his letters were “weighty and forceful,” but his physical presence was unimpressive and his speaking “of no account” (10:10). The rivals arrived with letters of recommendation (3:1), accepted support from the church and presented themselves as “Hebrews” and “servants of Christ” (11:22–23); Paul, by contrast, had refused to be supported by the Corinthians and had lived on the help of the churches of Macedonia (11:7–9). The collection for the poor Christians of Jerusalem, which takes up chapters 8 and 9, was for Paul a concrete sign of the unity between the churches of Gentile origin and the Jewish community from which the movement had come.","places":["Corinth and Achaia","Ephesus and the province of Asia","Troas","Macedonia","Jerusalem and Judea","Damascus"],"themes":["comfort in suffering","strength in weakness","the new covenant","reconciliation","generosity","true apostleship"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"4:7","text":"Now we have this treasure in jars of clay to show that this surpassingly great power is from God and not from us."},{"ref":"5:17","text":"Therefore if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old has passed away. Behold, the new has come!"},{"ref":"9:7","text":"Each one should give what he has decided in his heart to give, not out of regret or compulsion. For God loves a cheerful giver."},{"ref":"12:9","text":"But He said to me, “My grace is sufficient for you, for My power is perfected in weakness.” Therefore I will boast all the more gladly in my weaknesses, so that the power of Christ may rest on me."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The BSB divides the end of chapter 13 into three verses (13:12–14), and the Greek text into two (13:12–13); the app matches them up. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"GAL":{"t":{"1":"No other gospel; Paul's call","2":"Agreement in Jerusalem; conflict with Cephas in Antioch","3":"The faith of Abraham and the purpose of the Law","4":"Sons, not slaves; the slave woman and the free woman","5":"Freedom, love and the fruit of the Spirit","6":"Bearing burdens; sowing and reaping"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Γαλάτας","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Galatas","summary":"Galatians is a letter written under tension. After Paul had passed through the region, teachers arrived in the churches of Galatia who demanded that non-Jewish Christians be circumcised and keep the Law of Moses, and Paul reacts without the thanksgiving with which he usually opens his letters: he is amazed that the Galatians have so quickly turned to “a different gospel” (1:6). In the first two chapters he tells his own story to show that his gospel did not come from men, including the agreement with the leaders in Jerusalem and the confrontation with Cephas in Antioch. In chapters 3 and 4 he argues from the story of Abraham and from the Scriptures that righteousness comes by faith, and that the Law had a provisional role. The final part speaks of freedom in Christ, which must not become a pretext for the flesh, and of life led by the Spirit.","name":"The title Pros Galatas, “To the Galatians,” comes from the greeting: the letter is addressed “to the churches of Galatia” (1:2), and not to a single city. The name of the region recalls the Galatians, Celtic peoples (the same ones the Romans called Gauls) who settled in central Asia Minor in the 3rd century BC.","facts":[["Position","9th book of the New Testament; 4th of Paul's letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","6"],["Verses","149"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","circular letter to several churches, polemical in tone"]],"structure":[{"title":"Opening and rebuke","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Paul affirms that he is an apostle not by human choice and, instead of giving thanks, rebukes the Galatians for listening to those who pervert the gospel (1:1–10)."},{"title":"Paul's story","from":1,"to":2,"text":"The former persecutor receives the revelation of Christ, goes to Jerusalem only years later, obtains from the leaders there the recognition of his mission among the Gentiles and confronts Cephas in Antioch over table fellowship with non-Jews (1:11–2:21)."},{"title":"Faith, Abraham and the Law","from":3,"to":4,"text":"The Galatians received the Spirit by faith; Abraham believed and was counted righteous; the Law came later, as a guardian until Christ; now all are sons and heirs, not slaves. The comparison between Hagar and Sarah closes the argument."},{"title":"Freedom and life in the Spirit","from":5,"to":6,"text":"Whoever lets himself be circumcised to be justified returns to the yoke; freedom is lived out in love, and the works of the flesh are contrasted with the fruit of the Spirit. Practical counsel and a closing written in his own hand (6:11–18)."}],"extra":{"title":"A letter without thanksgiving","text":"Almost all of Paul's letters begin with a thanksgiving for the recipients. Galatians goes straight from the greeting to the rebuke, a sign of the climate in which it was written. The same tone appears in the outburst “O foolish Galatians!” (3:1) and at the end, when Paul takes the pen from the secretary and draws attention to the size of the letters he writes with his own hand (6:11)."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1–2; 4–6","ch":1,"note":"defends the divine origin of his call and the gospel he preached to the Galatians; recalls that he arrived in the region ill (4:13)"},{"name":"Cephas (Peter)","chapters":"1–2","ch":2,"note":"Paul visits him for fifteen days in Jerusalem; later rebukes him in Antioch for withdrawing from the Gentiles' table"},{"name":"James, the Lord's brother","chapters":"1–2","ch":2,"note":"one of the three considered “pillars” of the church in Jerusalem, alongside Cephas and John (2:9)"},{"name":"Barnabas","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"Paul's companion on the trip to Jerusalem; in Antioch, he follows Peter in the conduct that is criticized"},{"name":"Titus","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"a Greek Christian taken to Jerusalem, where he was not compelled to be circumcised"},{"name":"Abraham","chapters":"3–4","ch":3,"note":"the one who believed and was counted righteous; those who have faith are called his sons"},{"name":"Hagar and Sarah","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"the slave woman and the free woman, read as figures of two covenants (4:21–31)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Paul's authorship was never questioned by the early Church. The letter does not say where it was written, and tradition pointed to various places; an ancient scribal note mentions Rome, but most interpreters link it to the Ephesus period, on the third journey.","academic":"Galatians is among the seven letters that practically all scholars attribute to Paul, alongside Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Philippians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon. The debate is about the recipients and the date. On the “North Galatian” hypothesis, the letter goes to the regions of the ancient Galatians, around Ancyra (today Ankara), which Paul would have visited on the second journey (Acts 16:6), and would date from c. 53–56. On the “South Galatian” hypothesis, it goes to the churches of the Roman province founded on the first journey (Pisidian Antioch, Iconium, Lystra and Derbe, Acts 13–14) and may be older, c. 48–51; some place it even before the Jerusalem meeting of Acts 15, which would make it one of Paul's first letters. It is also debated whether the visit of 2:1–10 corresponds to Acts 15 or to Acts 11:30."},"events":{"intro":"In the letter Paul gives some indications of time (three years after his conversion, a visit to Jerusalem; fourteen years later, another, 1:18; 2:1) that are usually combined with the account of Acts. The dates are approximate and vary according to the hypothesis adopted about the recipients.","axis":[30,60],"band":{"label":"Reign of Claudius","from":41,"to":54},"timeline":[{"label":"Conversion of Paul","from":33,"to":36,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate date (1:15–16; Acts 9)"},{"label":"First visit to Jerusalem","from":35,"to":38,"kind":"evento","note":"fifteen days with Cephas, three years later (1:18)"},{"label":"First missionary journey","from":46,"to":48,"kind":"evento","note":"churches in the south of the province of Galatia (Acts 13–14)"},{"label":"Agreement in Jerusalem","from":48,"to":49,"kind":"evento","note":"fourteen years later (2:1–10); many identify it with Acts 15"},{"label":"Conflict in Antioch","from":49,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"Paul rebukes Cephas (2:11–14); approximate date"},{"label":"Writing of the letter","from":48,"to":56,"kind":"escrita","note":"a wide range: c. 48–51 on the southern hypothesis; c. 53–56 on the northern"}]},"context":"Galatia lay on the central plateau of Asia Minor, in present-day Turkey. From c. 278–277 BC, Celtic groups coming from Europe went there and gave the region its name. In 25 BC, with the death of King Amyntas, the territory became a Roman province, extended southward to include cities such as Pisidian Antioch, Iconium, Lystra and Derbe; so “Galatia” could indicate both the land of the ancient Galatians and the whole province. In the churches, made up largely of non-Jews, the question was whether, to be part of God's people, they would need to adopt circumcision, the calendar and the rules of the Law, a subject that divided the first Christians and also appears in Acts 15.","places":["Galatia","Jerusalem","Antioch in Syria","Arabia","Damascus","Syria and Cilicia"],"themes":["the gospel and Paul's authority","justification by faith","the Law and circumcision","Abraham and the promise","sons and heirs","freedom","flesh and Spirit"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:20","text":"I have been crucified with Christ, and I no longer live, but Christ lives in me. The life I live in the body, I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself up for me."},{"ref":"3:28","text":"There is neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female, for you are all one in Christ Jesus."},{"ref":"5:1","text":"It is for freedom that Christ has set us free. Stand firm, then, and do not be encumbered once more by a yoke of slavery."},{"ref":"5:22–23","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control. Against such things there is no law."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source and the hypothesis about the recipients. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"EPH":{"t":{"1":"Blessings in Christ; a prayer for wisdom","2":"Saved by grace; Jews and Gentiles one people","3":"The mystery revealed to the Gentiles","4":"The unity of the body and the new life","5":"Walking in love and in the light; husbands and wives","6":"Children, servants and the armor of God"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Ἐφεσίους","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Ephesious","summary":"Ephesians is less an answer to concrete problems than a broad reflection on what God has done in Christ and on the Church. The first half, in a tone of praise and prayer, speaks of God's plan to bring all things together in Christ, of salvation “by grace… through faith” (2:8) and of the union of Jews and Gentiles in one people, without “the dividing wall” (2:14). The second half draws the consequences: the unity of the body of Christ, the diversity of gifts, the abandonment of the old life, relationships within the household and the image of the “armor of God” for facing spiritual forces. Many expressions and themes coincide with those of Colossians.","name":"The title Pros Ephesious, “To the Ephesians,” was given when Paul's letters began to circulate as a collection, and rests on the greeting to “the saints in Ephesus” (1:1). But the words “in Ephesus” are missing in some of the oldest manuscripts, and around 140 Marcion knew the letter as addressed to the Laodiceans; for this reason many think it was, from the beginning, a circular letter for several churches of Asia.","facts":[["Position","10th book of the New Testament; 5th of the Pauline letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","6"],["Verses","155"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter, general in character, in a tone of praise and exhortation"]],"structure":[{"title":"Greeting and praise","from":1,"to":1,"text":"A long blessing (in the Greek, a single sentence, 1:3–14) celebrates election, redemption and the seal of the Spirit, followed by a prayer that the readers may understand the power of God shown in Christ."},{"title":"From dead to one people","from":2,"to":2,"text":"Those who were dead in their sins receive life by grace; Gentiles once far off are brought near, and Jews and Gentiles form one new humanity."},{"title":"The mystery and Paul's mission","from":3,"to":3,"text":"Paul, a prisoner, presents himself as a servant of the “mystery”: the Gentiles are fellow heirs. It ends with a prayer and a doxology."},{"title":"Unity and new life","from":4,"to":5,"text":"One body and one Spirit, gifts given to build up the Church; putting off the “old self,” walking in love and as children of light (4:1–5:20)."},{"title":"The household and the battle","from":5,"to":6,"text":"Duties of wives and husbands, children and fathers, slaves and masters, with marriage compared to the union of Christ and the Church; the armor of God; Tychicus will bring news (5:21–6:24)."}],"extra":{"title":"Long sentences and the language of praise","text":"The style of Ephesians stands out even in translation: very long periods, piling up of synonyms and chains of expressions such as “in Christ” and “in the heavenly realms.” Several passages sound like prayer or hymn (1:3–14; 3:14–21), and some scholars see traces of language used in baptism or worship. This style, different from that of Paul's more polemical letters, is one of the central points in the debate about authorship."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1; 3–4; 6","ch":3,"note":"writes as “the prisoner of Christ Jesus” on behalf of the Gentiles (3:1; 4:1) and asks for prayer to proclaim the gospel boldly (6:19–20)"},{"name":"Tychicus","chapters":"6","ch":6,"note":"“the beloved brother and faithful servant,” charged with carrying the letter and giving news of Paul (6:21–22); appears in almost the same words in Colossians 4:7"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition has always read Ephesians as a letter of Paul, and 2nd-century authors such as Irenaeus quote it under his name. As one of the “prison letters,” together with Philippians, Colossians and Philemon, it was associated with Paul's two years of imprisonment in Rome (Acts 28:30), c. 60–62, and would have been carried by Tychicus together with Colossians.","academic":"Ephesians falls outside the group of seven letters that practically all scholars attribute to Paul (Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon). The others, among them Ephesians, are called by many “deutero-Pauline” or “disputed.” A large part of scholarship considers Ephesians the work of a disciple who wrote in Paul's name, c. 80–100, relying on the different vocabulary and style, on the view of the Church as a universal reality, on the emphasis on a salvation already present (2:5–6) and on its dependence on Colossians, whose structure and phrases it seems to take up. Other scholars defend Paul's authorship, explaining the differences by the general and liturgical character of the letter, by the use of a secretary or by a later phase of his thought; in that case, the date would be c. 60–62, and some propose another imprisonment, in Caesarea or Ephesus. Writing in the name of a teacher was a known practice in antiquity, and how to assess it is also a matter of debate."},"events":{"intro":"The letter mentions no datable events besides Paul's imprisonment. The timeline shows the two positions: the traditional one, which places it during the imprisonment in Rome, and that of many scholars, who place it a few decades later. The dates are approximate.","axis":[45,100],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Paul in Ephesus","from":53,"to":56,"kind":"evento","note":"about three years of work in the city (Acts 19; 20:31)"},{"label":"Paul a prisoner in Rome","from":60,"to":62,"kind":"evento","note":"two years in a rented house (Acts 28:30)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":60,"to":62,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Rome, sent by Tychicus together with Colossians"},{"label":"Death of Paul, according to tradition","from":64,"to":67,"kind":"pessoa","note":"in Rome, under Nero; date uncertain"},{"label":"Writing, according to many scholars","from":80,"to":100,"kind":"escrita","note":"by a disciple of Paul, probably in Asia Minor"}]},"context":"Ephesus was the main city of the Roman province of Asia, a great port on the coast of present-day Turkey, with a population estimated at more than a hundred thousand. It was famous for the temple of Artemis, one of the wonders of the ancient world, and had an established Jewish community. According to Acts, Paul spent about three years there, and from the city the gospel reached other places in the region, such as Colossae, Laodicea and Hierapolis. The letter, however, gives almost no local information: it names no people of the church, speaks to the readers as one who has only “heard” of their faith (1:15) and deals with general themes, which strengthens the idea of a text intended for several communities, made up largely of non-Jews.","places":["Ephesus","the province of Asia","Laodicea","Colossae","Rome"],"themes":["God's plan in Christ","grace and faith","Jews and Gentiles in one people","the Church as the body of Christ","unity and gifts","the new life","relationships in the household","the spiritual battle"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:8–10","text":"For it is by grace you have been saved through faith, and this not from yourselves; it is the gift of God, not by works, so that no one can boast. For we are God’s workmanship, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance as our way of life."},{"ref":"2:14","text":"For He Himself is our peace, who has made the two one and has torn down the dividing wall of hostility"},{"ref":"4:4–6","text":"There is one body and one Spirit, just as you were called to one hope when you were called; one Lord, one faith, one baptism; one God and Father of all, who is over all and through all and in all."},{"ref":"6:11","text":"Put on the full armor of God, so that you can make your stand against the devil’s schemes."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. In 1:1, the words “in Ephesus” are missing in some ancient manuscripts; the SBLGNT, the Greek text used in the app, prints them in brackets, indicating doubt, and the BSB keeps them. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"PHP":{"t":{"1":"Paul's imprisonment and the advance of the gospel","2":"The example of Christ; Timothy and Epaphroditus","3":"Losing everything to gain Christ","4":"Joy, peace and thanks for the gift"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Φιλιππησίους","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Philippesious","summary":"Philippians is the letter of Paul most marked by affection. In prison and not knowing whether he will be released or condemned, he thanks the church in Philippi for the help it sent him through Epaphroditus and tells how his imprisonment has served to spread the gospel. He asks for unity and humility, presenting the example of Christ, who “emptied Himself” and was obedient to death on a cross (2:5–11). In chapter 3 he changes tone to warn against those who insist on circumcision and recalls his own story: everything he had counted as gain came to be worth little compared with knowing Christ. Joy, even in the midst of imprisonment, runs through the whole letter.","name":"The title Pros Philippesious, “To the Philippians,” comes from the greeting to the saints “at Philippi” (1:1); in 4:15, Paul calls the readers “Philippians.” The city bore the name of Philip II of Macedon, father of Alexander the Great, who refounded it in the 4th century BC.","facts":[["Position","11th book of the New Testament; 6th of Paul's letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","4"],["Verses","104"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter of friendship and thanks, written in prison"]],"structure":[{"title":"Gratitude and news from prison","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Paul gives thanks for the Philippians' partnership, tells that his chains have become known and that others preach more boldly, and weighs departing to be with Christ against remaining with them."},{"title":"Unity and the example of Christ","from":1,"to":2,"text":"A call to live in a manner worthy of the gospel and with the same mind as Christ, who humbled Himself and was exalted; plans to send Timothy and the return of Epaphroditus, who was near death (1:27–2:30)."},{"title":"Warning and the goal","from":3,"to":3,"text":"Against those who put confidence in the flesh, Paul lists his own credentials and says he counts them as loss; he has not yet reached the end, but presses on toward the goal, as a citizen of heaven."},{"title":"Counsel and thanks","from":4,"to":4,"text":"A plea for reconciliation between Euodia and Syntyche, an invitation to joy and prayer, and a careful thank-you for the gift received, with greetings from “the household of Caesar.”"}],"extra":{"title":"The hymn of 2:6–11","text":"Verses 2:6–11 have their own rhythm and describe a movement in two parts: Christ descends, from the form of God to the form of a servant and to death on a cross, and God exalts Him, so that every knee should bow and every tongue confess that He is Lord, an echo of Isaiah 45:23. Many scholars think Paul is quoting a hymn or poem already used by the churches; others attribute it to Paul himself. In either case, it is one of the oldest texts about who Christ is and one of the most discussed in the New Testament."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1–4","ch":1,"note":"in prison, awaits the outcome of the trial; recalls that he was a Pharisee and a persecutor of the Church (3:5–6)"},{"name":"Timothy","chapters":"1–2","ch":2,"note":"co-author of the greeting; Paul intends to send him soon, since “as a child with his father he has served with me to advance the gospel” (2:22)"},{"name":"Epaphroditus","chapters":"2; 4","ch":2,"note":"sent by the Philippians with the help for Paul; fell seriously ill and returns home, probably carrying the letter"},{"name":"Euodia and Syntyche","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"two women who contended for the gospel alongside Paul, whom he asks to agree with each other (4:2–3)"},{"name":"Clement","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"a fellow worker mentioned with the others whose names “are in the book of life” (4:3)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Paul's authorship was never questioned in the early Church; around 110–135, Polycarp of Smyrna, writing to the Philippians themselves, recalls that Paul had written to them. The reference to the palace guard (1:13) and to “the household of Caesar” (4:22) led tradition to place the letter in Paul's imprisonment in Rome, c. 60–62.","academic":"Philippians is among the seven letters that practically all scholars attribute to Paul, alongside Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Galatians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon. The place and date of the imprisonment are debated: Rome (c. 60–62) remains the most common proposal, but many prefer Ephesus (c. 54–55), whose distance from Philippi would make simpler the several journeys the letter assumes; Caesarea (c. 57–59) is also defended. The praetorium and “Caesar's household” may indicate guards and imperial officials in the provinces as well. Another discussion is unity: the abrupt change of tone in 3:1–2 led some to see the letter as a combination of two or three letters to the Philippians, while others read it as a single text."},"events":{"intro":"The church in Philippi was the first Paul founded in Europe, on the second journey, and kept a close relationship with him, sending help more than once (4:15–16). The date of the letter depends on where Paul was imprisoned; the dates are approximate.","axis":[45,70],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Founding of the church in Philippi","from":49,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"Lydia and the jailer; Paul and Silas imprisoned and released (Acts 16)"},{"label":"Help sent to Thessalonica","from":50,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"“again and again” (4:16); approximate date"},{"label":"Writing, if in Ephesus","from":54,"to":55,"kind":"escrita","note":"hypothesis of many scholars"},{"label":"Imprisonment in Caesarea","from":57,"to":59,"kind":"evento","note":"Acts 23–26; another place proposed for the letter"},{"label":"Writing, if in Rome","from":60,"to":62,"kind":"escrita","note":"the traditional position, still widely followed"}]},"context":"Philippi lay in eastern Macedonia, on the Via Egnatia, the Roman road that linked the Adriatic Sea to Byzantium. Near it, in 42 BC, Antony and Octavian defeated Brutus and Cassius, the assassins of Julius Caesar, and the city was made a Roman colony, receiving war veterans; after 31 BC it received new colonists. Its inhabitants had the privileges of Roman citizens, and Latin was used in the administration, which gives weight to the phrase “our citizenship is in heaven” (3:20). According to Acts 16, the Jewish community was small, gathered at a place of prayer by the river, and the church was born in the house of Lydia, a dealer in purple cloth from Thyatira.","places":["Philippi","Macedonia","Thessalonica","the palace guard and “the household of Caesar”"],"themes":["joy","partnership in the gospel","the example of Christ","humility and unity","life and death","confidence in the flesh","contentment"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:21","text":"For to me, to live is Christ, and to die is gain."},{"ref":"2:5–8","text":"Let this mind be in you which was also in Christ Jesus: Who, existing in the form of God, did not consider equality with God something to be grasped, but emptied Himself, taking the form of a servant, being made in human likeness. And being found in appearance as a man, He humbled Himself and became obedient to death— even death on a cross."},{"ref":"3:13–14","text":"Brothers, I do not consider myself yet to have taken hold of it. But one thing I do: Forgetting what is behind and straining toward what is ahead, I press on toward the goal to win the prize of God’s heavenly calling in Christ Jesus."},{"ref":"4:6–7","text":"Be anxious for nothing, but in everything, by prayer and petition, with thanksgiving, present your requests to God. And the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and your minds in Christ Jesus."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source and the place adopted for Paul's imprisonment. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"COL":{"t":{"1":"Christ, the image of the invisible God","2":"Fullness in Christ against human rules","3":"The things above and life at home","4":"Prayer, news and final greetings"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Κολοσσαεῖς","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Kolossaeis","summary":"Colossians is addressed to a church Paul neither founded nor visited, formed through the work of Epaphras. The center of the letter is the person of Christ, presented in a hymnic passage as “the image of the invisible God,” through whom all things were created and reconciled (1:15–20). From this comes the warning against a teaching the readers were hearing, made up of “philosophy,” rules about food and holy days, “the worship of angels” and visions (2:8–23): for the author, none of this adds to what one already has in Christ. The second part speaks of the new life, of relationships within the household and ends with a long list of news and greetings.","name":"The title Pros Kolossaeis, “To the Colossians,” comes from the greeting to the saints “at Colossae” (1:2). Colossae was a small city of Phrygia, in Asia Minor.","facts":[["Position","12th book of the New Testament; 7th of the Pauline letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","4"],["Verses","95"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter, with an exposition about Christ and a warning against a rival teaching"]],"structure":[{"title":"Gratitude and prayer","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Thanksgiving for the faith of the Colossians, who learned the gospel from Epaphras, and a prayer that they may grow in the knowledge of God's will (1:1–14)."},{"title":"Christ and Paul's mission","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Christ, firstborn over creation and from the dead, reconciles all things; Paul rejoices to suffer for the Church and struggles for those who do not know him personally (1:15–2:5)."},{"title":"Against the rival teaching","from":2,"to":2,"text":"In Christ all the fullness dwells; the readers were circumcised in Him and buried with Him in baptism; rules about eating, festivals and the worship of angels are “a shadow of the things to come” (2:6–23)."},{"title":"The new life","from":3,"to":4,"text":"Seeking the things above, putting off the old life and putting on compassion and love; duties of wives and husbands, children and fathers, slaves and masters; persevering in prayer (3:1–4:6)."},{"title":"News and greetings","from":4,"to":4,"text":"Tychicus and Onesimus will bring news; greetings from Aristarchus, Mark, Epaphras, Luke and Demas; an exchange of letters with Laodicea and a signature in Paul's own hand (4:7–18)."}],"extra":{"title":"The letter to Laodicea","text":"In 4:16, the Colossians are told to have the letter read also in Laodicea and to read “the one from Laodicea.” That other letter has not been preserved, or it is not known which it is: some identify it with Ephesians, which would have been a circular letter. A short text called the “Epistle to the Laodiceans,” in Latin, circulated in the Middle Ages, but is generally considered a later composition, made of sentences taken from other letters of Paul."},"people":[{"name":"Paul and Timothy","chapters":"1; 4","ch":1,"note":"the senders; Paul writes in prison and signs the final greeting with his own hand (4:18)"},{"name":"Epaphras","chapters":"1; 4","ch":1,"note":"a Colossian who proclaimed the gospel in the city and worked also in Laodicea and Hierapolis (1:7; 4:12–13)"},{"name":"Tychicus","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"carrier of the letter, charged with giving news of Paul (4:7–8)"},{"name":"Onesimus","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"“one of you” (4:9); the same name as the slave for whom Paul intercedes in Philemon"},{"name":"Aristarchus, Mark and Jesus Justus","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"fellow workers of Jewish origin; Aristarchus is in prison with Paul and Mark is Barnabas's cousin (4:10–11)"},{"name":"Luke and Demas","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"Luke is called “the beloved physician” (4:14); both also appear in Philemon 24"},{"name":"Nympha and Archippus","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"a church meets in Nympha's house; Archippus must complete the ministry he received (4:15–17)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition has always received Colossians as a letter of Paul, and it is quoted under his name by 2nd-century authors such as Irenaeus. Written in prison and with almost the same names as Philemon, it was placed together with Ephesians and Philemon in Paul's two years of imprisonment in Rome, c. 60–62.","academic":"Colossians is not among the seven letters that practically all scholars attribute to Paul (Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon), and scholarship is divided. Many consider it written by a disciple of Paul, perhaps shortly after his death, c. 70–90, based on the style, heavier than that of the undisputed letters, and on ideas such as the Church understood as a body whose head is Christ and the resurrection with Christ described as already having taken place (2:12; 3:1). Others, in considerable number, maintain Paul's authorship, pointing to the close link with Philemon and the personal news of chapter 4; in that case, the letter would come from the imprisonment in Rome (c. 60–62) or in Ephesus (c. 54–55). Some also propose that Timothy, named as co-author, drafted the text in Paul's lifetime. The relationship with Ephesians is also studied: many think Ephesians used Colossians."},"events":{"intro":"The letter gives no dates. The timeline shows the traditional position and that of many scholars, as well as the earthquake that, according to the historian Tacitus, struck Laodicea around 60. The dates are approximate.","axis":[45,95],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Paul in Ephesus","from":53,"to":56,"kind":"evento","note":"at this time Epaphras would have taken the gospel to the Lycus valley"},{"label":"Writing, if in Ephesus","from":54,"to":55,"kind":"escrita","note":"a proposal of some of those who defend Paul's authorship"},{"label":"Earthquake at Laodicea","from":60,"to":61,"kind":"evento","note":"according to Tacitus; neighboring Colossae was probably also hit"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":60,"to":62,"kind":"escrita","note":"in prison in Rome, together with Philemon and Ephesians"},{"label":"Writing, according to many scholars","from":70,"to":90,"kind":"escrita","note":"by a disciple of Paul"}]},"context":"Colossae lay in the valley of the Lycus River, in Phrygia, about 200 km east of Ephesus, a few kilometers from Laodicea and Hierapolis, larger and more prosperous cities. It had been a center of the wool trade, but by the 1st century it had lost importance. The region had a Jewish population since, around 200 BC, according to the historian Josephus, the Seleucid king Antiochus III settled Jewish families in Lydia and Phrygia. The teaching combated in the letter is hard to reconstruct: it mixes Jewish elements, such as Sabbaths, New Moons and food rules, with ascetic practices, visions and the veneration of angels, and scholars propose everything from forms of Jewish mysticism to influences of local religions or of popular philosophy.","places":["Colossae","Laodicea","Hierapolis","the Lycus valley, in Phrygia"],"themes":["the supremacy of Christ","fullness in Christ","reconciliation","against rules and visions","baptism and new life","relationships in the household","prayer"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:15–17","text":"The Son is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn over all creation. For in Him all things were created, things in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, whether thrones or dominions or rulers or authorities. All things were created through Him and for Him. He is before all things, and in Him all things hold together."},{"ref":"2:9","text":"For in Christ all the fullness of the Deity dwells in bodily form."},{"ref":"3:1–2","text":"Therefore, since you have been raised with Christ, strive for the things above, where Christ is seated at the right hand of God. Set your minds on things above, not on earthly things."},{"ref":"3:17","text":"And whatever you do, in word or deed, do it all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God the Father through Him."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"1TH":{"t":{"1":"The faith, love and hope of the Thessalonians","2":"Paul's work in Thessalonica","3":"Timothy brings good news","4":"A holy life and those who have died","5":"The day of the Lord; final exhortations"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Θεσσαλονικεῖς Αʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Thessalonikeis A","summary":"1 Thessalonians is probably Paul's oldest surviving letter and, for that reason, one of the first Christian writings. Paul, Silvanus and Timothy had been forced to leave Thessalonica shortly after founding there a church made up mainly of people who had turned away from idols (1:9). On receiving from Timothy good news about their steadfastness, Paul writes with relief, recalls the time he spent in the city and reinforces the teaching they received about holy living and work. He also answers a question that was grieving them: what happens to the brothers who die before the coming of the Lord (4:13–18).","name":"The title Pros Thessalonikeis A, “First to the Thessalonians,” comes from the greeting “to the church of the Thessalonians” (1:1); the number distinguishes it from the second letter to the same church. The city was named after Thessalonike, sister of Alexander the Great and wife of Cassander, who founded it around 316 BC.","facts":[["Position","13th book of the New Testament; 8th of Paul's letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","5"],["Verses","89"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","pastoral letter of encouragement"]],"structure":[{"title":"Gratitude for the church","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The faith, love and hope of the Thessalonians have become known in Macedonia and Achaia."},{"title":"Memories of the mission","from":2,"to":3,"text":"Paul recalls how he worked with his own hands so as not to be a burden to anyone, the church's suffering, the forced separation, the sending of Timothy from Athens and the joy at the news he brought."},{"title":"Instructions","from":4,"to":5,"text":"Holiness in the body, brotherly love and work; hope regarding those who have died; the day of the Lord, which will come by surprise; and a series of brief counsels for the life of the church."}],"extra":{"title":"Paul's oldest letter","text":"If the most common dating is right, this letter was written some twenty years after the death of Jesus, before any gospel. In it already appear faith, love and hope together (1:3; 5:8), the expectation of the Lord's coming and the order that the letter “be read to all the brothers” (5:27), a sign that letters were read aloud at the gatherings."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1–5","ch":2,"note":"founder of the church; tried more than once to return, but was hindered (2:17–18)"},{"name":"Silvanus","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"co-author of the greeting; he is the Silas of Acts, Paul's companion on the second journey"},{"name":"Timothy","chapters":"1; 3","ch":3,"note":"sent from Athens to strengthen the church, returns with good news (3:1–6)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Paul's authorship was accepted without dispute in the early Church. Since Timothy rejoined Paul after the stay in Athens, the letter was placed in Corinth, during the year and a half Paul spent there (Acts 18:5, 11).","academic":"1 Thessalonians is among the seven letters that practically all scholars attribute to Paul, alongside Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians and Philemon, and is generally dated c. 50–51, in Corinth. Paul's stay in that city is dated with relative certainty by an inscription at Delphi that mentions the proconsul Gallio (Acts 18:12). A debated point is 2:14–16, which speaks harshly of the Jews who killed the Lord and persecuted the prophets: some scholars consider it a later addition, perhaps after the destruction of Jerusalem in 70, but most keep it as part of the letter, read in the context of local conflicts. It is also debated whether the letter is a single one or the combination of two, a minority hypothesis."},"events":{"intro":"The founding of the church and the letter fit into a few months of Paul's second journey, according to the itinerary of Acts 17–18. The dates are approximate.","axis":[45,55],"band":{"label":"Paul's second journey","from":49,"to":52},"timeline":[{"label":"Paul in Philippi","from":49,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"where he was mistreated before arriving in Thessalonica (2:2)"},{"label":"Founding of the church in Thessalonica","from":50,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"Paul preaches in the synagogue and has to leave in haste (Acts 17:1–10)"},{"label":"Berea and Athens","from":50,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"from Athens, Timothy is sent back to Thessalonica (3:1–2)"},{"label":"Writing of the letter","from":50,"to":51,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Corinth, after Timothy's return"},{"label":"Gallio, proconsul of Achaia","from":51,"to":52,"kind":"pessoa","note":"date known from an inscription at Delphi (Acts 18:12)"}]},"context":"Thessalonica (today's Thessaloniki, in Greece) was the capital of the Roman province of Macedonia, a port on the Thermaic Gulf crossed by the Via Egnatia. It was a free city, with its own magistrates (the “politarchs” of Acts 17:6, a title confirmed by inscriptions) and had temples of various gods, a local cult of Cabirus and honors paid to Rome and the emperors. According to Acts, Paul preached in the synagogue for three Sabbaths and won over some Jews and many devout Greeks, but opposition led to a riot and his departure by night. The letter indicates that the converts continued to suffer hostility from their own countrymen (2:14).","places":["Thessalonica","Macedonia and Achaia","Philippi","Athens","Judea"],"themes":["faith, love and hope","the missionaries' example","suffering and steadfastness","holiness","work","the dead in Christ","the coming of the Lord"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:9–10","text":"For they themselves report what kind of welcome you gave us, and how you turned to God from idols to serve the living and true God and to await His Son from heaven, whom He raised from the dead—Jesus our deliverer from the coming wrath."},{"ref":"4:16–17","text":"For the Lord Himself will descend from heaven with a loud command, with the voice of an archangel, and with the trumpet of God, and the dead in Christ will be the first to rise. After that, we who are alive and remain will be caught up together with them in the clouds to meet the Lord in the air. And so we will always be with the Lord."},{"ref":"5:16–18","text":"Rejoice at all times. Pray without ceasing. Give thanks in every circumstance, for this is God’s will for you in Christ Jesus."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"2TH":{"t":{"1":"Perseverance in persecution and just judgment","2":"The man of lawlessness before the day","3":"Against idleness; a greeting in Paul's own hand"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Θεσσαλονικεῖς Βʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Thessalonikeis B","summary":"2 Thessalonians is a short letter, with the same heading as 1 Thessalonians, addressed to a church that was still suffering persecution. The first chapter encourages it with the certainty that God will do justice when the Lord Jesus is revealed. The second corrects an idea that was frightening the readers, that the day of the Lord had already come, perhaps supported by a letter attributed to the missionaries themselves (2:2): before that day, the text says, the rebellion and the “man of lawlessness” will come, now held back by something or someone who “restrains” him. The last chapter deals with those who had stopped working and were living “idly.”","name":"The title Pros Thessalonikeis B, “Second to the Thessalonians,” comes from the greeting “to the church of the Thessalonians” (1:1), the same as in the first letter; the number indicates the order it was given in the collection, not necessarily the order in which it was written.","facts":[["Position","14th book of the New Testament; 9th of the Pauline letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","3"],["Verses","47"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter of encouragement and correction, with an apocalyptic passage"]],"structure":[{"title":"Comfort in persecution","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Thanksgiving for the faith that grows amid afflictions and the announcement of God's just judgment when the Lord Jesus appears with His angels."},{"title":"The day has not yet come","from":2,"to":2,"text":"Before the day the rebellion and the “man of lawlessness,” who sets himself up in the temple of God, will come; for now, something restrains him. The readers must hold to the traditions received by word or by letter."},{"title":"Work and discipline","from":3,"to":3,"text":"A request for prayer, an order that the idle should work and that those who do not obey be avoided, and a greeting written in Paul's own hand, presented as a mark in every letter (3:17)."}],"people":[{"name":"Paul, Silvanus and Timothy","chapters":"1; 3","ch":1,"note":"the senders, as in 1 Thessalonians; the final greeting is signed by Paul (3:17)"},{"name":"The man of lawlessness","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"a figure who opposes everything called God and will be destroyed by the Lord; also called “the son of destruction” (2:3–8)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The early Church received the letter as Paul's, and it seems to echo in Polycarp, in the early 2nd century, and is quoted by Irenaeus, c. 180. Because of its similarity to 1 Thessalonians and its same senders, it was placed shortly after it, still in Corinth, c. 50–51, when the church was still persecuted and the questions about the end continued.","academic":"2 Thessalonians is among the disputed letters, outside the group of seven that practically all scholars attribute to Paul, and scholarship is quite divided. Those who think of another author, writing in Paul's name perhaps at the end of the 1st century, observe that the structure and many phrases closely follow 1 Thessalonians, but the tone is more formal; that the first letter says the day will come “like a thief in the night” (1 Thessalonians 5:2), while this one describes signs that precede it; and that the warning against false letters (2:2) and the emphasis on the signature (3:17) would make sense at a time when letters in Paul's name were already circulating. Those who defend Paul's authorship answer that the closeness to the first letter is explained by their having been written a short time apart, to the same church, and that the expectation of an imminent end and the announcement of signs coexist in other Jewish and Christian apocalyptic texts. Some also propose that this letter was written before 1 Thessalonians."},"events":{"intro":"If the letter is by Paul, it is among the oldest in the New Testament, written months after the first; if it is later, its most likely date falls at the end of the 1st century. The timeline shows both positions; the dates are approximate.","axis":[45,100],"timeline":[{"label":"Founding of the church in Thessalonica","from":50,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"on Paul's second journey (Acts 17:1–10)"},{"label":"Writing of 1 Thessalonians","from":50,"to":51,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Corinth"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":51,"to":52,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Corinth, shortly after the first letter"},{"label":"Destruction of the temple in Jerusalem","from":70,"to":70,"kind":"evento","note":"some read 2:4 in light of this event; others, of earlier crises"},{"label":"Writing, according to those who consider it later","from":80,"to":100,"kind":"escrita","note":"by a continuator of Paul"}]},"context":"The setting is that of the church of Thessalonica, capital of the province of Macedonia, made up largely of non-Jews and treated with hostility by their own countrymen. The passage about the “man of lawlessness” uses images from the Jewish apocalyptic tradition, such as that of the ruler who exalts himself against God in the book of Daniel (Daniel 11:36), and many also recall the attempt of the emperor Caligula, in 40, to place his statue in the temple in Jerusalem. What or who “restrains” him (2:6–7) has never been identified with certainty: already in antiquity people thought of the Roman Empire, and other proposals include the preaching of the gospel, an angel or God's own plan. The problem of those who did not work may be linked to the expectation of an imminent end or simply to dependence on patrons and on the help of the brothers.","places":["Thessalonica","Macedonia","the temple of God"],"themes":["perseverance in persecution","God's just judgment","the day of the Lord","the man of lawlessness","the traditions received","work and idleness"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:1–3","text":"Now concerning the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ and our being gathered together to Him, we ask you, brothers, not to be easily disconcerted or alarmed by any spirit or message or letter seeming to be from us, alleging that the Day of the Lord has already come. Let no one deceive you in any way, for it will not come until the rebellion occurs and the man of lawlessness—the son of destruction—is revealed."},{"ref":"2:15","text":"Therefore, brothers, stand firm and cling to the traditions we taught you, whether by speech or by letter."},{"ref":"3:10","text":"For even while we were with you, we gave you this command: “If anyone is unwilling to work, he shall not eat.”"}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source. In 2:2, the Greek verb is usually understood as “has already come” or “is already present,” as in the BSB; some translations render it “is at hand.” The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"1TI":{"t":{"1":"Against strange doctrine; the mercy Paul received","2":"Prayer for all; men and women in worship","3":"Qualifications of overseers and deacons","4":"False teaching and Timothy's example","5":"Widows and elders","6":"Servants, wealth and the good fight of faith"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Τιμόθεον Αʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Timotheon A","summary":"1 Timothy is a letter of instructions to a co-worker charged with caring for the church in Ephesus while Paul goes on to Macedonia (1:3). It begins and ends with warnings against teachers who teach “false doctrine,” linked to genealogies, arguments about the Law and the prohibition of marriage and of certain foods. In between, it deals with the organization of the community: public prayer, the conduct of men and women in worship, the qualifications required of overseers and deacons, the care of widows, the pay and discipline of elders, the situation of slaves and the danger of love of money. It also contains personal advice to the young Timothy.","name":"The title Pros Timotheon A, “First to Timothy,” comes from the recipient, called “my true child in the faith” (1:2); the number distinguishes it from the second letter to him. Since the 18th century, 1 and 2 Timothy and Titus have been called the “Pastoral Epistles,” because they deal with the care of the churches and their leaders.","facts":[["Position","15th book of the New Testament; 10th of the Pauline letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","6"],["Verses","113"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter of instructions to a co-worker (pastoral letter)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Against false doctrine","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Timothy must restrain those who get lost in myths and genealogies; Paul recalls that he was a blasphemer and persecutor and received mercy, and mentions Hymenaeus and Alexander as examples of shipwreck in the faith."},{"title":"Order in worship and leaders","from":2,"to":3,"text":"Prayers for all, including rulers; instructions to men and women; qualifications of overseers and deacons; the church as “the pillar and foundation of the truth,” and a short hymn to Christ (3:16)."},{"title":"False teachings and Timothy's conduct","from":4,"to":4,"text":"Against those who forbid marriage and foods created by God; Timothy must be an example, devote himself to reading and teaching and not neglect the gift he received."},{"title":"Groups in the church","from":5,"to":6,"text":"How to treat older and younger people, the support and enrollment of widows, the honor and correction of elders, the duty of slaves (5:1–6:2)."},{"title":"Wealth and final charge","from":6,"to":6,"text":"The love of money as a root of evils, counsel to the rich and the final appeal: fight the good fight of the faith and guard what has been entrusted (6:3–21)."}],"extra":{"title":"“A trustworthy saying”","text":"The Pastoral Epistles use five times the Greek formula pistos ho logos (in the BSB, “this is a trustworthy saying”) to introduce or confirm a brief sentence, which seems to have circulated in the churches as a summary of the faith or a known rule: in 1 Timothy 1:15, 3:1 and 4:9, in 2 Timothy 2:11 and in Titus 3:8. The formula does not appear in Paul's other letters."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1–6","ch":1,"note":"presents himself as a former persecutor who received mercy, “of whom I am the worst” (1:15)"},{"name":"Timothy","chapters":"1–6","ch":4,"note":"young co-worker left in Ephesus; must be an example despite his youth (4:12) and take care of his health (5:23)"},{"name":"Hymenaeus and Alexander","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"two who “shipwrecked” their faith and were handed over to Satan to be taught not to blaspheme (1:19–20)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The letter was received as Paul's and is quoted under his name by Irenaeus, c. 180; Polycarp, at the beginning of the 2nd century, already seems to know it. Since the journey of 1:3 does not fit well into Acts, the ancient tradition, recorded by Eusebius in the 4th century, supposed that Paul was released after his first imprisonment in Rome, returned to the East (Ephesus, Macedonia, Crete) and only later was arrested again and killed. In this framework, 1 Timothy would date from c. 62–64.","academic":"The three Pastoral Epistles are, among the thirteen that bear Paul's name, the most disputed, and most critical scholars, since the 19th century, consider them written by a disciple in Paul's name, generally between c. 90 and 120. The arguments are the vocabulary and style, very different from those of the undisputed letters; a more settled organization of the churches, with overseers, elders, deacons and an enrolled group of widows; the emphasis on “sound doctrine” and on the “deposit” to be guarded; and the difficulty of fitting the journeys into what is known of Paul's life. It is also noted that Marcion, c. 140, did not include them in his collection, which can be explained in more than one way. An important minority defends Paul's authorship: the differences would come from the kind of letter, the subject, a late phase of the apostle's life or the use of a secretary with freedom in drafting, and the release after the first Roman imprisonment would be plausible. Others propose an intermediate position, with authentic fragments incorporated by a later author."},"events":{"intro":"The situation described (Paul going to Macedonia and Timothy left in Ephesus) does not clearly correspond to any moment narrated in Acts. For this reason the dating depends on the position on authorship. The dates are approximate.","axis":[45,125],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Timothy joins Paul","from":49,"to":50,"kind":"pessoa","note":"at Lystra, on the second journey (Acts 16:1–3)"},{"label":"Paul in Ephesus","from":53,"to":56,"kind":"evento","note":"about three years (Acts 19)"},{"label":"Paul a prisoner in Rome","from":60,"to":62,"kind":"evento","note":"end of the account in Acts"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":62,"to":64,"kind":"escrita","note":"after a supposed release of Paul"},{"label":"Death of Paul, according to tradition","from":64,"to":67,"kind":"pessoa","note":"in Rome, under Nero; date uncertain"},{"label":"Writing, according to many scholars","from":90,"to":120,"kind":"escrita","note":"by a disciple of Paul, perhaps in Asia Minor"}]},"context":"Ephesus, capital of the province of Asia, was one of the main centers of early Christianity; Paul worked there for about three years, and later the city was also associated with the tradition of John. The churches met in houses, and the household (with husband, wife, children and slaves) serves as a model for describing the church as “God's household” (3:15). The teachings combated are not described in detail: there is talk of “myths” and “endless genealogies” (1:4), of teachers of the Law, of the prohibition of marriage and of foods and of a “knowledge” (in Greek, gnōsis) falsely so called (6:20). Some see in this early forms of Gnostic currents, others speculative Jewish-Christian currents; the text does not allow a secure identification.","places":["Ephesus","Macedonia"],"themes":["sound doctrine","false teachers","prayer and worship","overseers, deacons and elders","widows","wealth and contentment","the leader's example"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:15","text":"This is a trustworthy saying, worthy of full acceptance: Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, of whom I am the worst."},{"ref":"2:5","text":"For there is one God, and there is one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus,"},{"ref":"4:12","text":"Let no one despise your youth, but set an example for the believers in speech, in conduct, in love, in faith, in purity."},{"ref":"6:10","text":"For the love of money is the root of all kinds of evil. By craving it, some have wandered away from the faith and pierced themselves with many sorrows."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source and the position on authorship. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"2TI":{"t":{"1":"Not ashamed of the gospel","2":"A soldier of Christ and an approved worker","3":"Difficult times and the value of the Scriptures","4":"Preach the word; the end of the race"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Τιμόθεον Βʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Timotheon B","summary":"2 Timothy has the form of a farewell letter. Paul appears imprisoned in Rome, abandoned by many and sure that death is approaching: “I am already being poured out like a drink offering” (4:6). He writes to Timothy, whom he calls “my beloved child,” to encourage him not to be ashamed of the gospel, to endure hardship like a soldier, to pass the teaching on to faithful people and to remain in the Scriptures he has known since childhood, at a time of deceiving teachers. The last chapter, with the review of his own life and a series of requests and news about co-workers, is one of the most personal passages attributed to Paul.","name":"The title Pros Timotheon B, “Second to Timothy,” comes from the recipient, called “my beloved child” (1:2); the number distinguishes it from the first letter to him. With 1 Timothy and Titus, it forms the group of the “Pastoral Epistles.”","facts":[["Position","16th book of the New Testament; 11th of the Pauline letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","4"],["Verses","83"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","personal letter of exhortation, in a farewell tone (pastoral letter)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Not being ashamed","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Paul recalls Timothy's faith, inherited from his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice, asks him to fan into flame the gift of God and not to be ashamed of the prisoner; he contrasts those who deserted him in Asia with Onesiphorus, who searched for him in Rome."},{"title":"Steadfastness in service","from":2,"to":2,"text":"Images of the soldier, the athlete and the farmer; a saying about dying and living with Christ; avoiding useless quarrels, like those of Hymenaeus and Philetus, and correcting with gentleness."},{"title":"Difficult times and the Scriptures","from":3,"to":3,"text":"A portrait of the wicked in the last days; Paul's example in persecutions; the Scriptures, inspired by God and useful for teaching and correction."},{"title":"Final charge and news","from":4,"to":4,"text":"“Preach the word”; Paul sees his own life coming to an end; asks Timothy to come before winter, to bring Mark, the cloak and the parchments, and tells how he was left alone at his first defense."}],"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1–4","ch":4,"note":"imprisoned and awaiting death, reviews his life: “I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith” (4:7)"},{"name":"Timothy","chapters":"1–4","ch":1,"note":"recipient; son of a Jewish mother and a Greek father (Acts 16:1), formed in the Scriptures since childhood (3:15)"},{"name":"Lois and Eunice","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"Timothy's grandmother and mother, remembered for their sincere faith (1:5)"},{"name":"Onesiphorus","chapters":"1; 4","ch":1,"note":"searched for Paul in Rome and was not ashamed of his chains (1:16–17)"},{"name":"Hymenaeus and Philetus","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"said that the resurrection had already taken place (2:17–18)"},{"name":"Luke, Mark, Demas and Tychicus","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"“Only Luke is with me”; Mark should come; Demas has gone to Thessalonica; Tychicus was sent to Ephesus (4:10–12)"},{"name":"Alexander the coppersmith","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"strongly opposed Paul's message (4:14–15)"},{"name":"Prisca and Aquila","chapters":"4","ch":4,"note":"Paul's old fellow workers, greeted at the end (4:19)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition considers 2 Timothy Paul's last letter, written in Rome during a second imprisonment, shortly before his death under Nero, c. 64–67. Eusebius, in the 4th century, already understood the “first defense” of 4:16 as a sign that Paul had been released once and later arrested again.","academic":"With 1 Timothy and Titus, 2 Timothy forms the group of the Pastoral Epistles, which most critical scholars attribute to a disciple of Paul writing in his name between c. 90 and 120, because of their own vocabulary and style, their concern for the faithful transmission of teaching and the difficulty of fitting the letters into Paul's life. 2 Timothy, however, is often treated separately: it is the most personal of the three, full of names, requests and concrete details, such as the cloak left in Troas (4:13). For this reason some researchers who doubt 1 Timothy and Titus accept that 2 Timothy is by Paul or preserves authentic notes of his, especially in chapter 4; others see these details as a device to bring the figure of the apostle to life; and a minority defends Paul's authorship of all three letters."},"events":{"intro":"The letter presupposes Paul imprisoned in Rome, already after a first hearing, and awaiting condemnation. If it is his, it is from the last years of Nero; if it is by a disciple, it is some decades later. The dates are approximate.","axis":[45,125],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Timothy joins Paul","from":49,"to":50,"kind":"pessoa","note":"at Lystra, on the second journey (Acts 16:1–3)"},{"label":"Paul a prisoner in Rome","from":60,"to":62,"kind":"evento","note":"end of the account in Acts"},{"label":"Fire of Rome and Nero's persecution","from":64,"to":64,"kind":"evento","note":"Tacitus describes the punishment of Christians in the city"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":64,"to":67,"kind":"escrita","note":"in Rome, shortly before Paul's death"},{"label":"Death of Paul, according to tradition","from":64,"to":67,"kind":"pessoa","note":"beheaded in Rome, according to later authors; date uncertain"},{"label":"Writing, according to many scholars","from":90,"to":120,"kind":"escrita","note":"by a disciple of Paul"}]},"context":"The letter places Paul in Rome (1:17), imprisoned in chains and without the relative freedom of the rented house described at the end of Acts. The Christians of the city had suffered Nero's violence after the fire of 64, and ancient tradition, from Clement of Rome (c. 96) on, speaks of the deaths of Peter and Paul in the capital. Timothy, for his part, seems to be in Asia, probably in Ephesus, in a region where many had deserted Paul (1:15). The text reflects a generation of transition: the founders are disappearing, and the concern is that the teaching received pass to “faithful men who will be qualified to teach others as well” (2:2).","places":["Rome","Ephesus and the province of Asia","Troas","Thessalonica, Galatia and Dalmatia","Corinth and Miletus"],"themes":["not being ashamed of the gospel","suffering and perseverance","passing on the teaching","the Scriptures","false teachers","Paul's farewell"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:8","text":"So do not be ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, or of me, His prisoner. Instead, join me in suffering for the gospel by the power of God."},{"ref":"2:15","text":"Make every effort to present yourself approved to God, an unashamed workman who accurately handles the word of truth."},{"ref":"3:16–17","text":"All Scripture is God-breathed and is useful for instruction, for conviction, for correction, and for training in righteousness, so that the man of God may be complete, fully equipped for every good work."},{"ref":"4:7–8","text":"I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith. From now on there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous Judge, will award to me on that day—and not only to me, but to all who crave His appearing."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source and the position on authorship. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"TIT":{"t":{"1":"Elders in Crete and the rebellious","2":"Teaching for each group; grace has appeared","3":"Good works and renewal by the Spirit"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Τίτον","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Titon","summary":"Titus is a brief letter to a co-worker left in Crete to organize the churches and appoint elders “in every town” (1:5). It describes the qualifications required of these leaders and asks for firmness against “rebellious” teachers, especially those of the circumcision group, interested in myths and purity rules. It then directs what to teach each group (older men, younger men, women, slaves) and grounds this behavior in the grace of God, which has appeared and trains us for a sober and upright life. It ends with the reminder that salvation comes from God's mercy, not from works, and with travel instructions.","name":"The title Pros Titon, “To Titus,” comes from the recipient, called “my true child in our common faith” (1:4). With 1 and 2 Timothy, it forms the group of the “Pastoral Epistles.”","facts":[["Position","17th book of the New Testament; 12th of the Pauline letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","3"],["Verses","46"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter of instructions to a co-worker (pastoral letter)"]],"structure":[{"title":"Leaders and opponents","from":1,"to":1,"text":"A long greeting; Titus must appoint blameless elders and silence those who teach for dishonest gain, with an unflattering quotation about the Cretans (1:12)."},{"title":"Teaching for each group","from":2,"to":2,"text":"Counsel to older men, older women, young people and slaves, grounded in the grace of God that has appeared and in the hope of Christ's coming."},{"title":"Public life and salvation","from":3,"to":3,"text":"Respect for the authorities and gentleness toward all, because God saved by mercy, through “the washing of new birth”; avoiding disputes; requests about Artemas, Tychicus, Zenas and Apollos."}],"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1; 3","ch":1,"note":"sender; intends to spend the winter in Nicopolis (3:12)"},{"name":"Titus","chapters":"1–3","ch":1,"note":"Paul's Greek co-worker, who was not compelled to be circumcised (Galatians 2:3) and was his envoy to Corinth (2 Corinthians 7–8)"},{"name":"Artemas and Tychicus","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"one of them will replace Titus in Crete (3:12)"},{"name":"Zenas and Apollos","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"Zenas is a lawyer; Titus must help them on their journey (3:13)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition received the letter as Paul's. Since Acts mentions Crete only in passing, on Paul's voyage as a prisoner to Rome (Acts 27:7–13), tradition places it in the same period as 1 Timothy: after a release from the first Roman imprisonment, when Paul would have worked in Crete and planned to spend the winter in Nicopolis, c. 62–64.","academic":"Like 1 and 2 Timothy, Titus is considered by most critical scholars the work of a disciple who wrote in Paul's name, generally between c. 90 and 120, for the same reasons: its own vocabulary and style, the emphasis on “sound doctrine” and on the organization of leaders, and an itinerary that appears neither in the undisputed letters nor in Acts. Those who defend Paul's authorship place the letter after Acts, in the final phase of his life, and explain the differences by the subject and the use of a secretary. It is noted that, in Titus 1:5–7, the terms elder and overseer (literally “supervisor”) seem to designate the same office, as in Acts 20:17, 28, which some take as a sign of a still little hierarchical organization."},"events":{"intro":"No episode of the letter can be dated with certainty. The timeline presents the traditional position and that of many scholars; the dates are approximate.","axis":[45,125],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Titus in Jerusalem with Paul","from":48,"to":49,"kind":"pessoa","note":"Galatians 2:1–3; approximate date"},{"label":"Titus sent to Corinth","from":55,"to":56,"kind":"pessoa","note":"2 Corinthians 7–8"},{"label":"Paul passes Crete as a prisoner","from":59,"to":60,"kind":"evento","note":"on the way to Rome (Acts 27:7–13)"},{"label":"Writing, according to tradition","from":62,"to":64,"kind":"escrita","note":"after a supposed release of Paul"},{"label":"Writing, according to many scholars","from":90,"to":120,"kind":"escrita","note":"by a disciple of Paul"}]},"context":"Crete, a large island south of the Aegean Sea, was conquered by Rome in 67 BC and later joined in a single province with Cyrenaica, in North Africa, and had cities with Jewish communities (Acts 2:11 mentions Cretans in Jerusalem at Pentecost). The Greeks repeated an old saying that the Cretans were liars; the line quoted in 1:12 has been attributed since antiquity to Epimenides, a Cretan poet and sage of the 6th century BC. Nicopolis, where Paul intends to spend the winter, is probably the city in Epirus, on the western coast of Greece, founded by Octavian after the battle of Actium (31 BC).","places":["Crete","Nicopolis"],"themes":["sound doctrine","elders and overseers","false teachers","the conduct of each group","the grace that trains","salvation by mercy","good works"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"2:11–13","text":"For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to everyone. It instructs us to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live sensible, upright, and godly lives in the present age, as we await the blessed hope and glorious appearance of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ."},{"ref":"3:4–5","text":"But when the kindness of God our Savior and His love for mankind appeared, He saved us, not by the righteous deeds we had done, but according to His mercy, through the washing of new birth and renewal by the Holy Spirit."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source and the position on authorship. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"PHM":{"t":{"1":"Paul intercedes for Onesimus"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Φιλήμονα","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Philemona","summary":"Philemon is the shortest of Paul's letters, a note of a single chapter. In prison, Paul writes to Philemon, a Christian in whose house a church meets, about Onesimus, his slave, who became a Christian while with Paul. Without giving orders, although he says he could, Paul asks Philemon to receive him “no longer as a slave” but “as a beloved brother” (1:16), offers to pay for any loss and hints that he expects even more than he asks. The letter is a living example of the art of persuasion in antiquity and an important text in discussions about the Christian faith and slavery.","name":"The title Pros Philemona, “To Philemon,” comes from the main recipient, called “our beloved fellow worker” (1:1). Despite the personal tone, the greeting also includes Apphia, Archippus and the church that meets in the house, so that the request would be heard by the whole community.","facts":[["Position","18th book of the New Testament; 13th and last of Paul's letters, in canonical order"],["Chapters","1"],["Verses","25"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","personal letter of recommendation and request"]],"structure":[{"title":"Greeting and gratitude","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Paul and Timothy greet Philemon, Apphia, Archippus and the church in the house; Paul gives thanks for Philemon's love and faith, which has refreshed the saints (1:1–7)."},{"title":"The request for Onesimus","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Paul appeals instead of ordering; Onesimus, formerly “useless,” is now useful; may Philemon receive him as he would receive Paul himself, who takes on any debt (1:8–21)."},{"title":"Plans and greetings","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Paul asks them to prepare a guest room for him, since he hopes to be released, and sends greetings from Epaphras, Mark, Aristarchus, Demas and Luke (1:22–25)."}],"extra":{"title":"A play on words","text":"The name Onesimus, common among slaves, means “useful” or “profitable” in Greek. Paul plays on this when he says that the one who was formerly “useless” is now “useful both to you and to me” (1:11), using the Greek words achrēstos and euchrēstos, which, according to many interpreters, also echoed in sound the title Christos. Further on, when he asks “let me have some benefit from you” (1:20), he uses a verb from the same root as the name."},"people":[{"name":"Paul","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"“a prisoner of Christ Jesus” (1:1); writes in his own hand the promise to pay (1:19)"},{"name":"Philemon","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"a well-to-do Christian, Paul's fellow worker, in whose house a church meets; the owner of Onesimus"},{"name":"Onesimus","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"Philemon's slave, whom Paul calls “my child,” whom he fathered in his chains (1:10)"},{"name":"Apphia and Archippus","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"greeted with Philemon; many think Apphia was his wife and Archippus, perhaps, his son; Archippus also appears in Colossians 4:17"},{"name":"Epaphras, Mark, Aristarchus, Demas and Luke","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"Paul's companions who send greetings; the same names appear in Colossians 4:10–14"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Paul's authorship was never disputed; the letter was already part of Marcion's collection, c. 140, and appears in the Muratorian Fragment. Because of the link with Colossians (Onesimus, Archippus and the same companions), tradition placed it in Paul's imprisonment in Rome, c. 60–62, sent together with Colossians.","academic":"Philemon is among the seven letters that practically all scholars attribute to Paul, alongside Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians and 1 Thessalonians. The debated point is the place of imprisonment: Rome (c. 60–62) or Ephesus (c. 54–55), the latter closer to Colossae, which would make easier Onesimus's journey to Paul and the request for lodging in verse 22; Caesarea has also been proposed. Onesimus's situation is also debated: the traditional reading sees him as a runaway slave, perhaps after causing his master some loss; others think he sought Paul out as a friend of his master to intercede in a disagreement, a practice known in Roman law; others still, that he was sent by the church itself to serve Paul. And what exactly Paul is asking for is debated: forgiveness, Onesimus's return to help him or his freedom."},"events":{"intro":"The letter gives no dates, and the date depends on the place of Paul's imprisonment. The dates are approximate.","axis":[45,115],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Paul in Ephesus","from":53,"to":56,"kind":"evento","note":"at this time the gospel reaches Colossae, through Epaphras"},{"label":"Writing, if in Ephesus","from":54,"to":55,"kind":"escrita","note":"hypothesis of many scholars"},{"label":"Writing, if in Rome","from":60,"to":62,"kind":"escrita","note":"traditional position, together with Colossians"},{"label":"Ignatius mentions a bishop Onesimus in Ephesus","from":107,"to":110,"kind":"pessoa","note":"some identify him with the Onesimus of the letter, a hypothesis without confirmation"}]},"context":"Slavery was a structural part of Roman society: slaves worked in houses, workshops and fields, could hold positions of trust and sometimes obtained their freedom, but legally belonged to their owners, who could severely punish runaways. The churches gathered, in the same houses, masters and slaves. From the letter to the Colossians, which mentions Onesimus as “one of you” (Colossians 4:9) and Archippus, it is understood that Philemon lived in Colossae, in the Lycus valley, in Asia Minor. A letter of the Roman writer Pliny the Younger, from the early 2nd century, interceding with a friend for a repentant freedman, is often read alongside Philemon as an example of the same kind of request.","places":["Colossae","the place of Paul's imprisonment (Rome or Ephesus)"],"themes":["reconciliation","brotherhood in Christ","slavery","persuasion and freedom","fellowship and hospitality"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:8–9","text":"So although in Christ I am bold enough to order you to do what is proper, I prefer to appeal on the basis of love. For I, Paul, am now aged, and a prisoner of Christ Jesus as well."},{"ref":"1:10–11","text":"I appeal to you for my child Onesimus, whose father I became while I was in chains. Formerly he was useless to you, but now he has become useful both to you and to me."},{"ref":"1:15–16","text":"For perhaps this is why he was separated from you for a while, so that you might have him back for good— no longer as a slave, but better than a slave, as a beloved brother. He is especially beloved to me, but even more so to you, both in person and in the Lord."}],"note":"Dates are approximate and vary according to the source and the place adopted for Paul's imprisonment. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"HEB":{"t":{"1":"The Son, superior to the angels","2":"Such a great salvation; the Son made a brother","3":"Jesus and Moses; the danger of unbelief","4":"The promised rest and the living word","5":"The high priest; a call to maturity","6":"A warning and the certainty of the promise","7":"A priest in the order of Melchizedek","8":"The heavenly sanctuary and the new covenant","9":"The old worship and the blood of Christ","10":"One sacrifice; persevere in confidence","11":"The faith of the ancients","12":"Running with eyes on Jesus; discipline","13":"Final exhortations and greetings"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πρὸς Ἑβραίους","title_orig_xlit":"Pros Hebraious","summary":"Hebrews is a long appeal to Christians tempted to lose heart and abandon the faith. The author argues that God, after speaking in many ways through the prophets, has spoken definitively through the Son, superior to the angels and to Moses. The center of the book presents Jesus as high priest “in the order of Melchizedek,” who entered the heavenly sanctuary with His own blood and offered a single sacrifice, inaugurating the new covenant announced by Jeremiah. Between the parts of the exposition come serious warnings and encouragement, and chapter 11 runs through the history of Israel recalling those who lived by faith. The book ends with practical counsel and greetings, like a letter.","name":"The title “To the Hebrews” (Pros Hebraious) is not part of the text; it already appears in the oldest manuscripts, from the end of the 2nd century, and reflects the impression that the recipients were Christians of Jewish origin, because of the central place the book gives to the priesthood, the sacrifices and the Scriptures of Israel. The author himself calls what he wrote a “word of exhortation” (13:22).","facts":[["Position","19th book of the New Testament; comes right after Paul's letters and before the General Epistles"],["Chapters","13"],["Verses","303"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","written sermon (exhortation), with a letter ending"]],"structure":[{"title":"The Son, God's final word","from":1,"to":4,"text":"The Son is greater than the angels and than Moses; those who have heard the message are called not to harden their hearts, like the wilderness generation, and to enter God's rest (1:1–4:13)."},{"title":"The high priest","from":4,"to":7,"text":"Jesus is the high priest who sympathizes with human weaknesses; after an appeal to maturity, the author compares Him to Melchizedek, a priest without genealogy and forever (4:14–7:28)."},{"title":"New covenant and single sacrifice","from":8,"to":10,"text":"The earthly sanctuary was a copy and shadow; Christ entered the heavenly sanctuary and, with a single offering, does what the repeated sacrifices could not do (8:1–10:18)."},{"title":"Persevering in faith","from":10,"to":12,"text":"A call to draw near to God with confidence, a warning against falling away, the gallery of those who lived by faith, the race with eyes fixed on Jesus and the contrast between Sinai and Mount Zion (10:19–12:29)."},{"title":"Final exhortations","from":13,"to":13,"text":"Brotherly love, hospitality, care for prisoners, faithfulness in marriage, respect for leaders, a request for prayer and greetings."}],"extra":{"title":"A sermon in the form of a letter","text":"Hebrews does not begin as a letter: there is no sender's name or greeting, and only the last chapter has the personal tone of letters. The body of the text alternates exposition and appeal, like a sermon, and the warnings return at intervals (2:1–4; 3:7–4:13; 5:11–6:12; 10:26–31; 12:14–29). The argument rests on comparisons: the Son and the angels, Jesus and Moses, the priesthood of Aaron and that of Melchizedek, the first and the new covenant, and the word “better” appears many times. The Greek is the most polished in the New Testament, and the quotations follow the Greek translation of the Scriptures (the Septuagint), with emphasis on Psalms 2, 95 and 110 and on Jeremiah 31, quoted at length in 8:8–12."},"people":[{"name":"Jesus, the Son","chapters":"1–2; 4–10; 12","ch":1,"note":"presented as Son, brother of human beings, high priest and mediator of a new covenant"},{"name":"Moses","chapters":"3; 11","ch":3,"note":"a faithful servant in God's house, compared with the Son; also remembered among the examples of faith"},{"name":"Melchizedek","chapters":"5–7","ch":7,"note":"king of Salem and priest who blessed Abraham (Genesis 14); a model of Christ's priesthood"},{"name":"Abraham","chapters":"6–7; 11","ch":11,"note":"received the promise with an oath, gave a tenth to Melchizedek and set out without knowing where he was going"},{"name":"Witnesses of faith","chapters":"11","ch":11,"note":"Abel, Enoch, Noah, Sarah, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, Rahab, the judges, David, the prophets and others"},{"name":"Timothy","chapters":"13","ch":13,"note":"the author announces that he has been released and hopes to visit the recipients with him (13:23)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The book does not say who wrote it, and the early Church was divided. In the East, especially in Alexandria, it was received among Paul's letters; Clement of Alexandria thought Paul had written it in Hebrew and Luke had translated it, and Origen, noting that the style is not Paul's, judged that the ideas were his and the composition another's, concluding that only God knows who wrote it. In the West, Pauline authorship was slow to be accepted; Tertullian attributed it to Barnabas. From the 4th and 5th centuries on, the attribution to Paul prevailed and held for a long time.","academic":"Today most scholars, from various traditions, consider that Paul is not the author: the vocabulary, the style and the way of arguing are different, and the author says he received the message from those who heard the Lord (2:3). Barnabas, Apollos, Silas, Luke, Priscilla and others have been proposed, without any name prevailing. The date is also debated. The book is quoted (or at least known) in the letter of Clement of Rome, from c. 96, which sets a limit. Many place it before 70, because it speaks of worship as something still being practiced and does not mention the destruction of the temple; others observe that the author describes the tabernacle of the wilderness, not the temple, and prefer the 80s or 90s. The recipients are generally seen as Christians of Jewish origin, perhaps in Rome or Italy (13:24), but this too is debated."},"events":{"intro":"Hebrews does not narrate a story, but it was written for a community that already had a history: it received the message from witnesses, went through a time of persecution, with imprisonments and loss of property (10:32–34), and was now in danger of losing heart. The dates are approximate; that of the writing is the most debated.","axis":[25,100],"timeline":[{"label":"Death and resurrection of Jesus","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Expulsion of Jews from Rome by Claudius","from":49,"to":49,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 49; some relate it to 10:32–34, if the recipients were in Rome"},{"label":"Nero's persecution in Rome","from":64,"to":64,"kind":"evento","note":"after the fire of the city"},{"label":"Destruction of the temple in Jerusalem","from":70,"to":70,"kind":"evento","note":"the book does not mention it, which weighs in the debate about the date"},{"label":"Writing of the book","from":60,"to":95,"kind":"escrita","note":"a wide range: before 70 for some, in the 80s–90s for others"},{"label":"Letter of Clement of Rome","from":95,"to":97,"kind":"escrita","note":"c. 96; uses expressions from Hebrews, which shows the book was already circulating"}]},"context":"The author writes to a community that knew the Scriptures of Israel well in the Greek version and had been in the faith for some time, to the point of needing to be teachers (5:12). It had suffered insults, imprisonments and confiscation of property, but without reaching martyrdom (12:4), and some were giving up meeting together (10:25). The argument about the priesthood and the sacrifices assumes readers for whom Israel's worship carried great weight, but the book always speaks of the tabernacle described in Exodus and Leviticus, not of Herod's temple. The greeting “those from Italy send you greetings” (13:24) may indicate that the author was in Italy or that he was writing to it, together with Italians living abroad.","places":["the tabernacle of the wilderness and the heavenly sanctuary","Salem","Mount Sinai and Mount Zion","Egypt and the wilderness","Italy"],"themes":["the superiority of the Son","the high priesthood of Christ","the new covenant","sacrifice and purification","faith and perseverance","warning against falling away","God's rest"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"4:12","text":"For the word of God is living and active. Sharper than any double-edged sword, it pierces even to dividing soul and spirit, joints and marrow. It judges the thoughts and intentions of the heart."},{"ref":"4:15–16","text":"For we do not have a high priest who is unable to sympathize with our weaknesses, but we have one who was tempted in every way that we are, yet was without sin. Let us then approach the throne of grace with confidence, so that we may receive mercy and find grace to help us in our time of need."},{"ref":"11:1","text":"Now faith is the assurance of what we hope for and the certainty of what we do not see."},{"ref":"12:1–2","text":"Therefore, since we are surrounded by such a great cloud of witnesses, let us throw off every encumbrance and the sin that so easily entangles, and let us run with endurance the race set out for us. Let us fix our eyes on Jesus, the author and perfecter of our faith, who for the joy set before Him endured the cross, scorning its shame, and sat down at the right hand of the throne of God."}],"note":"The authorship, date and recipients of Hebrews are uncertain; the dates of the timeline are approximate and the positions presented are the most discussed, without claiming to resolve the question. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"JAS":{"t":{"1":"Trials, wisdom and doing the word","2":"Favoritism; faith without works is dead","3":"The tongue and the wisdom from above","4":"Quarrels, friendship with the world and arrogance","5":"A warning to the rich; patience and prayer"},"about":{"title_orig":"Ἰακώβου","title_orig_xlit":"Iakobou","summary":"James is a set of practical teachings addressed to “the twelve tribes of the Dispersion” (1:1). In short sentences and images drawn from everyday life (the mirror, the horse's bit, the ship's rudder, the flower that withers, the farmer who waits for the rain), it deals with trials, controlling the tongue, favoritism toward the rich, the exploitation of workers, arrogance in making plans and prayer. The best-known passage states that faith without deeds is dead (2:14–26). There is almost no doctrinal exposition: the interest is in a faith that shows itself in conduct and in care for the poor.","name":"In the Greek manuscripts, the title is simply “of James” (Iakobou), sometimes with “letter.” The name Iakobos is the Greek form of Jacob; in English, “James” comes, through the Late Latin Iacomus and Old French, from Iacobus. In the canon, the letter opens the group of the seven General, or Catholic, Epistles (of James, Peter, John and Jude), so called because they are not addressed to a particular church or person.","facts":[["Position","20th book of the New Testament; 1st of the General (or Catholic) Epistles"],["Chapters","5"],["Verses","108"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter of exhortation, close to wisdom literature"]],"structure":[{"title":"Trials and the word","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Trials produce perseverance; wisdom is asked of God; temptation is born of one's own desire; the word must be done and not merely heard."},{"title":"Favoritism and faith with deeds","from":2,"to":2,"text":"Honoring the rich and despising the poor goes against the law of love; a faith that does not act, like that of someone who merely wishes the needy well, is dead; Abraham and Rahab are the examples."},{"title":"The tongue and wisdom","from":3,"to":4,"text":"The tongue, small like a bit or a rudder, can set everything on fire; the wisdom from above is peaceable, while envy and ambition breed quarrels; whoever wants to be a friend of the world opposes God (3:1–4:10)."},{"title":"Judgments, plans and riches","from":4,"to":5,"text":"Not speaking against a brother, not making plans as if tomorrow were guaranteed; a denunciation of the rich who withhold the workers' wages (4:11–5:6)."},{"title":"Patience and prayer","from":5,"to":5,"text":"Waiting for the coming of the Lord as the farmer waits for the harvest, not swearing oaths, praying for the sick, confessing faults to one another and bringing back the one who has strayed (5:7–20)."}],"extra":{"title":"Wisdom in short sentences","text":"James has some fifty imperatives in a little over a hundred verses and moves from one subject to another without long transitions, in the manner of the wisdom books, such as Proverbs and Sirach. Many of its sentences recall sayings of Jesus that appear in the Sermon on the Mount in Matthew (the blessedness of the poor, asking and receiving, not swearing oaths, 5:12), although almost never as a quotation. The name of Jesus appears only twice (1:1; 2:1)."},"people":[{"name":"James","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"introduces himself only as “a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ”"},{"name":"Abraham","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"his faith “was working with his actions” when he offered Isaac (2:21–23)"},{"name":"Rahab","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"welcomed Israel's messengers in Jericho; another example of a faith that acts (2:25)"},{"name":"Job","chapters":"5","ch":5,"note":"remembered for his perseverance in suffering (5:11)"},{"name":"Elijah","chapters":"5","ch":5,"note":"“a man just like us,” an example of the power of prayer (5:17–18)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition identifies the author with James, the brother of the Lord (Mark 6:3; Galatians 1:19), who led the church in Jerusalem (Acts 15; 21:18) and, according to the historian Josephus, was killed in 62. In that case, the letter would be earlier than 62; some place it in the 40s, which would make it one of the first Christian writings, and others near the end of James's life. The letter was slow to be accepted by all the churches: Eusebius, in the 4th century, still lists it among the books disputed by some, although read publicly in most churches.","academic":"Scholars are divided. Many hold that the letter comes from James, the brother of Jesus, or from his circle, pointing to its Jewish tone, its closeness to the sayings of Jesus and the absence of questions from later periods. Many others consider it written in James's name, perhaps between 70 and 100, noting the careful Greek, hardly expected of a Galilean (although the use of secretaries and the bilingualism of the region make the argument debatable), and the apparent response to a reading of Paul on faith and works. It is also debated whether 2:14–26 argues against Paul, against a distorted reading of Paul or against neither. At the Reformation, Luther called it an “epistle of straw” compared with other books, but kept it in his Bible."},"events":{"intro":"The letter mentions no events. The timeline shows what is known of James, the brother of the Lord, and the two date ranges most proposed for the writing. The dates are approximate.","axis":[25,105],"timeline":[{"label":"Death and resurrection of Jesus","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"James would be among the witnesses of the resurrection (1 Corinthians 15:7)"},{"label":"James at the head of the church in Jerusalem","from":40,"to":62,"kind":"pessoa","note":"approximate date (Galatians 2:9; Acts 15; 21)"},{"label":"Jerusalem meeting","from":48,"to":50,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 49; James speaks at the meeting about the Gentiles (Acts 15)"},{"label":"Death of James","from":62,"to":62,"kind":"evento","note":"stoned in Jerusalem, according to Josephus"},{"label":"Writing, if by James","from":45,"to":62,"kind":"escrita","note":"traditional position and that of some scholars"},{"label":"Writing, if in James's name","from":70,"to":100,"kind":"escrita","note":"position of other scholars"}]},"context":"The recipients are called “the twelve tribes of the Dispersion,” an expression that recalls the Jews scattered outside the land of Israel and that here may designate Christians of Jewish origin or, figuratively, the whole church. They meet in an “assembly” (2:2; in Greek, synagōgē), live among merchants who travel to do business (4:13) and landowners who fail to pay those who mow their fields (5:4). The poor are the majority and suffer from courts and creditors (2:6). The picture fits the agricultural and commercial life of the eastern Mediterranean in the 1st century, without indicating a city.","places":["the Dispersion (the communities outside the land of Israel)","Jerusalem, where James led the church"],"themes":["trial and perseverance","faith and deeds","the use of the tongue","rich and poor","wisdom from above","humility","prayer"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:22","text":"Be doers of the word, and not hearers only. Otherwise, you are deceiving yourselves."},{"ref":"1:27","text":"Pure and undefiled religion before our God and Father is this: to care for orphans and widows in their distress, and to keep oneself from being polluted by the world."},{"ref":"2:26","text":"As the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without deeds is dead."}],"note":"The authorship and date of the letter are debated; the dates of the timeline are approximate. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"1PE":{"t":{"1":"A living hope and a call to holiness","2":"Living stones; conduct among the pagans","3":"Life at home; suffering for doing good","4":"Suffering as a Christian; serving with gifts","5":"To the elders; humility and watchfulness"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πέτρου Αʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Petrou A","summary":"1 Peter is a letter of encouragement to Christians scattered through the provinces of northern and central Asia Minor, called “exiles” (1:1). They suffered suspicion, slander and hostility for having left the customs of the society in which they lived. The letter reminds them that they have been born again to a living hope, that they form the people of God (“a royal priesthood, a holy nation,” 2:9) and that unjust suffering, endured while doing good, unites them to Christ, who also suffered. It gives guidance for life in society and at home, for the elders who care for the communities and for watchfulness against the adversary.","name":"The title “First of Peter” (Petrou A) was given when the General Epistles were gathered, to distinguish it from 2 Peter. The name Peter comes from the Greek Petros, “rock,” a translation of the Aramaic Kepha (Cephas), the nickname Jesus gave to Simon.","facts":[["Position","21st book of the New Testament; 2nd of the General (or Catholic) Epistles"],["Chapters","5"],["Verses","105"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","circular letter of exhortation"]],"structure":[{"title":"A people born again","from":1,"to":2,"text":"Praise for the living hope, joy amid trials, a call to holiness and love, and the image of the living stones that form a spiritual house (1:1–2:10)."},{"title":"Conduct among outsiders","from":2,"to":3,"text":"Living so that accusers may see good deeds: respect for the authorities, counsel to household slaves, to wives and to husbands, and the example of Christ who suffered without retaliating (2:11–3:12)."},{"title":"Suffering for doing good","from":3,"to":4,"text":"Being ready to give a reason for the hope with gentleness; Christ suffered for the unrighteous; whoever suffers “as a Christian” should not be ashamed (3:13–4:19)."},{"title":"Elders and farewell","from":5,"to":5,"text":"The elders must shepherd without greed or domineering; all must be humble and watchful; greetings from Silvanus, from Mark and from the church “in Babylon.”"}],"extra":{"title":"Christ and the spirits in prison","text":"One of the most difficult passages of the New Testament is in 3:18–20 (and perhaps in 4:6): Christ, put to death in the flesh and made alive in the spirit, “went and preached to the spirits in prison,” linked to the days of Noah. There are several ancient and modern readings: a proclamation to the dead between death and resurrection, the preaching made through Noah to his generation, or an announcement of victory to rebellious spiritual beings, in light of Jewish traditions about the angels of Genesis 6. The line of the Creed about Christ's descent to the realm of the dead was often associated with this text."},"people":[{"name":"Peter","chapters":"1; 5","ch":1,"note":"presents himself as an apostle and as a “fellow elder” and witness of Christ's sufferings (5:1)"},{"name":"Silvanus","chapters":"5","ch":5,"note":"through him the letter was written or sent (5:12); possibly the Silas who was Paul's companion"},{"name":"Mark","chapters":"5","ch":5,"note":"called by Peter “my son” (5:13); tradition links him to the Gospel of Mark"},{"name":"Sarah","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"remembered as an example for wives (3:6)"},{"name":"Noah","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"in his days, a few people were saved through water, a figure of baptism (3:20–21)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The letter presents itself as Peter's, and the early Church accepted it without difficulty: it seems to be known by Polycarp, at the beginning of the 2nd century, and is attributed to Peter by Irenaeus, at the end of that century. The “Babylon” from which it is written (5:13) is understood as a figurative name for Rome, where, according to tradition, Peter lived in his last years and died in Nero's persecution. For this reason the letter is placed in the early 60s, before the apostle's death, c. 64–68.","academic":"Scholars are divided. For many, the letter was written in Peter's name by someone of the circle linked to him in Rome, perhaps between 70 and 95: the Greek is careful and close to literary style, the quotations follow the Septuagint, there are points of contact with Paul's letters, and the use of “Babylon” for Rome is more common after the destruction of Jerusalem in 70. For others, the letter is Peter's, who would have used Silvanus as secretary or drafter (5:12), which would explain the quality of the Greek, and is earlier than 68. There is greater consensus that the sufferings described are social and local hostility, not an official imperial persecution throughout the region."},"events":{"intro":"The letter narrates no events. The timeline places the recipients and the two positions on the date. The dates are approximate.","axis":[25,115],"band":{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68},"timeline":[{"label":"Death and resurrection of Jesus","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Peter in the Jerusalem church and on journeys","from":33,"to":50,"kind":"pessoa","note":"Acts 1–12; 15; Galatians 2"},{"label":"Fire of Rome and Nero's persecution","from":64,"to":64,"kind":"evento","note":"according to tradition, Peter dies in Rome between 64 and 68"},{"label":"Writing, if by Peter","from":60,"to":68,"kind":"escrita","note":"traditional position and that of some scholars"},{"label":"Writing, if in Peter's name","from":70,"to":95,"kind":"escrita","note":"position of other scholars"},{"label":"Pliny writes to Trajan about the Christians of Bithynia","from":110,"to":112,"kind":"evento","note":"c. 111–112; shows Christians being denounced in one of the letter's regions"}]},"context":"Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia and Bithynia were Roman provinces covering much of present-day Turkey; the order in which they appear may follow the route of whoever carried the letter. The recipients seem to be, in large part, of non-Jewish origin, since the letter recalls the life they led before, doing “what the pagans choose to do” (4:3). By leaving the cults, the festivals and certain habits of the city, they came to be seen as strange and suspect, and were spoken against “as evildoers” (2:12). The letter calls them foreigners and exiles, applying to them titles of Israel, and guides them within the structures of the ancient household: masters and slaves, husbands and wives.","places":["Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia and Bithynia","“Babylon” (probably Rome)"],"themes":["living hope","suffering and glory","holiness","the people of God","conduct among outsiders","the example of Christ","humility and watchfulness"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:3","text":"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ! By His great mercy He has given us new birth into a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead,"},{"ref":"2:9","text":"But you are a chosen people, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession, to proclaim the virtues of Him who called you out of darkness into His marvelous light."},{"ref":"5:7","text":"Cast all your anxiety on Him, because He cares for you."}],"note":"Authorship and date are debated; the dates of the timeline are approximate. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"2PE":{"t":{"1":"Growing in faith; witness and prophecy","2":"False teachers and their judgment","3":"The scoffers and the day of the Lord"},"about":{"title_orig":"Πέτρου Βʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Petrou B","summary":"2 Peter has the form of a testament: the apostle, knowing that his death is near (1:14), leaves a last reminder to the faithful. He asks them to grow in a chain of virtues that runs from faith to love, recalls that the message about Christ is not based on myths but on the testimony of those who saw His glory on the mountain, and on the word of the prophets. He harshly denounces false teachers who exploit the community and live without restraint, and answers those who mocked the delay of the Lord's coming: for God, a thousand years are like a day, and the delay is patience to give time for repentance.","name":"The title “Second of Peter” (Petrou B) distinguishes it from 1 Peter, to which the letter itself refers: “this is now my second letter” (3:1).","facts":[["Position","22nd book of the New Testament; 3rd of the General (or Catholic) Epistles"],["Chapters","3"],["Verses","61"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter in the form of a testament"]],"structure":[{"title":"Growing and remembering","from":1,"to":1,"text":"God has given everything for a life of godliness; faith must be accompanied by virtue, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly kindness and love; Peter recalls the transfiguration and the divine origin of prophecy."},{"title":"The false teachers","from":2,"to":2,"text":"As there were false prophets, there will be false teachers; the examples of the angels who sinned, the flood and Sodom show that God judges the ungodly and rescues the righteous, like Noah and Lot."},{"title":"The promise of His coming","from":3,"to":3,"text":"The scoffers ask about the promised coming; God is not slow, but patient; the day of the Lord will come like a thief, and new heavens and a new earth are awaited; Paul's letters are mentioned as hard to understand."}],"extra":{"title":"Kinship with Jude","text":"Much of chapter 2 corresponds closely to the letter of Jude: the angels who sinned, Sodom and Gomorrah, Balaam, the waterless springs and the darkness reserved for the ungodly. The similarities are too great to be chance. Most scholars think 2 Peter used Jude, omitting the references to books outside the Hebrew canon, such as 1 Enoch; others propose the opposite or a common source."},"people":[{"name":"Simon Peter","chapters":"1; 3","ch":1,"note":"speaks as one about to die and as an eyewitness of the transfiguration (1:14–18)"},{"name":"Noah","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"“a preacher of righteousness,” preserved in the flood (2:5)"},{"name":"Lot","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"a righteous man distressed by what he saw in Sodom, who was rescued (2:7–8)"},{"name":"Balaam","chapters":"2","ch":2,"note":"a prophet who loved the wages of wickedness and was rebuked by a donkey (2:15–16)"},{"name":"Paul","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"called “our beloved brother”; his letters are mentioned alongside “the rest of the Scriptures” (3:15–16)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The letter presents itself as Simon Peter's, written shortly before his death, which tradition places in Rome between 64 and 68. Its acceptance, however, was the slowest among the books of the New Testament: Origen, in the 3rd century, mentions that some doubted it, and Eusebius, in the 4th century, places it among the disputed books, although read by many. Jerome records that many doubted it because of the difference in style from 1 Peter and explains this difference by the use of different secretaries. From the end of the 4th century, the letter is part of the canon lists in East and West.","academic":"Most scholars consider 2 Peter a writing in Peter's name, in the testament genre common at the time, and place it between c. 80 and 125, which would make it one of the last books of the New Testament. The arguments are the Greek, quite different from 1 Peter, the probable use of Jude, the reference to Paul's letters already collected and read as Scripture (3:15–16) and the mention of the death of “our fathers” (3:4) as a thing of the past. A minority defends that the letter is Peter's, perhaps with another secretary, written in the 60s, and explains the differences of style by the subject and the collaborators."},"events":{"intro":"The letter refers to events of Peter's life and to the expectation of Christ's coming. The dates are approximate; that of the writing varies a great deal according to the position on authorship.","axis":[25,130],"timeline":[{"label":"Transfiguration of Jesus","from":29,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate date; recalled in 1:16–18"},{"label":"Letters of Paul","from":50,"to":62,"kind":"escrita","note":"c. 50–62 for the most accepted letters; later they circulate as a collection"},{"label":"Death of Peter in Rome","from":64,"to":68,"kind":"evento","note":"according to tradition, in Nero's persecution"},{"label":"Writing, if by Peter","from":64,"to":68,"kind":"escrita","note":"traditional position and that of a minority of scholars"},{"label":"Writing, if in Peter's name","from":80,"to":125,"kind":"escrita","note":"position of most scholars"}]},"context":"The letter indicates neither recipients nor place. Because of the mention of an earlier letter (3:1), the same communities as 1 Peter, in Asia Minor, come to mind, but this is not certain. The problem is internal: teachers who promise freedom, live dissolutely, seek profit and mock the waiting for the Lord's coming, since “our fathers fell asleep” and everything continues as before (3:4). The answer uses resources of the Greek world (it speaks of virtue and of becoming “partakers of the divine nature,” 1:4) and of the Jewish tradition about judgment and the destruction of the world by fire.","places":["the holy mountain of the transfiguration","Sodom and Gomorrah"],"themes":["growth in virtue","apostolic testimony","prophecy and Scripture","false teachers","the day of the Lord","God's patience","new heavens and a new earth"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:16","text":"For we did not follow cleverly devised fables when we made known to you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but we were eyewitnesses of His majesty."},{"ref":"1:20–21","text":"Above all, you must understand that no prophecy of Scripture comes from one’s own interpretation. For no such prophecy was ever brought forth by the will of man, but men spoke from God as they were carried along by the Holy Spirit."},{"ref":"3:8–9","text":"Beloved, do not let this one thing escape your notice: With the Lord a day is like a thousand years, and a thousand years are like a day. The Lord is not slow in keeping His promise as some understand slowness, but is patient with you, not wanting anyone to perish but everyone to come to repentance."}],"note":"Authorship and date are debated; the dates of the timeline are approximate. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"1JN":{"t":{"1":"The Word of life; walking in the light","2":"Christ our advocate; the world and the antichrists","3":"Children of God and love in practice","4":"Testing the spirits; God is love","5":"The faith that overcomes; testimony and eternal life"},"about":{"title_orig":"Ἰωάννου Αʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Ioannou A","summary":"1 John is a writing of exhortation to communities shaken by the departure of a group that had begun to teach differently about Jesus (2:19). The author, who speaks as a witness of what he heard, saw and touched (1:1), offers criteria for recognizing true fellowship with God: walking in the light, acknowledging one's own sin, keeping the commandments, confessing that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh and, above all, loving the brothers. Two statements sum up the book: “God is light” (1:5) and “God is love” (4:8). The declared purpose is to give assurance to those who believe: “so that you may know that you have eternal life” (5:13).","name":"The title “First of John” (Ioannou A) was given by tradition, which attributed this writing and the two following letters to the apostle John. The text gives neither the author's name nor the recipients'.","facts":[["Position","23rd book of the New Testament; 4th of the General (or Catholic) Epistles"],["Chapters","5"],["Verses","105"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","exhortation in letter form, with no greeting or farewell"]],"structure":[{"title":"Prologue: the Word of life","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The author proclaims what he heard, saw and touched, so that the readers may have fellowship with him, with the Father and with the Son (1:1–4)."},{"title":"Walking in the light","from":1,"to":2,"text":"God is light; whoever confesses sin is forgiven; knowing God is keeping His commandments; not loving the world; those who went out are “antichrists” (1:5–2:27)."},{"title":"Children of God","from":2,"to":4,"text":"We are children of God and will be like Him; whoever is of God practices righteousness and loves in deed and truth, unlike Cain; the spirits must be tested (2:28–4:6)."},{"title":"God is love","from":4,"to":5,"text":"Love comes from God and was shown in the sending of the Son; whoever loves God loves his brother; the faith that overcomes the world and the testimony of the Spirit, the water and the blood (4:7–5:12)."},{"title":"Conclusion","from":5,"to":5,"text":"The assurance of eternal life, prayer for brothers who sin and the final warning: “keep yourselves from idols” (5:13–21)."}],"extra":{"title":"Pairs of opposites","text":"The text moves in a spiral, returning to the same themes with small variations, and thinks in pairs: light and darkness, truth and falsehood, love and hate, life and death, children of God and children of the devil, the Spirit of truth and the spirit of deception. The vocabulary is very close to the Gospel of John (“beginning,” “Word,” “life,” “remain,” “know”), and the prologue recalls the opening of the Gospel."},"people":[{"name":"The author (“we”)","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"speaks as a witness and calls the readers “little children”"},{"name":"Jesus Christ","chapters":"1–5","ch":2,"note":"the Son who came in the flesh, the Righteous One, advocate with the Father and atoning sacrifice for sins"},{"name":"Those who went out","chapters":"2; 4","ch":2,"note":"a group that left the community and denied that Jesus is the Christ come in the flesh; called antichrists and false prophets"},{"name":"Cain","chapters":"3","ch":3,"note":"an example of one who hates his brother (3:12)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"From the end of the 2nd century (Irenaeus, the Muratorian Canon), the letter has been attributed to the apostle John son of Zebedee, author also of the Gospel. According to tradition, he lived to an advanced age in Ephesus, in the province of Asia, and wrote there, in the 90s. The letter was already known in the first half of the 2nd century: Polycarp, bishop of Smyrna, uses a sentence very similar to 4:2–3.","academic":"Scholars generally agree that 1 John comes from the same milieu as the Gospel of John, often called the Johannine community or school, but debate whether the author is the same as the Gospel's, the apostle, the “elder” of 2 and 3 John (perhaps the “John the Elder” mentioned by Papias) or another member of the group. The most proposed date is c. 90–110, shortly after the Gospel, whose message the letter seems to defend against interpretations that separated the divine Christ from the human Jesus. The link with Ephesus comes from tradition; the text mentions no place."},"events":{"intro":"The letter reflects a division within a community at the end of the 1st century. The dates are approximate.","axis":[25,140],"timeline":[{"label":"Ministry, death and resurrection of Jesus","from":28,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"the author speaks as a witness of what was seen and touched"},{"label":"Destruction of the temple in Jerusalem","from":70,"to":70,"kind":"evento","note":"according to tradition, John was already living in Asia"},{"label":"Gospel of John","from":85,"to":100,"kind":"escrita","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Writing of the letter","from":90,"to":110,"kind":"escrita","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Polycarp's letter to the Philippians","from":110,"to":135,"kind":"escrita","note":"date debated; echoes 1 John 4:2–3"}]},"context":"The letter has no greeting or recipients, but presupposes a network of churches that know the author and the preaching about Jesus. The crisis comes from within: a group has gone out (2:19), and the author insists that Jesus Christ “has come in the flesh” (4:2), that love is proved by goods given to a brother in need (3:17) and that sin cannot be denied (1:8). Many relate the opponents to currents that would later be called Docetist (for which the Son of God only seemed human) or to ideas associated with Cerinthus, but the letter gives no names, and the identification is uncertain.","places":["Ephesus and the province of Asia (by tradition)"],"themes":["fellowship with God","light and darkness","sin and forgiveness","love for one's brother","the truth about Jesus","assurance of eternal life","remaining in God"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:5","text":"And this is the message we have heard from Him and announce to you: God is light, and in Him there is no darkness at all."},{"ref":"1:8–9","text":"If we say we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us. If we confess our sins, He is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness."},{"ref":"4:7–8","text":"Beloved, let us love one another, because love comes from God. Everyone who loves has been born of God and knows God. Whoever does not love does not know God, because God is love."},{"ref":"4:19","text":"We love because He first loved us."}],"note":"In 5:7–8, some older Bibles, such as the King James Version, have an addition about “the Father, the Word and the Holy Ghost” in heaven (the so-called Johannine Comma). It is not in the ancient Greek manuscripts and entered the text from Latin manuscripts; the BSB and the SBLGNT do not include it. Dates are approximate. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"2JN":{"t":{"1":"To the elect lady: truth and love"},"about":{"title_orig":"Ἰωάννου Βʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Ioannou B","summary":"2 John is a note from “the elder” to a “chosen lady” and her children. He rejoices because some of them are walking in the truth, recalls the old commandment to love one another and warns against deceivers who do not acknowledge that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh: they must not be received into the house. It ends with the hope of a visit.","name":"The title “Second of John” (Ioannou B) follows the traditional attribution to the apostle John; in the text, the author identifies himself only as “the elder” (1:1).","facts":[["Position","24th book of the New Testament; 5th of the General (or Catholic) Epistles"],["Chapters","1"],["Verses","13"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","brief letter"]],"structure":[{"title":"Greeting","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The elder greets the chosen lady and her children, “whom I love in the truth” (1:1–3)."},{"title":"Walking in love","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Joy over those who walk in the truth and the request that they love one another, keeping the commandments (1:4–6)."},{"title":"Beware of deceivers","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Whoever does not confess the coming of Jesus Christ in the flesh must not be received or greeted (1:7–11)."},{"title":"Farewell","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The elder prefers to speak “face to face” and sends greetings from the children of the chosen sister (1:12–13)."}],"people":[{"name":"The elder","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"an author who introduces himself by his title, as in 3 John"},{"name":"The chosen lady and her children","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"probably a local church and its members, in figurative language; some think of an actual woman"},{"name":"The chosen sister","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"probably the church from which the elder writes (1:13)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition attributes the letter to the apostle John, who in his old age would have identified himself as “the elder,” and places it in Ephesus, at the end of the 1st century. Its acceptance was slower than that of 1 John: Eusebius, in the 4th century, mentions it among the books disputed by some.","academic":"Scholars link 2 John to the same milieu as 1 John, with almost identical vocabulary and concerns, and many attribute it to the same author who wrote 3 John. It is debated whether this elder is the apostle, a leader known as “John the Elder” (mentioned by Papias) or someone else. The most proposed date is c. 90–110."},"events":{"intro":"The three letters of John come from the same period and milieu. The dates are approximate.","axis":[60,130],"timeline":[{"label":"Gospel of John","from":85,"to":100,"kind":"escrita","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Writing of the letters of John","from":90,"to":110,"kind":"escrita","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Papias writes about the elders","from":110,"to":130,"kind":"escrita","note":"date debated; mentions a “John the Elder”"}]},"context":"Traveling preachers depended on the hospitality of the churches, which met in houses. For this reason, refusing to receive someone was the practical way of not supporting their message. The elder applies this measure to those who deny that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh, the same question that appears in 1 John.","places":["the community of the chosen lady (unidentified)","Ephesus and the province of Asia (by tradition)"],"themes":["truth","mutual love","the commandment from the beginning","Jesus Christ come in the flesh","hospitality and discernment"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:5–6","text":"And now I urge you, dear lady—not as a new commandment to you, but one we have had from the beginning—that we love one another. And this is love, that we walk according to His commandments. This is the very commandment you have heard from the beginning, that you must walk in love."},{"ref":"1:7","text":"For many deceivers have gone out into the world, refusing to confess the coming of Jesus Christ in the flesh. Any such person is the deceiver and the antichrist."}],"note":"Approximate dates. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"3JN":{"t":{"1":"To Gaius: hospitality, Diotrephes and Demetrius"},"about":{"title_orig":"Ἰωάννου Γʹ","title_orig_xlit":"Ioannou G","summary":"3 John is a personal note from “the elder” to his beloved Gaius, praised for welcoming brothers who traveled to proclaim the gospel. The elder criticizes Diotrephes, who wants to rule the church, does not receive the envoys and expels those who welcome them, and recommends Demetrius. It is the shortest book of the Bible in number of words in the original Greek.","name":"The title “Third of John” (Ioannou G; the letter gamma stands for 3) follows the traditional attribution to the apostle John; in the text, the author identifies himself only as “the elder” (1:1).","facts":[["Position","25th book of the New Testament; 6th of the General (or Catholic) Epistles"],["Chapters","1"],["Verses","14"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","brief personal letter"]],"structure":[{"title":"Greeting","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Wishes for good health and joy at knowing that Gaius walks in the truth (1:1–4)."},{"title":"Gaius's hospitality","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Gaius welcomes the traveling brothers; supporting them is to be a fellow worker for the truth (1:5–8)."},{"title":"Diotrephes and Demetrius","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Diotrephes rejects the elder and the envoys; Demetrius is well spoken of by everyone (1:9–12)."},{"title":"Farewell","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The elder hopes to see Gaius soon and sends greetings from the friends (1:13–14)."}],"people":[{"name":"The elder","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"an author who introduces himself by his title, as in 2 John"},{"name":"Gaius","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"recipient, known for welcoming the missionaries; a very common name, with no certain link to other men named Gaius in the New Testament"},{"name":"Diotrephes","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"a local leader who “loves to be first” and does not receive the elder or the envoys (1:9–10)"},{"name":"Demetrius","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"recommended by the elder; perhaps the bearer of the letter (1:12)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"Tradition attributes the letter to the apostle John, “the elder,” who would have written from Ephesus at the end of the 1st century. Like 2 John, it was accepted more slowly than 1 John, and Eusebius mentions it among the books disputed by some.","academic":"Scholars generally attribute 2 and 3 John to the same author, called “the elder,” linked to the milieu of 1 John and the Gospel of John. It is debated whether he is the apostle, “John the Elder” (mentioned by Papias) or another leader. The most proposed date is c. 90–110. The conflict with Diotrephes is read in various ways: a dispute over authority, a doctrinal disagreement or tension between a local leadership that was establishing itself and the authority of an elder from outside."},"events":{"intro":"The three letters of John come from the same period and milieu. The dates are approximate.","axis":[60,130],"timeline":[{"label":"Gospel of John","from":85,"to":100,"kind":"escrita","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Writing of the letters of John","from":90,"to":110,"kind":"escrita","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Papias writes about the elders","from":110,"to":130,"kind":"escrita","note":"date debated; mentions a “John the Elder”"}]},"context":"In the 1st century, missionaries traveled from city to city and depended on Christian families to lodge them and help them on their way, “accepting nothing from the pagans” (1:7). The letter shows a network of churches in contact through letters and envoys, and a moment when the authority of a leader from outside could be refused by a local leader.","places":["the church of Gaius (unidentified)","Ephesus and the province of Asia (by tradition)"],"themes":["truth","hospitality","support for missionaries","authority in the church","imitating what is good"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:4","text":"I have no greater joy than to hear that my children are walking in the truth."},{"ref":"1:11","text":"Beloved, do not imitate what is evil, but what is good. The one who does good is of God; the one who does evil has not seen God."}],"note":"Approximate dates. Some editions divide the last verse in two (14 and 15), as the BSB does; the Greek text used in the app has 14 verses. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"JUD":{"t":{"1":"Contending for the faith against the ungodly"},"about":{"title_orig":"Ἰούδα","title_orig_xlit":"Iouda","summary":"Jude is a short and forceful letter. The author intended to write about the salvation we share, but changed subject to warn against people who had slipped into the community and turned grace into a pretext for immorality. He draws on examples of judgment from the Scriptures and from Jewish traditions (the people in the wilderness, the rebellious angels, Sodom and Gomorrah, Cain, Balaam and Korah) and quotes a prophecy attributed to Enoch. He closes with counsel of mercy and with one of the best-known doxologies of the New Testament.","name":"The title “of Jude” (Iouda) comes from the author's name, the Greek form of Judah; English Bibles call him Jude to distinguish him from Judas Iscariot, with whom he has no connection. Some traditions identify him with the apostle Judas Thaddaeus, an identification many do not accept.","facts":[["Position","26th book of the New Testament; 7th and last of the General (or Catholic) Epistles"],["Chapters","1"],["Verses","25"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","letter of warning"]],"structure":[{"title":"Greeting and reason","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Jude greets those who are called and explains why he writes: it is necessary to contend for the faith, because ungodly people have crept in unnoticed (1:1–4)."},{"title":"Examples of judgment","from":1,"to":1,"text":"The people who did not believe in the wilderness, the angels who abandoned their dwelling and Sodom and Gomorrah show that God judges (1:5–7)."},{"title":"Portrait of the intruders","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Compared to Cain, Balaam and Korah, to clouds without water and trees without fruit; Enoch's prophecy announces judgment on them (1:8–16)."},{"title":"Exhortation and doxology","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Remembering the words of the apostles, building oneself up in the faith, showing mercy to those who doubt and praising God, who is able to keep from stumbling (1:17–25)."}],"extra":{"title":"Books beyond the Hebrew canon","text":"Jude expressly quotes a prophecy of Enoch (1:14–15), which corresponds to a passage of 1 Enoch, a Jewish work preserved complete in Ethiopic and in Aramaic fragments found at Qumran. The dispute between the archangel Michael and the devil over the body of Moses (1:9) is not in the Old Testament; ancient writers, such as Origen, linked it to a work called the Assumption of Moses, now known only in part. This use caused doubts about the letter in some places in the early Church, as Jerome and Eusebius record."},"people":[{"name":"Jude","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"introduces himself as “a servant of Jesus Christ and a brother of James”"},{"name":"Michael the archangel","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"did not dare to slander the devil, but said, “The Lord rebuke you!” (1:9)"},{"name":"Enoch","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"“the seventh from Adam,” to whom a prophecy of judgment is attributed (1:14)"},{"name":"Cain, Balaam and Korah","chapters":"1","ch":1,"note":"models of envy, greed and rebellion applied to the intruders (1:11)"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The author is identified as Jude, brother of James and, therefore, one of the brothers of Jesus mentioned in the Gospels (Mark 6:3). According to Hegesippus, quoted by Eusebius, grandsons of Jude were still living in Galilee in the time of Domitian. The letter was accepted early in several churches (it appears in the Muratorian Canon and is used by Tertullian and Clement of Alexandria), but, because of the quotation of Enoch, it was questioned by some and is among the disputed books in Eusebius's list.","academic":"Scholars are divided. Many accept that the letter comes from Jude, the brother of Jesus, or from his circle, in a Jewish-Christian setting in or near Palestine, and date it between 50 and 90. Many others see it as written in Jude's name, at the end of the 1st century, noting the reference to the faith “once for all entrusted” and to the words spoken “by the apostles” (1:3; 1:17) as things of the past. There is broad agreement that 2 Peter 2 depends on Jude."},"events":{"intro":"The letter refers to no datable events. The timeline shows what is known of Jesus' family and the ranges proposed for the writing. The dates are approximate.","axis":[25,110],"timeline":[{"label":"Death and resurrection of Jesus","from":30,"to":33,"kind":"evento","note":"approximate date"},{"label":"Brothers of Jesus among the first Christians","from":33,"to":60,"kind":"pessoa","note":"Acts 1:14; 1 Corinthians 9:5"},{"label":"Death of James, the brother of Jesus","from":62,"to":62,"kind":"evento","note":"according to Josephus"},{"label":"Writing, if by Jude","from":50,"to":90,"kind":"escrita","note":"traditional position and that of some scholars"},{"label":"Writing, if in Jude's name","from":80,"to":100,"kind":"escrita","note":"position of other scholars"},{"label":"Grandsons of Jude before Domitian","from":81,"to":96,"kind":"evento","note":"account of Hegesippus, quoted by Eusebius"}]},"context":"The letter assumes readers familiar with the Scriptures of Israel and with Jewish traditions that circulated outside them, such as those of 1 Enoch, which suggests a Jewish-Christian setting. The intruders take part in the community's love feasts (the agape meals, 1:12), reject authority, seek advantage and divide the community (1:19). The text does not say where the recipients were.","places":["Egypt and the wilderness","Sodom and Gomorrah and the surrounding cities"],"themes":["the faith entrusted to the saints","false teachers","God's judgment","mercy","perseverance","praise to God"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:3","text":"Beloved, although I made every effort to write to you about the salvation we share, I felt it necessary to write and urge you to contend earnestly for the faith entrusted once for all to the saints."},{"ref":"1:24–25","text":"Now to Him who is able to keep you from stumbling and to present you unblemished in His glorious presence, with great joy— to the only God our Savior be glory, majesty, dominion, and authority through Jesus Christ our Lord before all time, and now, and for all eternity. Amen."}],"note":"Authorship and date are debated; the dates of the timeline are approximate. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}},"REV":{"t":{"1":"The revelation to John on Patmos","2":"Letters to Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum and Thyatira","3":"Letters to Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea","4":"The throne in heaven","5":"The sealed scroll and the Lamb","6":"The first six seals","7":"The sealed and the great multitude","8":"The seventh seal; the first trumpets","9":"The fifth and sixth trumpets","10":"The angel and the little scroll","11":"The two witnesses; the seventh trumpet","12":"The woman, the child and the dragon","13":"The beasts from the sea and from the earth","14":"The Lamb on Zion; the harvests of the earth","15":"The song of the victors; seven bowls","16":"The seven bowls of wrath","17":"The great prostitute and the beast","18":"The fall of Babylon","19":"The wedding of the Lamb; the faithful rider","20":"The thousand years and the final judgment","21":"A new heaven, a new earth, a new Jerusalem","22":"The river of life; “Come, Lord Jesus”"},"about":{"title_orig":"Ἀποκάλυψις Ἰωάννου","title_orig_xlit":"Apokalypsis Ioannou","summary":"Revelation reports the visions that a Christian named John received on the island of Patmos and that he sends to seven churches of the province of Asia. It begins with a vision of the risen Christ and with seven messages, one for each church, which praise, rebuke and call to faithfulness. Then the gaze turns to God's throne in heaven, where the Lamb who was slain receives the sealed scroll; there follow series of seven seals, seven trumpets and seven bowls, interspersed with visions of the woman and the dragon, the two beasts and the fall of Babylon, the great city that oppresses the peoples. The book ends with the final defeat of evil, the judgment and the vision of a new heaven, a new earth and the new Jerusalem, where God dwells with His people and there is no more death or mourning.","name":"The first word of the book in Greek is apokalypsis, “revelation,” literally the act of removing a veil (1:1). From it comes the title “Apocalypse,” or “Revelation of John,” which appears in various forms in the manuscripts. Because of the book, the word came to designate a literary genre and, in everyday language, the idea of catastrophe, a meaning the text does not have.","facts":[["Position","27th and last book of the New Testament and of the Bible"],["Chapters","22"],["Verses","405"],["Original language","Greek"],["Genre","apocalypse and prophecy, within a letter frame"]],"structure":[{"title":"Prologue and opening vision","from":1,"to":1,"text":"Title, greeting to the seven churches and the vision of “one like a son of man” among seven lampstands, who tells John to write."},{"title":"Messages to the seven churches","from":2,"to":3,"text":"Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea receive praise, censure and promises “to the one who overcomes.”"},{"title":"The throne and the Lamb","from":4,"to":5,"text":"Heavenly worship around the throne; only the Lamb who was slain is worthy to open the scroll sealed with seven seals."},{"title":"The seven seals","from":6,"to":8,"text":"The horsemen, the martyrs, the cosmic signs; a pause shows the 144,000 sealed and an innumerable multitude; the seventh seal brings silence in heaven (6:1–8:5)."},{"title":"The seven trumpets","from":8,"to":11,"text":"Calamities on the earth, the sea and the heavens; the angel with the little scroll, the two witnesses and the seventh trumpet announcing the kingdom of God (8:6–11:19)."},{"title":"The conflict with the dragon","from":12,"to":14,"text":"The woman, the child and the dragon; the beast that rises from the sea and the one that rises from the earth; the Lamb on Mount Zion, the angels' proclamations and the harvests of the earth."},{"title":"The seven bowls","from":15,"to":16,"text":"The victors sing beside the sea of glass; seven angels pour out the bowls of wrath; the kings gather at the place called Armageddon."},{"title":"The fall of Babylon","from":17,"to":19,"text":"The great prostitute seated on the beast, the fall of the city, the lament of the kings and merchants and the praise in heaven (17:1–19:10)."},{"title":"Victory and judgment","from":19,"to":20,"text":"The rider called Faithful and True defeats the beast; Satan is bound for a thousand years, released and defeated; the dead are judged before the great white throne (19:11–20:15)."},{"title":"The new creation","from":21,"to":22,"text":"A new heaven and a new earth; the new Jerusalem comes down out of heaven; the river and the tree of life (21:1–22:5)."},{"title":"Epilogue","from":22,"to":22,"text":"Warnings, the promise “Yes, I am coming soon” and the church's answer: “Come, Lord Jesus!” (22:6–21)."}],"extra":{"title":"Symbols, numbers and ways of reading","text":"Revelation speaks in images, many drawn from Ezekiel, Daniel, Zechariah and Isaiah: a Lamb with seven horns and seven eyes, living creatures full of eyes, a woman clothed with the sun, beasts with heads and horns. The numbers carry symbolic weight: seven (fullness) organizes the book; twelve and its multiples, such as the 144,000, recall the people of God; three and a half years (“forty-two months,” “1,260 days”) indicate a limited time of trial. The number of the beast, 666 (13:18), appears as 616 in some manuscripts; many explain it by the numerical value of the letters of a name, and the most cited proposal is “Nero Caesar” written in Hebrew letters, which gives 666 or 616 depending on the spelling. Throughout history, the book has been read in different ways: as referring to events of the 1st century (preterist reading), as a map of the history of the Church (historicist), as an announcement of events at the end of time (futurist) or as a symbolic portrait of the ongoing struggle between good and evil (idealist); many interpreters combine these readings."},"people":[{"name":"John","chapters":"1; 22","ch":1,"note":"a servant of Jesus Christ and brother of the readers, who was on Patmos “because of the word of God” (1:9)"},{"name":"Jesus Christ, the Lamb","chapters":"1; 5; 19","ch":5,"note":"appears as the glorious Son of Man, the Lamb who was slain and the rider Faithful and True"},{"name":"The elders and the four living creatures","chapters":"4–5","ch":4,"note":"twenty-four elders and four living creatures who worship before the throne"},{"name":"The two witnesses","chapters":"11","ch":11,"note":"prophesy, are killed by the beast and rise after three and a half days"},{"name":"The woman, the child and the dragon","chapters":"12","ch":12,"note":"the dragon, “that ancient serpent,” pursues the woman who gave birth to a son; Michael and his angels defeat him in heaven"},{"name":"The two beasts","chapters":"13","ch":13,"note":"one rises from the sea and receives power from the dragon; the other rises from the earth and leads the peoples to worship it"},{"name":"The great prostitute, Babylon","chapters":"17–18","ch":17,"note":"a city that rules over the kings of the earth and is drunk with the blood of the saints"}],"writing":{"tradition":"The author calls himself John (1:1, 4, 9; 22:8) and presents himself as a prophet, without saying he is an apostle. From Justin, in the 2nd century, tradition identified him with the apostle John son of Zebedee, who would have lived in Ephesus. As to the date, Irenaeus, around 180, wrote that the vision was seen “toward the end of Domitian's reign,” that is, c. 95, and this was the predominant position in the early Church; some ancient writings, however, link John's exile to the time of Nero. In the East, the book was slow to be accepted: Dionysius of Alexandria, in the 3rd century, observed that the Greek and the style differ greatly from the Gospel of John and attributed the book to another John, and several Greek- and Syriac-speaking churches included it in the canon only late.","academic":"Many scholars think the author is a Christian prophet named John, known to the churches of Asia, distinct from the author of the Gospel, based on the differences of language and thought; others keep the link with the apostle or with the Johannine circle. On the date, there are two main positions. One follows Irenaeus and places the book at the end of Domitian's reign, c. 95, pointing to the emphasis on emperor worship in Asia and the use of “Babylon” for Rome, common after 70. The other places it at the end of the 60s, soon after Nero's persecution or during the crisis of 68–69, based on the list of kings in 17:9–11, the mention of the temple in 11:1–2 and the possible allusion to Nero in the number of the beast. Both positions have weighty defenders, and some accept that the book was composed in stages."},"events":{"intro":"Revelation does not narrate history directly, but its images engage with the situation of the churches of Asia under the Roman Empire. The timeline shows the two periods proposed for the writing, without deciding between them. The dates are approximate.","axis":[50,110],"timeline":[{"label":"Reign of Nero","from":54,"to":68,"kind":"evento","note":"emperor from 54 to 68"},{"label":"Fire of Rome and persecution of Christians","from":64,"to":64,"kind":"evento","note":"Christians in Rome are killed on Nero's orders"},{"label":"Jewish war and destruction of the temple","from":66,"to":70,"kind":"evento","note":"Jerusalem and the temple are destroyed in 70"},{"label":"Year of the four emperors","from":68,"to":69,"kind":"evento","note":"after Nero's death, Galba, Otho, Vitellius and Vespasian contend for power"},{"label":"Writing, according to the earlier dating","from":64,"to":70,"kind":"escrita","note":"a proposal linking the book to Nero's time or soon after"},{"label":"Reign of Domitian","from":81,"to":96,"kind":"evento","note":"emperor from 81 to 96"},{"label":"Writing, according to Irenaeus","from":90,"to":96,"kind":"escrita","note":"“toward the end of Domitian's reign,” c. 95"}]},"context":"The seven churches were in cities of the Roman province of Asia, in the west of present-day Turkey, linked by roads: Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea. They were prosperous cities, with temples dedicated to Rome and to the emperors, trade guilds with religious banquets and old Jewish communities. The messages to the churches show varied problems: pressure to eat meat offered to idols, conflicts with local synagogues, a martyr named Antipas in Pergamum (2:13), but also complacency and wealth, as in Laodicea. Patmos, where John was, is a small island in the Aegean Sea, southwest of Ephesus. The critique of Babylon, seated on seven hills (17:9) and enriched by the trade of the peoples (18), is generally understood as a critique of Rome.","places":["Patmos","Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea","Mount Zion","the Euphrates River","Armageddon","“Babylon”","the new Jerusalem"],"themes":["God's sovereignty","the victorious Lamb","faithfulness and witness","judgment on evil","the power of empire","worship and adoration","new creation"],"key_verses":[{"ref":"1:9","text":"I, John, your brother and partner in the tribulation and kingdom and perseverance that are in Jesus, was on the island of Patmos because of the word of God and my testimony about Jesus."},{"ref":"11:15","text":"Then the seventh angel sounded his trumpet, and loud voices called out in heaven: “The kingdom of the world has become the kingdom of our Lord and of His Christ, and He will reign forever and ever.”"},{"ref":"21:3–4","text":"And I heard a loud voice from the throne saying: “Behold, the dwelling place of God is with man, and He will dwell with them. They will be His people, and God Himself will be with them as their God. ‘He will wipe away every tear from their eyes,’ and there will be no more death or mourning or crying or pain, for the former things have passed away.”"},{"ref":"22:20","text":"He who testifies to these things says, “Yes, I am coming soon.” Amen. Come, Lord Jesus!"}],"note":"The date of Revelation (the time of Nero or of Domitian) and the identity of the author remain under discussion; both positions are presented without taking sides, and the dates are approximate. The sentence “And the dragon stood on the shore of the sea” is numbered 12:18 in some Bibles and is part of 13:1 in others; the BSB places it at the end of 12:17. The verses quoted are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB)."}}};